window.NOTES={"docs": [{"slug": "guide", "title": "Complete Study Guide", "blurb": "Full theory with code, from basics to embedded C", "docx": "downloads/C_and_Embedded_C_Complete_Study_Guide.docx"}, {"slug": "cheatsheet", "title": "Interview Cheat Sheet", "blurb": "One-line answers for the night before", "docx": "downloads/C_and_Embedded_C_Interview_Cheat_Sheet.docx"}, {"slug": "datatypes", "title": "Data Types in C", "blurb": "Sizes, ranges, int in depth", "docx": "downloads/Data_Types_in_C_with_int_Explained.docx"}, {"slug": "plan", "title": "Study Plan", "blurb": "Day-by-day calendar, 30 Oct to 30 Nov", "docx": "downloads/C_and_Embedded_C_Study_Plan.docx"}], "index": [{"doc": "guide", "id": "how-to-use-this-guide", "title": "How to use this guide", "text": "Reading the code examples Part A examples run on any PC with GCC or on OnlineGDB. Part B examples marked with setup() and loop() are Arduino-style sketches for your ESP32, ESP8266 or Uno. Register-level examples (DDRB, PORTB, TCCR2A) are for the Arduino Uno (AVR) and show the idea; addresses in other examples are illustrations, so always check the datasheet of your chip. Reading the code examples Part A examples run on any PC with GCC or on OnlineGDB. Part B examples marked with setup() and loop() are Arduino-style sketches for your ESP32, ESP8266 or Uno. Register-level examples (DDRB, PORTB, TCCR2A) are for the Arduino Uno (AVR) and show the idea; addresses in other examples are illustrations, so always check the datasheet of your chip."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "contents-and-study-order", "title": "Contents and study order", "text": "No. Topic Part Suggested dates (2026) 1 Basics and Data Types A. C language Oct 30 2 Operators and Control Flow A. C language Oct 31 - Nov 1 3 Functions and Recursion A. C language Nov 2 - 3 4 Arrays and Strings A. C language Nov 4 - 6 5 Pointers A. C language Nov 9 - 10 6 Structures, Unions, Enums and typedef A. C language Nov 11 7 Dynamic Memory Allocation A. C language Nov 12 8 Storage Classes and the Preprocessor A. C language Nov 13 9 File Handling A. C language Nov 14 10 Bitwise Operators and Bit Manipulation B. Embedded C Nov 16 - 17 11 Type Qualifiers and Fixed-Width Types B. Embedded C Nov 18 12 Memory Layout, Startup Code, Padding and Endianness B. Embedded C Nov 19 13 Registers, Memory-Mapped I/O and Function Pointers B. Embedded C Nov 20 14 GPIO and Debouncing B. Embedded C Nov 23 15 Interrupts and ISR B. Embedded C Nov 24 16 Timers, PWM and ADC B. Embedded C Nov 25 - 26 17 Communication Protocols: UART, I2C and SPI B. Embedded C Nov 26 - 27 18 Embedded Design Patterns B. Embedded C Nov 28 - 29 No. Topic Part Suggested dates (2026) 1 Basics and Data Types A. C language Oct 30 2 Operators and Control Flow A. C language Oct 31 - Nov 1 3 Functions and Recursion A. C language Nov 2 - 3 4 Arrays and Strings A. C language Nov 4 - 6 5 Pointers A. C language Nov 9 - 10 6 Structures, Unions, Enums and typedef A. C language Nov 11 7 Dynamic Memory Allocation A. C language Nov 12 8 Storage Classes and the Preprocessor A. C language Nov 13 9 File Handling A. C language Nov 14 10 Bitwise Operators and Bit Manipulation B. Embedded C Nov 16 - 17 11 Type Qualifiers and Fixed-Width Types B. Embedded C Nov 18 12 Memory Layout, Startup Code, Padding and Endianness B. Embedded C Nov 19 13 Registers, Memory-Mapped I/O and Function Pointers B. Embedded C Nov 20 14 GPIO and Debouncing B. Embedded C Nov 23 15 Interrupts and ISR B. Embedded C Nov 24 16 Timers, PWM and ADC B. Embedded C Nov 25 - 26 17 Communication Protocols: UART, I2C and SPI B. Embedded C Nov 26 - 27 18 Embedded Design Patterns B. Embedded C Nov 28 - 29 The dates match the daily calendar from your study plan. If you fall behind, move the topic to the next weekend; do not skip it. TOPIC 1"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "basics-and-data-types", "title": "Basics and Data Types", "text": "In one line A C program is translated by the compiler into machine code; every variable has a data type that decides its size, how its bits are read and what you can do with it. In one line A C program is translated by the compiler into machine code; every variable has a data type that decides its size, how its bits are read and what you can do with it."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "1-1-how-a-c-program-becomes-a-running-program", "title": "1.1 How a C program becomes a running program", "text": "You write text (source code). The computer only understands machine code. Four tools turn one into the other, like a factory line. Stage Tool What it does 1. Preprocess Preprocessor Handles lines starting with # (include files, replace macros) 2. Compile Compiler Turns C into assembly and checks syntax 3. Assemble Assembler Turns assembly into an object file (machine code pieces) 4. Link Linker Joins object files and libraries into one executable Stage Tool What it does 1. Preprocess Preprocessor Handles lines starting with # (include files, replace macros) 2. Compile Compiler Turns C into assembly and checks syntax 3. Assemble Assembler Turns assembly into an object file (machine code pieces) 4. Link Linker Joins object files and libraries into one executable // hello.c #include <stdio.h> int main(void) { printf(\"Hello, embedded world!\\n\"); return 0; } Output: Hello, embedded world! Compile and run on a PC: gcc hello.c -o hello then ./hello"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "1-2-variables-and-constants", "title": "1.2 Variables and constants", "text": "A variable is a named box in memory. You choose the name, the compiler chooses the address. int age = 20; // variable: value can change const float PI = 3.14159f; // constant: compiler blocks changes #define MAX_USERS 50 // macro: text replaced before compiling age = 21; // fine // PI = 3.14; // ERROR: assignment of read-only variable"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "1-3-what-is-a-data-type", "title": "1.3 What is a data type?", "text": "A data type tells the compiler (1) how much memory to reserve, (2) how to read the bits stored there, and (3) which operations are allowed. Analogy: boxes with labels Memory is a warehouse of boxes. The type is the box size plus a label. The bits 01000001 mean the number 65 if the label says int, and the letter A if the label says char. Analogy: boxes with labels Memory is a warehouse of boxes. The type is the box size plus a label. The bits 01000001 mean the number 65 if the label says int, and the letter A if the label says char. Type Size (PC) Range / precision Format Use char 1 byte -128 to 127 (or 0 to 255) %c %d One character, small number short 2 bytes -32,768 to 32,767 %hd Small whole numbers int 4 bytes -2.1 billion to 2.1 billion %d Whole numbers long long 8 bytes about +/- 9.2 x 10^18 %lld Very large whole numbers float 4 bytes 6 to 7 digits %f Decimals double 8 bytes 15 to 16 digits %lf Precise decimals void - no value - No return value, generic pointer Type Size (PC) Range / precision Format Use char 1 byte -128 to 127 (or 0 to 255) %c %d One character, small number short 2 bytes -32,768 to 32,767 %hd Small whole numbers int 4 bytes -2.1 billion to 2.1 billion %d Whole numbers long long 8 bytes about +/- 9.2 x 10^18 %lld Very large whole numbers float 4 bytes 6 to 7 digits %f Decimals double 8 bytes 15 to 16 digits %lf Precise decimals void - no value - No return value, generic pointer Important for embedded The size of int is not fixed. It is 2 bytes on an Arduino Uno and 4 bytes on ESP32, ESP8266 and STM32. Embedded code uses uint8_t, uint16_t, uint32_t (see Topic 11). Important for embedded The size of int is not fixed. It is 2 bytes on an Arduino Uno and 4 bytes on ESP32, ESP8266 and STM32. Embedded code uses uint8_t, uint16_t, uint32_t (see Topic 11)."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "1-4-int-in-short", "title": "1.4 int in short", "text": ""}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "1-5-char-float-and-double", "title": "1.5 char, float and double", "text": "char ch = 'A'; float f = 0.1f + 0.2f; printf(\"%c %d\\n\", ch, ch); // A 65 printf(\"%c\\n\", ch + 1); // B printf(\"%.10f\\n\", f); // 0.3000000119 (not exactly 0.3) printf(\"%zu\\n\", sizeof(double)); // 8"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "1-6-input-and-output-printf-and-scanf", "title": "1.6 Input and output: printf and scanf", "text": "Specifier Type Example %d int printf(\"%d\", 25); %u unsigned int printf(\"%u\", 4000000000U); %ld %lld long, long long printf(\"%lld\", big); %f %lf float, double printf(\"%.2f\", 3.14159); prints 3.14 %c char printf(\"%c\", 'A'); %s string printf(\"%s\", \"hello\"); %x %X hexadecimal printf(\"%x\", 255); prints ff %p pointer address printf(\"%p\", (void *)&x); Specifier Type Example %d int printf(\"%d\", 25); %u unsigned int printf(\"%u\", 4000000000U); %ld %lld long, long long printf(\"%lld\", big); %f %lf float, double printf(\"%.2f\", 3.14159); prints 3.14 %c char printf(\"%c\", 'A'); %s string printf(\"%s\", \"hello\"); %x %X hexadecimal printf(\"%x\", 255); prints ff %p pointer address printf(\"%p\", (void *)&amp;x); int a; float b; printf(\"Enter an int and a float: \"); scanf(\"%d %f\", &a, &b); // note the & before each variable printf(\"You typed %d and %.2f\\n\", a, b);"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "1-7-type-casting", "title": "1.7 Type casting", "text": "int total = 7, count = 2; float wrong = total / count; // 3.0 (integer division first) float right = (float)total / count; // 3.5 (cast before dividing) int x = (int)3.99; // 3 (cut, not rounded)"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "common-mistakes", "title": "Common mistakes", "text": "Mistake Fix Using a variable before giving it a value Initialise: int x = 0; Wrong format specifier (%d for a float) Match the type; compiler warnings (-Wall) catch this Forgetting & in scanf(\"%d\", &x) scanf needs the address of the variable 5 / 2 expecting 2.5 Use 5 / 2.0 or a cast Comparing floats with == Use fabs(a - b) < 0.0001 Missing semicolon or unmatched brace Read the first compiler error; later errors are often side effects Mistake Fix Using a variable before giving it a value Initialise: int x = 0; Wrong format specifier (%d for a float) Match the type; compiler warnings (-Wall) catch this Forgetting &amp; in scanf(\"%d\", &amp;x) scanf needs the address of the variable 5 / 2 expecting 2.5 Use 5 / 2.0 or a cast Comparing floats with == Use fabs(a - b) &lt; 0.0001 Missing semicolon or unmatched brace Read the first compiler error; later errors are often side effects"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "practice-programs-type-each-one-yourself", "title": "Practice programs (type each one yourself)", "text": ""}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "interview-questions-with-short-answers", "title": "Interview questions with short answers", "text": "Q1. What is a data type? It defines the size, interpretation and allowed operations of a variable. Q2. What are the stages of compilation? Preprocessing, compiling, assembling, linking. Q3. Difference between const and #define? const creates a typed variable that the compiler protects; #define is plain text replacement before compiling and has no type. Q4. Why is sizeof(int) not always 4? The standard only sets a minimum (16 bits); the real size depends on the processor and compiler. Q5. What does void main() vs int main() mean? The standard form is int main(void), returning a status to the OS. void main is non-standard on hosted systems, though common in bare-metal code. TOPIC 2"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "operators-and-control-flow", "title": "Operators and Control Flow", "text": "In one line Operators calculate and compare values; control flow (if, switch, loops) decides which code runs and how many times. In one line Operators calculate and compare values; control flow (if, switch, loops) decides which code runs and how many times."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "2-1-operators", "title": "2.1 Operators", "text": "Group Operators Notes Arithmetic + - * / % % works only on integers Relational == != < > <= >= Result is 1 (true) or 0 (false) Logical && || ! AND, OR, NOT Assignment = += -= *= /= %= x += 5 means x = x + 5 Increment ++ -- Add or subtract 1 Ternary cond ? a : b Short if-else that gives a value Bitwise & | ^ ~ << >> Covered in Topic 10 sizeof sizeof(x) Size in bytes Group Operators Notes Arithmetic + - * / % % works only on integers Relational == != &lt; &gt; &lt;= &gt;= Result is 1 (true) or 0 (false) Logical &amp;&amp; || ! AND, OR, NOT Assignment = += -= *= /= %= x += 5 means x = x + 5 Increment ++ -- Add or subtract 1 Ternary cond ? a : b Short if-else that gives a value Bitwise &amp; | ^ ~ &lt;&lt; &gt;&gt; Covered in Topic 10 sizeof sizeof(x) Size in bytes In C, zero means false and any non-zero value means true."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "2-2-pre-increment-vs-post-increment", "title": "2.2 Pre-increment vs post-increment", "text": "int a = 5, b; b = a++; // use a first (b = 5), then add 1 (a = 6) printf(\"%d %d\\n\", a, b); a = 5; b = ++a; // add 1 first (a = 6), then use it (b = 6) printf(\"%d %d\\n\", a, b); Output: 6 5 6 6"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "2-3-short-circuit-evaluation", "title": "2.3 Short-circuit evaluation", "text": "With && and ||, C stops as soon as the answer is known. In (a != 0 && 10 / a > 2), if a is 0 the division is never evaluated, so there is no crash. Use this deliberately to guard risky checks. The classic bug: = versus == if (x = 5) assigns 5 to x and is always true. You wanted if (x == 5). A habit that helps: write if (5 == x) so a typo becomes a compile error. The classic bug: = versus == if (x = 5) assigns 5 to x and is always true. You wanted if (x == 5). A habit that helps: write if (5 == x) so a typo becomes a compile error."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "2-4-operator-precedence-high-to-low", "title": "2.4 Operator precedence (high to low)", "text": "When in doubt, use brackets. They cost nothing and remove the guesswork."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "2-5-decision-making-if-else-if-else", "title": "2.5 Decision making: if, else if, else", "text": "int marks = 72; if (marks >= 90) printf(\"Grade A\\n\"); else if (marks >= 75) printf(\"Grade B\\n\"); else if (marks >= 60) printf(\"Grade C\\n\"); else printf(\"Fail\\n\"); int max = (a > b) ? a : b; // ternary Output: Grade C"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "2-6-switch", "title": "2.6 switch", "text": "Use switch when one integer or char variable is compared with many fixed values. break ends the case; without it, execution falls through into the next case. char op = '*'; int a = 6, b = 3; switch (op) { case '+': printf(\"%d\\n\", a + b); break; case '-': printf(\"%d\\n\", a - b); break; case '*': printf(\"%d\\n\", a * b); break; case '/': printf(\"%d\\n\", a / b); break; default: printf(\"Unknown operator\\n\"); } Output: 18"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "2-7-loops", "title": "2.7 Loops", "text": "Loop Form Use when for for (init; condition; update) You know the number of repeats while while (condition) { ... } Repeat until something happens; may run 0 times do-while do { ... } while (condition); Must run at least once (menus, input checks) Loop Form Use when for for (init; condition; update) You know the number of repeats while while (condition) { ... } Repeat until something happens; may run 0 times do-while do { ... } while (condition); Must run at least once (menus, input checks) for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) printf(\"%d \", i); // 1 2 3 4 5 int n = 1234, sum = 0; while (n > 0) { sum += n % 10; // take last digit n /= 10; // drop last digit } printf(\"\\nDigit sum = %d\\n\", sum); // 10 while (1) { // infinite loop: the main loop of /* read sensors, update outputs */ // almost every embedded program }"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "2-8-nested-loops-patterns", "title": "2.8 Nested loops: patterns", "text": "for (int i = 1; i <= 4; i++) { // rows for (int j = 1; j <= i; j++) // stars in this row printf(\"* \"); printf(\"\\n\"); } Output: * * * * * * * * * * Trace it by hand: for row 1 the inner loop runs 1 time, row 2 runs 2 times, and so on. Tracing on paper is the best way to understand loops."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "common-mistakes-2", "title": "Common mistakes", "text": "Mistake Fix Using = instead of == in a condition Compare with ==; compile with -Wall Semicolon after if or for: if (x > 3); { } Remove the stray semicolon; the block then belongs to the if Forgetting break in a switch Add break unless fall-through is intentional (and comment it) Off-by-one: i <= n when array size is n Array indexes go from 0 to n-1, so use i < n Infinite loop because the variable is never updated Check that the condition can become false Comparing floats in loop conditions Loop on integers, convert to float inside Mistake Fix Using = instead of == in a condition Compare with ==; compile with -Wall Semicolon after if or for: if (x &gt; 3); { } Remove the stray semicolon; the block then belongs to the if Forgetting break in a switch Add break unless fall-through is intentional (and comment it) Off-by-one: i &lt;= n when array size is n Array indexes go from 0 to n-1, so use i &lt; n Infinite loop because the variable is never updated Check that the condition can become false Comparing floats in loop conditions Loop on integers, convert to float inside"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "practice-programs-type-each-one-yourself-2", "title": "Practice programs (type each one yourself)", "text": ""}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "interview-questions-with-short-answers-2", "title": "Interview questions with short answers", "text": "Q1. Difference between ++a and a++? Pre-increment adds 1 then uses the value; post-increment uses the value then adds 1. Q2. Difference between while and do-while? do-while checks the condition after the body, so it always runs at least once. Q3. What is short-circuit evaluation? In a && b, b is skipped if a is false; in a || b, b is skipped if a is true. Q4. What happens if you forget break in switch? Execution falls through into the next case. Q5. Why do embedded programs use while(1)? There is no OS to return to, so main loops forever handling tasks. TOPIC 3"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "functions-and-recursion", "title": "Functions and Recursion", "text": "In one line A function is a named block of code you write once and call many times; recursion is a function that calls itself to solve a smaller version of the same problem. In one line A function is a named block of code you write once and call many times; recursion is a function that calls itself to solve a smaller version of the same problem."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "3-1-why-functions", "title": "3.1 Why functions?", "text": "Analogy: a recipe card The function is the recipe. The parameters are the ingredients you hand over. The return value is the dish you get back. Calling the function is cooking from that card. Analogy: a recipe card The function is the recipe. The parameters are the ingredients you hand over. The return value is the dish you get back. Calling the function is cooking from that card."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "3-2-anatomy-of-a-function", "title": "3.2 Anatomy of a function", "text": "#include <stdio.h> int add(int a, int b); // prototype (declaration) int main(void) { int result = add(3, 4); // call printf(\"%d\\n\", result); return 0; } int add(int a, int b) { // definition return a + b; } Output: 7 Part Meaning Return type Type of the value sent back (int, float, void if nothing) Name How you call it. Use verb-like names: read_adc, set_led Parameters Inputs; types must be written for each one Prototype Tells the compiler about the function before it is used. Goes at the top or in a header file return Ends the function and hands back a value Part Meaning Return type Type of the value sent back (int, float, void if nothing) Name How you call it. Use verb-like names: read_adc, set_led Parameters Inputs; types must be written for each one Prototype Tells the compiler about the function before it is used. Goes at the top or in a header file return Ends the function and hands back a value"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "3-3-call-by-value", "title": "3.3 Call by value", "text": "C passes copies of the arguments. Changing the parameter inside the function does not change the original variable. void bad_swap(int a, int b) { int t = a; a = b; b = t; // swaps only the copies } int x = 1, y = 2; bad_swap(x, y); printf(\"%d %d\\n\", x, y); // still 1 2 Output: 1 2 To change the original you pass its address (a pointer). That is the swap fix in Topic 5."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "3-4-scope-and-lifetime", "title": "3.4 Scope and lifetime", "text": "Kind Where declared Lives Local Inside a function or block Created on entry, destroyed on exit (stack) Global Outside all functions Whole program run (data memory) static local Inside a function with static Whole program, visible only in that function Kind Where declared Lives Local Inside a function or block Created on entry, destroyed on exit (stack) Global Outside all functions Whole program run (data memory) static local Inside a function with static Whole program, visible only in that function Prefer local variables. Too many globals make code hard to debug, because any function can change them. In embedded code, globals shared with an ISR must be volatile (Topic 11)."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "3-5-recursion", "title": "3.5 Recursion", "text": "A recursive function must have (1) a base case that stops the recursion and (2) a recursive case that moves toward the base case. unsigned long factorial(unsigned int n) { if (n <= 1) // base case return 1; return n * factorial(n - 1); // recursive case } Trace of factorial(4) (calls go down, answers come back up): Call Waits for Returns factorial(4) 4 * factorial(3) 24 factorial(3) 3 * factorial(2) 6 factorial(2) 2 * factorial(1) 2 factorial(1) base case 1 Call Waits for Returns factorial(4) 4 * factorial(3) 24 factorial(3) 3 * factorial(2) 6 factorial(2) 2 * factorial(1) 2 factorial(1) base case 1"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "3-6-what-happens-in-memory-the-stack", "title": "3.6 What happens in memory: the stack", "text": "Every call pushes a stack frame (parameters, local variables, return address). When the function returns, the frame is removed. Deep recursion means many frames; run out of stack and the program crashes (stack overflow). Recursion on microcontrollers An ESP8266 or Uno has very little RAM and stack. Many embedded coding standards (MISRA) forbid recursion. Prefer loops in firmware; use recursion to learn and in tree or parsing problems on PCs. Recursion on microcontrollers An ESP8266 or Uno has very little RAM and stack. Many embedded coding standards (MISRA) forbid recursion. Prefer loops in firmware; use recursion to learn and in tree or parsing problems on PCs. int fib(int n) { // easy to read, but slow: it repeats work if (n < 2) return n; return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2); } int fib_loop(int n) { // fast and uses constant stack int a = 0, b = 1; for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { int t = a + b; a = b; b = t; } return a; }"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "3-7-splitting-code-into-files-header-files", "title": "3.7 Splitting code into files (header files)", "text": "// mathutil.h #ifndef MATHUTIL_H #define MATHUTIL_H int add(int a, int b); int max(int a, int b); #endif // mathutil.c #include \"mathutil.h\" int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; } int max(int a, int b) { return a > b ? a : b; } // main.c #include <stdio.h> #include \"mathutil.h\" int main(void) { printf(\"%d\\n\", add(2, 3)); return 0; } Build: gcc main.c mathutil.c -o app. The header is the public menu; the .c file is the kitchen."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "common-mistakes-3", "title": "Common mistakes", "text": "Mistake Fix Calling a function before declaring it Add a prototype or move the definition above main Expecting a function to modify the caller's variable Pass a pointer Missing base case in recursion Write the base case first Returning the address of a local variable Local memory disappears on return; use static or let the caller pass a buffer Not returning a value from a non-void function Every path must return Function with empty () thinking it means no arguments Write (void) to say no arguments Mistake Fix Calling a function before declaring it Add a prototype or move the definition above main Expecting a function to modify the caller's variable Pass a pointer Missing base case in recursion Write the base case first Returning the address of a local variable Local memory disappears on return; use static or let the caller pass a buffer Not returning a value from a non-void function Every path must return Function with empty () thinking it means no arguments Write (void) to say no arguments"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "practice-programs-type-each-one-yourself-3", "title": "Practice programs (type each one yourself)", "text": ""}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "interview-questions-with-short-answers-3", "title": "Interview questions with short answers", "text": "Q1. What is call by value? The function receives copies of the arguments, so changes do not affect the originals. Q2. Difference between declaration and definition? A declaration tells the compiler the signature; the definition provides the body. Q3. What is a stack frame? The block of stack memory holding a call's parameters, locals and return address. Q4. What is the base case in recursion? The condition where the function stops calling itself. Q5. Why avoid recursion in firmware? Stack is small and usage is hard to predict; overflow causes a crash that is hard to debug. Q6. What does static do to a local variable? It keeps its value between calls and lives for the whole program. TOPIC 4"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "arrays-and-strings", "title": "Arrays and Strings", "text": "In one line An array is a row of same-type variables stored side by side in memory; a string in C is simply a char array that ends with the null character '\\0'. In one line An array is a row of same-type variables stored side by side in memory; a string in C is simply a char array that ends with the null character '\\0'."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "4-1-one-dimensional-arrays", "title": "4.1 One-dimensional arrays", "text": "Analogy: a row of lockers Lockers are numbered 0, 1, 2... All lockers are the same size and sit next to each other. The array name is the building; the index picks the locker. Analogy: a row of lockers Lockers are numbered 0, 1, 2... All lockers are the same size and sit next to each other. The array name is the building; the index picks the locker. int marks[5] = {90, 85, 70, 60, 95}; // 5 ints, 20 bytes on a 32-bit int system int zeros[5] = {0}; // all zeros int auto_size[] = {1, 2, 3}; // compiler counts: size 3 printf(\"%d\\n\", marks[0]); // 90 (first element) printf(\"%d\\n\", marks[4]); // 95 (last element) for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) printf(\"%d \", marks[i]); int n = sizeof(marks) / sizeof(marks[0]); // number of elements = 5"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "4-2-two-dimensional-arrays", "title": "4.2 Two-dimensional arrays", "text": "int m[2][3] = { {1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6} }; // 2 rows, 3 columns for (int r = 0; r < 2; r++) { for (int c = 0; c < 3; c++) printf(\"%d \", m[r][c]); printf(\"\\n\"); } Output: 1 2 3 4 5 6 Memory is one long line; C stores rows one after another (row-major). So m[1][0] sits right after m[0][2]."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "4-3-passing-arrays-to-functions", "title": "4.3 Passing arrays to functions", "text": "An array passed to a function becomes a pointer to its first element, so the function can change the original. The size is lost, so pass it separately. void print_array(const int arr[], int n) { // const: we only read it for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) printf(\"%d \", arr[i]); } int find_max(const int arr[], int n) { int max = arr[0]; for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) if (arr[i] > max) max = arr[i]; return max; }"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "4-4-searching-and-sorting", "title": "4.4 Searching and sorting", "text": "// Bubble sort: repeatedly swap neighbours that are out of order void bubble_sort(int a[], int n) { for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) for (int j = 0; j < n - 1 - i; j++) if (a[j] > a[j + 1]) { int t = a[j]; a[j] = a[j + 1]; a[j + 1] = t; } } After pass 1 the largest element has \"bubbled\" to the end; after pass 2 the second largest is in place, and so on. Fine for learning; real code uses qsort or better algorithms."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "4-5-strings", "title": "4.5 Strings", "text": "C has no string type. A string is a char array whose last element is '\\0' (the null terminator, value 0). Functions find the end of the string by looking for it. char s1[] = \"hello\"; // size 6: h e l l o \\0 char s2[10] = \"hi\"; // h i \\0 and 7 more zeros char s3[] = {'o', 'k', '\\0'}; // same idea, written by hand printf(\"%s\\n\", s1); // hello printf(\"%zu\\n\", sizeof(s1)); // 6 (array size) printf(\"%zu\\n\", strlen(s1)); // 5 (characters before \\0) Function (string.h) What it does strlen(s) Number of characters, not counting \\0 strcpy(dst, src) Copies src into dst (dst must be big enough!) strncpy(dst, src, n) Safer copy with a limit; may not add \\0, so add it yourself strcmp(a, b) Returns 0 if equal, negative if a < b, positive if a > b strcat(dst, src) Appends src to the end of dst strchr(s, c) Pointer to first occurrence of c, or NULL strstr(s, sub) Pointer to first occurrence of sub, or NULL Function (string.h) What it does strlen(s) Number of characters, not counting \\0 strcpy(dst, src) Copies src into dst (dst must be big enough!) strncpy(dst, src, n) Safer copy with a limit; may not add \\0, so add it yourself strcmp(a, b) Returns 0 if equal, negative if a &lt; b, positive if a &gt; b strcat(dst, src) Appends src to the end of dst strchr(s, c) Pointer to first occurrence of c, or NULL strstr(s, sub) Pointer to first occurrence of sub, or NULL Never compare strings with == if (a == b) compares addresses, not text. Use strcmp(a, b) == 0. Never compare strings with == if (a == b) compares addresses, not text. Use strcmp(a, b) == 0."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "4-6-reading-strings-safely", "title": "4.6 Reading strings safely", "text": "char name[20]; scanf(\"%19s\", name); // reads one word, max 19 chars (leaves room for \\0) fgets(name, sizeof(name), stdin); // reads a whole line including spaces name[strcspn(name, \"\\n\")] = '\\0'; // remove the newline fgets keeps"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "4-7-writing-your-own-strlen-and-reverse", "title": "4.7 Writing your own strlen and reverse", "text": "int my_strlen(const char *s) { int n = 0; while (s[n] != '\\0') n++; return n; } void reverse(char s[]) { int i = 0, j = my_strlen(s) - 1; while (i < j) { char t = s[i]; s[i] = s[j]; s[j] = t; i++; j--; } } Embedded note: serial commands, sensor names and display text are all strings. Always size buffers carefully: a UART buffer overflow can crash the device or corrupt memory."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "common-mistakes-4", "title": "Common mistakes", "text": "Mistake Fix Index out of range (arr[5] on size 5) Loop with i < size; validate indexes Forgetting the \\0 slot: char s[5] = \"hello\"; Size must be 6, or leave it empty: char s[] = \"hello\"; Comparing strings with == Use strcmp strcpy into a buffer that is too small Check lengths or use snprintf / strncpy Using sizeof on an array parameter It gives pointer size; pass the length Modifying a string literal: char *p = \"hi\"; p[0] = 'H'; Literals are read-only; use char p[] = \"hi\"; Mistake Fix Index out of range (arr[5] on size 5) Loop with i &lt; size; validate indexes Forgetting the \\0 slot: char s[5] = \"hello\"; Size must be 6, or leave it empty: char s[] = \"hello\"; Comparing strings with == Use strcmp strcpy into a buffer that is too small Check lengths or use snprintf / strncpy Using sizeof on an array parameter It gives pointer size; pass the length Modifying a string literal: char *p = \"hi\"; p[0] = 'H'; Literals are read-only; use char p[] = \"hi\";"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "practice-programs-type-each-one-yourself-4", "title": "Practice programs (type each one yourself)", "text": ""}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "interview-questions-with-short-answers-4", "title": "Interview questions with short answers", "text": "Q1. Why do arrays start at index 0? The index is an offset from the first element: address = base + index x size. Q2. What is the null terminator? The character '\\0' (value 0) that marks the end of a C string. Q3. sizeof(\"hello\") vs strlen(\"hello\")? sizeof is 6 (includes \\0); strlen is 5. Q4. Does C check array bounds? No. Out-of-range access is undefined behaviour. Q5. Difference between char s[] = \"hi\" and char *s = \"hi\"? The first is a modifiable array copy; the second points to a read-only literal. Q6. How is a 2D array stored in memory? Row by row, in one contiguous block (row-major order). TOPIC 5"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "pointers", "title": "Pointers", "text": "In one line A pointer is a variable that stores the memory address of another variable. Pointers let functions change the caller's data, walk through arrays, and talk directly to hardware. In one line A pointer is a variable that stores the memory address of another variable. Pointers let functions change the caller's data, walk through arrays, and talk directly to hardware."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "5-1-the-idea", "title": "5.1 The idea", "text": "Analogy: house and address A variable is a house. Its value is the person living inside. The address is the house number written on a paper slip. A pointer is the slip of paper: it is not the house, it only tells you where the house is. Analogy: house and address A variable is a house. Its value is the person living inside. The address is the house number written on a paper slip. A pointer is the slip of paper: it is not the house, it only tells you where the house is. Symbol Meaning &x Address of x (\"where does x live?\") int *p Declare p as a pointer to an int *p Dereference: go to the address stored in p and use the value there NULL A pointer that points to nothing (address 0) Symbol Meaning &amp;x Address of x (\"where does x live?\") int *p Declare p as a pointer to an int *p Dereference: go to the address stored in p and use the value there NULL A pointer that points to nothing (address 0) int x = 10; int *p = &x; // p holds the address of x printf(\"%d\\n\", x); // 10 printf(\"%p\\n\", (void *)p); // address, for example 0x7ffd5c3a printf(\"%d\\n\", *p); // 10 (value at that address) *p = 99; // change x through the pointer printf(\"%d\\n\", x); // 99 Name Address (example) Value Comment x 0x1000 99 int variable p 0x1004 0x1000 pointer holding the address of x Name Address (example) Value Comment x 0x1000 99 int variable p 0x1004 0x1000 pointer holding the address of x Draw this table on paper whenever pointers confuse you. Boxes with addresses and values explain almost every pointer question."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "5-2-pointer-size", "title": "5.2 Pointer size", "text": "A pointer holds an address, so its size depends on the machine, not on what it points to: 8 bytes on 64-bit PCs, 4 bytes on ESP32, ESP8266 and ARM Cortex-M, 2 bytes on 8-bit AVR (Arduino Uno)."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "5-3-call-by-reference-the-swap-that-works", "title": "5.3 Call by reference: the swap that works", "text": "void swap(int *a, int *b) { int t = *a; *a = *b; *b = t; } int x = 1, y = 2; swap(&x, &y); printf(\"%d %d\\n\", x, y); // 2 1 Output: 2 1 C still passes by value, but the value passed is an address, so the function can reach the original. Functions that must return more than one result also use pointers (output parameters)."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "5-4-pointer-arithmetic", "title": "5.4 Pointer arithmetic", "text": "Adding 1 to a pointer moves it by one element, not one byte. For int *p, p + 1 adds sizeof(int) to the address. int arr[4] = {10, 20, 30, 40}; int *p = arr; // array name = address of first element printf(\"%d\\n\", *p); // 10 printf(\"%d\\n\", *(p + 2)); // 30 p++; // now points to arr[1] printf(\"%d\\n\", *p); // 20 // arr[i] is exactly the same as *(arr + i)"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "5-5-arrays-strings-and-pointers", "title": "5.5 Arrays, strings and pointers", "text": "char *s = \"embedded\"; // points to a read-only literal int count = 0; while (*s != '\\0') { // walk until the terminator count++; s++; } printf(\"%d\\n\", count); // 8"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "5-6-pointer-to-pointer-void-pointer-null", "title": "5.6 Pointer to pointer, void pointer, NULL", "text": ""}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "5-7-const-and-pointers", "title": "5.7 const and pointers", "text": "Declaration Meaning (read right to left) const int *p p points to a constant int: cannot change *p, can move p int *const p p is a constant pointer: cannot move p, can change *p const int *const p Cannot change either Declaration Meaning (read right to left) const int *p p points to a constant int: cannot change *p, can move p int *const p p is a constant pointer: cannot move p, can change *p const int *const p Cannot change either Use const int *p for function parameters that only read data. It documents intent and the compiler enforces it."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "5-8-function-pointers", "title": "5.8 Function pointers", "text": "A function also lives at an address. A function pointer stores it, so you can choose which function to call at run time (callbacks, state machines, driver tables). int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; } int sub(int a, int b) { return a - b; } int (*op)(int, int); // op = pointer to function(int,int) returning int op = add; printf(\"%d\\n\", op(5, 3)); // 8 op = sub; printf(\"%d\\n\", op(5, 3)); // 2 Output: 8 2"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "5-9-dangerous-pointers", "title": "5.9 Dangerous pointers", "text": "Kind What it is Result Wild Declared but never initialised: int *p; Random address; crash or corruption Dangling Points to memory that is gone (freed or out of scope) Garbage or crash NULL dereference *p when p is NULL Crash (or silent fault on some MCUs) Kind What it is Result Wild Declared but never initialised: int *p; Random address; crash or corruption Dangling Points to memory that is gone (freed or out of scope) Garbage or crash NULL dereference *p when p is NULL Crash (or silent fault on some MCUs) int *bad(void) { int local = 5; return &local; // WRONG: local dies when the function returns } Habit: initialise pointers to NULL, set to NULL after free, check before use."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "common-mistakes-5", "title": "Common mistakes", "text": "Mistake Fix Dereferencing an uninitialised or NULL pointer Initialise, and check for NULL Returning the address of a local variable Return a value, use static, or write into a caller-provided buffer Forgetting the & when passing to a function that wants a pointer swap(&x, &y), not swap(x, y) Confusing p, *p and &p Draw the box diagram: p is the slip, *p is the house, &p is where the slip is kept Pointer arithmetic beyond the array Stay within arr[0]..arr[n-1] (one past the end is allowed only for comparing) Writing through char *s = \"text\" Use char s[] = \"text\" if you need to modify Mistake Fix Dereferencing an uninitialised or NULL pointer Initialise, and check for NULL Returning the address of a local variable Return a value, use static, or write into a caller-provided buffer Forgetting the &amp; when passing to a function that wants a pointer swap(&amp;x, &amp;y), not swap(x, y) Confusing p, *p and &amp;p Draw the box diagram: p is the slip, *p is the house, &amp;p is where the slip is kept Pointer arithmetic beyond the array Stay within arr[0]..arr[n-1] (one past the end is allowed only for comparing) Writing through char *s = \"text\" Use char s[] = \"text\" if you need to modify"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "practice-programs-type-each-one-yourself-5", "title": "Practice programs (type each one yourself)", "text": ""}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "interview-questions-with-short-answers-5", "title": "Interview questions with short answers", "text": "Q1. What is a pointer? A variable that stores a memory address. Q2. Difference between * in a declaration and * in an expression? In int *p it means \"p is a pointer\"; in *p it means \"the value at the address in p\". Q3. What is a dangling pointer? A pointer to memory that has been freed or has gone out of scope. Q4. What is a void pointer? A generic pointer without a type; cast it before dereferencing. Q5. Difference between const int *p and int *const p? The first protects the data, the second protects the pointer itself. Q6. Why is arr[i] the same as *(arr + i)? The compiler defines indexing as pointer arithmetic: base address plus i elements. Q7. Where do you use function pointers in embedded code? Interrupt vector tables, callbacks, state machines and driver interfaces. TOPIC 6"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "structures-unions-enums-and-typedef", "title": "Structures, Unions, Enums and typedef", "text": "In one line A struct groups different variables under one name, a union lets members share the same memory, an enum gives names to integer constants, and typedef gives a type a shorter name. In one line A struct groups different variables under one name, a union lets members share the same memory, an enum gives names to integer constants, and typedef gives a type a shorter name."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "6-1-structures", "title": "6.1 Structures", "text": "Analogy: a form An array is a stack of identical sheets. A struct is one form with different fields: name, roll number, marks. One form describes one student. Analogy: a form An array is a stack of identical sheets. A struct is one form with different fields: name, roll number, marks. One form describes one student. struct Student { char name[20]; int roll; float marks; }; struct Student s1 = {\"Ravi\", 101, 88.5f}; struct Student s2; s2.roll = 102; // . accesses a member strcpy(s2.name, \"Anu\"); s2.marks = 91.0f; printf(\"%s %d %.1f\\n\", s1.name, s1.roll, s1.marks); Output: Ravi 101 88.5"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "6-2-pointers-to-structures-and-the-arrow-operator", "title": "6.2 Pointers to structures and the arrow operator", "text": "Structures can be large. Passing a pointer avoids copying the whole struct, and the function can modify the original. Use -> with a pointer, . with a variable. void print_student(const struct Student *s) { printf(\"%s %d %.1f\\n\", s->name, s->roll, s->marks); // same as (*s).name } void give_bonus(struct Student *s) { s->marks += 5; } print_student(&s1); give_bonus(&s1);"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "6-3-array-of-structures", "title": "6.3 Array of structures", "text": "struct Student cls[3] = { {\"Ravi\", 101, 88.5f}, {\"Anu\", 102, 91.0f}, {\"Kiran\",103, 79.0f} }; for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) printf(\"%d %s %.1f\\n\", cls[i].roll, cls[i].name, cls[i].marks);"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "6-4-typedef-shorter-names", "title": "6.4 typedef: shorter names", "text": "typedef struct { float temperature; float humidity; uint32_t timestamp; } SensorData; SensorData reading = {27.5f, 60.0f, 1000}; // no need to write \"struct\" typedef does not create a new type; it just gives a new name. It is used everywhere in embedded code: uint8_t itself is a typedef."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "6-5-struct-padding-and-size", "title": "6.5 Struct padding and size", "text": "CPUs read memory fastest when a 4-byte int starts at an address divisible by 4. The compiler inserts unused padding bytes to keep members aligned, so sizeof(struct) can be bigger than the sum of its members. struct A { char a; int b; char c; }; // a, 3 pad, b (4), c, 3 pad -> 12 bytes struct B { int b; char a; char c; }; // b (4), a, c, 2 pad -> 8 bytes printf(\"%zu %zu\\n\", sizeof(struct A), sizeof(struct B)); // 12 8 Output: 12 8 Tip: list members from largest to smallest to save RAM. This matters when you have thousands of records on a microcontroller."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "6-6-unions", "title": "6.6 Unions", "text": "In a union all members start at the same address. The size is that of the largest member, and only one member holds a meaningful value at a time. union Data { int i; float f; uint8_t bytes[4]; }; union Data d; d.f = 1.0f; printf(\"%02X %02X %02X %02X\\n\", d.bytes[0], d.bytes[1], d.bytes[2], d.bytes[3]); printf(\"%zu\\n\", sizeof(union Data)); Output: 00 00 80 3F 4 The output shows the four bytes of the float 1.0 (0x3F800000) on a little-endian machine, lowest byte first. Embedded uses of unions: viewing a 32-bit register as bytes or as bit fields, and sending different message types in one buffer. struct union Memory Each member has its own space Members share one space Size Sum of members + padding Size of the largest member Use Group related data Save memory, reinterpret the same bytes struct union Memory Each member has its own space Members share one space Size Sum of members + padding Size of the largest member Use Group related data Save memory, reinterpret the same bytes"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "6-7-enumerations", "title": "6.7 Enumerations", "text": "An enum gives readable names to integer constants. Values start at 0 and increase by 1 unless you set them. enum Color { RED, GREEN, BLUE }; // 0, 1, 2 enum State { IDLE = 10, RUNNING, ERROR }; // 10, 11, 12 enum Color c = GREEN; if (c == GREEN) printf(\"green = %d\\n\", c); // green = 1 Use enums for states in a state machine (Topic 18) instead of magic numbers like 0, 1, 2."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "6-8-bit-fields", "title": "6.8 Bit-fields", "text": "struct Flags { unsigned int power : 1; // 1 bit unsigned int mode : 2; // 2 bits (0 to 3) unsigned int error : 1; unsigned int : 4; // 4 unused bits }; struct Flags f = {0}; f.power = 1; f.mode = 2; Bit-fields are not portable The order of bits and the packing are decided by the compiler. For hardware registers and communication protocols, most engineers use masks and shifts (Topic 10) instead. Bit-fields are not portable The order of bits and the packing are decided by the compiler. For hardware registers and communication protocols, most engineers use masks and shifts (Topic 10) instead."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "common-mistakes-6", "title": "Common mistakes", "text": "Mistake Fix Using . with a pointer (p.name) Use -> with pointers: p->name Forgetting the semicolon after the struct definition struct X { ... }; Comparing two structs with == Compare members one by one (or memcmp with care for padding) Assuming sizeof(struct) is the sum of members Print sizeof; remember padding Reading a different union member than you wrote (expecting conversion) A union reinterprets bytes; it does not convert values Assigning strings with = (s.name = \"Ravi\") Use strcpy for char arrays Mistake Fix Using . with a pointer (p.name) Use -&gt; with pointers: p-&gt;name Forgetting the semicolon after the struct definition struct X { ... }; Comparing two structs with == Compare members one by one (or memcmp with care for padding) Assuming sizeof(struct) is the sum of members Print sizeof; remember padding Reading a different union member than you wrote (expecting conversion) A union reinterprets bytes; it does not convert values Assigning strings with = (s.name = \"Ravi\") Use strcpy for char arrays"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "practice-programs-type-each-one-yourself-6", "title": "Practice programs (type each one yourself)", "text": ""}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "interview-questions-with-short-answers-6", "title": "Interview questions with short answers", "text": "Q1. Difference between structure and union? Struct members have separate memory; union members share the same memory. Q2. What is structure padding? Unused bytes added so each member is aligned to its natural boundary for fast access. Q3. When do you use ->? To access a member through a pointer to a structure. Q4. What is typedef? A way to give a new name to an existing type. Q5. How can you reduce the size of a struct? Order members from large to small, or use packed attribute (with a speed or portability cost). Q6. What is an enum used for? Named integer constants, mainly states and options. TOPIC 7"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "dynamic-memory-allocation", "title": "Dynamic Memory Allocation", "text": "In one line Dynamic memory lets a program request memory from the heap while it runs (malloc) and must give it back (free). On small microcontrollers it is usually avoided. In one line Dynamic memory lets a program request memory from the heap while it runs (malloc) and must give it back (free). On small microcontrollers it is usually avoided."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "7-1-stack-vs-heap", "title": "7.1 Stack vs heap", "text": "Stack Heap Managed by Compiler (automatic) You (malloc / free) Holds Local variables, function calls Memory you request at run time Size known At compile time Decided while running Speed Very fast Slower Freed When the function returns When you call free Risk Stack overflow Leaks, fragmentation, dangling pointers Stack Heap Managed by Compiler (automatic) You (malloc / free) Holds Local variables, function calls Memory you request at run time Size known At compile time Decided while running Speed Very fast Slower Freed When the function returns When you call free Risk Stack overflow Leaks, fragmentation, dangling pointers Analogy: hotel rooms Stack is your own room that is cleaned automatically when you leave. Heap is renting a hall: you ask the manager (malloc), use it as long as you like, and must hand the keys back (free). If you forget, nobody else can use that hall. Analogy: hotel rooms Stack is your own room that is cleaned automatically when you leave. Heap is renting a hall: you ask the manager (malloc), use it as long as you like, and must hand the keys back (free). If you forget, nobody else can use that hall."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "7-2-the-four-functions-stdlib-h", "title": "7.2 The four functions (stdlib.h)", "text": "Function What it does malloc(size) Reserves size bytes; memory is NOT cleared; returns NULL on failure calloc(n, size) Reserves n x size bytes and sets all to zero realloc(ptr, size) Resizes a block (may move it); returns the new address free(ptr) Returns the block to the heap Function What it does malloc(size) Reserves size bytes; memory is NOT cleared; returns NULL on failure calloc(n, size) Reserves n x size bytes and sets all to zero realloc(ptr, size) Resizes a block (may move it); returns the new address free(ptr) Returns the block to the heap #include <stdio.h> #include <stdlib.h> int main(void) { int n = 5; int *arr = malloc(n * sizeof *arr); // space for 5 ints if (arr == NULL) { // ALWAYS check printf(\"Out of memory\\n\"); return 1; } for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) arr[i] = i * i; // grow to 10 elements: use a temporary pointer int *tmp = realloc(arr, 10 * sizeof *arr); if (tmp == NULL) { free(arr); return 1; } arr = tmp; for (int i = 5; i < 10; i++) arr[i] = i * i; for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) printf(\"%d \", arr[i]); free(arr); // give it back arr = NULL; // avoid a dangling pointer return 0; } Output: 0 1 4 9 16 25 36 49 64 81 Why sizeof *arr? It stays correct even if you change the pointer type later. Why a temporary pointer in realloc? If realloc fails it returns NULL, and writing that straight into arr would lose the old block (a leak)."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "7-3-memory-problems", "title": "7.3 Memory problems", "text": "Problem Example Result Memory leak malloc without free, or losing the pointer RAM slowly runs out Double free free(p); free(p); Crash or heap corruption Use after free free(p); *p = 3; Undefined behaviour Buffer overflow Writing past the allocated size Corrupts neighbouring data Uninitialised read Reading malloc memory before writing Garbage values Problem Example Result Memory leak malloc without free, or losing the pointer RAM slowly runs out Double free free(p); free(p); Crash or heap corruption Use after free free(p); *p = 3; Undefined behaviour Buffer overflow Writing past the allocated size Corrupts neighbouring data Uninitialised read Reading malloc memory before writing Garbage values"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "7-4-linked-list-a-classic-heap-use", "title": "7.4 Linked list: a classic heap use", "text": "An array has a fixed size. A linked list grows one node at a time; each node holds data and a pointer to the next node. typedef struct Node { int data; struct Node *next; } Node; Node *push_front(Node *head, int value) { Node *n = malloc(sizeof *n); if (n == NULL) return head; n->data = value; n->next = head; // new node points to old first node return n; // new node is now the head } void print_list(const Node *head) { for (const Node *p = head; p != NULL; p = p->next) printf(\"%d -> \", p->data); printf(\"NULL\\n\"); } void free_list(Node *head) { while (head != NULL) { Node *next = head->next; // save before freeing free(head); head = next; } } int main(void) { Node *list = NULL; list = push_front(list, 3); list = push_front(list, 2); list = push_front(list, 1); print_list(list); free_list(list); return 0; } Output: 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> NULL"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "7-5-dynamic-memory-in-embedded-systems", "title": "7.5 Dynamic memory in embedded systems", "text": ""}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "common-mistakes-7", "title": "Common mistakes", "text": "Mistake Fix Not checking malloc for NULL Always test the result Forgetting free (leak) Pair every malloc with one free; free in the same module that allocated Using the pointer after free Set the pointer to NULL after free malloc(n) instead of malloc(n * sizeof(type)) Multiply by the element size arr = realloc(arr, size) losing the block on failure Use a temporary pointer Freeing stack memory or a pointer not from malloc Only free what malloc, calloc or realloc returned Mistake Fix Not checking malloc for NULL Always test the result Forgetting free (leak) Pair every malloc with one free; free in the same module that allocated Using the pointer after free Set the pointer to NULL after free malloc(n) instead of malloc(n * sizeof(type)) Multiply by the element size arr = realloc(arr, size) losing the block on failure Use a temporary pointer Freeing stack memory or a pointer not from malloc Only free what malloc, calloc or realloc returned"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "practice-programs-type-each-one-yourself-7", "title": "Practice programs (type each one yourself)", "text": ""}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "interview-questions-with-short-answers-7", "title": "Interview questions with short answers", "text": "Q1. Difference between malloc and calloc? calloc takes count and size and zeroes the memory; malloc does not initialise. Q2. What is a memory leak? Allocated memory that is never freed and can no longer be reached. Q3. What does realloc do if it cannot extend in place? It allocates a new block, copies the data and frees the old one. Q4. Why avoid malloc in embedded systems? Fragmentation, unpredictable timing and failure handling on tiny RAM. Q5. Stack vs heap? Stack is automatic and fast for locals; heap is manual and flexible for run-time sizes. Q6. What happens if you free the same pointer twice? Undefined behaviour, often a crash or heap corruption. TOPIC 8"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "storage-classes-and-the-preprocessor", "title": "Storage Classes and the Preprocessor", "text": "In one line Storage classes decide where a variable lives, how long it lives and who can see it; the preprocessor edits your source text (includes, macros, conditions) before compilation. In one line Storage classes decide where a variable lives, how long it lives and who can see it; the preprocessor edits your source text (includes, macros, conditions) before compilation."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "8-1-the-four-storage-classes", "title": "8.1 The four storage classes", "text": "Class Scope (who sees it) Lifetime Default value Stored in auto Block Until block ends Garbage Stack register Block Until block ends Garbage CPU register (a hint) static Block or file Whole program 0 Data / BSS extern Whole program Whole program 0 Data / BSS Class Scope (who sees it) Lifetime Default value Stored in auto Block Until block ends Garbage Stack register Block Until block ends Garbage CPU register (a hint) static Block or file Whole program 0 Data / BSS extern Whole program Whole program 0 Data / BSS auto is the default for local variables, so you almost never type it. register is a request to the compiler that modern compilers ignore (they decide better)."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "8-2-static", "title": "8.2 static", "text": "static does two different jobs depending on where it is written. void counter(void) { static int count = 0; // initialised once only count++; printf(\"called %d times\\n\", count); } counter(); // called 1 times counter(); // called 2 times counter(); // called 3 times Embedded use Use static for file-private variables and helper functions in a driver. It works like \"private\" in other languages and prevents name clashes between files. Embedded use Use static for file-private variables and helper functions in a driver. It works like \"private\" in other languages and prevents name clashes between files."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "8-3-extern", "title": "8.3 extern", "text": "extern says \"this variable exists, it is defined in another file\". It declares without creating. // config.c int baud_rate = 115200; // definition: memory is created here // main.c #include <stdio.h> extern int baud_rate; // declaration: refers to the one above int main(void) { printf(\"%d\\n\", baud_rate); return 0; } Normally you put the extern declaration in a header file (config.h) so every file shares the same one."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "8-4-the-preprocessor", "title": "8.4 The preprocessor", "text": "Lines starting with # are processed before compilation. The preprocessor only edits text; it knows nothing about C types. Directive Purpose #include <file> Search system folders (standard headers) #include \"file\" Search your project folder first (your headers) #define NAME value Replace NAME with value everywhere #undef NAME Remove a definition #ifdef / #ifndef / #if Compile code only if a condition is true #else / #elif / #endif Alternatives and end of a conditional block #error / #warning Stop or warn at compile time with a message Directive Purpose #include &lt;file&gt; Search system folders (standard headers) #include \"file\" Search your project folder first (your headers) #define NAME value Replace NAME with value everywhere #undef NAME Remove a definition #ifdef / #ifndef / #if Compile code only if a condition is true #else / #elif / #endif Alternatives and end of a conditional block #error / #warning Stop or warn at compile time with a message"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "8-5-macros", "title": "8.5 Macros", "text": "#define PI 3.14159 #define LED_PIN 13 #define SQUARE(x) ((x) * (x)) #define MAX(a, b) ((a) > (b) ? (a) : (b)) int r = SQUARE(2 + 3); // becomes ((2 + 3) * (2 + 3)) = 25 Brackets are not optional If you write #define SQUARE(x) x * x, then SQUARE(2 + 3) becomes 2 + 3 * 2 + 3 = 11, not 25. Put every parameter and the whole expression in brackets. Side effects: MAX(i++, j) may evaluate i++ twice. Use an inline function when you can. Brackets are not optional If you write #define SQUARE(x) x * x, then SQUARE(2 + 3) becomes 2 + 3 * 2 + 3 = 11, not 25. Put every parameter and the whole expression in brackets. Side effects: MAX(i++, j) may evaluate i++ twice. Use an inline function when you can. Macro const / inline function Type checking None Yes Debugging Hard (text replaced) Easier Memory None until used const may take memory Best for Pin numbers, register addresses, bit masks, conditional builds Typed constants and small functions Macro const / inline function Type checking None Yes Debugging Hard (text replaced) Easier Memory None until used const may take memory Best for Pin numbers, register addresses, bit masks, conditional builds Typed constants and small functions"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "8-6-include-guards", "title": "8.6 Include guards", "text": "If two headers include the same header, the compiler sees duplicate definitions and fails. An include guard makes a header count only once. #ifndef SENSOR_H #define SENSOR_H int read_sensor(void); #endif"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "8-7-conditional-compilation", "title": "8.7 Conditional compilation", "text": "#define DEBUG 1 #if DEBUG #define LOG(msg) printf(\"[LOG] %s\\n\", msg) #else #define LOG(msg) // expands to nothing in release builds #endif #ifdef ESP32 // code only for the ESP32 board #elif defined(ARDUINO_AVR_UNO) // code only for the Arduino Uno #endif Conditional compilation lets one code base support several boards or switch debug output on and off with zero cost in the final firmware."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "common-mistakes-8", "title": "Common mistakes", "text": "Mistake Fix Macro without brackets Wrap each parameter and the whole expression Semicolon at the end of #define Do not add one: #define N 10 Defining a variable in a header Define in one .c file, declare with extern in the header Missing include guard Add #ifndef / #define / #endif to every header Expecting a static local to reset on each call It keeps its value; initialisation runs only once Using #define for typed values Prefer const or enum when type safety matters Mistake Fix Macro without brackets Wrap each parameter and the whole expression Semicolon at the end of #define Do not add one: #define N 10 Defining a variable in a header Define in one .c file, declare with extern in the header Missing include guard Add #ifndef / #define / #endif to every header Expecting a static local to reset on each call It keeps its value; initialisation runs only once Using #define for typed values Prefer const or enum when type safety matters"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "practice-programs-type-each-one-yourself-8", "title": "Practice programs (type each one yourself)", "text": ""}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "interview-questions-with-short-answers-8", "title": "Interview questions with short answers", "text": "Q1. What does static do? Inside a function it keeps the value between calls; at file level it hides the name from other files. Q2. Difference between declaration and definition of a global? A definition creates storage; extern declarations only refer to it. Q3. What is an include guard? Preprocessor lines that make sure a header is included only once per file. Q4. Macro vs function? A macro is text replacement without type checks and no call overhead; a function is type-safe and debuggable. Q5. What is the default value of a static variable? Zero. Q6. #include <> vs \"\"? Angle brackets search system paths; quotes search the project folder first. TOPIC 9"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "file-handling", "title": "File Handling", "text": "In one line File handling lets a program store data permanently by reading and writing files using a FILE pointer and functions such as fopen, fprintf, fread and fclose. In one line File handling lets a program store data permanently by reading and writing files using a FILE pointer and functions such as fopen, fprintf, fread and fclose."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "9-1-why-files", "title": "9.1 Why files?", "text": "Variables vanish when the program ends. Files keep data: logs, configuration, records. On microcontrollers the same ideas apply to an SD card, SPIFFS or LittleFS on ESP32 (the function names differ slightly but the steps are the same). Analogy: a notebook Open the notebook (fopen), read or write on it (fprintf, fscanf, fread, fwrite), and close it (fclose) so everything is saved. Analogy: a notebook Open the notebook (fopen), read or write on it (fprintf, fscanf, fread, fwrite), and close it (fclose) so everything is saved."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "9-2-steps-for-every-file-operation", "title": "9.2 Steps for every file operation", "text": "Mode Meaning \"r\" Read. File must exist \"w\" Write. Creates the file, or ERASES it if it exists \"a\" Append. Writes at the end; creates the file if missing \"r+\" Read and write an existing file \"w+\" Read and write; erases existing content \"rb\" \"wb\" \"ab\" Same as above but binary (add b) Mode Meaning \"r\" Read. File must exist \"w\" Write. Creates the file, or ERASES it if it exists \"a\" Append. Writes at the end; creates the file if missing \"r+\" Read and write an existing file \"w+\" Read and write; erases existing content \"rb\" \"wb\" \"ab\" Same as above but binary (add b)"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "9-3-text-files", "title": "9.3 Text files", "text": "#include <stdio.h> int main(void) { FILE *fp = fopen(\"data.txt\", \"w\"); if (fp == NULL) { perror(\"fopen\"); // prints why it failed return 1; } fprintf(fp, \"Ravi %d %.1f\\n\", 101, 88.5); fprintf(fp, \"Anu %d %.1f\\n\", 102, 91.0); fclose(fp); fp = fopen(\"data.txt\", \"r\"); if (fp == NULL) return 1; char name[20]; int roll; float marks; while (fscanf(fp, \"%19s %d %f\", name, &roll, &marks) == 3) printf(\"%s %d %.1f\\n\", name, roll, marks); fclose(fp); return 0; } Output: Ravi 101 88.5 Anu 102 91.0 fscanf returns the number of items it managed to read. Looping while it returns 3 stops cleanly at the end of the file."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "9-4-reading-line-by-line", "title": "9.4 Reading line by line", "text": "char line[100]; FILE *fp = fopen(\"log.txt\", \"r\"); if (fp != NULL) { while (fgets(line, sizeof line, fp) != NULL) printf(\"%s\", line); // fgets keeps the newline fclose(fp); } Function Use fprintf / fscanf Formatted text, like printf / scanf but for files fputs / fgets Write or read a line of text fputc / fgetc Write or read one character fwrite / fread Write or read raw bytes (binary files) fseek / ftell / rewind Move to a position, find the position, go back to start feof / ferror Check end of file or an error Function Use fprintf / fscanf Formatted text, like printf / scanf but for files fputs / fgets Write or read a line of text fputc / fgetc Write or read one character fwrite / fread Write or read raw bytes (binary files) fseek / ftell / rewind Move to a position, find the position, go back to start feof / ferror Check end of file or an error"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "9-5-binary-files-save-structs-directly", "title": "9.5 Binary files: save structs directly", "text": "typedef struct { char name[20]; int roll; float marks; } Student; Student s = {\"Ravi\", 101, 88.5f}; FILE *fp = fopen(\"students.bin\", \"wb\"); if (fp) { fwrite(&s, sizeof(Student), 1, fp); // 1 record fclose(fp); } Student t; fp = fopen(\"students.bin\", \"rb\"); if (fp) { if (fread(&t, sizeof(Student), 1, fp) == 1) printf(\"%s %d %.1f\\n\", t.name, t.roll, t.marks); fclose(fp); } Output: Ravi 101 88.5 Binary files are compact and fast, but they depend on the compiler, struct padding and endianness, so they are not portable between different systems. Text files are bigger but readable."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "9-6-jumping-around-a-file", "title": "9.6 Jumping around a file", "text": "fseek(fp, 0, SEEK_END); // go to the end long size = ftell(fp); // position = file size in bytes rewind(fp); // back to the start // jump straight to the 3rd record (index 2) of a binary file fseek(fp, 2 * sizeof(Student), SEEK_SET); The while(!feof(fp)) bug feof becomes true only after a read has failed, so this loop runs one extra time and processes garbage. Test the return value of the read function instead (fscanf, fgets, fread). The while(!feof(fp)) bug feof becomes true only after a read has failed, so this loop runs one extra time and processes garbage. Test the return value of the read function instead (fscanf, fgets, fread)."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "common-mistakes-9", "title": "Common mistakes", "text": "Mistake Fix Not checking if fopen returned NULL Always check; print the reason with perror Forgetting fclose Data may stay in the buffer and be lost; always close Opening with \"w\" and erasing data by accident Use \"a\" to add or \"r+\" to modify Using while(!feof(fp)) Loop on the read function's return value Reading a binary file in text mode (or the reverse) Match the mode: \"rb\" with fread fscanf(\"%s\") overflow on long words Use a width: %19s for char[20] Mistake Fix Not checking if fopen returned NULL Always check; print the reason with perror Forgetting fclose Data may stay in the buffer and be lost; always close Opening with \"w\" and erasing data by accident Use \"a\" to add or \"r+\" to modify Using while(!feof(fp)) Loop on the read function's return value Reading a binary file in text mode (or the reverse) Match the mode: \"rb\" with fread fscanf(\"%s\") overflow on long words Use a width: %19s for char[20]"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "practice-programs-type-each-one-yourself-9", "title": "Practice programs (type each one yourself)", "text": ""}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "interview-questions-with-short-answers-9", "title": "Interview questions with short answers", "text": "Q1. What is FILE *? A pointer to a structure that holds the state of an open file (buffer, position, flags). Q2. Difference between \"w\" and \"a\"? \"w\" erases existing content; \"a\" appends at the end. Q3. Text vs binary file? Text stores characters (human readable, newline conversion on some systems); binary stores raw bytes exactly as in memory. Q4. Why must you call fclose? It flushes buffered data to storage and releases the file handle. Q5. What do fread and fwrite return? The number of items successfully read or written. Q6. How do you find the size of a file? fseek to SEEK_END then ftell. TOPIC 10"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "bitwise-operators-and-bit-manipulation", "title": "Bitwise Operators and Bit Manipulation", "text": "In one line Bitwise operators work on the individual bits of a number. They are the main tool for controlling hardware registers, flags and protocols in embedded C. In one line Bitwise operators work on the individual bits of a number. They are the main tool for controlling hardware registers, flags and protocols in embedded C."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "10-1-why-bits-matter-in-embedded", "title": "10.1 Why bits matter in embedded", "text": "A hardware register is a number where each bit (or group of bits) controls one thing: bit 5 turns an LED pin on, bits 2 to 4 select a clock speed, bit 7 says \"data ready\". To change one bit without disturbing the others, you need bitwise operators. Analogy: a row of light switches A byte is a panel of 8 switches. Bitwise operators let you flip one switch, check one switch, or set a pattern, without touching the others. Analogy: a row of light switches A byte is a panel of 8 switches. Bitwise operators let you flip one switch, check one switch, or set a pattern, without touching the others."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "10-2-the-six-operators", "title": "10.2 The six operators", "text": "Op Name Rule & AND 1 only if both bits are 1 (used to CLEAR bits and to CHECK bits) | OR 1 if either bit is 1 (used to SET bits) ^ XOR 1 if the bits are different (used to TOGGLE bits) ~ NOT Flips every bit << Left shift Moves bits left, fills with 0; x << n multiplies by 2^n >> Right shift Moves bits right; x >> n divides by 2^n (for unsigned) Op Name Rule &amp; AND 1 only if both bits are 1 (used to CLEAR bits and to CHECK bits) | OR 1 if either bit is 1 (used to SET bits) ^ XOR 1 if the bits are different (used to TOGGLE bits) ~ NOT Flips every bit &lt;&lt; Left shift Moves bits left, fills with 0; x &lt;&lt; n multiplies by 2^n &gt;&gt; Right shift Moves bits right; x &gt;&gt; n divides by 2^n (for unsigned) Worked example with a = 12 (1100) and b = 10 (1010): Expression Binary work Result Decimal 12 & 10 1100 & 1010 1000 8 12 | 10 1100 | 1010 1110 14 12 ^ 10 1100 ^ 1010 0110 6 12 << 1 1100 -> 11000 11000 24 12 >> 1 1100 -> 0110 0110 6 ~12 (8-bit) ~00001100 11110011 243 unsigned, -13 signed Expression Binary work Result Decimal 12 &amp; 10 1100 &amp; 1010 1000 8 12 | 10 1100 | 1010 1110 14 12 ^ 10 1100 ^ 1010 0110 6 12 &lt;&lt; 1 1100 -&gt; 11000 11000 24 12 &gt;&gt; 1 1100 -&gt; 0110 0110 6 ~12 (8-bit) ~00001100 11110011 243 unsigned, -13 signed Do not confuse & with && && is logical AND (true/false of whole values). & is bitwise AND (bit by bit). 4 && 2 is 1 (both non-zero), but 4 & 2 is 0 (no common bits). Do not confuse &amp; with &amp;&amp; &amp;&amp; is logical AND (true/false of whole values). &amp; is bitwise AND (bit by bit). 4 &amp;&amp; 2 is 1 (both non-zero), but 4 &amp; 2 is 0 (no common bits)."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "10-3-the-four-essential-operations", "title": "10.3 The four essential operations", "text": "Goal Code Why it works Set bit n x |= (1 << n); OR with a 1 forces the bit to 1, others unchanged Clear bit n x &= ~(1 << n); AND with a 0 forces the bit to 0, others unchanged Toggle bit n x ^= (1 << n); XOR with 1 flips the bit Check bit n if (x & (1 << n)) AND leaves only that bit; non-zero means it was 1 Goal Code Why it works Set bit n x |= (1 &lt;&lt; n); OR with a 1 forces the bit to 1, others unchanged Clear bit n x &amp;= ~(1 &lt;&lt; n); AND with a 0 forces the bit to 0, others unchanged Toggle bit n x ^= (1 &lt;&lt; n); XOR with 1 flips the bit Check bit n if (x &amp; (1 &lt;&lt; n)) AND leaves only that bit; non-zero means it was 1 Step by step: set bit 3 of x = 0b00000101 (5). #define SET_BIT(x, n) ((x) |= (1U << (n))) #define CLEAR_BIT(x, n) ((x) &= ~(1U << (n))) #define TOGGLE_BIT(x, n) ((x) ^= (1U << (n))) #define CHECK_BIT(x, n) (((x) >> (n)) & 1U) int main(void) { unsigned char x = 5; // 00000101 SET_BIT(x, 3); // 00001101 printf(\"%u\\n\", x); // 13 CLEAR_BIT(x, 0); // 00001100 printf(\"%u\\n\", x); // 12 TOGGLE_BIT(x, 7); // 10001100 printf(\"%u\\n\", x); // 140 printf(\"%u\\n\", CHECK_BIT(x, 2)); // 1 return 0; } Output: 13 12 140 1"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "10-4-multi-bit-fields-mask-shift-insert", "title": "10.4 Multi-bit fields: mask, shift, insert", "text": "Suppose bits 4 to 6 of a register hold a 3-bit \"mode\" value. #define MODE_SHIFT 4 #define MODE_MASK (0x7U << MODE_SHIFT) // 0111 0000 uint8_t reg = 0xA5; // 1010 0101 // READ the field: shift it down, then mask uint8_t mode = (reg >> MODE_SHIFT) & 0x7U; // 010 = 2 // WRITE the field: clear the old bits, then OR in the new value reg = (reg & ~MODE_MASK) | ((5U << MODE_SHIFT) & MODE_MASK); // mode = 101 This \"clear then set\" pattern is the standard way to update part of a register safely."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "10-5-classic-tricks-frequent-interview-questions", "title": "10.5 Classic tricks (frequent interview questions)", "text": "// even or odd if (n & 1) { /* odd */ } // power of two (n must be > 0) int is_pow2 = (n > 0) && ((n & (n - 1)) == 0); // count set bits (Kernighan): each loop removes the lowest 1 bit int count_bits(unsigned int n) { int c = 0; while (n) { n &= (n - 1); c++; } return c; } // swap two numbers without a temporary variable a ^= b; b ^= a; a ^= b; // swap the two nibbles of a byte uint8_t swapped = (uint8_t)((x << 4) | (x >> 4)); // multiply and divide by 8 (unsigned) y = x << 3; // x * 8 z = x >> 3; // x / 8 Why n & (n-1) works: subtracting 1 flips the lowest 1 bit to 0 and all the zeros below it to 1, so AND-ing removes exactly that lowest 1."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "10-6-traps-to-remember", "title": "10.6 Traps to remember", "text": ""}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "10-7-real-hardware-example-arduino-uno-avr", "title": "10.7 Real hardware example (Arduino Uno, AVR)", "text": "// Pin 13 on the Uno is PB5. Blink it with direct register access. void setup() { DDRB |= (1 << DDB5); // make PB5 an output } void loop() { PORTB ^= (1 << PORTB5); // toggle only PB5, other pins untouched delay(500); } DDRB is the direction register (1 = output). PORTB holds the output levels. One line toggles one pin and leaves the other seven alone. This is exactly why you learn bit manipulation."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "common-mistakes-10", "title": "Common mistakes", "text": "Mistake Fix Using = instead of |= when setting a bit (wipes other bits) Use x |= mask x & 4 == 4 (precedence) Add brackets: (x & 4) == 4 1 << 31 on a signed int Use 1U << 31 Using && when you meant & Bitwise & works per bit Right shifting negative signed numbers and assuming division Use unsigned types for bit work Forgetting to clear a field before setting a new value Clear the mask first, then OR the new bits Mistake Fix Using = instead of |= when setting a bit (wipes other bits) Use x |= mask x &amp; 4 == 4 (precedence) Add brackets: (x &amp; 4) == 4 1 &lt;&lt; 31 on a signed int Use 1U &lt;&lt; 31 Using &amp;&amp; when you meant &amp; Bitwise &amp; works per bit Right shifting negative signed numbers and assuming division Use unsigned types for bit work Forgetting to clear a field before setting a new value Clear the mask first, then OR the new bits"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "practice-programs-type-each-one-yourself-10", "title": "Practice programs (type each one yourself)", "text": ""}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "interview-questions-with-short-answers-10", "title": "Interview questions with short answers", "text": "Q1. How do you set, clear, toggle and check bit n? Set: x |= 1<<n. Clear: x &= ~(1<<n). Toggle: x ^= 1<<n. Check: (x>>n)&1. Q2. Difference between & and &&? & works on each bit; && works on whole values as true or false. Q3. How do you check for a power of 2? n > 0 and (n & (n-1)) == 0. Q4. What does x << n do? Shifts bits left by n places, equal to multiplying by 2^n if no overflow. Q5. How do you swap two variables without a temp? a ^= b; b ^= a; a ^= b; (or with + and -). Q6. Why use unsigned for bit operations? Signed shifts and overflow have implementation-defined or undefined behaviour. Q7. How do you update a 3-bit field in a register without touching others? Clear the field with a mask, then OR in the new value shifted into position. TOPIC 11"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "type-qualifiers-and-fixed-width-types", "title": "Type Qualifiers and Fixed-Width Types", "text": "In one line volatile, const and static tell the compiler extra facts about a variable; fixed-width types (uint8_t, uint32_t...) make sizes identical on every chip. In one line volatile, const and static tell the compiler extra facts about a variable; fixed-width types (uint8_t, uint32_t...) make sizes identical on every chip."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "11-1-volatile", "title": "11.1 volatile", "text": "Compilers optimise. If your code reads a variable in a loop and nothing in that loop changes it, the compiler assumes it never changes and reads it only once. That is wrong if hardware or an interrupt changes it behind the compiler's back. volatile means: \"this value can change at any time, always read it from memory and never cache it.\" Analogy: a whiteboard in a busy office Without volatile, the compiler copies the whiteboard into its notebook once and keeps reading its notes. With volatile, it must look at the whiteboard every single time, because someone else keeps writing on it. Analogy: a whiteboard in a busy office Without volatile, the compiler copies the whiteboard into its notebook once and keeps reading its notes. With volatile, it must look at the whiteboard every single time, because someone else keeps writing on it. int flag = 0; // BUG: not volatile void ISR(void) { flag = 1; } // interrupt sets the flag int main(void) { while (flag == 0) { } // compiler may turn this into an endless loop do_work(); } // FIX volatile int flag = 0; Three situations that need volatile: Situation Example Hardware register volatile uint32_t *status = (volatile uint32_t *)0x40021018; Variable changed in an ISR and read in main (or the reverse) volatile uint8_t button_pressed; Variable shared between tasks / threads (with proper locking) volatile bool running; Situation Example Hardware register volatile uint32_t *status = (volatile uint32_t *)0x40021018; Variable changed in an ISR and read in main (or the reverse) volatile uint8_t button_pressed; Variable shared between tasks / threads (with proper locking) volatile bool running; volatile does not make code safe volatile only stops the compiler from caching the value. It does not make a multi-byte read atomic. On an 8-bit AVR, reading a 16-bit volatile variable takes two instructions; an interrupt in between can give a half-old, half-new value. Disable interrupts around such reads (Topic 15). volatile does not make code safe volatile only stops the compiler from caching the value. It does not make a multi-byte read atomic. On an 8-bit AVR, reading a 16-bit volatile variable takes two instructions; an interrupt in between can give a half-old, half-new value. Disable interrupts around such reads (Topic 15)."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "11-2-const", "title": "11.2 const", "text": "const int MAX_RETRY = 3; // cannot change const uint8_t sine_table[8] = {0, 90, 127, 90, 0, 90, 127, 90}; // lookup table in flash int length(const char *s); // promises not to modify s"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "11-3-const-volatile-together", "title": "11.3 const volatile together", "text": "This looks contradictory but is common for read-only hardware registers, for example a status register or an input-port register. Your code must not write it (const), but the hardware changes it all the time (volatile). const volatile uint32_t *const STATUS_REG = (const volatile uint32_t *)0x40010000; while ((*STATUS_REG & 0x01) == 0) { } // wait until the ready bit is set"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "11-4-static-recap", "title": "11.4 static (recap)", "text": "Local static: keeps its value across calls. File-level static: hidden from other files. Both live in the data or BSS area, not on the stack. Details in Topic 8."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "11-5-fixed-width-integer-types", "title": "11.5 Fixed-width integer types", "text": "Because int, long and even char can differ between chips, include <stdint.h> and use types whose width is part of the name. Type Bits Range Typical use int8_t / uint8_t 8 -128..127 / 0..255 Bytes, flags, 8-bit registers int16_t / uint16_t 16 -32768..32767 / 0..65535 ADC values, 16-bit registers int32_t / uint32_t 32 +/-2.1 billion / 0..4.29 billion 32-bit registers, timestamps int64_t / uint64_t 64 very large Long counters (slow on 8-bit chips) size_t varies unsigned, holds a size Array sizes, sizeof results, loop indexes bool (stdbool.h) 8 typically true or false Flags and conditions Type Bits Range Typical use int8_t / uint8_t 8 -128..127 / 0..255 Bytes, flags, 8-bit registers int16_t / uint16_t 16 -32768..32767 / 0..65535 ADC values, 16-bit registers int32_t / uint32_t 32 +/-2.1 billion / 0..4.29 billion 32-bit registers, timestamps int64_t / uint64_t 64 very large Long counters (slow on 8-bit chips) size_t varies unsigned, holds a size Array sizes, sizeof results, loop indexes bool (stdbool.h) 8 typically true or false Flags and conditions #include <stdint.h> #include <stdbool.h> uint8_t led_state = 0; uint16_t adc_value = 3120; uint32_t millis_now = 0; bool connected = false; // guaranteed on every platform: // sizeof(uint8_t) == 1, sizeof(uint16_t) == 2, sizeof(uint32_t) == 4"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "11-6-choosing-the-right-type", "title": "11.6 Choosing the right type", "text": "Situation Use Hardware register, communication buffer, protocol field uint8_t / uint16_t / uint32_t Loop counter on a PC or 32-bit MCU int or size_t Value that is never negative unsigned type Small signed sensor value int16_t Code must run on Uno and ESP32 Fixed-width types only Might exceed 4 billion uint64_t Situation Use Hardware register, communication buffer, protocol field uint8_t / uint16_t / uint32_t Loop counter on a PC or 32-bit MCU int or size_t Value that is never negative unsigned type Small signed sensor value int16_t Code must run on Uno and ESP32 Fixed-width types only Might exceed 4 billion uint64_t Printing: the simplest portable way is to cast, for example printf(\"%lu\", (unsigned long)millis_now) and printf(\"%d\", (int)led_state)."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "common-mistakes-11", "title": "Common mistakes", "text": "Mistake Fix Missing volatile on a flag shared with an ISR Add volatile; test with optimisation on (-O2) Believing volatile makes access atomic Disable interrupts or use atomic operations for multi-byte data Using int for registers Use uintN_t of the exact register width Declaring a lookup table without const (wastes RAM) Mark constant tables const Mixing signed and unsigned comparisons Keep both sides the same signedness Printing uint32_t with %d Cast to unsigned long and use %lu, or use PRIu32 Mistake Fix Missing volatile on a flag shared with an ISR Add volatile; test with optimisation on (-O2) Believing volatile makes access atomic Disable interrupts or use atomic operations for multi-byte data Using int for registers Use uintN_t of the exact register width Declaring a lookup table without const (wastes RAM) Mark constant tables const Mixing signed and unsigned comparisons Keep both sides the same signedness Printing uint32_t with %d Cast to unsigned long and use %lu, or use PRIu32"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "practice-programs-type-each-one-yourself-11", "title": "Practice programs (type each one yourself)", "text": ""}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "interview-questions-with-short-answers-11", "title": "Interview questions with short answers", "text": "Q1. What does volatile do? It forbids the compiler from caching or removing reads and writes of that variable, because it may change outside the program flow. Q2. Give three uses of volatile in embedded systems. Hardware registers, ISR-shared variables, memory shared with DMA or other tasks. Q3. Can a variable be const and volatile? Yes: read-only to your code but changed by hardware, such as a status register. Q4. Does volatile guarantee atomicity? No. Multi-byte accesses can still be interrupted. Q5. Why use uint8_t instead of unsigned char? Clear intent and a guaranteed 8-bit width. Q6. Where does const data live on a microcontroller? Usually in flash (read-only memory), which saves RAM. Q7. What is size_t? The unsigned type returned by sizeof, big enough to index any object. TOPIC 12"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "memory-layout-startup-code-padding-and-endianness", "title": "Memory Layout, Startup Code, Padding and Endianness", "text": "In one line Knowing where each variable lives (flash, RAM, stack, heap), what runs before main(), and how bytes are ordered explains many embedded bugs and interview questions. In one line Knowing where each variable lives (flash, RAM, stack, heap), what runs before main(), and how bytes are ordered explains many embedded bugs and interview questions."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "12-1-two-kinds-of-memory-in-a-microcontroller", "title": "12.1 Two kinds of memory in a microcontroller", "text": "Flash (ROM) RAM (SRAM) Keeps data without power Yes No Writable at run time Slow and limited; used for firmware Yes, fast Holds Program code, constants Variables, stack, heap Typical size ESP32 4 MB, Uno 32 KB ESP32 about 520 KB, Uno 2 KB Flash (ROM) RAM (SRAM) Keeps data without power Yes No Writable at run time Slow and limited; used for firmware Yes, fast Holds Program code, constants Variables, stack, heap Typical size ESP32 4 MB, Uno 32 KB ESP32 about 520 KB, Uno 2 KB RAM is precious. Every wasted byte matters on a chip with 2 KB."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "12-2-memory-sections-of-a-c-program", "title": "12.2 Memory sections of a C program", "text": "Section What goes in Lives in .text Machine code (your functions) Flash .rodata const data and string literals Flash .data Global and static variables with a non-zero initial value Flash (initial values) copied to RAM .bss Global and static variables initialised to zero or not initialised RAM (zeroed at start) Heap malloc memory; grows upward RAM Stack Locals, return addresses; grows downward RAM Section What goes in Lives in .text Machine code (your functions) Flash .rodata const data and string literals Flash .data Global and static variables with a non-zero initial value Flash (initial values) copied to RAM .bss Global and static variables initialised to zero or not initialised RAM (zeroed at start) Heap malloc memory; grows upward RAM Stack Locals, return addresses; grows downward RAM #include <stdlib.h> int counter = 5; // .data (initialised, non-zero) int total; // .bss (uninitialised, zeroed) const int LIMIT = 100; // .rodata (flash) static int hidden = 0; // .bss (static, zero) void demo(void) { int local = 1; // stack static int calls = 0; // .bss (static local) int *p = malloc(16); // pointer on stack, 16 bytes on heap char *msg = \"hello\"; // pointer on stack, text in .rodata free(p); } Typical RAM picture (high address at the top): Region Behaviour Stack (top of RAM) Grows downward as functions are called ... free gap ... If stack and heap meet, the program crashes Heap Grows upward as malloc is used .bss Zero-initialised variables .data (bottom of RAM) Initialised variables, copied from flash Region Behaviour Stack (top of RAM) Grows downward as functions are called ... free gap ... If stack and heap meet, the program crashes Heap Grows upward as malloc is used .bss Zero-initialised variables .data (bottom of RAM) Initialised variables, copied from flash"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "12-3-what-happens-before-main-runs", "title": "12.3 What happens before main() runs", "text": "The CPU does not start at main. After power-on or reset it runs startup code: The linker script (.ld file) decides the addresses of flash and RAM and where each section goes. You rarely write it as a beginner, but you should know it exists. In Arduino and ESP-IDF these steps are hidden inside the core. Why interviewers ask this It shows you understand why a global initialised to 5 survives reset, why uninitialised globals are zero, and why local variables contain garbage. Why interviewers ask this It shows you understand why a global initialised to 5 survives reset, why uninitialised globals are zero, and why local variables contain garbage."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "12-4-stack-overflow", "title": "12.4 Stack overflow", "text": ""}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "12-5-structure-padding-recap-with-detail", "title": "12.5 Structure padding (recap with detail)", "text": "Processors prefer a 4-byte value at an address divisible by 4. The compiler adds padding to guarantee alignment. struct A { char a; int b; char c; }; // offsets: a=0, b=4, c=8, size 12 struct B { int b; char a; char c; }; // offsets: b=0, a=4, c=5, size 8 // remove padding (slower access, not portable, may fault on some CPUs) struct __attribute__((packed)) P { char a; int b; char c; }; // size 6 Packed structs are useful for matching a communication packet byte by byte, but unaligned access can be slow or even cause a hardware fault on some ARM cores. Prefer ordering members largest first."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "12-6-endianness", "title": "12.6 Endianness", "text": "A 32-bit number takes 4 bytes. Endianness is the order in which those bytes are stored. Little-endian Big-endian Stores first Least significant byte Most significant byte 0x12345678 in memory 78 56 34 12 12 34 56 78 Used by x86 PCs, ESP32, ESP8266, AVR, most ARM Cortex-M Network byte order, some old CPUs Little-endian Big-endian Stores first Least significant byte Most significant byte 0x12345678 in memory 78 56 34 12 12 34 56 78 Used by x86 PCs, ESP32, ESP8266, AVR, most ARM Cortex-M Network byte order, some old CPUs Analogy: writing a date Little-endian is like writing 29-10-2026 (small unit first). Big-endian is like 2026-10-29 (biggest unit first). Both hold the same date; both sides must agree on the order. Analogy: writing a date Little-endian is like writing 29-10-2026 (small unit first). Big-endian is like 2026-10-29 (biggest unit first). Both hold the same date; both sides must agree on the order. #include <stdio.h> #include <stdint.h> int main(void) { uint32_t x = 0x12345678; uint8_t *p = (uint8_t *)&x; // look at the first byte in memory if (*p == 0x78) printf(\"Little-endian\\n\"); else printf(\"Big-endian\\n\"); return 0; } Output: Little-endian Endianness matters when you send multi-byte values over UART, I2C, SPI or a network, or read a binary file made on another system. Convert explicitly, for example by sending the high byte first: tx[0] = (value >> 8) & 0xFF; tx[1] = value & 0xFF;"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "common-mistakes-12", "title": "Common mistakes", "text": "Mistake Fix Assuming local variables start at 0 They hold garbage; only globals and statics are zeroed Large arrays declared as locals Make them static or global, or shrink them Assuming sizeof(struct) equals the sum of members Print it, and order members by size Sending a struct directly over a network or SPI Serialise field by field with a fixed byte order Ignoring heap and stack collision on tiny RAM Track free memory and avoid dynamic allocation Assuming every system is little-endian Convert explicitly when data crosses systems Mistake Fix Assuming local variables start at 0 They hold garbage; only globals and statics are zeroed Large arrays declared as locals Make them static or global, or shrink them Assuming sizeof(struct) equals the sum of members Print it, and order members by size Sending a struct directly over a network or SPI Serialise field by field with a fixed byte order Ignoring heap and stack collision on tiny RAM Track free memory and avoid dynamic allocation Assuming every system is little-endian Convert explicitly when data crosses systems"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "practice-programs-type-each-one-yourself-12", "title": "Practice programs (type each one yourself)", "text": ""}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "interview-questions-with-short-answers-12", "title": "Interview questions with short answers", "text": "Q1. What are .text, .data and .bss? .text is code, .data is initialised globals, .bss is zero-initialised globals. Q2. Why are uninitialised globals zero? Startup code clears the .bss section before main. Q3. Where is a const global stored on a microcontroller? In flash (.rodata), not in RAM. Q4. What happens before main()? Reset handler: set stack pointer, copy .data, clear .bss, init clocks, call main. Q5. What is stack overflow? The stack grows into other memory because of deep calls or big locals. Q6. What is endianness? Which is ESP32? It is the byte order of multi-byte values; the ESP32 is little-endian. Q7. What is structure padding and how do you avoid wasting memory? Alignment gaps between members; order members from largest to smallest. TOPIC 13"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "registers-memory-mapped-i-o-and-function-pointers", "title": "Registers, Memory-Mapped I/O and Function Pointers", "text": "In one line In a microcontroller, peripherals (GPIO, timers, UART) are controlled by writing numbers to special memory addresses called registers; C does this with volatile pointers to fixed addresses. In one line In a microcontroller, peripherals (GPIO, timers, UART) are controlled by writing numbers to special memory addresses called registers; C does this with volatile pointers to fixed addresses."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "13-1-what-is-a-register", "title": "13.1 What is a register?", "text": "A register is a small memory location, 8, 16 or 32 bits wide, that is connected to hardware instead of ordinary RAM. Writing to it changes hardware behaviour; reading it tells you the hardware state. Analogy: the control panel of a machine Every switch and gauge on the panel has a fixed position. Memory-mapped I/O means each switch and gauge has a fixed memory address. Writing to the address flips the switch; reading the address reads the gauge. Analogy: the control panel of a machine Every switch and gauge on the panel has a fixed position. Memory-mapped I/O means each switch and gauge has a fixed memory address. Writing to the address flips the switch; reading the address reads the gauge. Register type Purpose Control Configure the peripheral (enable it, set mode, pick speed) Status Hardware reports state (data ready, buffer empty, error) Data Hold the data to send or the data received Direction (GPIO) Choose input or output per pin Interrupt enable / flag Turn interrupts on and show which one fired Register type Purpose Control Configure the peripheral (enable it, set mode, pick speed) Status Hardware reports state (data ready, buffer empty, error) Data Hold the data to send or the data received Direction (GPIO) Choose input or output per pin Interrupt enable / flag Turn interrupts on and show which one fired"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "13-2-accessing-a-fixed-address", "title": "13.2 Accessing a fixed address", "text": "The pattern is: cast the address to a pointer to volatile, then dereference. // NOTE: 0x40020014 is an example address. Real addresses come from // the chip's reference manual or its header file. #define GPIOA_ODR (*(volatile uint32_t *)0x40020014) GPIOA_ODR |= (1U << 5); // set pin 5 high GPIOA_ODR &= ~(1U << 5); // set pin 5 low GPIOA_ODR ^= (1U << 5); // toggle pin 5 Read the macro from the inside out: 0x40020014 is a number; (volatile uint32_t *) says \"treat it as the address of a volatile 32-bit value\"; the leading * says \"the value at that address\". The macro therefore behaves like a variable that is really hardware."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "13-3-grouping-registers-in-a-struct", "title": "13.3 Grouping registers in a struct", "text": "A peripheral usually has many registers in a row. A struct that matches the layout lets you name them (this is what vendor headers like STM32 CMSIS do). typedef struct { volatile uint32_t MODER; // offset 0x00 : pin mode (input/output) volatile uint32_t OTYPER; // offset 0x04 volatile uint32_t OSPEEDR; // offset 0x08 volatile uint32_t PUPDR; // offset 0x0C volatile uint32_t IDR; // offset 0x10 : input data volatile uint32_t ODR; // offset 0x14 : output data } GPIO_TypeDef; #define GPIOA ((GPIO_TypeDef *)0x40020000) GPIOA->MODER |= (1U << (5 * 2)); // pin 5 as output (2 bits per pin) GPIOA->ODR ^= (1U << 5); // toggle pin 5 if (GPIOA->IDR & (1U << 0)) { /* pin 0 is high */ }"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "13-4-practice-safely-simulate-a-register-on-a-pc", "title": "13.4 Practice safely: simulate a register on a PC", "text": "You can test all the bit logic without hardware by pointing at a normal variable. #include <stdio.h> #include <stdint.h> static volatile uint32_t fake_odr = 0; // pretend hardware register #define ODR (*(&fake_odr)) #define LED_PIN 5 int main(void) { ODR |= (1U << LED_PIN); // LED on printf(\"ODR = 0x%08X\\n\", (unsigned)ODR); ODR ^= (1U << LED_PIN); // toggle -> off printf(\"ODR = 0x%08X\\n\", (unsigned)ODR); return 0; } Output: ODR = 0x00000020 ODR = 0x00000000"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "13-5-read-modify-write-and-its-risks", "title": "13.5 Read-modify-write and its risks", "text": "ODR |= mask looks like one operation, but the CPU does three: read the register, change the bits, write it back. If an interrupt changes the same register between the read and the write, its change is lost. Solutions: disable interrupts around it, or use hardware set and clear registers (BSRR on STM32, set/clear registers on ESP32 such as GPIO_OUT_W1TS and GPIO_OUT_W1TC) that change a bit with one write."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "13-6-function-pointers-and-callbacks", "title": "13.6 Function pointers and callbacks", "text": "A function pointer stores the address of a function. Embedded software uses them to let low-level drivers call back into your application code. typedef void (*ButtonCallback)(void); static ButtonCallback on_press = NULL; void button_register(ButtonCallback cb) { // driver API on_press = cb; } void button_isr(void) { // driver calls your code if (on_press != NULL) on_press(); } void my_handler(void) { printf(\"Button pressed!\\n\"); } // application code int main(void) { button_register(my_handler); button_isr(); // simulate an interrupt return 0; } Output: Button pressed! The driver does not need to know what your application does, and your application does not need to know how the button is read. This separation is the heart of clean driver design."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "13-7-table-of-function-pointers-jump-table", "title": "13.7 Table of function pointers (jump table)", "text": "void cmd_on(void) { printf(\"LED on\\n\"); } void cmd_off(void) { printf(\"LED off\\n\"); } void cmd_blink(void) { printf(\"LED blink\\n\"); } void (*commands[])(void) = { cmd_on, cmd_off, cmd_blink }; int index = 1; // received from UART, for example if (index >= 0 && index < 3) // validate before using commands[index](); // calls cmd_off Output: LED off The interrupt vector table on an ARM chip is the same idea: an array of function addresses, one for each interrupt source."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "common-mistakes-13", "title": "Common mistakes", "text": "Mistake Fix Forgetting volatile on register pointers Always cast to volatile Using the wrong width (uint8_t for a 32-bit register) Match the register width in the datasheet Overwriting a register with = instead of |= or &= Change only the bits you intend Using an address from memory or the wrong chip Take addresses from the reference manual or vendor headers Calling a NULL function pointer Check for NULL, or provide a default handler Index out of range in a function pointer table Validate the index before calling Mistake Fix Forgetting volatile on register pointers Always cast to volatile Using the wrong width (uint8_t for a 32-bit register) Match the register width in the datasheet Overwriting a register with = instead of |= or &amp;= Change only the bits you intend Using an address from memory or the wrong chip Take addresses from the reference manual or vendor headers Calling a NULL function pointer Check for NULL, or provide a default handler Index out of range in a function pointer table Validate the index before calling"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "practice-programs-type-each-one-yourself-13", "title": "Practice programs (type each one yourself)", "text": ""}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "interview-questions-with-short-answers-13", "title": "Interview questions with short answers", "text": "Q1. What is memory-mapped I/O? Peripheral registers are placed in the normal address space, so you control hardware with ordinary reads and writes. Q2. Explain *(volatile uint32_t *)0x40020014 = 1; Treat the number as the address of a volatile 32-bit value and write 1 there. Q3. Why must register pointers be volatile? The hardware can change the value and every access has an effect, so the compiler must not cache or remove accesses. Q4. Why is read-modify-write dangerous? An interrupt between the read and the write can be overwritten; use atomic set/clear registers or disable interrupts. Q5. What is a callback? A function you pass to a driver so that the driver calls it when an event happens. Q6. What is the interrupt vector table? An array of function addresses, one per interrupt source, used by the CPU to jump to the right handler. TOPIC 14"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "gpio-and-debouncing", "title": "GPIO and Debouncing", "text": "In one line GPIO (General Purpose Input/Output) pins let the microcontroller read buttons and drive LEDs; debouncing removes the false presses caused by a mechanical switch's contacts bouncing. In one line GPIO (General Purpose Input/Output) pins let the microcontroller read buttons and drive LEDs; debouncing removes the false presses caused by a mechanical switch's contacts bouncing."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "14-1-what-is-a-gpio-pin", "title": "14.1 What is a GPIO pin?", "text": "A pin you can configure in software as an input (read high or low) or an output (drive high or low). High means close to the supply voltage (3.3 V on ESP32 and ESP8266, 5 V on Arduino Uno); low means 0 V. Mode Meaning Input Pin listens. Reads 1 if voltage is high, 0 if low Input with pull-up Internal resistor pulls the pin to high when nothing drives it Input with pull-down Internal resistor pulls the pin to low when nothing drives it Output push-pull Pin actively drives both high and low (normal LED output) Output open-drain Pin can only pull low; a pull-up resistor makes high (I2C uses this) Mode Meaning Input Pin listens. Reads 1 if voltage is high, 0 if low Input with pull-up Internal resistor pulls the pin to high when nothing drives it Input with pull-down Internal resistor pulls the pin to low when nothing drives it Output push-pull Pin actively drives both high and low (normal LED output) Output open-drain Pin can only pull low; a pull-up resistor makes high (I2C uses this)"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "14-2-floating-inputs-and-pull-up-resistors", "title": "14.2 Floating inputs and pull-up resistors", "text": "Analogy: a door that needs a spring A door with no spring flaps open and shut in the wind. An input pin that is connected to nothing (floating) picks up noise and reads random 0s and 1s. A pull-up or pull-down resistor is the spring that holds the pin in a known state. Analogy: a door that needs a spring A door with no spring flaps open and shut in the wind. An input pin that is connected to nothing (floating) picks up noise and reads random 0s and 1s. A pull-up or pull-down resistor is the spring that holds the pin in a known state. Common button wiring: connect the button between the pin and GND and enable the internal pull-up. Not pressed: pin reads HIGH. Pressed: pin reads LOW. This is called active-low and needs no external resistor. ESP32 pin note GPIO 34 to 39 on the ESP32 are input only and have no internal pull-up or pull-down. Use an external resistor there. Also avoid pins used for boot (such as GPIO 0, 2, 12 and 15) for buttons until you read the datasheet. ESP32 pin note GPIO 34 to 39 on the ESP32 are input only and have no internal pull-up or pull-down. Use an external resistor there. Also avoid pins used for boot (such as GPIO 0, 2, 12 and 15) for buttons until you read the datasheet."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "14-3-output-driving-an-led", "title": "14.3 Output: driving an LED", "text": "const int LED_PIN = 2; void setup() { pinMode(LED_PIN, OUTPUT); } void loop() { digitalWrite(LED_PIN, HIGH); delay(500); digitalWrite(LED_PIN, LOW); delay(500); } Always use a series resistor with an external LED. Example: supply 3.3 V, LED drop 2.0 V, wanted current 10 mA. R = (3.3 - 2.0) / 0.010 = 130 ohm. Use the next standard value, 150 or 220 ohm. Without a resistor the LED (or the pin) burns out."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "14-4-input-reading-a-button", "title": "14.4 Input: reading a button", "text": "const int BTN_PIN = 4; const int LED_PIN = 2; void setup() { pinMode(BTN_PIN, INPUT_PULLUP); // pressed = LOW pinMode(LED_PIN, OUTPUT); } void loop() { if (digitalRead(BTN_PIN) == LOW) // button pressed digitalWrite(LED_PIN, HIGH); else digitalWrite(LED_PIN, LOW); }"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "14-5-switch-bounce", "title": "14.5 Switch bounce", "text": "A mechanical button is two pieces of metal. When they touch, they vibrate and make and break contact many times for about 1 to 20 milliseconds before settling. The microcontroller is fast enough to see every one of these as a separate press. What you do What the pin sees What your code may think One press HIGH, LOW, HIGH, LOW, HIGH, LOW (settles) 3 presses One release LOW, HIGH, LOW, HIGH (settles) 2 releases What you do What the pin sees What your code may think One press HIGH, LOW, HIGH, LOW, HIGH, LOW (settles) 3 presses One release LOW, HIGH, LOW, HIGH (settles) 2 releases The result: a counter that jumps by 3 or a toggle that flips randomly. Debouncing makes one physical press produce exactly one event."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "14-6-software-debouncing-without-blocking", "title": "14.6 Software debouncing without blocking", "text": "The simple fix delay(20) after a press blocks the whole program. A better method: accept a new value only if it has stayed the same for a debounce time, using millis() for timing. const int BTN_PIN = 4; const unsigned long DEBOUNCE_MS = 30; int stableState = HIGH; // debounced state of the button int lastReading = HIGH; // raw reading from the previous loop unsigned long lastChange = 0; // when the raw reading last changed int pressCount = 0; void setup() { pinMode(BTN_PIN, INPUT_PULLUP); Serial.begin(115200); } void loop() { int reading = digitalRead(BTN_PIN); if (reading != lastReading) { // raw value changed: restart timer lastChange = millis(); lastReading = reading; } if ((millis() - lastChange) > DEBOUNCE_MS && reading != stableState) { stableState = reading; // it stayed stable long enough if (stableState == LOW) { // falling edge = real press pressCount++; Serial.println(pressCount); } } } The program stays free to do other work in loop() because nothing waits. The trick: millis() - lastChange measures how long the raw reading has stayed the same."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "14-7-edge-detection-act-on-the-change-not-the-level", "title": "14.7 Edge detection: act on the change, not the level", "text": ""}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "14-8-other-debounce-methods", "title": "14.8 Other debounce methods", "text": "Method Notes Hardware RC filter + Schmitt trigger Resistor and capacitor smooth the signal; no code needed; uses parts Timer sampling Read the pin every 5 ms in a timer interrupt; count consecutive equal samples Shift-register debounce Shift each sample into a byte; accept a press when the byte equals 0x00 or 0xFF Libraries Bounce2 on Arduino; but understand the method first Method Notes Hardware RC filter + Schmitt trigger Resistor and capacitor smooth the signal; no code needed; uses parts Timer sampling Read the pin every 5 ms in a timer interrupt; count consecutive equal samples Shift-register debounce Shift each sample into a byte; accept a press when the byte equals 0x00 or 0xFF Libraries Bounce2 on Arduino; but understand the method first"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "common-mistakes-14", "title": "Common mistakes", "text": "Mistake Fix Leaving an input floating Enable INPUT_PULLUP or add a resistor Connecting an LED without a resistor Calculate R = (Vsupply - Vled) / I Driving a motor or relay straight from a GPIO Use a transistor or driver and a flyback diode Confusing active-low and active-high logic Write down: pressed = LOW with pull-up Using delay() to debounce in a larger program Use millis-based timing Feeding 5 V into a 3.3 V ESP pin Use a level shifter or a voltage divider Mistake Fix Leaving an input floating Enable INPUT_PULLUP or add a resistor Connecting an LED without a resistor Calculate R = (Vsupply - Vled) / I Driving a motor or relay straight from a GPIO Use a transistor or driver and a flyback diode Confusing active-low and active-high logic Write down: pressed = LOW with pull-up Using delay() to debounce in a larger program Use millis-based timing Feeding 5 V into a 3.3 V ESP pin Use a level shifter or a voltage divider"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "practice-programs-type-each-one-yourself-14", "title": "Practice programs (type each one yourself)", "text": ""}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "interview-questions-with-short-answers-14", "title": "Interview questions with short answers", "text": "Q1. What is a pull-up resistor? A resistor that holds an input at a high level when nothing else drives it. Q2. What is a floating pin? An unconnected or undriven input that reads random values from noise. Q3. Push-pull vs open-drain? Push-pull drives both high and low; open-drain only pulls low and needs an external pull-up. Q4. What is switch bounce and how do you fix it? Contacts make and break rapidly for milliseconds; fix with a hardware RC filter or software timing. Q5. Why not use delay() for debouncing? It blocks the processor, so other tasks stop. Q6. How do you calculate an LED resistor? R = (supply voltage - LED forward voltage) / desired current. TOPIC 15"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "interrupts-and-isr", "title": "Interrupts and ISR", "text": "In one line An interrupt makes the CPU pause its normal work, run a short function (ISR) to deal with an urgent event, and then continue exactly where it stopped. In one line An interrupt makes the CPU pause its normal work, run a short function (ISR) to deal with an urgent event, and then continue exactly where it stopped."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "15-1-polling-vs-interrupts", "title": "15.1 Polling vs interrupts", "text": "Analogy: waiting for a visitor Polling is walking to the door every few seconds to see if someone is there. An interrupt is a doorbell: you work normally, and when it rings you go to the door, then return to your work. Analogy: waiting for a visitor Polling is walking to the door every few seconds to see if someone is there. An interrupt is a doorbell: you work normally, and when it rings you go to the door, then return to your work. Polling Interrupt Reaction time Depends on how often you check Very fast, hardware triggered CPU use Wastes time checking Uses CPU only when needed Complexity Simple Needs care (shared data, timing) Good for Slow, non-urgent inputs Urgent events, short pulses, communication, wake from sleep Polling Interrupt Reaction time Depends on how often you check Very fast, hardware triggered CPU use Wastes time checking Uses CPU only when needed Complexity Simple Needs care (shared data, timing) Good for Slow, non-urgent inputs Urgent events, short pulses, communication, wake from sleep"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "15-2-what-happens-when-an-interrupt-fires", "title": "15.2 What happens when an interrupt fires", "text": "The delay between the event and the first ISR instruction is the interrupt latency. Keeping ISRs short keeps latency for other interrupts low."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "15-3-the-golden-rules-of-an-isr", "title": "15.3 The golden rules of an ISR", "text": "Rule Reason Keep it short and fast While an ISR runs, other work and (often) other interrupts wait No delay(), no waiting loops The timer that delay depends on may be blocked; the system freezes No printf or Serial.print They are slow, may use interrupts themselves and are not safe here No malloc / free Not re-entrant and unpredictable in time Do not call functions that block Anything that waits can hang the whole program Share data through volatile variables Otherwise the compiler may never see the change Set a flag or push data to a buffer, process in main Work in the ISR, think in main Rule Reason Keep it short and fast While an ISR runs, other work and (often) other interrupts wait No delay(), no waiting loops The timer that delay depends on may be blocked; the system freezes No printf or Serial.print They are slow, may use interrupts themselves and are not safe here No malloc / free Not re-entrant and unpredictable in time Do not call functions that block Anything that waits can hang the whole program Share data through volatile variables Otherwise the compiler may never see the change Set a flag or push data to a buffer, process in main Work in the ISR, think in main"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "15-4-example-button-interrupt-with-a-flag", "title": "15.4 Example: button interrupt with a flag", "text": "const int BTN_PIN = 4; volatile bool buttonFlag = false; // shared with the ISR: volatile volatile unsigned long lastIsrTime = 0; void IRAM_ATTR onButton() { // ESP32: ISR must live in IRAM unsigned long now = millis(); if (now - lastIsrTime > 50) { // ignore bounces within 50 ms buttonFlag = true; // just set a flag lastIsrTime = now; } } void setup() { Serial.begin(115200); pinMode(BTN_PIN, INPUT_PULLUP); attachInterrupt(digitalPinToInterrupt(BTN_PIN), onButton, FALLING); } void loop() { if (buttonFlag) { // main code does the real work buttonFlag = false; Serial.println(\"Button pressed\"); } // other tasks keep running here }"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "15-5-race-conditions-and-atomic-access", "title": "15.5 Race conditions and atomic access", "text": "A race condition happens when main code and an ISR use the same data and the result depends on timing. Example of the problem An 8-bit AVR reads a 16-bit variable in two steps. If an interrupt changes the variable between the two reads, main gets the low byte of the old value and the high byte of the new value: a number that never existed. Example of the problem An 8-bit AVR reads a 16-bit variable in two steps. If an interrupt changes the variable between the two reads, main gets the low byte of the old value and the high byte of the new value: a number that never existed. volatile uint16_t pulseCount = 0; // incremented in an ISR uint16_t readCount(void) { noInterrupts(); // start critical section uint16_t copy = pulseCount; // read safely interrupts(); // end critical section return copy; } Keep critical sections as short as possible. On 32-bit chips a 32-bit read is a single instruction and is already atomic, but read-modify-write sequences like count++ still are not."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "15-6-priority-nesting-and-flags", "title": "15.6 Priority, nesting and flags", "text": ""}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "15-7-using-the-avr-timer-interrupt-uno", "title": "15.7 Using the AVR timer interrupt (Uno)", "text": "volatile unsigned long ticks = 0; ISR(TIMER2_COMPA_vect) { // ISR macro from avr/interrupt.h ticks++; } The Timer topic (16) shows how to configure this timer so the ISR fires every millisecond."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "common-mistakes-15", "title": "Common mistakes", "text": "Mistake Fix Not declaring shared variables volatile Add volatile for everything the ISR and main both use Calling Serial.print or delay inside an ISR Set a flag and print in loop() Long processing inside the ISR Store data and exit quickly; process in main Not debouncing a button interrupt Ignore interrupts that come within a few tens of milliseconds Reading multi-byte shared data without protection Disable interrupts briefly while copying Forgetting IRAM_ATTR on ESP32 ISRs Add it; otherwise random crashes may occur Mistake Fix Not declaring shared variables volatile Add volatile for everything the ISR and main both use Calling Serial.print or delay inside an ISR Set a flag and print in loop() Long processing inside the ISR Store data and exit quickly; process in main Not debouncing a button interrupt Ignore interrupts that come within a few tens of milliseconds Reading multi-byte shared data without protection Disable interrupts briefly while copying Forgetting IRAM_ATTR on ESP32 ISRs Add it; otherwise random crashes may occur"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "practice-programs-type-each-one-yourself-15", "title": "Practice programs (type each one yourself)", "text": ""}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "interview-questions-with-short-answers-15", "title": "Interview questions with short answers", "text": "Q1. What is an interrupt? A hardware signal that makes the CPU pause, run a handler and then resume. Q2. What is an ISR and what rules does it follow? The handler function; keep it short, no blocking or printing, use volatile shared data. Q3. Why not call printf in an ISR? It is slow, may block and is not re-entrant. Q4. What is interrupt latency? The time from the event to the first instruction of the ISR. Q5. What is a race condition? A bug where the result depends on the timing between an ISR and main code accessing shared data. Q6. Polling vs interrupt: when to use which? Polling for simple, slow, non-urgent inputs; interrupts for urgent or short events and to save power. Q7. What is the vector table? A table of handler addresses, one per interrupt source. TOPIC 16"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "timers-pwm-and-adc", "title": "Timers, PWM and ADC", "text": "In one line A timer counts clock ticks to measure and create precise time; PWM uses fast on-off switching to control power; an ADC converts a real-world voltage into a number. In one line A timer counts clock ticks to measure and create precise time; PWM uses fast on-off switching to control power; an ADC converts a real-world voltage into a number."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "16-1-timers", "title": "16.1 Timers", "text": "A hardware timer is a counter that increases by one on every tick of a clock, completely independent of your program. It can raise an interrupt on overflow or when it reaches a chosen compare value. Analogy: a kitchen timer You set the number of minutes, go on cooking, and it rings at the right time. A hardware timer keeps counting while the CPU does other jobs. Analogy: a kitchen timer You set the number of minutes, go on cooking, and it rings at the right time. A hardware timer keeps counting while the CPU does other jobs. Key terms: How to calculate a period, step by step (Uno: 16 MHz clock, want 1 ms): // Arduino Uno: Timer2 generates an interrupt every 1 ms volatile unsigned long ms = 0; void setup() { noInterrupts(); TCCR2A = (1 << WGM21); // CTC mode: clear counter on match TCCR2B = (1 << CS22); // prescaler 64 OCR2A = 249; // compare value TIMSK2 = (1 << OCIE2A); // enable compare-match interrupt interrupts(); } ISR(TIMER2_COMPA_vect) { ms++; // runs every 1 ms } void loop() { // use ms as a millisecond clock here } This register-level code is for the Uno (AVR). Using Timer2 leaves Timer0 free for millis() and delay(), but tone() also uses Timer2. On ESP32 and ESP8266 the timer API differs between core versions, so check the documentation of the core you installed. The calculation is the same on every chip."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "16-2-non-blocking-timing-with-millis", "title": "16.2 Non-blocking timing with millis()", "text": "unsigned long previous = 0; const unsigned long INTERVAL = 1000; // 1 second bool ledOn = false; void loop() { unsigned long now = millis(); if (now - previous >= INTERVAL) { // subtraction is safe when millis() wraps previous = now; ledOn = !ledOn; digitalWrite(2, ledOn); } // loop keeps running: read sensors, check buttons... }"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "16-3-pwm-pulse-width-modulation", "title": "16.3 PWM: Pulse Width Modulation", "text": "PWM switches a pin on and off very fast. The fraction of time it is ON is the duty cycle. A load that cannot follow such fast changes (an LED to the eye, a motor with inertia) sees the average. Duty cycle Average voltage (3.3 V) Effect 0% 0 V Always off 25% 0.83 V Dim LED, slow motor 50% 1.65 V Half brightness or speed 75% 2.48 V Bright 100% 3.3 V Always on Duty cycle Average voltage (3.3 V) Effect 0% 0 V Always off 25% 0.83 V Dim LED, slow motor 50% 1.65 V Half brightness or speed 75% 2.48 V Bright 100% 3.3 V Always on // Arduino Uno: fade an LED on pin 9 int brightness = 0; int step = 5; void setup() { pinMode(9, OUTPUT); } void loop() { analogWrite(9, brightness); // 0 = off, 255 = full brightness += step; if (brightness <= 0 || brightness >= 255) step = -step; delay(20); } Servo motors use a specific PWM: 50 Hz (20 ms period) with a pulse of 1 ms (one end), 1.5 ms (centre) and 2 ms (other end). Use a Servo library so the timing is exact."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "16-4-adc-analog-to-digital-converter", "title": "16.4 ADC: Analog to Digital Converter", "text": "The world is analog: temperature, light, sound and battery level are smoothly changing voltages. The CPU understands numbers. The ADC measures a voltage and returns a number. Board Resolution Count range Input range Arduino Uno 10 bit 0 to 1023 0 to 5 V ESP32 12 bit 0 to 4095 about 0 to 3.3 V ESP8266 (NodeMCU A0) 10 bit 0 to 1023 0 to 3.3 V (board divider) Board Resolution Count range Input range Arduino Uno 10 bit 0 to 1023 0 to 5 V ESP32 12 bit 0 to 4095 about 0 to 3.3 V ESP8266 (NodeMCU A0) 10 bit 0 to 1023 0 to 3.3 V (board divider) Conversion formula (using the full-scale count): voltage = count x Vref / maxCount Example (Uno): count 512, Vref 5 V, max 1023: voltage = 512 x 5 / 1023 = 2.50 V. Resolution (step size) = Vref / 2^N. A 10-bit ADC at 5 V has steps of about 4.9 mV; a 12-bit ADC at 3.3 V has steps of about 0.8 mV. voltage = count x Vref / maxCount Example (Uno): count 512, Vref 5 V, max 1023: voltage = 512 x 5 / 1023 = 2.50 V. Resolution (step size) = Vref / 2^N. A 10-bit ADC at 5 V has steps of about 4.9 mV; a 12-bit ADC at 3.3 V has steps of about 0.8 mV. // Read an LM35 temperature sensor (10 mV per degree C) on an Uno const int SENSOR = A0; void setup() { Serial.begin(9600); } void loop() { int count = analogRead(SENSOR); // 0 to 1023 float volts = count * 5.0f / 1023.0f; float tempC = volts * 100.0f; // 10 mV per degree Serial.println(tempC); delay(1000); }"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "16-5-getting-clean-adc-readings", "title": "16.5 Getting clean ADC readings", "text": "int readAveraged(int pin, int samples) { long sum = 0; for (int i = 0; i < samples; i++) { sum += analogRead(pin); delay(2); } return (int)(sum / samples); }"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "common-mistakes-16", "title": "Common mistakes", "text": "Mistake Fix Using delay() for all timing Use millis() or hardware timers Forgetting that counters start at 0 (compare value off by one) Use ticks - 1 Comparing millis() values as absolute times Use now - previous >= interval Expecting analogWrite to give a true analog voltage It is PWM; add an RC filter if you need a smooth voltage Feeding more than 3.3 V into an ESP ADC pin Use a voltage divider Using int for a time that overflows quickly Use unsigned long Mistake Fix Using delay() for all timing Use millis() or hardware timers Forgetting that counters start at 0 (compare value off by one) Use ticks - 1 Comparing millis() values as absolute times Use now - previous &gt;= interval Expecting analogWrite to give a true analog voltage It is PWM; add an RC filter if you need a smooth voltage Feeding more than 3.3 V into an ESP ADC pin Use a voltage divider Using int for a time that overflows quickly Use unsigned long"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "practice-programs-type-each-one-yourself-16", "title": "Practice programs (type each one yourself)", "text": ""}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "interview-questions-with-short-answers-16", "title": "Interview questions with short answers", "text": "Q1. What is a prescaler? A divider that slows the clock fed to a timer so it can count longer periods. Q2. How do you get a 1 ms interrupt from a 16 MHz clock? Prescaler 64 gives 250 kHz; count 250 ticks; compare value 249 in CTC mode. Q3. What is PWM and what is duty cycle? Fast on-off switching; duty cycle is the percentage of the period the signal is high. Q4. How is the average voltage of a PWM signal calculated? Duty cycle x supply voltage. Q5. What is the resolution of a 12-bit ADC with 3.3 V reference? 3.3 / 4096 which is about 0.8 mV per step. Q6. Why use millis() instead of delay()? millis() lets the program do other tasks while waiting. Q7. How can you reduce ADC noise? Average multiple samples, add a capacitor, keep wires short and use a stable reference. TOPIC 17"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "communication-protocols-uart-i2c-and-spi", "title": "Communication Protocols: UART, I2C and SPI", "text": "In one line Protocols are agreed rules for sending bytes between chips: UART is a simple two-wire serial link, I2C connects many devices on two shared wires, and SPI is a fast four-wire link. In one line Protocols are agreed rules for sending bytes between chips: UART is a simple two-wire serial link, I2C connects many devices on two shared wires, and SPI is a fast four-wire link."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "17-1-serial-vs-parallel-sync-vs-async", "title": "17.1 Serial vs parallel, sync vs async", "text": ""}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "17-2-uart", "title": "17.2 UART", "text": "UART (Universal Asynchronous Receiver Transmitter) connects exactly two devices. It is what the Serial Monitor, GPS modules, Bluetooth modules (HC-05) and many sensors use. Wire Connection TX Transmit of device A goes to RX of device B RX Receive of device A comes from TX of device B (so TX and RX are crossed) GND Common ground is mandatory Wire Connection TX Transmit of device A goes to RX of device B RX Receive of device A comes from TX of device B (so TX and RX are crossed) GND Common ground is mandatory Frame format 8N1 (the most common): when idle the line is HIGH. One byte is sent as: 1 start bit (LOW), 8 data bits (least significant bit first), no parity, 1 stop bit (HIGH). That is 10 bits for 8 bits of data. void setup() { Serial.begin(115200); // must match the Serial Monitor } void loop() { if (Serial.available() > 0) { // a byte has arrived char c = Serial.read(); Serial.print(\"Got: \"); Serial.println(c); } } Receiving commands properly: build a line in a buffer until you see a newline, then parse it. Check buffer length so a long message cannot overflow it. char line[32]; uint8_t len = 0; void loop() { while (Serial.available() > 0) { char c = Serial.read(); if (c == '\\n') { line[len] = '\\0'; handle_command(line); // e.g. compare with strcmp len = 0; } else if (len < sizeof(line) - 1) { line[len++] = c; // protect against overflow } } }"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "17-3-i2c", "title": "17.3 I2C", "text": "I2C (Inter-Integrated Circuit, \"I squared C\") uses just two shared wires for many devices: SDA (data) and SCL (clock). One master (your microcontroller) talks to several slaves (sensors, displays, EEPROM). Each slave has a 7-bit address. Analogy: a classroom The teacher (master) calls a student by name (the address) and asks a question. Only the student whose name was called answers. Everyone shares the same room (the two wires). Analogy: a classroom The teacher (master) calls a student by name (the address) and asks a question. Only the student whose name was called answers. Everyone shares the same room (the two wires). // I2C scanner: finds the addresses of all connected devices #include <Wire.h> void setup() { Serial.begin(115200); Wire.begin(); for (uint8_t addr = 1; addr < 127; addr++) { Wire.beginTransmission(addr); if (Wire.endTransmission() == 0) { // 0 = device answered (ACK) Serial.print(\"Found device at 0x\"); Serial.println(addr, HEX); } } } void loop() {} // Read two bytes from a sensor register (generic pattern) Wire.beginTransmission(0x48); // device address Wire.write(0x00); // register to read Wire.endTransmission(); Wire.requestFrom(0x48, 2); // ask for 2 bytes if (Wire.available() >= 2) { uint8_t hi = Wire.read(); uint8_t lo = Wire.read(); int16_t raw = (hi << 8) | lo; // combine: byte order from the datasheet } Typical I2C devices: OLED SSD1306 (0x3C), MPU6050 (0x68), BMP280 (0x76 or 0x77), RTC DS3231 (0x68), LCD backpack (0x27 or 0x3F). A common error: SDA and SCL swapped or missing pull-ups, so the scanner finds nothing."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "17-4-spi", "title": "17.4 SPI", "text": "SPI (Serial Peripheral Interface) is fast and simple: one master and one or more slaves. Data goes in both directions at the same time (full duplex). Wire Meaning SCK Clock from the master MOSI Master Out, Slave In: data from master to slave MISO Master In, Slave Out: data from slave to master CS (SS) Chip Select, one per slave; pull LOW to talk to that slave Wire Meaning SCK Clock from the master MOSI Master Out, Slave In: data from master to slave MISO Master In, Slave Out: data from slave to master CS (SS) Chip Select, one per slave; pull LOW to talk to that slave #include <SPI.h> const int CS_PIN = 5; void setup() { pinMode(CS_PIN, OUTPUT); digitalWrite(CS_PIN, HIGH); // idle: slave not selected SPI.begin(); } uint8_t spiReadRegister(uint8_t reg) { SPI.beginTransaction(SPISettings(1000000, MSBFIRST, SPI_MODE0)); digitalWrite(CS_PIN, LOW); // select the slave SPI.transfer(reg | 0x80); // send register address (read flag is device specific) uint8_t value = SPI.transfer(0x00); // send dummy byte, get the answer digitalWrite(CS_PIN, HIGH); // release the slave SPI.endTransaction(); return value; }"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "17-5-comparison", "title": "17.5 Comparison", "text": "UART I2C SPI Wires 2 (TX, RX) + GND 2 shared (SDA, SCL) 3 + one CS per slave Devices 2 Many (addresses) Many (CS lines) Clock None (baud rate) Master clock Master clock Speed Up to about 1 Mbps 100 kHz to 400 kHz (up to MHz) 10 MHz and more Direction Full duplex Half duplex Full duplex Best for PC link, GPS, modules Sensors, small displays, RTC SD card, fast displays, flash UART I2C SPI Wires 2 (TX, RX) + GND 2 shared (SDA, SCL) 3 + one CS per slave Devices 2 Many (addresses) Many (CS lines) Clock None (baud rate) Master clock Master clock Speed Up to about 1 Mbps 100 kHz to 400 kHz (up to MHz) 10 MHz and more Direction Full duplex Half duplex Full duplex Best for PC link, GPS, modules Sensors, small displays, RTC SD card, fast displays, flash Quick rule: need to talk to a PC or a module with a serial port, use UART; many slow sensors on few pins, use I2C; high data rate, use SPI."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "17-6-multi-byte-values-and-byte-order", "title": "17.6 Multi-byte values and byte order", "text": "A 16-bit sensor value arrives as two bytes. The datasheet says which byte comes first. Combine as (high << 8) | low if the high byte arrives first. Mixing this up is a very common bug (see endianness in Topic 12)."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "common-mistakes-17", "title": "Common mistakes", "text": "Mistake Fix Wrong baud rate or format Match both devices (for example 115200 8N1) TX connected to TX instead of RX Cross the lines: TX to RX, RX to TX No common ground between the devices Always connect GND I2C without pull-up resistors Add 4.7 kohm to the supply on SDA and SCL Two I2C devices with the same address Change the address pin or use a multiplexer SPI mode or bit order mismatch Read the datasheet; set CPOL, CPHA and MSB or LSB first Mixing 5 V and 3.3 V logic Use level shifters Mistake Fix Wrong baud rate or format Match both devices (for example 115200 8N1) TX connected to TX instead of RX Cross the lines: TX to RX, RX to TX No common ground between the devices Always connect GND I2C without pull-up resistors Add 4.7 kohm to the supply on SDA and SCL Two I2C devices with the same address Change the address pin or use a multiplexer SPI mode or bit order mismatch Read the datasheet; set CPOL, CPHA and MSB or LSB first Mixing 5 V and 3.3 V logic Use level shifters"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "practice-programs-type-each-one-yourself-17", "title": "Practice programs (type each one yourself)", "text": ""}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "interview-questions-with-short-answers-17", "title": "Interview questions with short answers", "text": "Q1. What is baud rate? The number of symbols (bits) sent per second on a serial line. Q2. What does 8N1 mean? 8 data bits, no parity, 1 stop bit. Q3. Why does I2C need pull-up resistors? The lines are open-drain, so devices can only pull them low; resistors pull them high. Q4. How many devices can an I2C bus have? Up to 112 usable 7-bit addresses in theory, fewer in practice because of capacitance and address clashes. Q5. What are MOSI, MISO, SCK and CS? Master out slave in, master in slave out, clock, and chip select. Q6. I2C vs SPI? I2C uses two wires and addresses but is slower; SPI is faster and full duplex but needs more pins. Q7. Why is the ground connection important? Signals are voltages relative to ground; without a common ground the receiver has no reference. TOPIC 18"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "embedded-design-patterns-buffers-state-machines-watchdog-sle", "title": "Embedded Design Patterns: Buffers, State Machines, Watchdog, Sleep and RTOS", "text": "In one line These are the reusable building blocks of real firmware: a ring buffer to pass data safely, a state machine to organise behaviour, a watchdog to recover from hangs, sleep modes to save power and an RTOS to run several tasks. In one line These are the reusable building blocks of real firmware: a ring buffer to pass data safely, a state machine to organise behaviour, a watchdog to recover from hangs, sleep modes to save power and an RTOS to run several tasks."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "18-1-circular-ring-buffer", "title": "18.1 Circular (ring) buffer", "text": "A fixed-size buffer that wraps around. New data is written at the head; old data is read from the tail. Both indexes wrap to 0 when they reach the end. It is used for UART receive data, sensor samples and logging. Analogy: a circular conveyor belt One person puts items on the belt (head), another takes them off (tail). The belt never ends, it loops. If the putter is faster than the taker for too long, the belt fills up (overflow). Analogy: a circular conveyor belt One person puts items on the belt (head), another takes them off (tail). The belt never ends, it loops. If the putter is faster than the taker for too long, the belt fills up (overflow). #include <stdint.h> #include <stdbool.h> #define BUF_SIZE 8 // use a power of 2 so we can wrap with a mask typedef struct { uint8_t data[BUF_SIZE]; volatile uint8_t head; // written by the producer (ISR) volatile uint8_t tail; // written by the consumer (main) } RingBuffer; bool rb_is_empty(const RingBuffer *rb) { return rb->head == rb->tail; } bool rb_is_full (const RingBuffer *rb) { return ((rb->head + 1) & (BUF_SIZE - 1)) == rb->tail; // one slot stays empty } bool rb_put(RingBuffer *rb, uint8_t byte) { if (rb_is_full(rb)) return false; // overflow: report it rb->data[rb->head] = byte; rb->head = (rb->head + 1) & (BUF_SIZE - 1); // wrap: same as % 8 return true; } bool rb_get(RingBuffer *rb, uint8_t *byte) { if (rb_is_empty(rb)) return false; *byte = rb->data[rb->tail]; rb->tail = (rb->tail + 1) & (BUF_SIZE - 1); return true; } Typical use: the UART receive ISR calls rb_put for each byte; loop() calls rb_get and processes whatever has arrived."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "18-2-finite-state-machine-fsm", "title": "18.2 Finite state machine (FSM)", "text": "Many devices behave differently depending on what they are doing: waiting, running, error. A state machine keeps a variable for the current state and decides what to do next from the state and the event. It replaces tangled if-else flags with clear, testable logic. Current state Event Action Next state RED Timer (10 s) Turn red off, green on GREEN GREEN Timer (10 s) Turn green off, yellow on YELLOW YELLOW Timer (3 s) Turn yellow off, red on RED Current state Event Action Next state RED Timer (10 s) Turn red off, green on GREEN GREEN Timer (10 s) Turn green off, yellow on YELLOW YELLOW Timer (3 s) Turn yellow off, red on RED typedef enum { STATE_RED, STATE_GREEN, STATE_YELLOW } State; State state = STATE_RED; unsigned long stateStart = 0; void setLights(bool r, bool y, bool g) { digitalWrite(RED_PIN, r); digitalWrite(YELLOW_PIN, y); digitalWrite(GREEN_PIN, g); } void loop() { unsigned long now = millis(); switch (state) { case STATE_RED: setLights(1, 0, 0); if (now - stateStart >= 10000) { state = STATE_GREEN; stateStart = now; } break; case STATE_GREEN: setLights(0, 0, 1); if (now - stateStart >= 10000) { state = STATE_YELLOW; stateStart = now; } break; case STATE_YELLOW: setLights(0, 1, 0); if (now - stateStart >= 3000) { state = STATE_RED; stateStart = now; } break; } } You already built a traffic signal project; rebuilding it as an explicit state machine is a very good interview story."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "18-3-watchdog-timer", "title": "18.3 Watchdog timer", "text": "A watchdog is a hardware timer that resets the chip if your software does not \"kick\" (reset) it within a set time. If the code hangs in an infinite loop or crashes, the watchdog restarts the device automatically. Analogy: a dead-man's switch A train driver must press a pedal every few seconds. If the driver stops pressing, the train brakes automatically. The watchdog is the pedal; the reset is the brake. Analogy: a dead-man's switch A train driver must press a pedal every few seconds. If the driver stops pressing, the train brakes automatically. The watchdog is the pedal; the reset is the brake. // Arduino Uno (AVR) #include <avr/wdt.h> void setup() { wdt_enable(WDTO_2S); // reset if not kicked within 2 seconds } void loop() { do_work(); wdt_reset(); // kick the watchdog: \"I am still alive\" }"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "18-4-low-power-and-sleep-modes", "title": "18.4 Low-power and sleep modes", "text": "Battery products spend most of their time asleep. A CPU running at full speed may draw tens of milliamps; deep sleep can be microamps. Mode What happens Wake-up sources Active Everything runs - Light / idle sleep CPU stops, peripherals and RAM stay on; quick wake Timer, UART, GPIO interrupts Deep sleep Almost everything off; RAM often lost; slow wake RTC timer, special pins, touch Mode What happens Wake-up sources Active Everything runs - Light / idle sleep CPU stops, peripherals and RAM stay on; quick wake Timer, UART, GPIO interrupts Deep sleep Almost everything off; RAM often lost; slow wake RTC timer, special pins, touch // ESP32: sleep 10 seconds, wake, read the sensor, sleep again void setup() { Serial.begin(115200); read_and_send_sensor(); // do the work quickly esp_sleep_enable_timer_wakeup(10ULL * 1000000ULL); // time in microseconds esp_deep_sleep_start(); // the chip restarts at setup() on wake } void loop() {} // never reached"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "18-5-introduction-to-an-rtos", "title": "18.5 Introduction to an RTOS", "text": "A Real-Time Operating System lets you write several independent tasks (read sensor, update display, handle Wi-Fi) that appear to run at the same time. A scheduler switches between them. The ESP32 Arduino core already runs on FreeRTOS, and your loop() is one task. Term Meaning Task An independent function with its own stack and priority Scheduler Decides which task runs, usually the highest priority one that is ready Delay vTaskDelay lets other tasks run while this one waits (unlike delay-busy waiting) Queue Thread-safe way to pass data between tasks or from an ISR to a task Semaphore / Mutex Signals an event or protects a shared resource from two tasks at once Term Meaning Task An independent function with its own stack and priority Scheduler Decides which task runs, usually the highest priority one that is ready Delay vTaskDelay lets other tasks run while this one waits (unlike delay-busy waiting) Queue Thread-safe way to pass data between tasks or from an ISR to a task Semaphore / Mutex Signals an event or protects a shared resource from two tasks at once // ESP32 (Arduino core): two tasks void blinkTask(void *param) { pinMode(2, OUTPUT); while (true) { digitalWrite(2, !digitalRead(2)); vTaskDelay(500 / portTICK_PERIOD_MS); // sleep 500 ms, others run } } void sensorTask(void *param) { while (true) { int v = analogRead(34); Serial.println(v); vTaskDelay(1000 / portTICK_PERIOD_MS); } } void setup() { Serial.begin(115200); xTaskCreate(blinkTask, \"blink\", 2048, NULL, 1, NULL); // name, stack bytes, param, priority, handle xTaskCreate(sensorTask, \"sensor\", 4096, NULL, 1, NULL); } void loop() {}"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "common-mistakes-18", "title": "Common mistakes", "text": "Mistake Fix Ring buffer size not a power of 2 but using a mask Use % or choose 8, 16, 32, 64... Not handling ring buffer overflow Return false, count dropped bytes, or enlarge the buffer Kicking the watchdog inside a timer interrupt Kick it from main code after checking system health Using delay() inside a state machine Use millis comparisons so the machine never blocks Forgetting that deep sleep restarts the program Do everything in setup() and store state in RTC memory Sharing variables between RTOS tasks without protection Use a queue or mutex Mistake Fix Ring buffer size not a power of 2 but using a mask Use % or choose 8, 16, 32, 64... Not handling ring buffer overflow Return false, count dropped bytes, or enlarge the buffer Kicking the watchdog inside a timer interrupt Kick it from main code after checking system health Using delay() inside a state machine Use millis comparisons so the machine never blocks Forgetting that deep sleep restarts the program Do everything in setup() and store state in RTC memory Sharing variables between RTOS tasks without protection Use a queue or mutex"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "practice-programs-type-each-one-yourself-18", "title": "Practice programs (type each one yourself)", "text": ""}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "interview-questions-with-short-answers-18", "title": "Interview questions with short answers", "text": "Q1. What is a circular buffer and where is it used? A fixed-size FIFO that wraps around; used for UART data, sensor streams and logs. Q2. How do you tell a full buffer from an empty one? Empty when head equals tail; full when head + 1 equals tail (one slot kept free), or use a count variable. Q3. What is a finite state machine? A model with a finite set of states and rules that move between them when events occur. Q4. What is a watchdog timer? A hardware timer that resets the system if the software fails to reset it in time. Q5. Why should you not kick the watchdog from an ISR? The ISR can keep running while main is stuck, hiding the hang. Q6. What is the difference between light sleep and deep sleep? Light sleep stops the CPU but keeps RAM and quick wake; deep sleep powers down almost everything for the lowest current but restarts the program. Q7. Super-loop vs RTOS? A super-loop is one endless loop (simple); an RTOS schedules several tasks with priorities and timing guarantees. Q8. What is a mutex? A lock that lets only one task use a shared resource at a time."}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "final-checklist", "title": "Final checklist", "text": "Tick a box only when you have actually done it, not when you have only read it. No. Topic I can explain it I wrote the code I can answer Qs 1 Basics and Data Types ☐ ☐ ☐ 2 Operators and Control Flow ☐ ☐ ☐ 3 Functions and Recursion ☐ ☐ ☐ 4 Arrays and Strings ☐ ☐ ☐ 5 Pointers ☐ ☐ ☐ 6 Structures, Unions, Enums and typedef ☐ ☐ ☐ 7 Dynamic Memory Allocation ☐ ☐ ☐ 8 Storage Classes and the Preprocessor ☐ ☐ ☐ 9 File Handling ☐ ☐ ☐ 10 Bitwise Operators and Bit Manipulation ☐ ☐ ☐ 11 Type Qualifiers and Fixed-Width Types ☐ ☐ ☐ 12 Memory Layout, Startup Code, Padding and Endianness ☐ ☐ ☐ 13 Registers, Memory-Mapped I/O and Function Pointers ☐ ☐ ☐ 14 GPIO and Debouncing ☐ ☐ ☐ 15 Interrupts and ISR ☐ ☐ ☐ 16 Timers, PWM and ADC ☐ ☐ ☐ 17 Communication Protocols: UART, I2C and SPI ☐ ☐ ☐ 18 Embedded Design Patterns ☐ ☐ ☐ No. Topic I can explain it I wrote the code I can answer Qs 1 Basics and Data Types ☐ ☐ ☐ 2 Operators and Control Flow ☐ ☐ ☐ 3 Functions and Recursion ☐ ☐ ☐ 4 Arrays and Strings ☐ ☐ ☐ 5 Pointers ☐ ☐ ☐ 6 Structures, Unions, Enums and typedef ☐ ☐ ☐ 7 Dynamic Memory Allocation ☐ ☐ ☐ 8 Storage Classes and the Preprocessor ☐ ☐ ☐ 9 File Handling ☐ ☐ ☐ 10 Bitwise Operators and Bit Manipulation ☐ ☐ ☐ 11 Type Qualifiers and Fixed-Width Types ☐ ☐ ☐ 12 Memory Layout, Startup Code, Padding and Endianness ☐ ☐ ☐ 13 Registers, Memory-Mapped I/O and Function Pointers ☐ ☐ ☐ 14 GPIO and Debouncing ☐ ☐ ☐ 15 Interrupts and ISR ☐ ☐ ☐ 16 Timers, PWM and ADC ☐ ☐ ☐ 17 Communication Protocols: UART, I2C and SPI ☐ ☐ ☐ 18 Embedded Design Patterns ☐ ☐ ☐"}, {"doc": "guide", "id": "the-10-most-asked-topics-interview-priority", "title": "The 10 most asked topics (interview priority)", "text": "Final advice Do not try to memorise this guide. Understand each idea, then prove it by writing a short program. A candidate who can write a clean bit-manipulation function and explain volatile in plain words beats one who recites definitions. Link every topic to something you built: debouncing in your door lock, ADC in your soil sensor, I2C in your OLED projects, interrupts in your traffic signal. Interviewers love real examples. Final advice Do not try to memorise this guide. Understand each idea, then prove it by writing a short program. A candidate who can write a clean bit-manipulation function and explain volatile in plain words beats one who recites definitions. Link every topic to something you built: debouncing in your door lock, ADC in your soil sensor, I2C in your OLED projects, interrupts in your traffic signal. Interviewers love real examples."}, {"doc": "cheatsheet", "id": "1-top-30-one-line-answers", "title": "1. Top 30 one-line answers", "text": "Question Answer Pointer? A variable that stores a memory address. & = address of, * = value at. Dangling pointer? Points to memory that was freed or went out of scope. Wild pointer? Pointer never initialised, holds a random address. NULL pointer? Pointer holding 0, points to nothing. Always check before use. void pointer? Generic pointer without a type. Cast before dereferencing. const vs #define? const is a typed variable the compiler protects; #define is typeless text replacement. static (local)? Keeps its value between calls; lives for the whole program. static (global/function)? Visible only inside that .c file. extern? Declares a variable defined in another file. volatile? Value can change outside the code (hardware, ISR). Compiler must not cache it. const volatile? Read-only for code, changed by hardware (status register). struct vs union? Struct: members have separate memory. Union: all members share one memory. Struct padding? Gaps added for alignment. Order members large to small to save RAM. Stack vs heap? Stack: automatic, fast, for locals. Heap: manual (malloc/free), flexible, can leak. Memory leak? malloc without free; memory becomes unreachable. malloc vs calloc? calloc zeroes memory and takes (count, size). .text .data .bss? Code | initialised globals | zero-initialised globals. Why globals are 0, locals garbage? Startup code clears .bss; locals sit on the stack with old data. What runs before main()? Reset handler: set stack, copy .data to RAM, zero .bss, init clock, call main. Endianness? Byte order of multi-byte values. ESP32, AVR, most ARM are little-endian. ISR rules? Short, no delay, no printf, no malloc, shared data volatile, set flag and return. Interrupt latency? Time from the event to the first instruction of the ISR. Polling vs interrupt? Polling checks repeatedly (simple, wastes CPU); interrupt reacts to events (fast, saves CPU). Race condition? Result depends on timing between ISR and main using the same data. Switch bounce? Contacts make/break for 1 to 20 ms. Fix: RC filter or software timing. Pull-up resistor? Holds an input HIGH when nothing drives it; avoids a floating pin. PWM? Fast on/off switching; duty cycle sets average power. Watchdog? Hardware timer that resets the chip if software does not kick it in time. UART / I2C / SPI? UART: 2 devices, async. I2C: 2 shared wires, addresses. SPI: 4 wires, fast, chip select. Why avoid malloc in firmware? Fragmentation, unpredictable timing, tiny RAM. Question Answer Pointer? A variable that stores a memory address. &amp; = address of, * = value at. Dangling pointer? Points to memory that was freed or went out of scope. Wild pointer? Pointer never initialised, holds a random address. NULL pointer? Pointer holding 0, points to nothing. Always check before use. void pointer? Generic pointer without a type. Cast before dereferencing. const vs #define? const is a typed variable the compiler protects; #define is typeless text replacement. static (local)? Keeps its value between calls; lives for the whole program. static (global/function)? Visible only inside that .c file. extern? Declares a variable defined in another file. volatile? Value can change outside the code (hardware, ISR). Compiler must not cache it. const volatile? Read-only for code, changed by hardware (status register). struct vs union? Struct: members have separate memory. Union: all members share one memory. Struct padding? Gaps added for alignment. Order members large to small to save RAM. Stack vs heap? Stack: automatic, fast, for locals. Heap: manual (malloc/free), flexible, can leak. Memory leak? malloc without free; memory becomes unreachable. malloc vs calloc? calloc zeroes memory and takes (count, size). .text .data .bss? Code | initialised globals | zero-initialised globals. Why globals are 0, locals garbage? Startup code clears .bss; locals sit on the stack with old data. What runs before main()? Reset handler: set stack, copy .data to RAM, zero .bss, init clock, call main. Endiann"}, {"doc": "cheatsheet", "id": "2-c-language-quick-reference", "title": "2. C language quick reference", "text": ""}, {"doc": "cheatsheet", "id": "type-sizes-bytes-and-ranges", "title": "Type sizes (bytes) and ranges", "text": "Type PC Uno (AVR) ESP32 / STM32 Range (signed) char 1 1 1 -128 to 127 short 2 2 2 -32,768 to 32,767 int 4 2 4 16-bit: +/-32K 32-bit: +/-2.1 billion long 4 or 8 4 4 +/-2.1 billion (4 bytes) long long 8 8 8 +/-9.2 x 10^18 float / double 4 / 8 4 / 4 4 / 8 6-7 / 15-16 digits pointer 8 2 4 holds an address Type PC Uno (AVR) ESP32 / STM32 Range (signed) char 1 1 1 -128 to 127 short 2 2 2 -32,768 to 32,767 int 4 2 4 16-bit: +/-32K 32-bit: +/-2.1 billion long 4 or 8 4 4 +/-2.1 billion (4 bytes) long long 8 8 8 +/-9.2 x 10^18 float / double 4 / 8 4 / 4 4 / 8 6-7 / 15-16 digits pointer 8 2 4 holds an address Unsigned: 8 bit 0..255, 16 bit 0..65,535, 32 bit 0..4,294,967,295. Signed n-bit: -2^(n-1) to 2^(n-1) - 1. Negative numbers: two's complement = invert bits, add 1. -5 in 8 bit = 11111011."}, {"doc": "cheatsheet", "id": "format-specifiers", "title": "Format specifiers", "text": "%d int %u unsigned %c char %ld long %lld long long %s string %f float/double %lf double (scanf) %p pointer %x hex %o octal %zu size_t %d int %u unsigned %c char %ld long %lld long long %s string %f float/double %lf double (scanf) %p pointer %x hex %o octal %zu size_t"}, {"doc": "cheatsheet", "id": "operator-precedence-high-to-low", "title": "Operator precedence (high to low)", "text": "Brackets > Unary (! ~ ++ -- * & sizeof) > * / % > + - > << >> > < <= > >= > == != > & > ^ > | > && > || > ?: > = Remember: & is LOWER than ==, so write (x & 4) == 4. When in doubt, use brackets."}, {"doc": "cheatsheet", "id": "pointers", "title": "Pointers", "text": "Declaration Meaning (read right to left) int *p p is a pointer to int const int *p pointer to constant int: cannot change *p int *const p constant pointer: cannot move p int **pp pointer to pointer to int int *arr[5] array of 5 pointers to int int (*fp)(int, int) pointer to function(int, int) returning int arr[i] == *(arr + i) indexing is pointer arithmetic; p + 1 moves by sizeof(type) Declaration Meaning (read right to left) int *p p is a pointer to int const int *p pointer to constant int: cannot change *p int *const p constant pointer: cannot move p int **pp pointer to pointer to int int *arr[5] array of 5 pointers to int int (*fp)(int, int) pointer to function(int, int) returning int arr[i] == *(arr + i) indexing is pointer arithmetic; p + 1 moves by sizeof(type) Mnemonic: & = WHERE (address), * = WHAT (value there)."}, {"doc": "cheatsheet", "id": "storage-classes", "title": "Storage classes", "text": "Class Scope Lifetime Note auto block block default for locals, garbage value register block block hint only, modern compilers ignore static block or file whole program default value 0, private to file extern whole program whole program declared here, defined elsewhere Class Scope Lifetime Note auto block block default for locals, garbage value register block block hint only, modern compilers ignore static block or file whole program default value 0, private to file extern whole program whole program declared here, defined elsewhere"}, {"doc": "cheatsheet", "id": "strings-and-memory-rules", "title": "Strings and memory rules", "text": ""}, {"doc": "cheatsheet", "id": "3-embedded-c-quick-reference", "title": "3. Embedded C quick reference", "text": ""}, {"doc": "cheatsheet", "id": "bit-manipulation", "title": "Bit manipulation", "text": "Goal Code Mnemonic Set bit n x |= (1U << n); SET = OR Clear bit n x &= ~(1U << n); CLEAR = AND-NOT Toggle bit n x ^= (1U << n); TOGGLE = XOR Check bit n (x >> n) & 1 CHECK = AND Read field (x >> pos) & mask shift down, then mask Write field x = (x & ~(mask << pos)) | (val << pos); clear it, then set it Goal Code Mnemonic Set bit n x |= (1U &lt;&lt; n); SET = OR Clear bit n x &amp;= ~(1U &lt;&lt; n); CLEAR = AND-NOT Toggle bit n x ^= (1U &lt;&lt; n); TOGGLE = XOR Check bit n (x &gt;&gt; n) &amp; 1 CHECK = AND Read field (x &gt;&gt; pos) &amp; mask shift down, then mask Write field x = (x &amp; ~(mask &lt;&lt; pos)) | (val &lt;&lt; pos); clear it, then set it Shift left n = multiply by 2^n. Shift right n = divide by 2^n (unsigned). Use unsigned types and 1U for bit work. 12 & 10 = 8 12 | 10 = 14 12 ^ 10 = 6 ~12 = -13 12 << 1 = 24 12 >> 1 = 6"}, {"doc": "cheatsheet", "id": "volatile-needed-for-3-things", "title": "volatile: needed for 3 things", "text": "Mnemonic RIS: Register (hardware) | Interrupt-changed flag | Shared between tasks. volatile does NOT make access atomic. For multi-byte shared data, disable interrupts briefly."}, {"doc": "cheatsheet", "id": "memory", "title": "Memory", "text": "Section Holds Where .text code Flash .rodata const data, string literals Flash .data initialised globals / statics Flash copy -> RAM .bss zero or uninitialised globals RAM (zeroed at startup) Heap malloc, grows up RAM Stack locals, return addresses, grows down RAM Section Holds Where .text code Flash .rodata const data, string literals Flash .data initialised globals / statics Flash copy -&gt; RAM .bss zero or uninitialised globals RAM (zeroed at startup) Heap malloc, grows up RAM Stack locals, return addresses, grows down RAM Startup order: vector table -> Reset handler -> set stack -> copy .data -> zero .bss -> init clocks -> main(). Little-endian: 0x12345678 stored as 78 56 34 12. Padding: struct {char; int; char} = 12 bytes, reorder to {int; char; char} = 8 bytes. Stack overflow causes: deep recursion, large local arrays. Fix: make buffers static, avoid recursion, increase task stack."}, {"doc": "cheatsheet", "id": "interrupts", "title": "Interrupts", "text": "Flow: event -> flag -> finish instruction -> save context -> vector table -> ISR -> restore -> resume. ISR mnemonic: SHORT, SIMPLE, no SLEEP (delay), no PRINT. Set a flag, return, do the work in main."}, {"doc": "cheatsheet", "id": "gpio", "title": "GPIO", "text": ""}, {"doc": "cheatsheet", "id": "4-formulas-to-remember", "title": "4. Formulas to remember", "text": "What Formula and example Timer tick rate f_timer = f_clock / prescaler. 16 MHz / 64 = 250 kHz (4 us per tick) Timer compare value ticks = time x f_timer, compare = ticks - 1. 1 ms: 250 ticks, OCR = 249 PWM duty cycle duty % = ON time / period x 100. Average voltage = duty x Vcc PWM 8-bit 256 levels (0 to 255). value 128 is about 50% ADC voltage V = count x Vref / maxCount. Uno: 512 x 5 / 1023 = 2.5 V ADC step size Vref / 2^N. 10-bit at 5 V = 4.9 mV; 12-bit at 3.3 V = 0.8 mV ADC counts Uno / ESP8266: 0 to 1023 (10-bit). ESP32: 0 to 4095 (12-bit) UART byte time 10 bits / baud. 9600 baud = 1.04 ms per byte; 115200 = 87 us LM35 temperature degC = volts x 100 (10 mV per degree) Average current (I_active x t_active + I_sleep x t_sleep) / t_total millis() safe compare now - previous >= interval (works across wrap-around, unsigned) Servo 50 Hz (20 ms), pulse 1 ms to 2 ms for full travel What Formula and example Timer tick rate f_timer = f_clock / prescaler. 16 MHz / 64 = 250 kHz (4 us per tick) Timer compare value ticks = time x f_timer, compare = ticks - 1. 1 ms: 250 ticks, OCR = 249 PWM duty cycle duty % = ON time / period x 100. Average voltage = duty x Vcc PWM 8-bit 256 levels (0 to 255). value 128 is about 50% ADC voltage V = count x Vref / maxCount. Uno: 512 x 5 / 1023 = 2.5 V ADC step size Vref / 2^N. 10-bit at 5 V = 4.9 mV; 12-bit at 3.3 V = 0.8 mV ADC counts Uno / ESP8266: 0 to 1023 (10-bit). ESP32: 0 to 4095 (12-bit) UART byte time 10 bits / baud. 9600 baud = 1.04 ms per byte; 115200 = 87 us LM35 temperature degC = volts x 100 (10 mV per degree) Average current (I_active x t_active + I_sleep x t_sleep) / t_total millis() safe compare now - previous &gt;= interval (works across wrap-around, unsigned) Servo 50 Hz (20 ms), pulse 1 ms to 2 ms for full travel"}, {"doc": "cheatsheet", "id": "5-protocols-side-by-side", "title": "5. Protocols side by side", "text": "UART I2C SPI Wires TX, RX, GND SDA, SCL (+ pull-ups) SCK, MOSI, MISO, CS Devices 2 only many (7-bit address) many (one CS each) Clock none, same baud master clock master clock Speed 9600 to 1M+ baud 100 / 400 kHz 1 to 50+ MHz Duplex full half full Used for PC, GPS, Bluetooth sensors, OLED, RTC SD card, TFT, flash Gotcha cross TX/RX, same baud, common GND pull-ups 4.7k, address clash mode (CPOL/CPHA), CS UART I2C SPI Wires TX, RX, GND SDA, SCL (+ pull-ups) SCK, MOSI, MISO, CS Devices 2 only many (7-bit address) many (one CS each) Clock none, same baud master clock master clock Speed 9600 to 1M+ baud 100 / 400 kHz 1 to 50+ MHz Duplex full half full Used for PC, GPS, Bluetooth sensors, OLED, RTC SD card, TFT, flash Gotcha cross TX/RX, same baud, common GND pull-ups 4.7k, address clash mode (CPOL/CPHA), CS UART frame 8N1: start bit, 8 data bits (LSB first), no parity, stop bit. I2C default pins: Uno A4/A5, ESP32 GPIO 21/22. Choose: PC or module with serial port = UART. Many slow sensors on few pins = I2C. High speed data = SPI."}, {"doc": "cheatsheet", "id": "6-design-patterns-in-one-line-each", "title": "6. Design patterns in one line each", "text": "Pattern Remember Ring buffer head writes, tail reads, wrap with (i + 1) & (SIZE - 1); empty: head == tail; full: next(head) == tail. State machine enum for states + switch; each state checks an event or millis() and sets the next state; never blocks. Watchdog Kick from main loop only when healthy, never from an ISR. Deep sleep Wake, measure, send, sleep. Restarts at setup(); keep data in RTC memory. RTOS task Own stack and priority; use vTaskDelay, queues and mutexes for sharing. Callback Driver stores a function pointer; calls it when the event happens. Super-loop while(1) { read inputs; update state; write outputs; } Pattern Remember Ring buffer head writes, tail reads, wrap with (i + 1) &amp; (SIZE - 1); empty: head == tail; full: next(head) == tail. State machine enum for states + switch; each state checks an event or millis() and sets the next state; never blocks. Watchdog Kick from main loop only when healthy, never from an ISR. Deep sleep Wake, measure, send, sleep. Restarts at setup(); keep data in RTC memory. RTOS task Own stack and priority; use vTaskDelay, queues and mutexes for sharing. Callback Driver stores a function pointer; calls it when the event happens. Super-loop while(1) { read inputs; update state; write outputs; }"}, {"doc": "cheatsheet", "id": "7-code-you-must-be-able-to-write-from-memory", "title": "7. Code you must be able to write from memory", "text": ""}, {"doc": "cheatsheet", "id": "bit-macros", "title": "Bit macros", "text": "#define SET_BIT(x, n) ((x) |= (1U << (n))) #define CLEAR_BIT(x, n) ((x) &= ~(1U << (n))) #define TOGGLE_BIT(x, n) ((x) ^= (1U << (n))) #define CHECK_BIT(x, n) (((x) >> (n)) & 1U)"}, {"doc": "cheatsheet", "id": "power-of-2-count-set-bits-swap", "title": "Power of 2, count set bits, swap", "text": "int is_pow2(unsigned n) { return n && !(n & (n - 1)); } int count_bits(unsigned n) { int c = 0; while (n) { n &= n - 1; c++; } // removes lowest 1 each loop return c; } void swap(int *a, int *b) { int t = *a; *a = *b; *b = t; } // no temp: a ^= b; b ^= a; a ^= b;"}, {"doc": "cheatsheet", "id": "strings", "title": "Strings", "text": "int my_strlen(const char *s) { int n = 0; while (*s++) n++; return n; } void reverse(char *s) { int i = 0, j = my_strlen(s) - 1; while (i < j) { char t = s[i]; s[i++] = s[j]; s[j--] = t; } }"}, {"doc": "cheatsheet", "id": "endianness-check", "title": "Endianness check", "text": "uint32_t x = 1; if (*(uint8_t *)&x == 1) puts(\"little-endian\"); else puts(\"big-endian\");"}, {"doc": "cheatsheet", "id": "register-access-and-function-pointer", "title": "Register access and function pointer", "text": "#define REG (*(volatile uint32_t *)0x40020014) // example address REG |= (1U << 5); typedef void (*Callback)(void); Callback cb = my_handler; cb();"}, {"doc": "cheatsheet", "id": "ring-buffer-put-and-get", "title": "Ring buffer (put and get)", "text": "bool rb_put(RB *r, uint8_t b) { uint8_t next = (r->head + 1) & (SIZE - 1); if (next == r->tail) return false; // full r->buf[r->head] = b; r->head = next; return true; } bool rb_get(RB *r, uint8_t *b) { if (r->head == r->tail) return false; // empty *b = r->buf[r->tail]; r->tail = (r->tail + 1) & (SIZE - 1); return true; }"}, {"doc": "cheatsheet", "id": "non-blocking-blink-and-debounce-skeleton", "title": "Non-blocking blink and debounce skeleton", "text": "if (millis() - previous >= 1000) { previous = millis(); toggle(); } if (reading != lastReading) lastChange = millis(); lastReading = reading; if (millis() - lastChange > 30 && reading != stable) { stable = reading; // accepted: real press or release }"}, {"doc": "cheatsheet", "id": "8-tricky-output-questions", "title": "8. Tricky output questions", "text": "Code Result Why 7 / 2 3 integer division -7 / 2 and -7 % 2 -3 and -1 truncates toward zero; remainder takes the sign of the left side sizeof(\"hello\") strlen(\"hello\") 6 5 sizeof counts \\0 sizeof('a') in C 4 (int size) a character constant is an int in C unsigned char c = 255; c++; 0 unsigned wraps around if (-1 < 1u) false -1 converts to a huge unsigned value 0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3 false binary floating point is approximate int x = 012; x = 10 leading 0 means octal 5 & 3 5 | 3 5 ^ 3 ~5 1 7 6 -6 bit by bit 5 && 3 !5 !0 1 0 1 logical operators give 0 or 1 int a = 5; b = a++; c = ++a; b = 5, c = 7 post uses then adds; pre adds then uses printf(\"%d\", printf(\"hi\")); hi2 printf returns the number of characters printed sizeof(arr) inside a function with int arr[] pointer size array parameter decays to a pointer char *p = \"hi\"; p[0] = 'H'; undefined behaviour string literal is read-only int arr[5]; arr[5] = 1; undefined behaviour out of bounds, C does not check 1 << 31 on a 32-bit signed int undefined behaviour shifts into the sign bit; use 1U << 31 i = i++ + ++i; undefined behaviour modified twice without a sequence point #define SQ(x) x*x SQ(2+3) 11 2+3*2+3; brackets missing Code Result Why 7 / 2 3 integer division -7 / 2 and -7 % 2 -3 and -1 truncates toward zero; remainder takes the sign of the left side sizeof(\"hello\") strlen(\"hello\") 6 5 sizeof counts \\0 sizeof('a') in C 4 (int size) a character constant is an int in C unsigned char c = 255; c++; 0 unsigned wraps around if (-1 &lt; 1u) false -1 converts to a huge unsigned value 0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3 false binary floating point is approximate int x = 012; x = 10 leading 0 means octal 5 &amp; 3 5 | 3 5 ^ 3 ~5 1 7 6 -6 bit by bit 5 &amp;&amp; 3 !5 !0 1 0 1 logical operators give 0 or 1 int a = 5; b = a++; c = ++a; b = 5, c = 7 post uses then adds; pre adds then uses printf(\"%d\", printf(\"hi\")); hi2 printf returns the number of characters printed sizeof(arr) inside a function with int arr[] pointer size array parameter decays to a pointer char *p = \"hi\"; p[0] = 'H'; undefined behaviour string literal is read-only int arr[5]; arr[5] = 1; undefined behaviour out of bounds, C does not check 1 &lt;&lt; 31 on a 32-bit signed int undefined behaviour shifts into the sign bit; use 1U &lt;&lt; 31 i = i++ + ++i; undefined behaviour modified twice without a sequence point #define SQ(x) x*x SQ(2+3) 11 2+3*2+3; brackets missing"}, {"doc": "cheatsheet", "id": "9-mnemonics", "title": "9. Mnemonics", "text": "Topic Memory trick Bit operations Set = OR, Clear = AND NOT, Toggle = XOR, Check = AND. Pointers & = where, * = what. Read declarations right to left. volatile (RIS) Register, Interrupt flag, Shared data. ISR Short, Simple, no Sleep, no Print. Memory sections Text = code (flash), Data = given values, BSS = Zeros at start. Heap vs stack Stack = automatic hotel room; heap = rented hall you must return. SPI wires SCK clock, MOSI master out, MISO master in, CS choose. I2C Two wires, many names (addresses), pull-ups required. Debounce Wait until the signal stays steady for 20 to 50 ms. Endianness Little-endian: little end (lowest byte) first. Topic Memory trick Bit operations Set = OR, Clear = AND NOT, Toggle = XOR, Check = AND. Pointers &amp; = where, * = what. Read declarations right to left. volatile (RIS) Register, Interrupt flag, Shared data. ISR Short, Simple, no Sleep, no Print. Memory sections Text = code (flash), Data = given values, BSS = Zeros at start. Heap vs stack Stack = automatic hotel room; heap = rented hall you must return. SPI wires SCK clock, MOSI master out, MISO master in, CS choose. I2C Two wires, many names (addresses), pull-ups required. Debounce Wait until the signal stays steady for 20 to 50 ms. Endianness Little-endian: little end (lowest byte) first."}, {"doc": "cheatsheet", "id": "10-interview-day-checklist", "title": "10. Interview day checklist", "text": "Last-minute rule If you remember only 5 things: pointers, bit manipulation, volatile, memory sections and ISR rules. These come up in almost every embedded interview. Last-minute rule If you remember only 5 things: pointers, bit manipulation, volatile, memory sections and ISR rules. These come up in almost every embedded interview."}, {"doc": "datatypes", "id": "1-what-is-a-data-type", "title": "1. What is a data type?", "text": "A data type tells the computer three things about a variable: Analogy: boxes and labels Think of memory as a warehouse full of boxes. A data type is the box size plus a label on the box. int is a medium box labelled \"whole numbers only\". char is a tiny box labelled \"one character\". double is a big box labelled \"very precise decimal numbers\". Without the label, the same contents can mean different things: the bits 01000001 can be the number 65 or the letter A. The data type decides how they are read. Analogy: boxes and labels Think of memory as a warehouse full of boxes. A data type is the box size plus a label on the box. int is a medium box labelled \"whole numbers only\". char is a tiny box labelled \"one character\". double is a big box labelled \"very precise decimal numbers\". Without the label, the same contents can mean different things: the bits 01000001 can be the number 65 or the letter A. The data type decides how they are read."}, {"doc": "datatypes", "id": "why-does-c-need-data-types", "title": "Why does C need data types?", "text": ""}, {"doc": "datatypes", "id": "how-you-declare-a-variable", "title": "How you declare a variable", "text": "int age; // declaration: reserves memory, value is garbage int marks = 85; // declaration + initialization float price = 99.5f; char grade = 'A'; General form: data_type variable_name = value;"}, {"doc": "datatypes", "id": "2-families-of-data-types", "title": "2. Families of data types", "text": "Family Examples Meaning Basic (primary) char, int, float, double, void Built into the language. Everything else is built from these. Derived array, pointer, function Made from basic types (for example, an array of int). User-defined struct, union, enum, typedef You design them to group or name data. Family Examples Meaning Basic (primary) char, int, float, double, void Built into the language. Everything else is built from these. Derived array, pointer, function Made from basic types (for example, an array of int). User-defined struct, union, enum, typedef You design them to group or name data."}, {"doc": "datatypes", "id": "basic-types-at-a-glance", "title": "Basic types at a glance", "text": "Sizes below are typical for a modern PC (64-bit). The C standard only guarantees minimums, so sizes can differ on microcontrollers (see Part 4). Type Size Typical range Format Stores char 1 byte -128 to 127 (or 0 to 255) %c or %d A single character or a small number short 2 bytes -32,768 to 32,767 %hd Small whole numbers int 4 bytes -2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647 %d Whole numbers (most common) long 4 or 8 bytes depends on system %ld Bigger whole numbers long long 8 bytes about -9.2 x 10^18 to 9.2 x 10^18 %lld Very big whole numbers float 4 bytes about 6 to 7 digits of precision %f Decimal numbers double 8 bytes about 15 to 16 digits of precision %lf Precise decimal numbers void - no value - \"Nothing\" (functions that return nothing, generic pointers) Type Size Typical range Format Stores char 1 byte -128 to 127 (or 0 to 255) %c or %d A single character or a small number short 2 bytes -32,768 to 32,767 %hd Small whole numbers int 4 bytes -2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647 %d Whole numbers (most common) long 4 or 8 bytes depends on system %ld Bigger whole numbers long long 8 bytes about -9.2 x 10^18 to 9.2 x 10^18 %lld Very big whole numbers float 4 bytes about 6 to 7 digits of precision %f Decimal numbers double 8 bytes about 15 to 16 digits of precision %lf Precise decimal numbers void - no value - \"Nothing\" (functions that return nothing, generic pointers) Tip: you can check the size on your own machine with sizeof: #include <stdio.h> int main(void) { printf(\"char : %zu byte\\n\", sizeof(char)); printf(\"short : %zu bytes\\n\", sizeof(short)); printf(\"int : %zu bytes\\n\", sizeof(int)); printf(\"long : %zu bytes\\n\", sizeof(long)); printf(\"long long : %zu bytes\\n\", sizeof(long long)); printf(\"float : %zu bytes\\n\", sizeof(float)); printf(\"double : %zu bytes\\n\", sizeof(double)); return 0; } Output: char : 1 byte short : 2 bytes int : 4 bytes long : 8 bytes (4 on Windows) long long : 8 bytes float : 4 bytes double : 8 bytes"}, {"doc": "datatypes", "id": "3-the-int-data-type-in-detail", "title": "3. The int data type in detail", "text": ""}, {"doc": "datatypes", "id": "3-1-what-is-int", "title": "3.1 What is int?", "text": "int is short for integer. It stores whole numbers: positive, negative or zero, with no decimal point. Examples: 0, 25, -400, 100000. Use int for things you count: number of students, loop counters, marks, age, array index. int students = 60; int temperature = -5; int total = students * 2; printf(\"Students = %d\\n\", students); // %d prints an int scanf(\"%d\", &students); // %d reads an int (note the &) Remember %d is the format specifier for int. Using the wrong one (for example %f for an int) prints garbage. Remember %d is the format specifier for int. Using the wrong one (for example %f for an int) prints garbage."}, {"doc": "datatypes", "id": "3-2-how-much-memory-does-int-use", "title": "3.2 How much memory does int use?", "text": "The C standard says int must be at least 16 bits (2 bytes). In practice it depends on the processor: Platform int size Range PC / laptop (Windows, Linux, macOS) 4 bytes -2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647 ESP32, ESP8266, ARM Cortex-M (STM32) 4 bytes -2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647 Arduino Uno / Nano (AVR, 8-bit) 2 bytes -32,768 to 32,767 Platform int size Range PC / laptop (Windows, Linux, macOS) 4 bytes -2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647 ESP32, ESP8266, ARM Cortex-M (STM32) 4 bytes -2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647 Arduino Uno / Nano (AVR, 8-bit) 2 bytes -32,768 to 32,767 Why this matters for you The same line of code, int x = 40000;, works on your ESP32 but breaks on an Arduino Uno, because 40000 does not fit in 2 bytes. This is why embedded engineers use fixed-width types like int32_t (see Part 4). Why this matters for you The same line of code, int x = 40000;, works on your ESP32 but breaks on an Arduino Uno, because 40000 does not fit in 2 bytes. This is why embedded engineers use fixed-width types like int32_t (see Part 4)."}, {"doc": "datatypes", "id": "3-3-how-the-range-is-calculated", "title": "3.3 How the range is calculated", "text": "A byte has 8 bits and each bit is 0 or 1. With n bits you can make 2^n different patterns. Bits Signed range Unsigned range 8 -128 to 127 0 to 255 16 -32,768 to 32,767 0 to 65,535 32 -2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647 0 to 4,294,967,295 64 -9,223,372,036,854,775,808 to 9,223,372,036,854,775,807 0 to 18,446,744,073,709,551,615 Bits Signed range Unsigned range 8 -128 to 127 0 to 255 16 -32,768 to 32,767 0 to 65,535 32 -2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647 0 to 4,294,967,295 64 -9,223,372,036,854,775,808 to 9,223,372,036,854,775,807 0 to 18,446,744,073,709,551,615 Print the exact limits of your compiler with limits.h: #include <stdio.h> #include <limits.h> int main(void) { printf(\"INT_MIN = %d\\n\", INT_MIN); printf(\"INT_MAX = %d\\n\", INT_MAX); printf(\"UINT_MAX = %u\\n\", UINT_MAX); return 0; } Output: INT_MIN = -2147483648 INT_MAX = 2147483647 UINT_MAX = 4294967295"}, {"doc": "datatypes", "id": "3-4-int-modifiers-short-long-signed-unsigned", "title": "3.4 int modifiers: short, long, signed, unsigned", "text": "Modifiers change the size or the sign of int. Declaration Typical size Format Notes short int (or short) 2 bytes %hd Saves memory for small values int 4 bytes %d The default, most common long int (or long) 4 or 8 bytes %ld 4 bytes on Windows, 8 on 64-bit Linux long long int 8 bytes %lld Use for very large values unsigned int 4 bytes %u Only 0 and positive values unsigned long long 8 bytes %llu Huge positive values (for example, microsecond counters) Declaration Typical size Format Notes short int (or short) 2 bytes %hd Saves memory for small values int 4 bytes %d The default, most common long int (or long) 4 or 8 bytes %ld 4 bytes on Windows, 8 on 64-bit Linux long long int 8 bytes %lld Use for very large values unsigned int 4 bytes %u Only 0 and positive values unsigned long long 8 bytes %llu Huge positive values (for example, microsecond counters) unsigned int count = 4000000000U; // fits only in unsigned 32-bit long long big = 9000000000LL; // LL suffix = long long unsigned int u = 10; printf(\"%u\\n\", count); printf(\"%lld\\n\", big);"}, {"doc": "datatypes", "id": "3-5-how-int-is-stored-in-binary-two-s-complement", "title": "3.5 How int is stored in binary (two's complement)", "text": "Computers only store 0s and 1s. A positive int is stored as plain binary. Example with 8 bits for simplicity (a real int uses 32): Decimal Binary (8-bit) Note 5 00000101 Plain binary: 4 + 1 127 01111111 Largest signed 8-bit value -1 11111111 All bits 1 means -1 in two's complement -5 11111011 See the steps below -128 10000000 Smallest signed 8-bit value Decimal Binary (8-bit) Note 5 00000101 Plain binary: 4 + 1 127 01111111 Largest signed 8-bit value -1 11111111 All bits 1 means -1 in two's complement -5 11111011 See the steps below -128 10000000 Smallest signed 8-bit value How negative numbers are stored (two's complement). To store -5: See the bits yourself: #include <stdio.h> void print_bits(unsigned char n) { for (int i = 7; i >= 0; i--) printf(\"%d\", (n >> i) & 1); printf(\"\\n\"); } int main(void) { print_bits(5); print_bits((unsigned char)-5); return 0; } Output: 00000101 11111011"}, {"doc": "datatypes", "id": "3-6-overflow-when-the-number-does-not-fit", "title": "3.6 Overflow: when the number does not fit", "text": "Analogy: the car odometer A 3-digit odometer shows 999. Drive one more kilometre and it rolls over to 000. A fixed number of bits behaves the same way: after the maximum value it wraps around. Analogy: the car odometer A 3-digit odometer shows 999. Drive one more kilometre and it rolls over to 000. A fixed number of bits behaves the same way: after the maximum value it wraps around. Unsigned overflow is well defined in C: the value wraps around (modulo 2^n). unsigned char x = 255; x = x + 1; printf(\"%d\\n\", x); // 0 unsigned char y = 0; y = y - 1; printf(\"%d\\n\", y); // 255 Signed overflow is undefined behaviour in C. It often wraps around in practice, but the compiler is allowed to assume it never happens, so your program can behave strangely. Never rely on it. int a = INT_MAX; a = a + 1; // undefined behaviour: do not do this Real example: factorial overflows quickly with 32-bit int. Embedded example millis() on Arduino returns an unsigned 32-bit counter that wraps to 0 after about 49.7 days. Code that compares times as (now - start) >= interval keeps working across the wrap, because unsigned subtraction also wraps correctly. Embedded example millis() on Arduino returns an unsigned 32-bit counter that wraps to 0 after about 49.7 days. Code that compares times as (now - start) &gt;= interval keeps working across the wrap, because unsigned subtraction also wraps correctly."}, {"doc": "datatypes", "id": "3-7-integer-division-and-the-operator", "title": "3.7 Integer division and the % operator", "text": "When both operands are int, the result is an int. The decimal part is thrown away (truncated toward zero). printf(\"%d\\n\", 7 / 2); // 3 (not 3.5) printf(\"%d\\n\", 7 % 2); // 1 (remainder) printf(\"%d\\n\", -7 / 2); // -3 (truncates toward zero) printf(\"%d\\n\", -7 % 2); // -1 (sign follows the left number) printf(\"%f\\n\", 7 / 2); // WRONG: int passed to %f printf(\"%f\\n\", 7 / 2.0); // 3.500000 (one operand is double) printf(\"%f\\n\", (float)7 / 2); // 3.500000 (type cast)"}, {"doc": "datatypes", "id": "3-8-writing-int-values-literals-and-number-systems", "title": "3.8 Writing int values (literals and number systems)", "text": "Literal Value Meaning 10 10 Decimal 012 10 Octal (starts with 0). Easy to write by mistake! 0xA or 0x0A 10 Hexadecimal (starts with 0x), very common in embedded 0b1010 10 Binary (GCC extension, standard in C23) 100U, 100L, 100UL, 100LL 100 Suffix sets the type: unsigned, long, unsigned long, long long Literal Value Meaning 10 10 Decimal 012 10 Octal (starts with 0). Easy to write by mistake! 0xA or 0x0A 10 Hexadecimal (starts with 0x), very common in embedded 0b1010 10 Binary (GCC extension, standard in C23) 100U, 100L, 100UL, 100LL 100 Suffix sets the type: unsigned, long, unsigned long, long long Careful int x = 012; stores 10, not 12. A leading zero makes the number octal. Do not pad numbers with zeros in C source code. Careful int x = 012; stores 10, not 12. A leading zero makes the number octal. Do not pad numbers with zeros in C source code. Printing the same value in different systems: int n = 255; printf(\"%d\\n\", n); // 255 decimal printf(\"%x\\n\", n); // ff hexadecimal printf(\"%X\\n\", n); // FF printf(\"%o\\n\", n); // 377 octal"}, {"doc": "datatypes", "id": "3-9-mixing-int-with-other-types", "title": "3.9 Mixing int with other types", "text": "When you mix types in one expression, C converts them automatically (implicit conversion). Two rules cause most bugs: int a = -1; unsigned int b = 1; if (a < b) printf(\"a is smaller\\n\"); else printf(\"b is smaller\\n\"); // this prints! Why? -1 is converted to unsigned, which becomes 4,294,967,295, bigger than 1. This is one of the most common hidden bugs. Avoid comparing signed and unsigned values. Type casting changes a type on purpose: int total = 7, count = 2; float avg = (float)total / count; // 3.5, cast BEFORE dividing int x = (int)3.99; // 3, decimals are cut, not rounded"}, {"doc": "datatypes", "id": "4-int-in-embedded-systems-use-fixed-width-types", "title": "4. int in embedded systems: use fixed-width types", "text": "Because int can be 2 bytes on one chip and 4 bytes on another, embedded code uses types from stdint.h whose size never changes. Type Size Range Typical use int8_t 1 byte -128 to 127 Small signed values uint8_t 1 byte 0 to 255 Raw bytes, 8-bit registers, buffers int16_t 2 bytes -32,768 to 32,767 Sensor readings with sign uint16_t 2 bytes 0 to 65,535 ADC values, 16-bit registers int32_t 4 bytes -2.1 billion to 2.1 billion General signed numbers uint32_t 4 bytes 0 to 4.29 billion 32-bit registers, timestamps int64_t / uint64_t 8 bytes very large Long counters Type Size Range Typical use int8_t 1 byte -128 to 127 Small signed values uint8_t 1 byte 0 to 255 Raw bytes, 8-bit registers, buffers int16_t 2 bytes -32,768 to 32,767 Sensor readings with sign uint16_t 2 bytes 0 to 65,535 ADC values, 16-bit registers int32_t 4 bytes -2.1 billion to 2.1 billion General signed numbers uint32_t 4 bytes 0 to 4.29 billion 32-bit registers, timestamps int64_t / uint64_t 8 bytes very large Long counters #include <stdint.h> uint8_t led_state = 0; // exactly 1 byte on every chip uint16_t adc_value = 3120; // 12-bit ADC result fits uint32_t timestamp_ms = 0; int32_t offset = -50000; Printing fixed-width types: the simplest portable way is to cast to a standard type. printf(\"%lu\\n\", (unsigned long)timestamp_ms); printf(\"%d\\n\", (int)led_state);"}, {"doc": "datatypes", "id": "which-type-should-i-choose", "title": "Which type should I choose?", "text": "Situation Use Loop counter, simple count on a PC or ESP32 int Value that is never negative (length, count, register) unsigned type (uint8_t, uint16_t, uint32_t) Hardware register or byte buffer uint8_t / uint16_t / uint32_t Value that can be negative and size matters int16_t / int32_t Number may exceed 2 billion int64_t or long long Code must run on both Arduino Uno and ESP32 Fixed-width types only Situation Use Loop counter, simple count on a PC or ESP32 int Value that is never negative (length, count, register) unsigned type (uint8_t, uint16_t, uint32_t) Hardware register or byte buffer uint8_t / uint16_t / uint32_t Value that can be negative and size matters int16_t / int32_t Number may exceed 2 billion int64_t or long long Code must run on both Arduino Uno and ESP32 Fixed-width types only"}, {"doc": "datatypes", "id": "5-common-mistakes-practice-and-interview-questions", "title": "5. Common mistakes, practice and interview questions", "text": ""}, {"doc": "datatypes", "id": "common-mistakes-with-int", "title": "Common mistakes with int", "text": "Mistake Fix Using an int before giving it a value (garbage inside) Always initialise: int x = 0; 5 / 2 expecting 2.5 Use 5 / 2.0 or cast: (float)5 / 2 Wrong format specifier (%f for int, %d for float) Match the type: %d int, %u unsigned, %ld long, %lld long long, %f float Forgetting & in scanf(\"%d\", &x) scanf needs the address of the variable Assuming int is always 4 bytes Check with sizeof or use int32_t Comparing signed and unsigned values Make both the same type, or cast carefully Overflow in factorial, sum or multiplication Use a bigger type and check ranges Leading zero in a number (012) Do not write leading zeros Mistake Fix Using an int before giving it a value (garbage inside) Always initialise: int x = 0; 5 / 2 expecting 2.5 Use 5 / 2.0 or cast: (float)5 / 2 Wrong format specifier (%f for int, %d for float) Match the type: %d int, %u unsigned, %ld long, %lld long long, %f float Forgetting &amp; in scanf(\"%d\", &amp;x) scanf needs the address of the variable Assuming int is always 4 bytes Check with sizeof or use int32_t Comparing signed and unsigned values Make both the same type, or cast carefully Overflow in factorial, sum or multiplication Use a bigger type and check ranges Leading zero in a number (012) Do not write leading zeros"}, {"doc": "datatypes", "id": "practice-programs-write-each-one-yourself", "title": "Practice programs (write each one yourself)", "text": ""}, {"doc": "datatypes", "id": "interview-questions-with-short-answers", "title": "Interview questions with short answers", "text": "Q1. What is the size of int in C? It depends on the compiler and the processor. At least 16 bits by the standard; 32 bits on most modern systems (PC, ESP32, ARM), 16 bits on 8-bit AVR (Arduino Uno). Q2. What is the range of a 32-bit signed int? -2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647, which is -2^31 to 2^31 - 1. Q3. How are negative numbers stored? In two's complement: invert all bits of the positive number and add 1. The left-most bit acts as the sign bit. Q4. Difference between signed and unsigned int? Signed can hold negative values; unsigned cannot but has twice the positive range. Q5. What happens on integer overflow? Unsigned wraps around (modulo 2^n). Signed overflow is undefined behaviour. Q6. What is the result of 7 / 2 in C? 3, because both operands are int and the decimal part is truncated. Q7. What is the result of -7 % 2? -1. The sign of the remainder follows the left operand (since C99). Q8. Why use uint8_t instead of unsigned char or int in embedded code? Its size is guaranteed (exactly 8 bits), so the code behaves the same on every chip, and it clearly shows the data is a raw byte. Q9. What does this print: int a = -1; unsigned int b = 1; printf(\"%d\", a < b); 0, because a is converted to unsigned (4,294,967,295) and the comparison is false. Q10. What is the difference between int x = 012 and int x = 12? 012 is octal and equals 10; 12 is decimal and equals 12."}, {"doc": "datatypes", "id": "6-quick-look-at-the-other-basic-types", "title": "6. Quick look at the other basic types", "text": "Each of these deserves its own detailed chapter. Here is the short version so the overview is complete."}, {"doc": "datatypes", "id": "char", "title": "char", "text": "char ch = 'A'; printf(\"%c %d\\n\", ch, ch); // A 65 printf(\"%c\\n\", ch + 1); // B"}, {"doc": "datatypes", "id": "float-and-double", "title": "float and double", "text": "float a = 0.1f + 0.2f; printf(\"%.10f\\n\", a); // 0.3000000119 (not exactly 0.3)"}, {"doc": "datatypes", "id": "void", "title": "void", "text": "Next step Practise all 10 programs from Part 5 before moving on. Once int feels easy, ask for the same detailed chapter on char, float/double and pointers. Next step Practise all 10 programs from Part 5 before moving on. Once int feels easy, ask for the same detailed chapter on char, float/double and pointers."}, {"doc": "plan", "id": "1-how-this-plan-works", "title": "1. How this plan works", "text": "The plan has 4 parts: the C language (2 weeks), Embedded C fundamentals (1 week), Embedded C on real hardware (1 week), and interview preparation (last days and December). Golden rule: for every 1 hour of theory, spend 2 hours writing code. You learn programming with your hands, not by watching videos."}, {"doc": "plan", "id": "daily-routine-college-days", "title": "Daily routine (college days)", "text": "Time What to do Commute (bus) Theory only: watch or read that day's topic. Note the 3 key points on your phone. 10 am to 4 pm College / course. 6:30 to 7:30 pm Reach home, freshen up, dinner, rest. 7:30 to 9:30 pm Practice: write code for that day's topic. Run it, break it, fix it. 9:30 to 9:45 pm Write 3 lines in a notebook: what I learned, what confused me, what I do tomorrow. Time What to do Commute (bus) Theory only: watch or read that day's topic. Note the 3 key points on your phone. 10 am to 4 pm College / course. 6:30 to 7:30 pm Reach home, freshen up, dinner, rest. 7:30 to 9:30 pm Practice: write code for that day's topic. Run it, break it, fix it. 9:30 to 9:45 pm Write 3 lines in a notebook: what I learned, what confused me, what I do tomorrow."}, {"doc": "plan", "id": "weekend-routine", "title": "Weekend routine", "text": "Day What to do Saturday (4 hrs) Finish the weekly mini-project. Push the code to GitHub with a README. Sunday (3 hrs) Revise the week, then solve 10 interview questions out loud. Keep the evening free to rest. Day What to do Saturday (4 hrs) Finish the weekly mini-project. Push the code to GitHub with a README. Sunday (3 hrs) Revise the week, then solve 10 interview questions out loud. Keep the evening free to rest."}, {"doc": "plan", "id": "2-weekly-overview", "title": "2. Weekly overview", "text": "Week Dates Focus and weekend project Start 30 Oct to 1 Nov C basics: data types, operators, control flow, loops Week 1 2 to 8 Nov Functions, recursion, arrays, strings. Project 1: Contact book Week 2 9 to 15 Nov Pointers, structs, dynamic memory, preprocessor, files. Project 2: Student record manager Week 3 16 to 22 Nov Embedded C fundamentals: bits, volatile, memory, registers. Project 3: Bit manipulation library Week 4 23 to 29 Nov GPIO, interrupts, timers, ADC, UART, I2C, SPI. Project 4: Ring buffer and state machine Final 30 Nov Mock interview, GitHub cleanup, resume update Week Dates Focus and weekend project Start 30 Oct to 1 Nov C basics: data types, operators, control flow, loops Week 1 2 to 8 Nov Functions, recursion, arrays, strings. Project 1: Contact book Week 2 9 to 15 Nov Pointers, structs, dynamic memory, preprocessor, files. Project 2: Student record manager Week 3 16 to 22 Nov Embedded C fundamentals: bits, volatile, memory, registers. Project 3: Bit manipulation library Week 4 23 to 29 Nov GPIO, interrupts, timers, ADC, UART, I2C, SPI. Project 4: Ring buffer and state machine Final 30 Nov Mock interview, GitHub cleanup, resume update"}, {"doc": "plan", "id": "3-part-a-c-language-topics-in-detail", "title": "3. Part A: C language topics in detail", "text": ""}, {"doc": "plan", "id": "1-basics-and-data-types", "title": "1. Basics and data types", "text": "Practice: Print the size of every data type and add two numbers typed by the user."}, {"doc": "plan", "id": "2-operators-and-control-flow", "title": "2. Operators and control flow", "text": "Practice: Calculator using switch; leap year; prime check; pyramid patterns."}, {"doc": "plan", "id": "3-functions-and-recursion", "title": "3. Functions and recursion", "text": "Practice: Factorial, Fibonacci, GCD, power, sum of digits using recursion."}, {"doc": "plan", "id": "4-arrays-and-strings", "title": "4. Arrays and strings", "text": "Practice: Write your own strlen, strcpy, strcmp; reverse a string; palindrome; matrix addition."}, {"doc": "plan", "id": "5-pointers-most-important", "title": "5. Pointers (most important)", "text": "Practice: Swap two numbers using pointers; traverse an array using only pointers; call a function through a function pointer."}, {"doc": "plan", "id": "6-structures-unions-enums-typedef", "title": "6. Structures, unions, enums, typedef", "text": "Practice: Student record array; print sizeof a struct and a union and explain the difference."}, {"doc": "plan", "id": "7-dynamic-memory", "title": "7. Dynamic memory", "text": "Practice: Create an array with malloc, grow it with realloc, free it; build a singly linked list (insert, delete, reverse)."}, {"doc": "plan", "id": "8-storage-classes-and-preprocessor", "title": "8. Storage classes and preprocessor", "text": "Practice: Static counter function; MAX/MIN macros; a project split into 3 files with a header and include guard."}, {"doc": "plan", "id": "9-file-handling", "title": "9. File handling", "text": "Practice: Save and load student records to a file."}, {"doc": "plan", "id": "4-part-b-embedded-c-topics-in-detail", "title": "4. Part B: Embedded C topics in detail", "text": ""}, {"doc": "plan", "id": "10-bitwise-operators-and-bit-manipulation", "title": "10. Bitwise operators and bit manipulation", "text": "Practice: Write macros SET_BIT, CLEAR_BIT, TOGGLE_BIT, CHECK_BIT and test them."}, {"doc": "plan", "id": "11-qualifiers-and-fixed-width-types", "title": "11. Qualifiers and fixed-width types", "text": "Practice: Explain in your own words why a flag changed in an ISR must be volatile."}, {"doc": "plan", "id": "12-memory-layout-startup-and-padding", "title": "12. Memory layout, startup and padding", "text": "Practice: Write a program that detects endianness; print struct sizes with and without padding."}, {"doc": "plan", "id": "13-registers-and-hardware-access", "title": "13. Registers and hardware access", "text": "Practice: Simulate a GPIO register in a plain C program and control its bits."}, {"doc": "plan", "id": "14-gpio-and-debouncing", "title": "14. GPIO and debouncing", "text": "Practice: Button toggles an LED with proper debounce (ESP32 or Arduino)."}, {"doc": "plan", "id": "15-interrupts-and-isr", "title": "15. Interrupts and ISR", "text": "Practice: Button interrupt that sets a flag; main loop handles the work."}, {"doc": "plan", "id": "16-timers-pwm-and-adc", "title": "16. Timers, PWM and ADC", "text": "Practice: Blink an LED with a timer (no delay); read a sensor with ADC and dim an LED with PWM."}, {"doc": "plan", "id": "17-communication-protocols", "title": "17. Communication protocols", "text": "Practice: Print sensor data over UART; scan I2C addresses; read one SPI device."}, {"doc": "plan", "id": "18-embedded-design-patterns", "title": "18. Embedded design patterns", "text": "Practice: Ring buffer for UART receive; traffic light as a state machine."}, {"doc": "plan", "id": "5-daily-calendar-30-oct-to-30-nov", "title": "5. Daily calendar (30 Oct to 30 Nov)", "text": "Tick the box when the day is done. Weekends are shaded. If you miss a day, do not panic: move the topic to the next weekend slot instead of skipping it. Date Day Topic Practice (evening) Done 30 Oct Fri Setup + data types, variables, I/O Install GCC or use OnlineGDB. Print sizeof of every type. Add two typed numbers. ☐ 31 Oct Sat Operators, if/else, switch Calculator with switch, even/odd, leap year. Solve 10 small programs. ☐ 1 Nov Sun Loops + revision Patterns, prime numbers, reverse a number, sum of digits. 10 questions. ☐ 2 Nov Mon Functions Write max, power, GCD, isPrime as functions. Split into .c and .h. ☐ 3 Nov Tue Recursion Factorial, Fibonacci, sum of digits, decimal to binary. ☐ 4 Nov Wed Arrays Min/max, reverse, sort, search, 2D matrix addition. ☐ 5 Nov Thu Strings Write your own strlen, strcpy, strcmp. Reverse, palindrome. ☐ 6 Nov Fri Matrix + string problems Transpose, multiply, vowel count, anagram check. ☐ 7 Nov Sat PROJECT 1: Contact book Arrays + strings + functions. Push to GitHub. ☐ 8 Nov Sun Revise week 1 + interview Qs Solve 10 interview questions on arrays, strings, loops. ☐ 9 Nov Mon Pointers: basics &, *, NULL, pointer to pointer. Draw memory diagrams on paper. ☐ 10 Nov Tue Pointer arithmetic + arrays Swap with pointers; traverse arrays and strings with pointers. ☐ 11 Nov Wed Structs, unions, enums, typedef Student struct array; compare sizeof struct vs union. ☐ 12 Nov Thu Dynamic memory malloc, realloc, free. Deliberately create a leak and fix it. ☐ 13 Nov Fri Storage classes + preprocessor Static counter, macros, include guards, 3-file project. ☐ 14 Nov Sat File handling + PROJECT 2 Student record manager that saves and loads from a file. ☐ 15 Nov Sun Revise + linked list Singly linked list: insert, delete, reverse. ☐ 16 Nov Mon Embedded: bitwise operators &, |, ^, ~, <<, >> with small examples. Binary and hex practice. ☐ 17 Nov Tue Bit manipulation SET_BIT, CLEAR_BIT, TOGGLE_BIT, CHECK_BIT macros. Count set bits. ☐ 18 Nov Wed volatile, const, static, fixed-width types Rewrite earlier code with uint8_t/uint32_t. Explain volatile aloud. ☐ 19 Nov Thu Memory layout, padding, endianness Endianness check program; struct padding experiments. ☐ 20 Nov Fri Registers + function pointers Simulate a GPIO register in C; driver using function pointers. ☐ 21 Nov Sat PROJECT 3: Bit manipulation library Header + source + test file. Push to GitHub. ☐ 22 Nov Sun Revise + 15 Embedded C questions Answer each question out loud in 1 minute. ☐ 23 Nov Mon GPIO + debouncing Button toggles LED with software debounce on your board. ☐ 24 Nov Tue Interrupts + ISR Button interrupt sets a volatile flag; main loop handles it. ☐ 25 Nov Wed Timers + PWM Blink with a timer (no delay). LED dimming with PWM. ☐ 26 Nov Thu ADC + UART Read a sensor, print values over serial. ☐ 27 Nov Fri I2C + SPI I2C scanner; read one I2C or SPI sensor. ☐ 28 Nov Sat PROJECT 4: Ring buffer + state machine UART ring buffer and traffic light state machine. Push to GitHub. ☐ 29 Nov Sun Watchdog, sleep modes, revision Read about watchdog and low power. Revise weak topics. ☐ 30 Nov Mon FINAL: mock interview + GitHub cleanup Explain each project in 2 minutes. Update README files and resume. ☐ Date Day Topic Practice (evening) Done 30 Oct Fri Setup + data types, variables, I/O Install GCC or use OnlineGDB. Print sizeof of every type. Add two typed numbers. ☐ 31 Oct Sat Operators, if/else, switch Calculator with switch, even/odd, leap year. Solve 10 small programs. ☐ 1 Nov Sun Loops + revision Patterns, prime numbers, reverse a number, sum of digits. 10 questions. ☐ 2 Nov Mon Functions Write max, power, GCD, isPrime as functions. Split into .c and .h. ☐ 3 Nov Tue Recursion Factorial, Fibonacci, sum of digits, decimal to binary. ☐ 4 Nov Wed Arrays Min/max, reverse, sort, search, 2D matrix addition. ☐ 5 Nov Thu Strings Write your own strlen, strcpy, strcmp. Reverse, palindrome. ☐ 6 Nov Fri Matrix + string problems Transpose, multiply, vowel count, anagram check. ☐ 7"}, {"doc": "plan", "id": "6-interview-questions-to-master", "title": "6. Interview questions to master", "text": "Answer each one out loud in under 1 minute. If you cannot explain it simply, you do not know it yet."}, {"doc": "plan", "id": "7-tools-and-resources", "title": "7. Tools and resources", "text": ""}, {"doc": "plan", "id": "8-after-30-november-december-plan", "title": "8. After 30 November (December plan)", "text": ""}, {"doc": "plan", "id": "9-tips-to-stay-on-track", "title": "9. Tips to stay on track", "text": ""}], "pages": {"guide": "<header class=\"doc-head\"><p class=\"title\">C and Embedded C<\/p><p class=\"subtitle\">The Complete Topic-by-Topic Study Guide<\/p><p class=\"meta\">18 topics with explanations, analogies, code with output, common mistakes, practice programs and interview questions<\/p><\/header><h2 id=\"how-to-use-this-guide\">How to use this guide<\/h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Read the \"In one line\" box first. If you can already say it in your own words, skim; otherwise read slowly.<\/li>\n<li>Type every code example yourself (do not copy and paste), run it and change something to see what breaks.<\/li>\n<li>Read \"Common mistakes\" before you write your own programs. These are the bugs you will otherwise meet in real life.<\/li>\n<li>Do the practice programs. Reading is 20% of learning; writing code is 80%.<\/li>\n<li>Answer the interview questions out loud in your own words, then compare with the short answers.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>Reading the code examples<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Part A examples run on any PC with GCC or on OnlineGDB.<\/p>\n<p>Part B examples marked with setup() and loop() are Arduino-style sketches for your ESP32, ESP8266 or Uno. Register-level examples (DDRB, PORTB, TCCR2A) are for the Arduino Uno (AVR) and show the idea; addresses in other examples are illustrations, so always check the datasheet of your chip.<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"contents-and-study-order\">Contents and study order<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>No.<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Topic<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Part<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Suggested dates (2026)<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>1<\/td><td>Basics and Data Types<\/td><td>A. C language<\/td><td>Oct 30<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>2<\/td><td>Operators and Control Flow<\/td><td>A. C language<\/td><td>Oct 31 - Nov 1<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>3<\/td><td>Functions and Recursion<\/td><td>A. C language<\/td><td>Nov 2 - 3<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>4<\/td><td>Arrays and Strings<\/td><td>A. C language<\/td><td>Nov 4 - 6<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>5<\/td><td>Pointers<\/td><td>A. C language<\/td><td>Nov 9 - 10<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>6<\/td><td>Structures, Unions, Enums and typedef<\/td><td>A. C language<\/td><td>Nov 11<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>7<\/td><td>Dynamic Memory Allocation<\/td><td>A. C language<\/td><td>Nov 12<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>8<\/td><td>Storage Classes and the Preprocessor<\/td><td>A. C language<\/td><td>Nov 13<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>9<\/td><td>File Handling<\/td><td>A. C language<\/td><td>Nov 14<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>10<\/td><td>Bitwise Operators and Bit Manipulation<\/td><td>B. Embedded C<\/td><td>Nov 16 - 17<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>11<\/td><td>Type Qualifiers and Fixed-Width Types<\/td><td>B. Embedded C<\/td><td>Nov 18<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>12<\/td><td>Memory Layout, Startup Code, Padding and Endianness<\/td><td>B. Embedded C<\/td><td>Nov 19<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>13<\/td><td>Registers, Memory-Mapped I/O and Function Pointers<\/td><td>B. Embedded C<\/td><td>Nov 20<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>14<\/td><td>GPIO and Debouncing<\/td><td>B. Embedded C<\/td><td>Nov 23<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>15<\/td><td>Interrupts and ISR<\/td><td>B. Embedded C<\/td><td>Nov 24<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>16<\/td><td>Timers, PWM and ADC<\/td><td>B. Embedded C<\/td><td>Nov 25 - 26<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>17<\/td><td>Communication Protocols: UART, I2C and SPI<\/td><td>B. Embedded C<\/td><td>Nov 26 - 27<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>18<\/td><td>Embedded Design Patterns<\/td><td>B. Embedded C<\/td><td>Nov 28 - 29<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><em>The dates match the daily calendar from your study plan. If you fall behind, move the topic to the next weekend; do not skip it.<\/em><\/p>\n<p><strong>TOPIC 1<\/strong><\/p>\n<h1 id=\"basics-and-data-types\">Basics and Data Types<\/h1>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>In one line<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>A C program is translated by the compiler into machine code; every variable has a data type that decides its size, how its bits are read and what you can do with it.<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"1-1-how-a-c-program-becomes-a-running-program\">1.1 How a C program becomes a running program<\/h2>\n<p>You write text (source code). The computer only understands machine code. Four tools turn one into the other, like a factory line.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Stage<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Tool<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>What it does<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>1. Preprocess<\/td><td>Preprocessor<\/td><td>Handles lines starting with # (include files, replace macros)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>2. Compile<\/td><td>Compiler<\/td><td>Turns C into assembly and checks syntax<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>3. Assemble<\/td><td>Assembler<\/td><td>Turns assembly into an object file (machine code pieces)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>4. Link<\/td><td>Linker<\/td><td>Joins object files and libraries into one executable<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>// hello.c\n#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n \nint main(void) {\n    printf(\"Hello, embedded world!\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong><\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Hello, embedded world!<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Compile and run on a PC: gcc hello.c -o hello   then   ./hello<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt; pastes the declarations of printf and scanf into your file.<\/li>\n<li>main is where every C program starts. return 0 tells the operating system \"success\".<\/li>\n<li>Every statement ends with a semicolon. Blocks use curly braces { }.<\/li>\n<li>On a microcontroller the same stages happen, but the result is flashed into the chip instead of run on the PC.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"1-2-variables-and-constants\">1.2 Variables and constants<\/h2>\n<p>A variable is a named box in memory. You choose the name, the compiler chooses the address.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Names can use letters, digits and underscore, and cannot start with a digit. count1 is fine, 1count is not.<\/li>\n<li>C is case sensitive: Total and total are different variables.<\/li>\n<li>You cannot use keywords (int, return, while...) as names.<\/li>\n<li>Use meaningful names: temperature_c is better than t.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>int age = 20;                 // variable: value can change\nconst float PI = 3.14159f;    // constant: compiler blocks changes\n#define MAX_USERS 50          // macro: text replaced before compiling\n \nage = 21;       // fine\n// PI = 3.14;   // ERROR: assignment of read-only variable<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"1-3-what-is-a-data-type\">1.3 What is a data type?<\/h2>\n<p>A data type tells the compiler (1) how much memory to reserve, (2) how to read the bits stored there, and (3) which operations are allowed.<\/p>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>Analogy: boxes with labels<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Memory is a warehouse of boxes. The type is the box size plus a label. The bits 01000001 mean the number 65 if the label says int, and the letter A if the label says char.<\/p><\/aside>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Type<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Size (PC)<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Range / precision<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Format<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Use<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>char<\/code><\/td><td>1 byte<\/td><td>-128 to 127 (or 0 to 255)<\/td><td>%c %d<\/td><td>One character, small number<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>short<\/code><\/td><td>2 bytes<\/td><td>-32,768 to 32,767<\/td><td>%hd<\/td><td>Small whole numbers<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>int<\/code><\/td><td>4 bytes<\/td><td>-2.1 billion to 2.1 billion<\/td><td>%d<\/td><td>Whole numbers<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>long long<\/code><\/td><td>8 bytes<\/td><td>about +/- 9.2 x 10^18<\/td><td>%lld<\/td><td>Very large whole numbers<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>float<\/code><\/td><td>4 bytes<\/td><td>6 to 7 digits<\/td><td>%f<\/td><td>Decimals<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>double<\/code><\/td><td>8 bytes<\/td><td>15 to 16 digits<\/td><td>%lf<\/td><td>Precise decimals<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>void<\/code><\/td><td>-<\/td><td>no value<\/td><td>-<\/td><td>No return value, generic pointer<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>Important for embedded<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The size of int is not fixed. It is 2 bytes on an Arduino Uno and 4 bytes on ESP32, ESP8266 and STM32. Embedded code uses uint8_t, uint16_t, uint32_t (see Topic 11).<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"1-4-int-in-short\">1.4 int in short<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Stores whole numbers. A signed 32-bit int covers -2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647.<\/li>\n<li>Negative numbers are stored in two's complement: invert the bits of the positive number and add 1.<\/li>\n<li>Unsigned overflow wraps around (255 + 1 becomes 0 in 8 bits). Signed overflow is undefined behaviour.<\/li>\n<li>7 / 2 is 3 (decimals are dropped) and 7 % 2 is 1 (remainder).<\/li>\n<li>The full int chapter is in the separate document \"Data Types in C with int Explained\".<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"1-5-char-float-and-double\">1.5 char, float and double<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>char stores a character as its ASCII number: 'A' = 65, 'a' = 97, '0' = 48. So 'A' + 1 is 66 which prints as B.<\/li>\n<li>float and double store numbers in binary scientific form (IEEE 754), so many decimals are approximate. 0.1 cannot be stored exactly.<\/li>\n<li>Do not compare floats with ==. Compare the difference with a small tolerance.<\/li>\n<li>Many small microcontrollers have no floating-point hardware, so float maths is slow. Use integers when you can (store 25.5 degrees as 255 tenths).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>char  ch = 'A';\nfloat f  = 0.1f + 0.2f;\n \nprintf(\"%c %d\\n\", ch, ch);      // A 65\nprintf(\"%c\\n\", ch + 1);          // B\nprintf(\"%.10f\\n\", f);            // 0.3000000119 (not exactly 0.3)\nprintf(\"%zu\\n\", sizeof(double)); // 8<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"1-6-input-and-output-printf-and-scanf\">1.6 Input and output: printf and scanf<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Specifier<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Type<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Example<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>%d<\/code><\/td><td>int<\/td><td>printf(\"%d\", 25);<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>%u<\/code><\/td><td>unsigned int<\/td><td>printf(\"%u\", 4000000000U);<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>%ld  %lld<\/code><\/td><td>long, long long<\/td><td>printf(\"%lld\", big);<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>%f  %lf<\/code><\/td><td>float, double<\/td><td>printf(\"%.2f\", 3.14159);  prints 3.14<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>%c<\/code><\/td><td>char<\/td><td>printf(\"%c\", 'A');<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>%s<\/code><\/td><td>string<\/td><td>printf(\"%s\", \"hello\");<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>%x  %X<\/code><\/td><td>hexadecimal<\/td><td>printf(\"%x\", 255);  prints ff<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>%p<\/code><\/td><td>pointer address<\/td><td>printf(\"%p\", (void *)&amp;x);<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>int a;\nfloat b;\nprintf(\"Enter an int and a float: \");\nscanf(\"%d %f\", &amp;a, &amp;b);          // note the &amp; before each variable\nprintf(\"You typed %d and %.2f\\n\", a, b);<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"1-7-type-casting\">1.7 Type casting<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>int total = 7, count = 2;\nfloat wrong = total / count;          // 3.0   (integer division first)\nfloat right = (float)total / count;   // 3.5   (cast before dividing)\nint x = (int)3.99;                    // 3     (cut, not rounded)<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Mistake<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Fix<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Using a variable before giving it a value<\/td><td>Initialise: int x = 0;<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Wrong format specifier (%d for a float)<\/td><td>Match the type; compiler warnings (-Wall) catch this<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Forgetting &amp; in scanf(\"%d\", &amp;x)<\/td><td>scanf needs the address of the variable<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>5 / 2 expecting 2.5<\/td><td>Use 5 / 2.0 or a cast<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Comparing floats with ==<\/td><td>Use fabs(a - b) &lt; 0.0001<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Missing semicolon or unmatched brace<\/td><td>Read the first compiler error; later errors are often side effects<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"practice-programs-type-each-one-yourself\">Practice programs (type each one yourself)<\/h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Print the size of every basic type on your computer.<\/li>\n<li>Read two numbers and print sum, difference, product and quotient (as decimals).<\/li>\n<li>Convert temperature from Celsius to Fahrenheit: F = C x 9/5 + 32 (watch integer division).<\/li>\n<li>Read a character and print its ASCII value, then print the next character.<\/li>\n<li>Show that 0.1 + 0.2 is not exactly 0.3 by printing 15 decimal places.<\/li>\n<li>Compile with gcc -Wall and fix every warning in your programs.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"interview-questions-with-short-answers\">Interview questions with short answers<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Q1. What is a data type? It defines the size, interpretation and allowed operations of a variable.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q2. What are the stages of compilation? Preprocessing, compiling, assembling, linking.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q3. Difference between const and #define? const creates a typed variable that the compiler protects; #define is plain text replacement before compiling and has no type.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q4. Why is sizeof(int) not always 4? The standard only sets a minimum (16 bits); the real size depends on the processor and compiler.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q5. What does void main() vs int main() mean? The standard form is int main(void), returning a status to the OS. void main is non-standard on hosted systems, though common in bare-metal code.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>TOPIC 2<\/strong><\/p>\n<h1 id=\"operators-and-control-flow\">Operators and Control Flow<\/h1>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>In one line<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Operators calculate and compare values; control flow (if, switch, loops) decides which code runs and how many times.<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"2-1-operators\">2.1 Operators<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Group<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Operators<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Notes<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Arithmetic<\/td><td><code>+  -  *  /  %<\/code><\/td><td>% works only on integers<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Relational<\/td><td><code>==  !=  &lt;  &gt;  &lt;=  &gt;=<\/code><\/td><td>Result is 1 (true) or 0 (false)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Logical<\/td><td><code>&amp;&amp;  ||  !<\/code><\/td><td>AND, OR, NOT<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Assignment<\/td><td><code>=  +=  -=  *=  /=  %=<\/code><\/td><td>x += 5 means x = x + 5<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Increment<\/td><td><code>++  --<\/code><\/td><td>Add or subtract 1<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Ternary<\/td><td><code>cond ? a : b<\/code><\/td><td>Short if-else that gives a value<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Bitwise<\/td><td><code>&amp;  |  ^  ~  &lt;&lt;  &gt;&gt;<\/code><\/td><td>Covered in Topic 10<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>sizeof<\/td><td><code>sizeof(x)<\/code><\/td><td>Size in bytes<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>In C, zero means false and any non-zero value means true.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"2-2-pre-increment-vs-post-increment\">2.2 Pre-increment vs post-increment<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>int a = 5, b;\nb = a++;   // use a first (b = 5), then add 1 (a = 6)\nprintf(\"%d %d\\n\", a, b);\n \na = 5;\nb = ++a;   // add 1 first (a = 6), then use it (b = 6)\nprintf(\"%d %d\\n\", a, b);<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong><\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>6 5\n6 6<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"2-3-short-circuit-evaluation\">2.3 Short-circuit evaluation<\/h2>\n<p>With &amp;&amp; and ||, C stops as soon as the answer is known. In (a != 0 &amp;&amp; 10 / a &gt; 2), if a is 0 the division is never evaluated, so there is no crash. Use this deliberately to guard risky checks.<\/p>\n<aside class=\"callout warn\" style=\"--c:#C0392B\"><p><strong>The classic bug: = versus ==<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>if (x = 5) assigns 5 to x and is always true. You wanted if (x == 5). A habit that helps: write if (5 == x) so a typo becomes a compile error.<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"2-4-operator-precedence-high-to-low\">2.4 Operator precedence (high to low)<\/h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Brackets ( ), array [ ], member . and -&gt;<\/li>\n<li>Unary: ! ~ ++ -- * (dereference) &amp; (address) sizeof<\/li>\n<li>* / %   then   + -<\/li>\n<li>&lt;&lt; &gt;&gt;   then   &lt; &lt;= &gt; &gt;=   then   == !=<\/li>\n<li>&amp;   then   ^   then   |   then   &amp;&amp;   then   ||<\/li>\n<li>?:   then   = and compound assignments<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>When in doubt, use brackets. They cost nothing and remove the guesswork.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"2-5-decision-making-if-else-if-else\">2.5 Decision making: if, else if, else<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>int marks = 72;\n \nif (marks &gt;= 90)       printf(\"Grade A\\n\");\nelse if (marks &gt;= 75)  printf(\"Grade B\\n\");\nelse if (marks &gt;= 60)  printf(\"Grade C\\n\");\nelse                   printf(\"Fail\\n\");\n \nint max = (a &gt; b) ? a : b;     // ternary<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong><\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Grade C<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"2-6-switch\">2.6 switch<\/h2>\n<p>Use switch when one integer or char variable is compared with many fixed values. break ends the case; without it, execution falls through into the next case.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>char op = '*';\nint a = 6, b = 3;\n \nswitch (op) {\n    case '+': printf(\"%d\\n\", a + b); break;\n    case '-': printf(\"%d\\n\", a - b); break;\n    case '*': printf(\"%d\\n\", a * b); break;\n    case '/': printf(\"%d\\n\", a / b); break;\n    default:  printf(\"Unknown operator\\n\");\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong><\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>18<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"2-7-loops\">2.7 Loops<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Loop<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Form<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Use when<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>for<\/td><td><code>for (init; condition; update)<\/code><\/td><td>You know the number of repeats<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>while<\/td><td><code>while (condition) { ... }<\/code><\/td><td>Repeat until something happens; may run 0 times<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>do-while<\/td><td><code>do { ... } while (condition);<\/code><\/td><td>Must run at least once (menus, input checks)<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 5; i++)\n    printf(\"%d \", i);                // 1 2 3 4 5\n \nint n = 1234, sum = 0;\nwhile (n &gt; 0) {\n    sum += n % 10;                   // take last digit\n    n /= 10;                         // drop last digit\n}\nprintf(\"\\nDigit sum = %d\\n\", sum);   // 10\n \nwhile (1) {                          // infinite loop: the main loop of\n    /* read sensors, update outputs */   // almost every embedded program\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<ul>\n<li>break leaves the loop immediately. continue skips the rest of this round and goes to the next.<\/li>\n<li>Embedded programs normally end with an infinite loop because there is no operating system to return to.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"2-8-nested-loops-patterns\">2.8 Nested loops: patterns<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 4; i++) {          // rows\n    for (int j = 1; j &lt;= i; j++)        // stars in this row\n        printf(\"* \");\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong><\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>* \n* * \n* * * \n* * * *<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Trace it by hand: for row 1 the inner loop runs 1 time, row 2 runs 2 times, and so on. Tracing on paper is the best way to understand loops.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes-2\">Common mistakes<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Mistake<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Fix<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Using = instead of == in a condition<\/td><td>Compare with ==; compile with -Wall<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Semicolon after if or for: if (x &gt; 3); { }<\/td><td>Remove the stray semicolon; the block then belongs to the if<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Forgetting break in a switch<\/td><td>Add break unless fall-through is intentional (and comment it)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Off-by-one: i &lt;= n when array size is n<\/td><td>Array indexes go from 0 to n-1, so use i &lt; n<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Infinite loop because the variable is never updated<\/td><td>Check that the condition can become false<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Comparing floats in loop conditions<\/td><td>Loop on integers, convert to float inside<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"practice-programs-type-each-one-yourself-2\">Practice programs (type each one yourself)<\/h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Check whether a year is a leap year (divisible by 4, but century years only if divisible by 400).<\/li>\n<li>Build a calculator with switch for + - * / and guard against division by zero.<\/li>\n<li>Print the multiplication table of a number read from the user.<\/li>\n<li>Check whether a number is prime.<\/li>\n<li>Reverse the digits of a number and check for palindrome.<\/li>\n<li>Print a right-angled triangle and a pyramid of stars.<\/li>\n<li>Print the Fibonacci series up to n terms.<\/li>\n<li>Find the largest of three numbers using the ternary operator.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"interview-questions-with-short-answers-2\">Interview questions with short answers<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Q1. Difference between ++a and a++? Pre-increment adds 1 then uses the value; post-increment uses the value then adds 1.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q2. Difference between while and do-while? do-while checks the condition after the body, so it always runs at least once.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q3. What is short-circuit evaluation? In a &amp;&amp; b, b is skipped if a is false; in a || b, b is skipped if a is true.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q4. What happens if you forget break in switch? Execution falls through into the next case.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q5. Why do embedded programs use while(1)? There is no OS to return to, so main loops forever handling tasks.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>TOPIC 3<\/strong><\/p>\n<h1 id=\"functions-and-recursion\">Functions and Recursion<\/h1>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>In one line<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>A function is a named block of code you write once and call many times; recursion is a function that calls itself to solve a smaller version of the same problem.<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"3-1-why-functions\">3.1 Why functions?<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Reuse: write once, call anywhere.<\/li>\n<li>Readability: main() reads like a story (read_sensor(), update_display()).<\/li>\n<li>Testing: test small pieces separately.<\/li>\n<li>Teamwork: each engineer writes different functions (drivers).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>Analogy: a recipe card<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The function is the recipe. The parameters are the ingredients you hand over. The return value is the dish you get back. Calling the function is cooking from that card.<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"3-2-anatomy-of-a-function\">3.2 Anatomy of a function<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n \nint add(int a, int b);          // prototype (declaration)\n \nint main(void) {\n    int result = add(3, 4);     // call\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", result);\n    return 0;\n}\n \nint add(int a, int b) {         // definition\n    return a + b;\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong><\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>7<\/code><\/pre>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Part<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Meaning<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Return type<\/td><td>Type of the value sent back (int, float, void if nothing)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Name<\/td><td>How you call it. Use verb-like names: read_adc, set_led<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Parameters<\/td><td>Inputs; types must be written for each one<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Prototype<\/td><td>Tells the compiler about the function before it is used. Goes at the top or in a header file<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>return<\/td><td>Ends the function and hands back a value<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"3-3-call-by-value\">3.3 Call by value<\/h2>\n<p>C passes copies of the arguments. Changing the parameter inside the function does not change the original variable.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>void bad_swap(int a, int b) {\n    int t = a;  a = b;  b = t;     // swaps only the copies\n}\n \nint x = 1, y = 2;\nbad_swap(x, y);\nprintf(\"%d %d\\n\", x, y);           // still 1 2<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong><\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>1 2<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>To change the original you pass its address (a pointer). That is the swap fix in Topic 5.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"3-4-scope-and-lifetime\">3.4 Scope and lifetime<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Kind<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Where declared<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Lives<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Local<\/td><td>Inside a function or block<\/td><td>Created on entry, destroyed on exit (stack)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Global<\/td><td>Outside all functions<\/td><td>Whole program run (data memory)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>static local<\/td><td>Inside a function with static<\/td><td>Whole program, visible only in that function<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>Prefer local variables. Too many globals make code hard to debug, because any function can change them. In embedded code, globals shared with an ISR must be volatile (Topic 11).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"3-5-recursion\">3.5 Recursion<\/h2>\n<p>A recursive function must have (1) a base case that stops the recursion and (2) a recursive case that moves toward the base case.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>unsigned long factorial(unsigned int n) {\n    if (n &lt;= 1)                    // base case\n        return 1;\n    return n * factorial(n - 1);   // recursive case\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Trace of factorial(4) (calls go down, answers come back up):<\/p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Call<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Waits for<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Returns<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>factorial(4)<\/code><\/td><td>4 * factorial(3)<\/td><td>24<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>factorial(3)<\/code><\/td><td>3 * factorial(2)<\/td><td>6<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>factorial(2)<\/code><\/td><td>2 * factorial(1)<\/td><td>2<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>factorial(1)<\/code><\/td><td>base case<\/td><td>1<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"3-6-what-happens-in-memory-the-stack\">3.6 What happens in memory: the stack<\/h2>\n<p>Every call pushes a stack frame (parameters, local variables, return address). When the function returns, the frame is removed. Deep recursion means many frames; run out of stack and the program crashes (stack overflow).<\/p>\n<aside class=\"callout warn\" style=\"--c:#C0392B\"><p><strong>Recursion on microcontrollers<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>An ESP8266 or Uno has very little RAM and stack. Many embedded coding standards (MISRA) forbid recursion. Prefer loops in firmware; use recursion to learn and in tree or parsing problems on PCs.<\/p><\/aside>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>int fib(int n) {                 // easy to read, but slow: it repeats work\n    if (n &lt; 2) return n;\n    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);\n}\n \nint fib_loop(int n) {            // fast and uses constant stack\n    int a = 0, b = 1;\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) { int t = a + b; a = b; b = t; }\n    return a;\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"3-7-splitting-code-into-files-header-files\">3.7 Splitting code into files (header files)<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>// mathutil.h\n#ifndef MATHUTIL_H\n#define MATHUTIL_H\nint add(int a, int b);\nint max(int a, int b);\n#endif\n \n// mathutil.c\n#include \"mathutil.h\"\nint add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }\nint max(int a, int b) { return a &gt; b ? a : b; }\n \n// main.c\n#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include \"mathutil.h\"\nint main(void) { printf(\"%d\\n\", add(2, 3)); return 0; }<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Build: gcc main.c mathutil.c -o app. The header is the public menu; the .c file is the kitchen.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes-3\">Common mistakes<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Mistake<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Fix<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Calling a function before declaring it<\/td><td>Add a prototype or move the definition above main<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Expecting a function to modify the caller's variable<\/td><td>Pass a pointer<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Missing base case in recursion<\/td><td>Write the base case first<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Returning the address of a local variable<\/td><td>Local memory disappears on return; use static or let the caller pass a buffer<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Not returning a value from a non-void function<\/td><td>Every path must return<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Function with empty () thinking it means no arguments<\/td><td>Write (void) to say no arguments<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"practice-programs-type-each-one-yourself-3\">Practice programs (type each one yourself)<\/h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write max, min, power(base, exp), gcd and is_prime as separate functions.<\/li>\n<li>Write factorial both with a loop and with recursion; compare the results for n = 1 to 12.<\/li>\n<li>Write sum_digits(n) recursively.<\/li>\n<li>Print decimal to binary using recursion.<\/li>\n<li>Write a function that returns the average of an array (pass array and size).<\/li>\n<li>Split your calculator into main.c, calc.c and calc.h.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"interview-questions-with-short-answers-3\">Interview questions with short answers<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Q1. What is call by value? The function receives copies of the arguments, so changes do not affect the originals.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q2. Difference between declaration and definition? A declaration tells the compiler the signature; the definition provides the body.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q3. What is a stack frame? The block of stack memory holding a call's parameters, locals and return address.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q4. What is the base case in recursion? The condition where the function stops calling itself.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q5. Why avoid recursion in firmware? Stack is small and usage is hard to predict; overflow causes a crash that is hard to debug.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q6. What does static do to a local variable? It keeps its value between calls and lives for the whole program.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>TOPIC 4<\/strong><\/p>\n<h1 id=\"arrays-and-strings\">Arrays and Strings<\/h1>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>In one line<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>An array is a row of same-type variables stored side by side in memory; a string in C is simply a char array that ends with the null character '\\0'.<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"4-1-one-dimensional-arrays\">4.1 One-dimensional arrays<\/h2>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>Analogy: a row of lockers<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Lockers are numbered 0, 1, 2... All lockers are the same size and sit next to each other. The array name is the building; the index picks the locker.<\/p><\/aside>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>int marks[5] = {90, 85, 70, 60, 95};     // 5 ints, 20 bytes on a 32-bit int system\nint zeros[5] = {0};                      // all zeros\nint auto_size[] = {1, 2, 3};             // compiler counts: size 3\n \nprintf(\"%d\\n\", marks[0]);                // 90  (first element)\nprintf(\"%d\\n\", marks[4]);                // 95  (last element)\n \nfor (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; i++)\n    printf(\"%d \", marks[i]);\n \nint n = sizeof(marks) / sizeof(marks[0]);   // number of elements = 5<\/code><\/pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Indexes run from 0 to size-1. An array of 5 has marks[0] to marks[4].<\/li>\n<li>Elements sit in consecutive memory: address of marks[1] = address of marks[0] + sizeof(int).<\/li>\n<li>C does not check bounds. marks[10] compiles and corrupts other memory or crashes: undefined behaviour.<\/li>\n<li>Without an initialiser, a local array holds garbage values.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"4-2-two-dimensional-arrays\">4.2 Two-dimensional arrays<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>int m[2][3] = { {1, 2, 3},\n                {4, 5, 6} };            // 2 rows, 3 columns\n \nfor (int r = 0; r &lt; 2; r++) {\n    for (int c = 0; c &lt; 3; c++)\n        printf(\"%d \", m[r][c]);\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong><\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>1 2 3 \n4 5 6<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Memory is one long line; C stores rows one after another (row-major). So m[1][0] sits right after m[0][2].<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"4-3-passing-arrays-to-functions\">4.3 Passing arrays to functions<\/h2>\n<p>An array passed to a function becomes a pointer to its first element, so the function can change the original. The size is lost, so pass it separately.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>void print_array(const int arr[], int n) {      // const: we only read it\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++)\n        printf(\"%d \", arr[i]);\n}\n \nint find_max(const int arr[], int n) {\n    int max = arr[0];\n    for (int i = 1; i &lt; n; i++)\n        if (arr[i] &gt; max) max = arr[i];\n    return max;\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"4-4-searching-and-sorting\">4.4 Searching and sorting<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>// Bubble sort: repeatedly swap neighbours that are out of order\nvoid bubble_sort(int a[], int n) {\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n - 1; i++)\n        for (int j = 0; j &lt; n - 1 - i; j++)\n            if (a[j] &gt; a[j + 1]) {\n                int t = a[j];  a[j] = a[j + 1];  a[j + 1] = t;\n            }\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>After pass 1 the largest element has \"bubbled\" to the end; after pass 2 the second largest is in place, and so on. Fine for learning; real code uses qsort or better algorithms.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"4-5-strings\">4.5 Strings<\/h2>\n<p>C has no string type. A string is a char array whose last element is '\\0' (the null terminator, value 0). Functions find the end of the string by looking for it.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>char s1[] = \"hello\";               // size 6: h e l l o \\0\nchar s2[10] = \"hi\";                // h i \\0 and 7 more zeros\nchar s3[] = {'o', 'k', '\\0'};      // same idea, written by hand\n \nprintf(\"%s\\n\", s1);                // hello\nprintf(\"%zu\\n\", sizeof(s1));       // 6  (array size)\nprintf(\"%zu\\n\", strlen(s1));       // 5  (characters before \\0)<\/code><\/pre>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Function (string.h)<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>What it does<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>strlen(s)<\/code><\/td><td>Number of characters, not counting \\0<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>strcpy(dst, src)<\/code><\/td><td>Copies src into dst (dst must be big enough!)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>strncpy(dst, src, n)<\/code><\/td><td>Safer copy with a limit; may not add \\0, so add it yourself<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>strcmp(a, b)<\/code><\/td><td>Returns 0 if equal, negative if a &lt; b, positive if a &gt; b<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>strcat(dst, src)<\/code><\/td><td>Appends src to the end of dst<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>strchr(s, c)<\/code><\/td><td>Pointer to first occurrence of c, or NULL<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>strstr(s, sub)<\/code><\/td><td>Pointer to first occurrence of sub, or NULL<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<aside class=\"callout warn\" style=\"--c:#C0392B\"><p><strong>Never compare strings with ==<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>if (a == b) compares addresses, not text. Use strcmp(a, b) == 0.<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"4-6-reading-strings-safely\">4.6 Reading strings safely<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>char name[20];\n \nscanf(\"%19s\", name);          // reads one word, max 19 chars (leaves room for \\0)\n \nfgets(name, sizeof(name), stdin);    // reads a whole line including spaces\nname[strcspn(name, \"\\n\")] = '\\0';    // remove the newline fgets keeps<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"4-7-writing-your-own-strlen-and-reverse\">4.7 Writing your own strlen and reverse<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>int my_strlen(const char *s) {\n    int n = 0;\n    while (s[n] != '\\0') n++;\n    return n;\n}\n \nvoid reverse(char s[]) {\n    int i = 0, j = my_strlen(s) - 1;\n    while (i &lt; j) {\n        char t = s[i];  s[i] = s[j];  s[j] = t;\n        i++;  j--;\n    }\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Embedded note: serial commands, sensor names and display text are all strings. Always size buffers carefully: a UART buffer overflow can crash the device or corrupt memory.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes-4\">Common mistakes<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Mistake<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Fix<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Index out of range (arr[5] on size 5)<\/td><td>Loop with i &lt; size; validate indexes<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Forgetting the \\0 slot: char s[5] = \"hello\";<\/td><td>Size must be 6, or leave it empty: char s[] = \"hello\";<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Comparing strings with ==<\/td><td>Use strcmp<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>strcpy into a buffer that is too small<\/td><td>Check lengths or use snprintf / strncpy<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Using sizeof on an array parameter<\/td><td>It gives pointer size; pass the length<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Modifying a string literal: char *p = \"hi\"; p[0] = 'H';<\/td><td>Literals are read-only; use char p[] = \"hi\";<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"practice-programs-type-each-one-yourself-4\">Practice programs (type each one yourself)<\/h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Find the maximum, minimum, sum and average of an array.<\/li>\n<li>Reverse an array in place.<\/li>\n<li>Sort an array with bubble sort and search with linear search.<\/li>\n<li>Add two 3x3 matrices, then find the transpose.<\/li>\n<li>Write your own strlen, strcpy, strcmp and strcat.<\/li>\n<li>Check whether a string is a palindrome and count its vowels.<\/li>\n<li>Count how many times each letter appears in a string (array of 26 counters).<\/li>\n<li>Read a sentence and print each word on a new line.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"interview-questions-with-short-answers-4\">Interview questions with short answers<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Q1. Why do arrays start at index 0? The index is an offset from the first element: address = base + index x size.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q2. What is the null terminator? The character '\\0' (value 0) that marks the end of a C string.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q3. sizeof(\"hello\") vs strlen(\"hello\")? sizeof is 6 (includes \\0); strlen is 5.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q4. Does C check array bounds? No. Out-of-range access is undefined behaviour.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q5. Difference between char s[] = \"hi\" and char *s = \"hi\"? The first is a modifiable array copy; the second points to a read-only literal.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q6. How is a 2D array stored in memory? Row by row, in one contiguous block (row-major order).<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>TOPIC 5<\/strong><\/p>\n<h1 id=\"pointers\">Pointers<\/h1>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>In one line<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>A pointer is a variable that stores the memory address of another variable. Pointers let functions change the caller's data, walk through arrays, and talk directly to hardware.<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"5-1-the-idea\">5.1 The idea<\/h2>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>Analogy: house and address<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>A variable is a house. Its value is the person living inside. The address is the house number written on a paper slip. A pointer is the slip of paper: it is not the house, it only tells you where the house is.<\/p><\/aside>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Symbol<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Meaning<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>&amp;x<\/code><\/td><td>Address of x (\"where does x live?\")<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>int *p<\/code><\/td><td>Declare p as a pointer to an int<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>*p<\/code><\/td><td>Dereference: go to the address stored in p and use the value there<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>NULL<\/code><\/td><td>A pointer that points to nothing (address 0)<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>int x = 10;\nint *p = &amp;x;          // p holds the address of x\n \nprintf(\"%d\\n\", x);    // 10\nprintf(\"%p\\n\", (void *)p);   // address, for example 0x7ffd5c3a\nprintf(\"%d\\n\", *p);   // 10  (value at that address)\n \n*p = 99;              // change x through the pointer\nprintf(\"%d\\n\", x);    // 99<\/code><\/pre>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Name<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Address (example)<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Value<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Comment<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>x<\/td><td><code>0x1000<\/code><\/td><td><code>99<\/code><\/td><td>int variable<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>p<\/td><td><code>0x1004<\/code><\/td><td><code>0x1000<\/code><\/td><td>pointer holding the address of x<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>Draw this table on paper whenever pointers confuse you. Boxes with addresses and values explain almost every pointer question.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"5-2-pointer-size\">5.2 Pointer size<\/h2>\n<p>A pointer holds an address, so its size depends on the machine, not on what it points to: 8 bytes on 64-bit PCs, 4 bytes on ESP32, ESP8266 and ARM Cortex-M, 2 bytes on 8-bit AVR (Arduino Uno).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"5-3-call-by-reference-the-swap-that-works\">5.3 Call by reference: the swap that works<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>void swap(int *a, int *b) {\n    int t = *a;\n    *a = *b;\n    *b = t;\n}\n \nint x = 1, y = 2;\nswap(&amp;x, &amp;y);\nprintf(\"%d %d\\n\", x, y);    // 2 1<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong><\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>2 1<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>C still passes by value, but the value passed is an address, so the function can reach the original. Functions that must return more than one result also use pointers (output parameters).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"5-4-pointer-arithmetic\">5.4 Pointer arithmetic<\/h2>\n<p>Adding 1 to a pointer moves it by one element, not one byte. For int *p, p + 1 adds sizeof(int) to the address.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>int arr[4] = {10, 20, 30, 40};\nint *p = arr;                 // array name = address of first element\n \nprintf(\"%d\\n\", *p);           // 10\nprintf(\"%d\\n\", *(p + 2));     // 30\np++;                          // now points to arr[1]\nprintf(\"%d\\n\", *p);           // 20\n \n// arr[i] is exactly the same as *(arr + i)<\/code><\/pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Allowed: pointer + integer, pointer - integer, pointer - pointer (same array), comparing pointers.<\/li>\n<li>Not allowed: adding two pointers, multiplying pointers.<\/li>\n<li>An array name is not a variable: you cannot write arr++ (but p++ is fine).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"5-5-arrays-strings-and-pointers\">5.5 Arrays, strings and pointers<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>char *s = \"embedded\";           // points to a read-only literal\nint count = 0;\nwhile (*s != '\\0') {            // walk until the terminator\n    count++;\n    s++;\n}\nprintf(\"%d\\n\", count);          // 8<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"5-6-pointer-to-pointer-void-pointer-null\">5.6 Pointer to pointer, void pointer, NULL<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>int **pp = &amp;p; : pp points to p, which points to an int. **pp is the int. Used for 2D data and to let a function change a pointer.<\/li>\n<li>void *: a pointer with no type. It can hold any address but must be cast before use. malloc returns void *.<\/li>\n<li>NULL: always check a pointer against NULL before using it: if (p != NULL) { ... }.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"5-7-const-and-pointers\">5.7 const and pointers<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Declaration<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Meaning (read right to left)<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>const int *p<\/code><\/td><td>p points to a constant int: cannot change *p, can move p<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>int *const p<\/code><\/td><td>p is a constant pointer: cannot move p, can change *p<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>const int *const p<\/code><\/td><td>Cannot change either<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>Use const int *p for function parameters that only read data. It documents intent and the compiler enforces it.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"5-8-function-pointers\">5.8 Function pointers<\/h2>\n<p>A function also lives at an address. A function pointer stores it, so you can choose which function to call at run time (callbacks, state machines, driver tables).<\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }\nint sub(int a, int b) { return a - b; }\n \nint (*op)(int, int);          // op = pointer to function(int,int) returning int\n \nop = add;\nprintf(\"%d\\n\", op(5, 3));     // 8\nop = sub;\nprintf(\"%d\\n\", op(5, 3));     // 2<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong><\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>8\n2<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"5-9-dangerous-pointers\">5.9 Dangerous pointers<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Kind<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>What it is<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Result<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Wild<\/td><td>Declared but never initialised: int *p;<\/td><td>Random address; crash or corruption<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Dangling<\/td><td>Points to memory that is gone (freed or out of scope)<\/td><td>Garbage or crash<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>NULL dereference<\/td><td>*p when p is NULL<\/td><td>Crash (or silent fault on some MCUs)<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>int *bad(void) {\n    int local = 5;\n    return &amp;local;        // WRONG: local dies when the function returns\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Habit: initialise pointers to NULL, set to NULL after free, check before use.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes-5\">Common mistakes<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Mistake<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Fix<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Dereferencing an uninitialised or NULL pointer<\/td><td>Initialise, and check for NULL<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Returning the address of a local variable<\/td><td>Return a value, use static, or write into a caller-provided buffer<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Forgetting the &amp; when passing to a function that wants a pointer<\/td><td>swap(&amp;x, &amp;y), not swap(x, y)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Confusing p, *p and &amp;p<\/td><td>Draw the box diagram: p is the slip, *p is the house, &amp;p is where the slip is kept<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Pointer arithmetic beyond the array<\/td><td>Stay within arr[0]..arr[n-1] (one past the end is allowed only for comparing)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Writing through char *s = \"text\"<\/td><td>Use char s[] = \"text\" if you need to modify<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"practice-programs-type-each-one-yourself-5\">Practice programs (type each one yourself)<\/h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Declare an int, a pointer to it, and print value, address and *p.<\/li>\n<li>Swap two numbers using pointers.<\/li>\n<li>Traverse an array using only pointer arithmetic (no square brackets).<\/li>\n<li>Write my_strlen and my_strcpy using pointers.<\/li>\n<li>Write a function that returns both min and max through pointer parameters.<\/li>\n<li>Reverse an array using two pointers (start and end).<\/li>\n<li>Use a function pointer to choose between add, sub, mul at run time (menu).<\/li>\n<li>Print sizeof of different pointer types and explain why they are equal.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"interview-questions-with-short-answers-5\">Interview questions with short answers<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Q1. What is a pointer? A variable that stores a memory address.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q2. Difference between * in a declaration and * in an expression? In int *p it means \"p is a pointer\"; in *p it means \"the value at the address in p\".<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q3. What is a dangling pointer? A pointer to memory that has been freed or has gone out of scope.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q4. What is a void pointer? A generic pointer without a type; cast it before dereferencing.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q5. Difference between const int *p and int *const p? The first protects the data, the second protects the pointer itself.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q6. Why is arr[i] the same as *(arr + i)? The compiler defines indexing as pointer arithmetic: base address plus i elements.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q7. Where do you use function pointers in embedded code? Interrupt vector tables, callbacks, state machines and driver interfaces.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>TOPIC 6<\/strong><\/p>\n<h1 id=\"structures-unions-enums-and-typedef\">Structures, Unions, Enums and typedef<\/h1>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>In one line<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>A struct groups different variables under one name, a union lets members share the same memory, an enum gives names to integer constants, and typedef gives a type a shorter name.<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"6-1-structures\">6.1 Structures<\/h2>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>Analogy: a form<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>An array is a stack of identical sheets. A struct is one form with different fields: name, roll number, marks. One form describes one student.<\/p><\/aside>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>struct Student {\n    char  name[20];\n    int   roll;\n    float marks;\n};\n \nstruct Student s1 = {\"Ravi\", 101, 88.5f};\nstruct Student s2;\n \ns2.roll = 102;                       // . accesses a member\nstrcpy(s2.name, \"Anu\");\ns2.marks = 91.0f;\n \nprintf(\"%s %d %.1f\\n\", s1.name, s1.roll, s1.marks);<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong><\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Ravi 101 88.5<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"6-2-pointers-to-structures-and-the-arrow-operator\">6.2 Pointers to structures and the arrow operator<\/h2>\n<p>Structures can be large. Passing a pointer avoids copying the whole struct, and the function can modify the original. Use -&gt; with a pointer, . with a variable.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>void print_student(const struct Student *s) {\n    printf(\"%s %d %.1f\\n\", s-&gt;name, s-&gt;roll, s-&gt;marks);   // same as (*s).name\n}\n \nvoid give_bonus(struct Student *s) {\n    s-&gt;marks += 5;\n}\n \nprint_student(&amp;s1);\ngive_bonus(&amp;s1);<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"6-3-array-of-structures\">6.3 Array of structures<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>struct Student cls[3] = {\n    {\"Ravi\", 101, 88.5f},\n    {\"Anu\",  102, 91.0f},\n    {\"Kiran\",103, 79.0f}\n};\n \nfor (int i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++)\n    printf(\"%d %s %.1f\\n\", cls[i].roll, cls[i].name, cls[i].marks);<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"6-4-typedef-shorter-names\">6.4 typedef: shorter names<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>typedef struct {\n    float temperature;\n    float humidity;\n    uint32_t timestamp;\n} SensorData;\n \nSensorData reading = {27.5f, 60.0f, 1000};   // no need to write \"struct\"<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>typedef does not create a new type; it just gives a new name. It is used everywhere in embedded code: uint8_t itself is a typedef.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"6-5-struct-padding-and-size\">6.5 Struct padding and size<\/h2>\n<p>CPUs read memory fastest when a 4-byte int starts at an address divisible by 4. The compiler inserts unused padding bytes to keep members aligned, so sizeof(struct) can be bigger than the sum of its members.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>struct A { char a; int b; char c; };   // a, 3 pad, b (4), c, 3 pad  -&gt; 12 bytes\nstruct B { int b; char a; char c; };   // b (4), a, c, 2 pad          -&gt;  8 bytes\n \nprintf(\"%zu %zu\\n\", sizeof(struct A), sizeof(struct B));   // 12 8<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong><\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>12 8<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Tip: list members from largest to smallest to save RAM. This matters when you have thousands of records on a microcontroller.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"6-6-unions\">6.6 Unions<\/h2>\n<p>In a union all members start at the same address. The size is that of the largest member, and only one member holds a meaningful value at a time.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>union Data {\n    int   i;\n    float f;\n    uint8_t bytes[4];\n};\n \nunion Data d;\nd.f = 1.0f;\nprintf(\"%02X %02X %02X %02X\\n\", d.bytes[0], d.bytes[1], d.bytes[2], d.bytes[3]);\nprintf(\"%zu\\n\", sizeof(union Data));<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong><\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>00 00 80 3F\n4<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The output shows the four bytes of the float 1.0 (0x3F800000) on a little-endian machine, lowest byte first. Embedded uses of unions: viewing a 32-bit register as bytes or as bit fields, and sending different message types in one buffer.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><\/th><th><strong>struct<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>union<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Memory<\/td><td>Each member has its own space<\/td><td>Members share one space<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Size<\/td><td>Sum of members + padding<\/td><td>Size of the largest member<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Use<\/td><td>Group related data<\/td><td>Save memory, reinterpret the same bytes<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"6-7-enumerations\">6.7 Enumerations<\/h2>\n<p>An enum gives readable names to integer constants. Values start at 0 and increase by 1 unless you set them.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>enum Color { RED, GREEN, BLUE };            // 0, 1, 2\nenum State { IDLE = 10, RUNNING, ERROR };   // 10, 11, 12\n \nenum Color c = GREEN;\nif (c == GREEN) printf(\"green = %d\\n\", c);  // green = 1<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Use enums for states in a state machine (Topic 18) instead of magic numbers like 0, 1, 2.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"6-8-bit-fields\">6.8 Bit-fields<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>struct Flags {\n    unsigned int power   : 1;    // 1 bit\n    unsigned int mode    : 2;    // 2 bits (0 to 3)\n    unsigned int error   : 1;\n    unsigned int         : 4;    // 4 unused bits\n};\n \nstruct Flags f = {0};\nf.power = 1;\nf.mode  = 2;<\/code><\/pre>\n<aside class=\"callout warn\" style=\"--c:#C0392B\"><p><strong>Bit-fields are not portable<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The order of bits and the packing are decided by the compiler. For hardware registers and communication protocols, most engineers use masks and shifts (Topic 10) instead.<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes-6\">Common mistakes<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Mistake<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Fix<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Using . with a pointer (p.name)<\/td><td>Use -&gt; with pointers: p-&gt;name<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Forgetting the semicolon after the struct definition<\/td><td>struct X { ... };<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Comparing two structs with ==<\/td><td>Compare members one by one (or memcmp with care for padding)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Assuming sizeof(struct) is the sum of members<\/td><td>Print sizeof; remember padding<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Reading a different union member than you wrote (expecting conversion)<\/td><td>A union reinterprets bytes; it does not convert values<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Assigning strings with = (s.name = \"Ravi\")<\/td><td>Use strcpy for char arrays<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"practice-programs-type-each-one-yourself-6\">Practice programs (type each one yourself)<\/h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Create a Student struct, read 3 students and print the topper.<\/li>\n<li>Write a function that takes a pointer to struct and updates its marks.<\/li>\n<li>Print sizeof for the structs A and B above and explain the difference.<\/li>\n<li>Use a union to print the bytes of an int and decide if your machine is little or big endian.<\/li>\n<li>Make an enum for days of the week and print the name using a switch.<\/li>\n<li>Define typedef struct for a sensor reading and keep an array of 5 readings.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"interview-questions-with-short-answers-6\">Interview questions with short answers<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Q1. Difference between structure and union? Struct members have separate memory; union members share the same memory.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q2. What is structure padding? Unused bytes added so each member is aligned to its natural boundary for fast access.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q3. When do you use -&gt;? To access a member through a pointer to a structure.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q4. What is typedef? A way to give a new name to an existing type.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q5. How can you reduce the size of a struct? Order members from large to small, or use packed attribute (with a speed or portability cost).<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q6. What is an enum used for? Named integer constants, mainly states and options.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>TOPIC 7<\/strong><\/p>\n<h1 id=\"dynamic-memory-allocation\">Dynamic Memory Allocation<\/h1>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>In one line<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Dynamic memory lets a program request memory from the heap while it runs (malloc) and must give it back (free). On small microcontrollers it is usually avoided.<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"7-1-stack-vs-heap\">7.1 Stack vs heap<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><\/th><th><strong>Stack<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Heap<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Managed by<\/td><td>Compiler (automatic)<\/td><td>You (malloc / free)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Holds<\/td><td>Local variables, function calls<\/td><td>Memory you request at run time<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Size known<\/td><td>At compile time<\/td><td>Decided while running<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Speed<\/td><td>Very fast<\/td><td>Slower<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Freed<\/td><td>When the function returns<\/td><td>When you call free<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Risk<\/td><td>Stack overflow<\/td><td>Leaks, fragmentation, dangling pointers<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>Analogy: hotel rooms<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Stack is your own room that is cleaned automatically when you leave. Heap is renting a hall: you ask the manager (malloc), use it as long as you like, and must hand the keys back (free). If you forget, nobody else can use that hall.<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"7-2-the-four-functions-stdlib-h\">7.2 The four functions (stdlib.h)<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Function<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>What it does<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>malloc(size)<\/code><\/td><td>Reserves size bytes; memory is NOT cleared; returns NULL on failure<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>calloc(n, size)<\/code><\/td><td>Reserves n x size bytes and sets all to zero<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>realloc(ptr, size)<\/code><\/td><td>Resizes a block (may move it); returns the new address<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>free(ptr)<\/code><\/td><td>Returns the block to the heap<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;\n \nint main(void) {\n    int n = 5;\n    int *arr = malloc(n * sizeof *arr);     // space for 5 ints\n    if (arr == NULL) {                      // ALWAYS check\n        printf(\"Out of memory\\n\");\n        return 1;\n    }\n \n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) arr[i] = i * i;\n \n    // grow to 10 elements: use a temporary pointer\n    int *tmp = realloc(arr, 10 * sizeof *arr);\n    if (tmp == NULL) { free(arr); return 1; }\n    arr = tmp;\n \n    for (int i = 5; i &lt; 10; i++) arr[i] = i * i;\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) printf(\"%d \", arr[i]);\n \n    free(arr);          // give it back\n    arr = NULL;         // avoid a dangling pointer\n    return 0;\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong><\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>0 1 4 9 16 25 36 49 64 81<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Why sizeof *arr? It stays correct even if you change the pointer type later. Why a temporary pointer in realloc? If realloc fails it returns NULL, and writing that straight into arr would lose the old block (a leak).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"7-3-memory-problems\">7.3 Memory problems<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Problem<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Example<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Result<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Memory leak<\/td><td>malloc without free, or losing the pointer<\/td><td>RAM slowly runs out<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Double free<\/td><td>free(p); free(p);<\/td><td>Crash or heap corruption<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Use after free<\/td><td>free(p); *p = 3;<\/td><td>Undefined behaviour<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Buffer overflow<\/td><td>Writing past the allocated size<\/td><td>Corrupts neighbouring data<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Uninitialised read<\/td><td>Reading malloc memory before writing<\/td><td>Garbage values<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"7-4-linked-list-a-classic-heap-use\">7.4 Linked list: a classic heap use<\/h2>\n<p>An array has a fixed size. A linked list grows one node at a time; each node holds data and a pointer to the next node.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>typedef struct Node {\n    int data;\n    struct Node *next;\n} Node;\n \nNode *push_front(Node *head, int value) {\n    Node *n = malloc(sizeof *n);\n    if (n == NULL) return head;\n    n-&gt;data = value;\n    n-&gt;next = head;          // new node points to old first node\n    return n;                // new node is now the head\n}\n \nvoid print_list(const Node *head) {\n    for (const Node *p = head; p != NULL; p = p-&gt;next)\n        printf(\"%d -&gt; \", p-&gt;data);\n    printf(\"NULL\\n\");\n}\n \nvoid free_list(Node *head) {\n    while (head != NULL) {\n        Node *next = head-&gt;next;     // save before freeing\n        free(head);\n        head = next;\n    }\n}\n \nint main(void) {\n    Node *list = NULL;\n    list = push_front(list, 3);\n    list = push_front(list, 2);\n    list = push_front(list, 1);\n    print_list(list);\n    free_list(list);\n    return 0;\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong><\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>1 -&gt; 2 -&gt; 3 -&gt; NULL<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"7-5-dynamic-memory-in-embedded-systems\">7.5 Dynamic memory in embedded systems<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Small chips have kilobytes of RAM. Repeated malloc and free cause fragmentation: enough total free memory but no single block big enough.<\/li>\n<li>Allocation time is unpredictable, which hurts real-time behaviour.<\/li>\n<li>Safety-critical standards (MISRA, automotive, medical) often forbid heap use after start-up.<\/li>\n<li>Preferred: static arrays, fixed-size buffers and memory pools.<\/li>\n<li>On ESP32 you do have a heap and FreeRTOS; allocate once at start-up and keep it. Check free heap with ESP.getFreeHeap() in Arduino code.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes-7\">Common mistakes<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Mistake<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Fix<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Not checking malloc for NULL<\/td><td>Always test the result<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Forgetting free (leak)<\/td><td>Pair every malloc with one free; free in the same module that allocated<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Using the pointer after free<\/td><td>Set the pointer to NULL after free<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>malloc(n) instead of malloc(n * sizeof(type))<\/td><td>Multiply by the element size<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>arr = realloc(arr, size) losing the block on failure<\/td><td>Use a temporary pointer<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Freeing stack memory or a pointer not from malloc<\/td><td>Only free what malloc, calloc or realloc returned<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"practice-programs-type-each-one-yourself-7\">Practice programs (type each one yourself)<\/h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Ask the user for n, allocate an array of n ints, fill it, print the average, free it.<\/li>\n<li>Read strings of unknown length by growing a buffer with realloc.<\/li>\n<li>Build a linked list: push_front, push_back, delete a value, reverse the list, free everything.<\/li>\n<li>Create a dynamic 2D array (array of pointers) and free it correctly.<\/li>\n<li>Write code with a deliberate leak, then find it with valgrind --leak-check=full ./a.out.<\/li>\n<li>Redo the array program using a fixed static buffer and compare the pros and cons.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"interview-questions-with-short-answers-7\">Interview questions with short answers<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Q1. Difference between malloc and calloc? calloc takes count and size and zeroes the memory; malloc does not initialise.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q2. What is a memory leak? Allocated memory that is never freed and can no longer be reached.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q3. What does realloc do if it cannot extend in place? It allocates a new block, copies the data and frees the old one.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q4. Why avoid malloc in embedded systems? Fragmentation, unpredictable timing and failure handling on tiny RAM.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q5. Stack vs heap? Stack is automatic and fast for locals; heap is manual and flexible for run-time sizes.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q6. What happens if you free the same pointer twice? Undefined behaviour, often a crash or heap corruption.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>TOPIC 8<\/strong><\/p>\n<h1 id=\"storage-classes-and-the-preprocessor\">Storage Classes and the Preprocessor<\/h1>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>In one line<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Storage classes decide where a variable lives, how long it lives and who can see it; the preprocessor edits your source text (includes, macros, conditions) before compilation.<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"8-1-the-four-storage-classes\">8.1 The four storage classes<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Class<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Scope (who sees it)<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Lifetime<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Default value<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Stored in<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>auto<\/code><\/td><td>Block<\/td><td>Until block ends<\/td><td>Garbage<\/td><td>Stack<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>register<\/code><\/td><td>Block<\/td><td>Until block ends<\/td><td>Garbage<\/td><td>CPU register (a hint)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>static<\/code><\/td><td>Block or file<\/td><td>Whole program<\/td><td>0<\/td><td>Data / BSS<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>extern<\/code><\/td><td>Whole program<\/td><td>Whole program<\/td><td>0<\/td><td>Data / BSS<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>auto is the default for local variables, so you almost never type it. register is a request to the compiler that modern compilers ignore (they decide better).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"8-2-static\">8.2 static<\/h2>\n<p>static does two different jobs depending on where it is written.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>static inside a function: the variable keeps its value between calls (lifetime = whole program) but only that function can see it.<\/li>\n<li>static at file level (global or function): it is private to this .c file (internal linkage). Other files cannot access it.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>void counter(void) {\n    static int count = 0;      // initialised once only\n    count++;\n    printf(\"called %d times\\n\", count);\n}\n \ncounter();   // called 1 times\ncounter();   // called 2 times\ncounter();   // called 3 times<\/code><\/pre>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>Embedded use<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Use static for file-private variables and helper functions in a driver. It works like \"private\" in other languages and prevents name clashes between files.<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"8-3-extern\">8.3 extern<\/h2>\n<p>extern says \"this variable exists, it is defined in another file\". It declares without creating.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>// config.c\nint baud_rate = 115200;          // definition: memory is created here\n \n// main.c\n#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\nextern int baud_rate;            // declaration: refers to the one above\n \nint main(void) { printf(\"%d\\n\", baud_rate); return 0; }<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Normally you put the extern declaration in a header file (config.h) so every file shares the same one.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"8-4-the-preprocessor\">8.4 The preprocessor<\/h2>\n<p>Lines starting with # are processed before compilation. The preprocessor only edits text; it knows nothing about C types.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Directive<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Purpose<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>#include &lt;file&gt;<\/code><\/td><td>Search system folders (standard headers)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>#include \"file\"<\/code><\/td><td>Search your project folder first (your headers)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>#define NAME value<\/code><\/td><td>Replace NAME with value everywhere<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>#undef NAME<\/code><\/td><td>Remove a definition<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>#ifdef / #ifndef / #if<\/code><\/td><td>Compile code only if a condition is true<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>#else / #elif / #endif<\/code><\/td><td>Alternatives and end of a conditional block<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>#error / #warning<\/code><\/td><td>Stop or warn at compile time with a message<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"8-5-macros\">8.5 Macros<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>#define PI 3.14159\n#define LED_PIN 13\n#define SQUARE(x) ((x) * (x))\n#define MAX(a, b) ((a) &gt; (b) ? (a) : (b))\n \nint r = SQUARE(2 + 3);          // becomes ((2 + 3) * (2 + 3)) = 25<\/code><\/pre>\n<aside class=\"callout warn\" style=\"--c:#C0392B\"><p><strong>Brackets are not optional<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>If you write #define SQUARE(x) x * x, then SQUARE(2 + 3) becomes 2 + 3 * 2 + 3 = 11, not 25. Put every parameter and the whole expression in brackets.<\/p>\n<p>Side effects: MAX(i++, j) may evaluate i++ twice. Use an inline function when you can.<\/p><\/aside>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><\/th><th><strong>Macro<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>const / inline function<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Type checking<\/td><td>None<\/td><td>Yes<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Debugging<\/td><td>Hard (text replaced)<\/td><td>Easier<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Memory<\/td><td>None until used<\/td><td>const may take memory<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Best for<\/td><td>Pin numbers, register addresses, bit masks, conditional builds<\/td><td>Typed constants and small functions<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"8-6-include-guards\">8.6 Include guards<\/h2>\n<p>If two headers include the same header, the compiler sees duplicate definitions and fails. An include guard makes a header count only once.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>#ifndef SENSOR_H\n#define SENSOR_H\n \nint read_sensor(void);\n \n#endif<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"8-7-conditional-compilation\">8.7 Conditional compilation<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>#define DEBUG 1\n \n#if DEBUG\n    #define LOG(msg) printf(\"[LOG] %s\\n\", msg)\n#else\n    #define LOG(msg)            // expands to nothing in release builds\n#endif\n \n#ifdef ESP32\n    // code only for the ESP32 board\n#elif defined(ARDUINO_AVR_UNO)\n    // code only for the Arduino Uno\n#endif<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Conditional compilation lets one code base support several boards or switch debug output on and off with zero cost in the final firmware.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes-8\">Common mistakes<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Mistake<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Fix<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Macro without brackets<\/td><td>Wrap each parameter and the whole expression<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Semicolon at the end of #define<\/td><td>Do not add one: #define N 10<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Defining a variable in a header<\/td><td>Define in one .c file, declare with extern in the header<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Missing include guard<\/td><td>Add #ifndef / #define / #endif to every header<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Expecting a static local to reset on each call<\/td><td>It keeps its value; initialisation runs only once<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Using #define for typed values<\/td><td>Prefer const or enum when type safety matters<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"practice-programs-type-each-one-yourself-8\">Practice programs (type each one yourself)<\/h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write a function that counts how many times it has been called using static.<\/li>\n<li>Create config.c with a global and use it in main.c through extern.<\/li>\n<li>Write SQUARE, MAX and MIN macros and test them with expressions like 2 + 3.<\/li>\n<li>Split a project into main.c, utils.c and utils.h with an include guard.<\/li>\n<li>Add a DEBUG switch so that log messages disappear when DEBUG is 0.<\/li>\n<li>Show the preprocessor output with gcc -E main.c and find where your macro was replaced.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"interview-questions-with-short-answers-8\">Interview questions with short answers<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Q1. What does static do? Inside a function it keeps the value between calls; at file level it hides the name from other files.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q2. Difference between declaration and definition of a global? A definition creates storage; extern declarations only refer to it.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q3. What is an include guard? Preprocessor lines that make sure a header is included only once per file.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q4. Macro vs function? A macro is text replacement without type checks and no call overhead; a function is type-safe and debuggable.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q5. What is the default value of a static variable? Zero.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q6. #include &lt;&gt; vs \"\"? Angle brackets search system paths; quotes search the project folder first.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>TOPIC 9<\/strong><\/p>\n<h1 id=\"file-handling\">File Handling<\/h1>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>In one line<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>File handling lets a program store data permanently by reading and writing files using a FILE pointer and functions such as fopen, fprintf, fread and fclose.<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"9-1-why-files\">9.1 Why files?<\/h2>\n<p>Variables vanish when the program ends. Files keep data: logs, configuration, records. On microcontrollers the same ideas apply to an SD card, SPIFFS or LittleFS on ESP32 (the function names differ slightly but the steps are the same).<\/p>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>Analogy: a notebook<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Open the notebook (fopen), read or write on it (fprintf, fscanf, fread, fwrite), and close it (fclose) so everything is saved.<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"9-2-steps-for-every-file-operation\">9.2 Steps for every file operation<\/h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Declare a FILE pointer: FILE *fp;<\/li>\n<li>Open the file with fopen and check it did not return NULL.<\/li>\n<li>Read or write.<\/li>\n<li>Close with fclose.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Mode<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Meaning<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>\"r\"<\/code><\/td><td>Read. File must exist<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>\"w\"<\/code><\/td><td>Write. Creates the file, or ERASES it if it exists<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>\"a\"<\/code><\/td><td>Append. Writes at the end; creates the file if missing<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>\"r+\"<\/code><\/td><td>Read and write an existing file<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>\"w+\"<\/code><\/td><td>Read and write; erases existing content<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>\"rb\" \"wb\" \"ab\"<\/code><\/td><td>Same as above but binary (add b)<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"9-3-text-files\">9.3 Text files<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n \nint main(void) {\n    FILE *fp = fopen(\"data.txt\", \"w\");\n    if (fp == NULL) {\n        perror(\"fopen\");              // prints why it failed\n        return 1;\n    }\n    fprintf(fp, \"Ravi %d %.1f\\n\", 101, 88.5);\n    fprintf(fp, \"Anu %d %.1f\\n\", 102, 91.0);\n    fclose(fp);\n \n    fp = fopen(\"data.txt\", \"r\");\n    if (fp == NULL) return 1;\n \n    char name[20];\n    int roll;\n    float marks;\n    while (fscanf(fp, \"%19s %d %f\", name, &amp;roll, &amp;marks) == 3)\n        printf(\"%s %d %.1f\\n\", name, roll, marks);\n    fclose(fp);\n    return 0;\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong><\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Ravi 101 88.5\nAnu 102 91.0<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>fscanf returns the number of items it managed to read. Looping while it returns 3 stops cleanly at the end of the file.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"9-4-reading-line-by-line\">9.4 Reading line by line<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>char line[100];\nFILE *fp = fopen(\"log.txt\", \"r\");\nif (fp != NULL) {\n    while (fgets(line, sizeof line, fp) != NULL)\n        printf(\"%s\", line);          // fgets keeps the newline\n    fclose(fp);\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Function<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Use<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>fprintf / fscanf<\/code><\/td><td>Formatted text, like printf / scanf but for files<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>fputs / fgets<\/code><\/td><td>Write or read a line of text<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>fputc / fgetc<\/code><\/td><td>Write or read one character<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>fwrite / fread<\/code><\/td><td>Write or read raw bytes (binary files)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>fseek / ftell / rewind<\/code><\/td><td>Move to a position, find the position, go back to start<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>feof / ferror<\/code><\/td><td>Check end of file or an error<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"9-5-binary-files-save-structs-directly\">9.5 Binary files: save structs directly<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>typedef struct { char name[20]; int roll; float marks; } Student;\n \nStudent s = {\"Ravi\", 101, 88.5f};\n \nFILE *fp = fopen(\"students.bin\", \"wb\");\nif (fp) {\n    fwrite(&amp;s, sizeof(Student), 1, fp);     // 1 record\n    fclose(fp);\n}\n \nStudent t;\nfp = fopen(\"students.bin\", \"rb\");\nif (fp) {\n    if (fread(&amp;t, sizeof(Student), 1, fp) == 1)\n        printf(\"%s %d %.1f\\n\", t.name, t.roll, t.marks);\n    fclose(fp);\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong><\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Ravi 101 88.5<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Binary files are compact and fast, but they depend on the compiler, struct padding and endianness, so they are not portable between different systems. Text files are bigger but readable.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"9-6-jumping-around-a-file\">9.6 Jumping around a file<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>fseek(fp, 0, SEEK_END);        // go to the end\nlong size = ftell(fp);         // position = file size in bytes\nrewind(fp);                    // back to the start\n \n// jump straight to the 3rd record (index 2) of a binary file\nfseek(fp, 2 * sizeof(Student), SEEK_SET);<\/code><\/pre>\n<aside class=\"callout warn\" style=\"--c:#C0392B\"><p><strong>The while(!feof(fp)) bug<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>feof becomes true only after a read has failed, so this loop runs one extra time and processes garbage. Test the return value of the read function instead (fscanf, fgets, fread).<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes-9\">Common mistakes<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Mistake<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Fix<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Not checking if fopen returned NULL<\/td><td>Always check; print the reason with perror<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Forgetting fclose<\/td><td>Data may stay in the buffer and be lost; always close<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Opening with \"w\" and erasing data by accident<\/td><td>Use \"a\" to add or \"r+\" to modify<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Using while(!feof(fp))<\/td><td>Loop on the read function's return value<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Reading a binary file in text mode (or the reverse)<\/td><td>Match the mode: \"rb\" with fread<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>fscanf(\"%s\") overflow on long words<\/td><td>Use a width: %19s for char[20]<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"practice-programs-type-each-one-yourself-9\">Practice programs (type each one yourself)<\/h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write 5 numbers into a file, then read and print their sum.<\/li>\n<li>Copy one text file into another, converting all letters to upper case.<\/li>\n<li>Count characters, words and lines of a text file.<\/li>\n<li>Build a student record manager: add, list and search records stored in a binary file.<\/li>\n<li>Append timestamped log lines to log.txt every time the program runs.<\/li>\n<li>Use fseek to read the last record of a binary file without reading the others.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"interview-questions-with-short-answers-9\">Interview questions with short answers<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Q1. What is FILE *? A pointer to a structure that holds the state of an open file (buffer, position, flags).<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q2. Difference between \"w\" and \"a\"? \"w\" erases existing content; \"a\" appends at the end.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q3. Text vs binary file? Text stores characters (human readable, newline conversion on some systems); binary stores raw bytes exactly as in memory.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q4. Why must you call fclose? It flushes buffered data to storage and releases the file handle.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q5. What do fread and fwrite return? The number of items successfully read or written.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q6. How do you find the size of a file? fseek to SEEK_END then ftell.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>TOPIC 10<\/strong><\/p>\n<h1 id=\"bitwise-operators-and-bit-manipulation\">Bitwise Operators and Bit Manipulation<\/h1>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>In one line<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Bitwise operators work on the individual bits of a number. They are the main tool for controlling hardware registers, flags and protocols in embedded C.<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"10-1-why-bits-matter-in-embedded\">10.1 Why bits matter in embedded<\/h2>\n<p>A hardware register is a number where each bit (or group of bits) controls one thing: bit 5 turns an LED pin on, bits 2 to 4 select a clock speed, bit 7 says \"data ready\". To change one bit without disturbing the others, you need bitwise operators.<\/p>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>Analogy: a row of light switches<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>A byte is a panel of 8 switches. Bitwise operators let you flip one switch, check one switch, or set a pattern, without touching the others.<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"10-2-the-six-operators\">10.2 The six operators<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Op<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Name<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Rule<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>&amp;<\/code><\/td><td>AND<\/td><td>1 only if both bits are 1 (used to CLEAR bits and to CHECK bits)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>|<\/code><\/td><td>OR<\/td><td>1 if either bit is 1 (used to SET bits)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>^<\/code><\/td><td>XOR<\/td><td>1 if the bits are different (used to TOGGLE bits)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>~<\/code><\/td><td>NOT<\/td><td>Flips every bit<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>&lt;&lt;<\/code><\/td><td>Left shift<\/td><td>Moves bits left, fills with 0; x &lt;&lt; n multiplies by 2^n<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>&gt;&gt;<\/code><\/td><td>Right shift<\/td><td>Moves bits right; x &gt;&gt; n divides by 2^n (for unsigned)<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>Worked example with a = 12 (1100) and b = 10 (1010):<\/p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Expression<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Binary work<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Result<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Decimal<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>12 &amp; 10<\/td><td><code>1100 &amp; 1010<\/code><\/td><td>1000<\/td><td>8<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>12 | 10<\/td><td><code>1100 | 1010<\/code><\/td><td>1110<\/td><td>14<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>12 ^ 10<\/td><td><code>1100 ^ 1010<\/code><\/td><td>0110<\/td><td>6<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>12 &lt;&lt; 1<\/td><td><code>1100 -&gt; 11000<\/code><\/td><td>11000<\/td><td>24<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>12 &gt;&gt; 1<\/td><td><code>1100 -&gt; 0110<\/code><\/td><td>0110<\/td><td>6<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>~12 (8-bit)<\/td><td><code>~00001100<\/code><\/td><td>11110011<\/td><td>243 unsigned, -13 signed<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<aside class=\"callout warn\" style=\"--c:#C0392B\"><p><strong>Do not confuse &amp; with &amp;&amp;<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>&amp;&amp; is logical AND (true/false of whole values). &amp; is bitwise AND (bit by bit). 4 &amp;&amp; 2 is 1 (both non-zero), but 4 &amp; 2 is 0 (no common bits).<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"10-3-the-four-essential-operations\">10.3 The four essential operations<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Goal<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Code<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Why it works<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Set bit n<\/td><td><code>x |= (1 &lt;&lt; n);<\/code><\/td><td>OR with a 1 forces the bit to 1, others unchanged<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Clear bit n<\/td><td><code>x &amp;= ~(1 &lt;&lt; n);<\/code><\/td><td>AND with a 0 forces the bit to 0, others unchanged<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Toggle bit n<\/td><td><code>x ^= (1 &lt;&lt; n);<\/code><\/td><td>XOR with 1 flips the bit<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Check bit n<\/td><td><code>if (x &amp; (1 &lt;&lt; n))<\/code><\/td><td>AND leaves only that bit; non-zero means it was 1<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>Step by step: set bit 3 of x = 0b00000101 (5).<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>1 &lt;&lt; 3 gives the mask 00001000.<\/li>\n<li>x | mask = 00000101 | 00001000 = 00001101 (13).<\/li>\n<li>Only bit 3 changed.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>#define SET_BIT(x, n)     ((x) |=  (1U &lt;&lt; (n)))\n#define CLEAR_BIT(x, n)   ((x) &amp;= ~(1U &lt;&lt; (n)))\n#define TOGGLE_BIT(x, n)  ((x) ^=  (1U &lt;&lt; (n)))\n#define CHECK_BIT(x, n)   (((x) &gt;&gt; (n)) &amp; 1U)\n \nint main(void) {\n    unsigned char x = 5;            // 00000101\n    SET_BIT(x, 3);                  // 00001101\n    printf(\"%u\\n\", x);              // 13\n    CLEAR_BIT(x, 0);                // 00001100\n    printf(\"%u\\n\", x);              // 12\n    TOGGLE_BIT(x, 7);               // 10001100\n    printf(\"%u\\n\", x);              // 140\n    printf(\"%u\\n\", CHECK_BIT(x, 2));   // 1\n    return 0;\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong><\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>13\n12\n140\n1<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"10-4-multi-bit-fields-mask-shift-insert\">10.4 Multi-bit fields: mask, shift, insert<\/h2>\n<p>Suppose bits 4 to 6 of a register hold a 3-bit \"mode\" value.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>#define MODE_SHIFT 4\n#define MODE_MASK  (0x7U &lt;&lt; MODE_SHIFT)       // 0111 0000\n \nuint8_t reg = 0xA5;                           // 1010 0101\n \n// READ the field: shift it down, then mask\nuint8_t mode = (reg &gt;&gt; MODE_SHIFT) &amp; 0x7U;    // 010 = 2\n \n// WRITE the field: clear the old bits, then OR in the new value\nreg = (reg &amp; ~MODE_MASK) | ((5U &lt;&lt; MODE_SHIFT) &amp; MODE_MASK);   // mode = 101<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This \"clear then set\" pattern is the standard way to update part of a register safely.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"10-5-classic-tricks-frequent-interview-questions\">10.5 Classic tricks (frequent interview questions)<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>// even or odd\nif (n &amp; 1) { /* odd */ }\n \n// power of two (n must be &gt; 0)\nint is_pow2 = (n &gt; 0) &amp;&amp; ((n &amp; (n - 1)) == 0);\n \n// count set bits (Kernighan): each loop removes the lowest 1 bit\nint count_bits(unsigned int n) {\n    int c = 0;\n    while (n) { n &amp;= (n - 1); c++; }\n    return c;\n}\n \n// swap two numbers without a temporary variable\na ^= b;  b ^= a;  a ^= b;\n \n// swap the two nibbles of a byte\nuint8_t swapped = (uint8_t)((x &lt;&lt; 4) | (x &gt;&gt; 4));\n \n// multiply and divide by 8 (unsigned)\ny = x &lt;&lt; 3;     // x * 8\nz = x &gt;&gt; 3;     // x / 8<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Why n &amp; (n-1) works: subtracting 1 flips the lowest 1 bit to 0 and all the zeros below it to 1, so AND-ing removes exactly that lowest 1.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"10-6-traps-to-remember\">10.6 Traps to remember<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Precedence: == binds tighter than &amp;. Write if ((x &amp; 4) == 4), not if (x &amp; 4 == 4).<\/li>\n<li>Use unsigned types (or 1U, 1UL) for shifts. Shifting a signed negative value is implementation-defined, and shifting 1 into the sign bit of an int is undefined.<\/li>\n<li>Do not shift by the width of the type or more (1 &lt;&lt; 32 on a 32-bit int is undefined).<\/li>\n<li>On 8-bit and 16-bit systems use 1UL &lt;&lt; n if n can be 16 or more, otherwise the 1 is only 16 bits wide.<\/li>\n<li>Integer promotion: uint8_t values become int in expressions, so ~x on a uint8_t gives a 32-bit result. Cast back: (uint8_t)~x.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"10-7-real-hardware-example-arduino-uno-avr\">10.7 Real hardware example (Arduino Uno, AVR)<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>// Pin 13 on the Uno is PB5. Blink it with direct register access.\nvoid setup() {\n    DDRB |= (1 &lt;&lt; DDB5);          // make PB5 an output\n}\n \nvoid loop() {\n    PORTB ^= (1 &lt;&lt; PORTB5);       // toggle only PB5, other pins untouched\n    delay(500);\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>DDRB is the direction register (1 = output). PORTB holds the output levels. One line toggles one pin and leaves the other seven alone. This is exactly why you learn bit manipulation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes-10\">Common mistakes<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Mistake<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Fix<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Using = instead of |= when setting a bit (wipes other bits)<\/td><td>Use x |= mask<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>x &amp; 4 == 4 (precedence)<\/td><td>Add brackets: (x &amp; 4) == 4<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>1 &lt;&lt; 31 on a signed int<\/td><td>Use 1U &lt;&lt; 31<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Using &amp;&amp; when you meant &amp;<\/td><td>Bitwise &amp; works per bit<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Right shifting negative signed numbers and assuming division<\/td><td>Use unsigned types for bit work<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Forgetting to clear a field before setting a new value<\/td><td>Clear the mask first, then OR the new bits<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"practice-programs-type-each-one-yourself-10\">Practice programs (type each one yourself)<\/h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write SET_BIT, CLEAR_BIT, TOGGLE_BIT, CHECK_BIT as functions and as macros; test on all 8 bits.<\/li>\n<li>Print any number in binary (8, 16 and 32 bits).<\/li>\n<li>Count the number of set bits in a number.<\/li>\n<li>Check if a number is a power of 2.<\/li>\n<li>Reverse the bits of a byte.<\/li>\n<li>Swap the upper and lower nibble of a byte.<\/li>\n<li>Pack three values (3 bits, 2 bits, 3 bits) into one byte and unpack them.<\/li>\n<li>Find the single non-repeating number in an array where all others appear twice (use XOR).<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"interview-questions-with-short-answers-10\">Interview questions with short answers<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Q1. How do you set, clear, toggle and check bit n? Set: x |= 1&lt;&lt;n. Clear: x &amp;= ~(1&lt;&lt;n). Toggle: x ^= 1&lt;&lt;n. Check: (x&gt;&gt;n)&amp;1.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q2. Difference between &amp; and &amp;&amp;? &amp; works on each bit; &amp;&amp; works on whole values as true or false.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q3. How do you check for a power of 2? n &gt; 0 and (n &amp; (n-1)) == 0.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q4. What does x &lt;&lt; n do? Shifts bits left by n places, equal to multiplying by 2^n if no overflow.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q5. How do you swap two variables without a temp? a ^= b; b ^= a; a ^= b; (or with + and -).<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q6. Why use unsigned for bit operations? Signed shifts and overflow have implementation-defined or undefined behaviour.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q7. How do you update a 3-bit field in a register without touching others? Clear the field with a mask, then OR in the new value shifted into position.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>TOPIC 11<\/strong><\/p>\n<h1 id=\"type-qualifiers-and-fixed-width-types\">Type Qualifiers and Fixed-Width Types<\/h1>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>In one line<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>volatile, const and static tell the compiler extra facts about a variable; fixed-width types (uint8_t, uint32_t...) make sizes identical on every chip.<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"11-1-volatile\">11.1 volatile<\/h2>\n<p>Compilers optimise. If your code reads a variable in a loop and nothing in that loop changes it, the compiler assumes it never changes and reads it only once. That is wrong if hardware or an interrupt changes it behind the compiler's back. volatile means: \"this value can change at any time, always read it from memory and never cache it.\"<\/p>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>Analogy: a whiteboard in a busy office<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Without volatile, the compiler copies the whiteboard into its notebook once and keeps reading its notes. With volatile, it must look at the whiteboard every single time, because someone else keeps writing on it.<\/p><\/aside>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>int flag = 0;                      // BUG: not volatile\n \nvoid ISR(void) { flag = 1; }       // interrupt sets the flag\n \nint main(void) {\n    while (flag == 0) { }          // compiler may turn this into an endless loop\n    do_work();\n}\n \n// FIX\nvolatile int flag = 0;<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Three situations that need volatile:<\/p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Situation<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Example<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Hardware register<\/td><td>volatile uint32_t *status = (volatile uint32_t *)0x40021018;<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Variable changed in an ISR and read in main (or the reverse)<\/td><td>volatile uint8_t button_pressed;<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Variable shared between tasks / threads (with proper locking)<\/td><td>volatile bool running;<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<aside class=\"callout warn\" style=\"--c:#C0392B\"><p><strong>volatile does not make code safe<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>volatile only stops the compiler from caching the value. It does not make a multi-byte read atomic. On an 8-bit AVR, reading a 16-bit volatile variable takes two instructions; an interrupt in between can give a half-old, half-new value. Disable interrupts around such reads (Topic 15).<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"11-2-const\">11.2 const<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>const means the program may not modify the value. The compiler reports an error if you try.<\/li>\n<li>It documents intent: a function taking const char *s promises not to change the string.<\/li>\n<li>Global const data is placed in flash (read-only memory) on most microcontrollers, saving RAM. On AVR Arduino you need PROGMEM to be sure; on ESP32 and ARM it happens automatically.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>const int MAX_RETRY = 3;                       // cannot change\nconst uint8_t sine_table[8] = {0, 90, 127, 90, 0, 90, 127, 90};   // lookup table in flash\n \nint length(const char *s);                     // promises not to modify s<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"11-3-const-volatile-together\">11.3 const volatile together<\/h2>\n<p>This looks contradictory but is common for read-only hardware registers, for example a status register or an input-port register. Your code must not write it (const), but the hardware changes it all the time (volatile).<\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>const volatile uint32_t *const STATUS_REG = (const volatile uint32_t *)0x40010000;\n \nwhile ((*STATUS_REG &amp; 0x01) == 0) { }   // wait until the ready bit is set<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"11-4-static-recap\">11.4 static (recap)<\/h2>\n<p>Local static: keeps its value across calls. File-level static: hidden from other files. Both live in the data or BSS area, not on the stack. Details in Topic 8.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"11-5-fixed-width-integer-types\">11.5 Fixed-width integer types<\/h2>\n<p>Because int, long and even char can differ between chips, include &lt;stdint.h&gt; and use types whose width is part of the name.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Type<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Bits<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Range<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Typical use<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>int8_t / uint8_t<\/code><\/td><td>8<\/td><td>-128..127 / 0..255<\/td><td>Bytes, flags, 8-bit registers<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>int16_t / uint16_t<\/code><\/td><td>16<\/td><td>-32768..32767 / 0..65535<\/td><td>ADC values, 16-bit registers<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>int32_t / uint32_t<\/code><\/td><td>32<\/td><td>+/-2.1 billion / 0..4.29 billion<\/td><td>32-bit registers, timestamps<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>int64_t / uint64_t<\/code><\/td><td>64<\/td><td>very large<\/td><td>Long counters (slow on 8-bit chips)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>size_t<\/code><\/td><td>varies<\/td><td>unsigned, holds a size<\/td><td>Array sizes, sizeof results, loop indexes<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>bool (stdbool.h)<\/code><\/td><td>8 typically<\/td><td>true or false<\/td><td>Flags and conditions<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>#include &lt;stdint.h&gt;\n#include &lt;stdbool.h&gt;\n \nuint8_t  led_state  = 0;\nuint16_t adc_value  = 3120;\nuint32_t millis_now = 0;\nbool     connected  = false;\n \n// guaranteed on every platform:\n// sizeof(uint8_t) == 1,  sizeof(uint16_t) == 2,  sizeof(uint32_t) == 4<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"11-6-choosing-the-right-type\">11.6 Choosing the right type<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Situation<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Use<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Hardware register, communication buffer, protocol field<\/td><td>uint8_t / uint16_t / uint32_t<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Loop counter on a PC or 32-bit MCU<\/td><td>int or size_t<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Value that is never negative<\/td><td>unsigned type<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Small signed sensor value<\/td><td>int16_t<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Code must run on Uno and ESP32<\/td><td>Fixed-width types only<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Might exceed 4 billion<\/td><td>uint64_t<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>Printing: the simplest portable way is to cast, for example printf(\"%lu\", (unsigned long)millis_now) and printf(\"%d\", (int)led_state).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes-11\">Common mistakes<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Mistake<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Fix<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Missing volatile on a flag shared with an ISR<\/td><td>Add volatile; test with optimisation on (-O2)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Believing volatile makes access atomic<\/td><td>Disable interrupts or use atomic operations for multi-byte data<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Using int for registers<\/td><td>Use uintN_t of the exact register width<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Declaring a lookup table without const (wastes RAM)<\/td><td>Mark constant tables const<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Mixing signed and unsigned comparisons<\/td><td>Keep both sides the same signedness<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Printing uint32_t with %d<\/td><td>Cast to unsigned long and use %lu, or use PRIu32<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"practice-programs-type-each-one-yourself-11\">Practice programs (type each one yourself)<\/h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write a program with a flag changed by a signal handler or timer. Remove volatile, compile with -O2 and observe.<\/li>\n<li>Print sizeof for char, short, int, long, long long and every stdint type on your PC and note differences from the Uno.<\/li>\n<li>Declare a const lookup table of 10 values and print it.<\/li>\n<li>Write a function with const char * that counts vowels; try to modify the string and read the compiler error.<\/li>\n<li>Make a table of the range of every stdint type using the limits macros (INT8_MIN, UINT32_MAX ...).<\/li>\n<li>Explain in one paragraph why a status register needs const volatile.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"interview-questions-with-short-answers-11\">Interview questions with short answers<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Q1. What does volatile do? It forbids the compiler from caching or removing reads and writes of that variable, because it may change outside the program flow.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q2. Give three uses of volatile in embedded systems. Hardware registers, ISR-shared variables, memory shared with DMA or other tasks.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q3. Can a variable be const and volatile? Yes: read-only to your code but changed by hardware, such as a status register.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q4. Does volatile guarantee atomicity? No. Multi-byte accesses can still be interrupted.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q5. Why use uint8_t instead of unsigned char? Clear intent and a guaranteed 8-bit width.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q6. Where does const data live on a microcontroller? Usually in flash (read-only memory), which saves RAM.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q7. What is size_t? The unsigned type returned by sizeof, big enough to index any object.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>TOPIC 12<\/strong><\/p>\n<h1 id=\"memory-layout-startup-code-padding-and-endianness\">Memory Layout, Startup Code, Padding and Endianness<\/h1>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>In one line<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Knowing where each variable lives (flash, RAM, stack, heap), what runs before main(), and how bytes are ordered explains many embedded bugs and interview questions.<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"12-1-two-kinds-of-memory-in-a-microcontroller\">12.1 Two kinds of memory in a microcontroller<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><\/th><th><strong>Flash (ROM)<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>RAM (SRAM)<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Keeps data without power<\/td><td>Yes<\/td><td>No<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Writable at run time<\/td><td>Slow and limited; used for firmware<\/td><td>Yes, fast<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Holds<\/td><td>Program code, constants<\/td><td>Variables, stack, heap<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Typical size<\/td><td>ESP32 4 MB, Uno 32 KB<\/td><td>ESP32 about 520 KB, Uno 2 KB<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>RAM is precious. Every wasted byte matters on a chip with 2 KB.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"12-2-memory-sections-of-a-c-program\">12.2 Memory sections of a C program<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Section<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>What goes in<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Lives in<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>.text<\/code><\/td><td>Machine code (your functions)<\/td><td>Flash<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>.rodata<\/code><\/td><td>const data and string literals<\/td><td>Flash<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>.data<\/code><\/td><td>Global and static variables with a non-zero initial value<\/td><td>Flash (initial values) copied to RAM<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>.bss<\/code><\/td><td>Global and static variables initialised to zero or not initialised<\/td><td>RAM (zeroed at start)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>Heap<\/code><\/td><td>malloc memory; grows upward<\/td><td>RAM<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>Stack<\/code><\/td><td>Locals, return addresses; grows downward<\/td><td>RAM<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;\n \nint counter = 5;            // .data  (initialised, non-zero)\nint total;                  // .bss   (uninitialised, zeroed)\nconst int LIMIT = 100;      // .rodata (flash)\nstatic int hidden = 0;      // .bss   (static, zero)\n \nvoid demo(void) {\n    int local = 1;                    // stack\n    static int calls = 0;             // .bss (static local)\n    int *p = malloc(16);              // pointer on stack, 16 bytes on heap\n    char *msg = \"hello\";              // pointer on stack, text in .rodata\n    free(p);\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Typical RAM picture (high address at the top):<\/p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Region<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Behaviour<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Stack (top of RAM)<\/td><td>Grows downward as functions are called<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>... free gap ...<\/td><td>If stack and heap meet, the program crashes<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Heap<\/td><td>Grows upward as malloc is used<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>.bss<\/td><td>Zero-initialised variables<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>.data (bottom of RAM)<\/td><td>Initialised variables, copied from flash<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"12-3-what-happens-before-main-runs\">12.3 What happens before main() runs<\/h2>\n<p>The CPU does not start at main. After power-on or reset it runs startup code:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>The CPU reads the vector table at a fixed address: the first entry is the initial stack pointer, the second is the address of the Reset handler.<\/li>\n<li>Reset_Handler starts. It sets up the stack pointer.<\/li>\n<li>It copies the initial values of .data from flash to RAM.<\/li>\n<li>It fills .bss with zeros. (This is why uninitialised globals are 0.)<\/li>\n<li>It sets up the clock and basic hardware (SystemInit on ARM).<\/li>\n<li>It calls main().<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>The linker script (.ld file) decides the addresses of flash and RAM and where each section goes. You rarely write it as a beginner, but you should know it exists. In Arduino and ESP-IDF these steps are hidden inside the core.<\/p>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>Why interviewers ask this<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>It shows you understand why a global initialised to 5 survives reset, why uninitialised globals are zero, and why local variables contain garbage.<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"12-4-stack-overflow\">12.4 Stack overflow<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Each function call and each local array uses stack. Large local arrays (char buf[2000]) or deep recursion can exhaust a small stack.<\/li>\n<li>The stack then overwrites other data, causing random crashes that are very hard to debug.<\/li>\n<li>Fixes: make big buffers global or static, avoid recursion, check stack usage (FreeRTOS: uxTaskGetStackHighWaterMark), increase task stack size.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"12-5-structure-padding-recap-with-detail\">12.5 Structure padding (recap with detail)<\/h2>\n<p>Processors prefer a 4-byte value at an address divisible by 4. The compiler adds padding to guarantee alignment.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>struct A { char a; int b; char c; };   // offsets: a=0, b=4, c=8, size 12\nstruct B { int b; char a; char c; };   // offsets: b=0, a=4, c=5, size 8\n \n// remove padding (slower access, not portable, may fault on some CPUs)\nstruct __attribute__((packed)) P { char a; int b; char c; };   // size 6<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Packed structs are useful for matching a communication packet byte by byte, but unaligned access can be slow or even cause a hardware fault on some ARM cores. Prefer ordering members largest first.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"12-6-endianness\">12.6 Endianness<\/h2>\n<p>A 32-bit number takes 4 bytes. Endianness is the order in which those bytes are stored.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><\/th><th><strong>Little-endian<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Big-endian<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Stores first<\/td><td>Least significant byte<\/td><td>Most significant byte<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>0x12345678 in memory<\/td><td>78 56 34 12<\/td><td>12 34 56 78<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Used by<\/td><td>x86 PCs, ESP32, ESP8266, AVR, most ARM Cortex-M<\/td><td>Network byte order, some old CPUs<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>Analogy: writing a date<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Little-endian is like writing 29-10-2026 (small unit first). Big-endian is like 2026-10-29 (biggest unit first). Both hold the same date; both sides must agree on the order.<\/p><\/aside>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;stdint.h&gt;\n \nint main(void) {\n    uint32_t x = 0x12345678;\n    uint8_t *p = (uint8_t *)&amp;x;      // look at the first byte in memory\n \n    if (*p == 0x78) printf(\"Little-endian\\n\");\n    else            printf(\"Big-endian\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong><\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Little-endian<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Endianness matters when you send multi-byte values over UART, I2C, SPI or a network, or read a binary file made on another system. Convert explicitly, for example by sending the high byte first: tx[0] = (value &gt;&gt; 8) &amp; 0xFF; tx[1] = value &amp; 0xFF;<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes-12\">Common mistakes<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Mistake<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Fix<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Assuming local variables start at 0<\/td><td>They hold garbage; only globals and statics are zeroed<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Large arrays declared as locals<\/td><td>Make them static or global, or shrink them<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Assuming sizeof(struct) equals the sum of members<\/td><td>Print it, and order members by size<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Sending a struct directly over a network or SPI<\/td><td>Serialise field by field with a fixed byte order<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Ignoring heap and stack collision on tiny RAM<\/td><td>Track free memory and avoid dynamic allocation<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Assuming every system is little-endian<\/td><td>Convert explicitly when data crosses systems<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"practice-programs-type-each-one-yourself-12\">Practice programs (type each one yourself)<\/h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Print the addresses of a global, a static, a local, a malloc block and a function; compare which are high and which are low.<\/li>\n<li>Run the endianness program on your PC and, with Serial.print, on your ESP32.<\/li>\n<li>Print sizeof and the offset of each member (offsetof from stddef.h) for struct A and B.<\/li>\n<li>Write functions to convert a uint32_t to 4 bytes in big-endian and little-endian order, and back.<\/li>\n<li>Run recursion until the stack overflows on your PC and note the depth.<\/li>\n<li>Use size or arm-none-eabi-size (or the Arduino build output) to read how much flash and RAM your sketch uses.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"interview-questions-with-short-answers-12\">Interview questions with short answers<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Q1. What are .text, .data and .bss? .text is code, .data is initialised globals, .bss is zero-initialised globals.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q2. Why are uninitialised globals zero? Startup code clears the .bss section before main.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q3. Where is a const global stored on a microcontroller? In flash (.rodata), not in RAM.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q4. What happens before main()? Reset handler: set stack pointer, copy .data, clear .bss, init clocks, call main.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q5. What is stack overflow? The stack grows into other memory because of deep calls or big locals.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q6. What is endianness? Which is ESP32? It is the byte order of multi-byte values; the ESP32 is little-endian.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q7. What is structure padding and how do you avoid wasting memory? Alignment gaps between members; order members from largest to smallest.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>TOPIC 13<\/strong><\/p>\n<h1 id=\"registers-memory-mapped-i-o-and-function-pointers\">Registers, Memory-Mapped I/O and Function Pointers<\/h1>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>In one line<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>In a microcontroller, peripherals (GPIO, timers, UART) are controlled by writing numbers to special memory addresses called registers; C does this with volatile pointers to fixed addresses.<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"13-1-what-is-a-register\">13.1 What is a register?<\/h2>\n<p>A register is a small memory location, 8, 16 or 32 bits wide, that is connected to hardware instead of ordinary RAM. Writing to it changes hardware behaviour; reading it tells you the hardware state.<\/p>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>Analogy: the control panel of a machine<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Every switch and gauge on the panel has a fixed position. Memory-mapped I/O means each switch and gauge has a fixed memory address. Writing to the address flips the switch; reading the address reads the gauge.<\/p><\/aside>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Register type<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Purpose<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Control<\/td><td>Configure the peripheral (enable it, set mode, pick speed)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Status<\/td><td>Hardware reports state (data ready, buffer empty, error)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Data<\/td><td>Hold the data to send or the data received<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Direction (GPIO)<\/td><td>Choose input or output per pin<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Interrupt enable / flag<\/td><td>Turn interrupts on and show which one fired<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"13-2-accessing-a-fixed-address\">13.2 Accessing a fixed address<\/h2>\n<p>The pattern is: cast the address to a pointer to volatile, then dereference.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>// NOTE: 0x40020014 is an example address. Real addresses come from\n// the chip's reference manual or its header file.\n#define GPIOA_ODR   (*(volatile uint32_t *)0x40020014)\n \nGPIOA_ODR |=  (1U &lt;&lt; 5);     // set pin 5 high\nGPIOA_ODR &amp;= ~(1U &lt;&lt; 5);     // set pin 5 low\nGPIOA_ODR ^=  (1U &lt;&lt; 5);     // toggle pin 5<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Read the macro from the inside out: 0x40020014 is a number; (volatile uint32_t *) says \"treat it as the address of a volatile 32-bit value\"; the leading * says \"the value at that address\". The macro therefore behaves like a variable that is really hardware.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"13-3-grouping-registers-in-a-struct\">13.3 Grouping registers in a struct<\/h2>\n<p>A peripheral usually has many registers in a row. A struct that matches the layout lets you name them (this is what vendor headers like STM32 CMSIS do).<\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>typedef struct {\n    volatile uint32_t MODER;     // offset 0x00 : pin mode (input/output)\n    volatile uint32_t OTYPER;    // offset 0x04\n    volatile uint32_t OSPEEDR;   // offset 0x08\n    volatile uint32_t PUPDR;     // offset 0x0C\n    volatile uint32_t IDR;       // offset 0x10 : input data\n    volatile uint32_t ODR;       // offset 0x14 : output data\n} GPIO_TypeDef;\n \n#define GPIOA ((GPIO_TypeDef *)0x40020000)\n \nGPIOA-&gt;MODER |= (1U &lt;&lt; (5 * 2));   // pin 5 as output (2 bits per pin)\nGPIOA-&gt;ODR   ^= (1U &lt;&lt; 5);          // toggle pin 5\nif (GPIOA-&gt;IDR &amp; (1U &lt;&lt; 0)) { /* pin 0 is high */ }<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"13-4-practice-safely-simulate-a-register-on-a-pc\">13.4 Practice safely: simulate a register on a PC<\/h2>\n<p>You can test all the bit logic without hardware by pointing at a normal variable.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;stdint.h&gt;\n \nstatic volatile uint32_t fake_odr = 0;          // pretend hardware register\n#define ODR (*(&amp;fake_odr))\n \n#define LED_PIN 5\n \nint main(void) {\n    ODR |= (1U &lt;&lt; LED_PIN);                      // LED on\n    printf(\"ODR = 0x%08X\\n\", (unsigned)ODR);\n    ODR ^= (1U &lt;&lt; LED_PIN);                      // toggle -&gt; off\n    printf(\"ODR = 0x%08X\\n\", (unsigned)ODR);\n    return 0;\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong><\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>ODR = 0x00000020\nODR = 0x00000000<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"13-5-read-modify-write-and-its-risks\">13.5 Read-modify-write and its risks<\/h2>\n<p>ODR |= mask looks like one operation, but the CPU does three: read the register, change the bits, write it back. If an interrupt changes the same register between the read and the write, its change is lost. Solutions: disable interrupts around it, or use hardware set and clear registers (BSRR on STM32, set/clear registers on ESP32 such as GPIO_OUT_W1TS and GPIO_OUT_W1TC) that change a bit with one write.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"13-6-function-pointers-and-callbacks\">13.6 Function pointers and callbacks<\/h2>\n<p>A function pointer stores the address of a function. Embedded software uses them to let low-level drivers call back into your application code.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>typedef void (*ButtonCallback)(void);\n \nstatic ButtonCallback on_press = NULL;\n \nvoid button_register(ButtonCallback cb) {      // driver API\n    on_press = cb;\n}\n \nvoid button_isr(void) {                        // driver calls your code\n    if (on_press != NULL) on_press();\n}\n \nvoid my_handler(void) { printf(\"Button pressed!\\n\"); }   // application code\n \nint main(void) {\n    button_register(my_handler);\n    button_isr();                               // simulate an interrupt\n    return 0;\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong><\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Button pressed!<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The driver does not need to know what your application does, and your application does not need to know how the button is read. This separation is the heart of clean driver design.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"13-7-table-of-function-pointers-jump-table\">13.7 Table of function pointers (jump table)<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>void cmd_on(void)  { printf(\"LED on\\n\"); }\nvoid cmd_off(void) { printf(\"LED off\\n\"); }\nvoid cmd_blink(void) { printf(\"LED blink\\n\"); }\n \nvoid (*commands[])(void) = { cmd_on, cmd_off, cmd_blink };\n \nint index = 1;                          // received from UART, for example\nif (index &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; index &lt; 3)            // validate before using\n    commands[index]();                  // calls cmd_off<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong><\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>LED off<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The interrupt vector table on an ARM chip is the same idea: an array of function addresses, one for each interrupt source.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes-13\">Common mistakes<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Mistake<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Fix<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Forgetting volatile on register pointers<\/td><td>Always cast to volatile<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Using the wrong width (uint8_t for a 32-bit register)<\/td><td>Match the register width in the datasheet<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Overwriting a register with = instead of |= or &amp;=<\/td><td>Change only the bits you intend<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Using an address from memory or the wrong chip<\/td><td>Take addresses from the reference manual or vendor headers<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Calling a NULL function pointer<\/td><td>Check for NULL, or provide a default handler<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Index out of range in a function pointer table<\/td><td>Validate the index before calling<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"practice-programs-type-each-one-yourself-13\">Practice programs (type each one yourself)<\/h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Simulate a GPIO output register with a variable and write set, clear, toggle functions.<\/li>\n<li>Create a struct that overlays a fake UART with CTRL, STATUS and DATA registers and write init and send functions that use it.<\/li>\n<li>Write a menu program with an array of function pointers (add, sub, mul, div).<\/li>\n<li>Implement a button driver with a callback and a main program that registers two different handlers.<\/li>\n<li>Find the register definitions for your board (ESP32 or Arduino) and write down the address of GPIO output registers.<\/li>\n<li>Blink pin 13 on an Uno using DDRB and PORTB only (no pinMode or digitalWrite).<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"interview-questions-with-short-answers-13\">Interview questions with short answers<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Q1. What is memory-mapped I/O? Peripheral registers are placed in the normal address space, so you control hardware with ordinary reads and writes.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q2. Explain *(volatile uint32_t *)0x40020014 = 1; Treat the number as the address of a volatile 32-bit value and write 1 there.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q3. Why must register pointers be volatile? The hardware can change the value and every access has an effect, so the compiler must not cache or remove accesses.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q4. Why is read-modify-write dangerous? An interrupt between the read and the write can be overwritten; use atomic set/clear registers or disable interrupts.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q5. What is a callback? A function you pass to a driver so that the driver calls it when an event happens.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q6. What is the interrupt vector table? An array of function addresses, one per interrupt source, used by the CPU to jump to the right handler.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>TOPIC 14<\/strong><\/p>\n<h1 id=\"gpio-and-debouncing\">GPIO and Debouncing<\/h1>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>In one line<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>GPIO (General Purpose Input/Output) pins let the microcontroller read buttons and drive LEDs; debouncing removes the false presses caused by a mechanical switch's contacts bouncing.<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"14-1-what-is-a-gpio-pin\">14.1 What is a GPIO pin?<\/h2>\n<p>A pin you can configure in software as an input (read high or low) or an output (drive high or low). High means close to the supply voltage (3.3 V on ESP32 and ESP8266, 5 V on Arduino Uno); low means 0 V.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Mode<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Meaning<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Input<\/td><td>Pin listens. Reads 1 if voltage is high, 0 if low<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Input with pull-up<\/td><td>Internal resistor pulls the pin to high when nothing drives it<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Input with pull-down<\/td><td>Internal resistor pulls the pin to low when nothing drives it<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Output push-pull<\/td><td>Pin actively drives both high and low (normal LED output)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Output open-drain<\/td><td>Pin can only pull low; a pull-up resistor makes high (I2C uses this)<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"14-2-floating-inputs-and-pull-up-resistors\">14.2 Floating inputs and pull-up resistors<\/h2>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>Analogy: a door that needs a spring<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>A door with no spring flaps open and shut in the wind. An input pin that is connected to nothing (floating) picks up noise and reads random 0s and 1s. A pull-up or pull-down resistor is the spring that holds the pin in a known state.<\/p><\/aside>\n<p>Common button wiring: connect the button between the pin and GND and enable the internal pull-up. Not pressed: pin reads HIGH. Pressed: pin reads LOW. This is called active-low and needs no external resistor.<\/p>\n<aside class=\"callout warn\" style=\"--c:#C0392B\"><p><strong>ESP32 pin note<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>GPIO 34 to 39 on the ESP32 are input only and have no internal pull-up or pull-down. Use an external resistor there. Also avoid pins used for boot (such as GPIO 0, 2, 12 and 15) for buttons until you read the datasheet.<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"14-3-output-driving-an-led\">14.3 Output: driving an LED<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>const int LED_PIN = 2;\n \nvoid setup() {\n    pinMode(LED_PIN, OUTPUT);\n}\n \nvoid loop() {\n    digitalWrite(LED_PIN, HIGH);\n    delay(500);\n    digitalWrite(LED_PIN, LOW);\n    delay(500);\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Always use a series resistor with an external LED. Example: supply 3.3 V, LED drop 2.0 V, wanted current 10 mA. R = (3.3 - 2.0) / 0.010 = 130 ohm. Use the next standard value, 150 or 220 ohm. Without a resistor the LED (or the pin) burns out.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Keep the current per pin small (a few mA up to about 10 mA is a safe habit). Check the datasheet limit.<\/li>\n<li>For motors, relays and solenoids, never connect them directly to a pin. Use a transistor, MOSFET or driver module (L298, ULN2003) with a separate supply and a flyback diode.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"14-4-input-reading-a-button\">14.4 Input: reading a button<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>const int BTN_PIN = 4;\nconst int LED_PIN = 2;\n \nvoid setup() {\n    pinMode(BTN_PIN, INPUT_PULLUP);    // pressed = LOW\n    pinMode(LED_PIN, OUTPUT);\n}\n \nvoid loop() {\n    if (digitalRead(BTN_PIN) == LOW)   // button pressed\n        digitalWrite(LED_PIN, HIGH);\n    else\n        digitalWrite(LED_PIN, LOW);\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"14-5-switch-bounce\">14.5 Switch bounce<\/h2>\n<p>A mechanical button is two pieces of metal. When they touch, they vibrate and make and break contact many times for about 1 to 20 milliseconds before settling. The microcontroller is fast enough to see every one of these as a separate press.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>What you do<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>What the pin sees<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>What your code may think<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>One press<\/td><td>HIGH, LOW, HIGH, LOW, HIGH, LOW (settles)<\/td><td>3 presses<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>One release<\/td><td>LOW, HIGH, LOW, HIGH (settles)<\/td><td>2 releases<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>The result: a counter that jumps by 3 or a toggle that flips randomly. Debouncing makes one physical press produce exactly one event.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"14-6-software-debouncing-without-blocking\">14.6 Software debouncing without blocking<\/h2>\n<p>The simple fix delay(20) after a press blocks the whole program. A better method: accept a new value only if it has stayed the same for a debounce time, using millis() for timing.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>const int BTN_PIN = 4;\nconst unsigned long DEBOUNCE_MS = 30;\n \nint stableState   = HIGH;      // debounced state of the button\nint lastReading   = HIGH;      // raw reading from the previous loop\nunsigned long lastChange = 0;  // when the raw reading last changed\nint pressCount = 0;\n \nvoid setup() {\n    pinMode(BTN_PIN, INPUT_PULLUP);\n    Serial.begin(115200);\n}\n \nvoid loop() {\n    int reading = digitalRead(BTN_PIN);\n \n    if (reading != lastReading) {          // raw value changed: restart timer\n        lastChange = millis();\n        lastReading = reading;\n    }\n \n    if ((millis() - lastChange) &gt; DEBOUNCE_MS &amp;&amp; reading != stableState) {\n        stableState = reading;             // it stayed stable long enough\n        if (stableState == LOW) {          // falling edge = real press\n            pressCount++;\n            Serial.println(pressCount);\n        }\n    }\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The program stays free to do other work in loop() because nothing waits. The trick: millis() - lastChange measures how long the raw reading has stayed the same.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"14-7-edge-detection-act-on-the-change-not-the-level\">14.7 Edge detection: act on the change, not the level<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Level: \"the button IS pressed\". If you toggle an LED while it is pressed, it flickers thousands of times per second.<\/li>\n<li>Edge: \"the button WAS JUST pressed\". Compare the current stable state with the previous one and act once.<\/li>\n<li>Falling edge = HIGH to LOW (press with pull-up). Rising edge = LOW to HIGH (release).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"14-8-other-debounce-methods\">14.8 Other debounce methods<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Method<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Notes<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Hardware RC filter + Schmitt trigger<\/td><td>Resistor and capacitor smooth the signal; no code needed; uses parts<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Timer sampling<\/td><td>Read the pin every 5 ms in a timer interrupt; count consecutive equal samples<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Shift-register debounce<\/td><td>Shift each sample into a byte; accept a press when the byte equals 0x00 or 0xFF<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Libraries<\/td><td>Bounce2 on Arduino; but understand the method first<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes-14\">Common mistakes<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Mistake<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Fix<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Leaving an input floating<\/td><td>Enable INPUT_PULLUP or add a resistor<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Connecting an LED without a resistor<\/td><td>Calculate R = (Vsupply - Vled) / I<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Driving a motor or relay straight from a GPIO<\/td><td>Use a transistor or driver and a flyback diode<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Confusing active-low and active-high logic<\/td><td>Write down: pressed = LOW with pull-up<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Using delay() to debounce in a larger program<\/td><td>Use millis-based timing<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Feeding 5 V into a 3.3 V ESP pin<\/td><td>Use a level shifter or a voltage divider<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"practice-programs-type-each-one-yourself-14\">Practice programs (type each one yourself)<\/h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Blink an LED with delay, then without delay using millis.<\/li>\n<li>Turn an LED on while a button is held, using INPUT_PULLUP.<\/li>\n<li>Toggle an LED on each press with edge detection; first without debouncing and watch it misbehave.<\/li>\n<li>Add software debouncing and count presses on the serial monitor.<\/li>\n<li>Make a 4-LED chaser; a button changes direction.<\/li>\n<li>Detect a short press and a long press (over 1 second) on the same button.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"interview-questions-with-short-answers-14\">Interview questions with short answers<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Q1. What is a pull-up resistor? A resistor that holds an input at a high level when nothing else drives it.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q2. What is a floating pin? An unconnected or undriven input that reads random values from noise.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q3. Push-pull vs open-drain? Push-pull drives both high and low; open-drain only pulls low and needs an external pull-up.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q4. What is switch bounce and how do you fix it? Contacts make and break rapidly for milliseconds; fix with a hardware RC filter or software timing.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q5. Why not use delay() for debouncing? It blocks the processor, so other tasks stop.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q6. How do you calculate an LED resistor? R = (supply voltage - LED forward voltage) / desired current.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>TOPIC 15<\/strong><\/p>\n<h1 id=\"interrupts-and-isr\">Interrupts and ISR<\/h1>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>In one line<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>An interrupt makes the CPU pause its normal work, run a short function (ISR) to deal with an urgent event, and then continue exactly where it stopped.<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"15-1-polling-vs-interrupts\">15.1 Polling vs interrupts<\/h2>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>Analogy: waiting for a visitor<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Polling is walking to the door every few seconds to see if someone is there. An interrupt is a doorbell: you work normally, and when it rings you go to the door, then return to your work.<\/p><\/aside>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><\/th><th><strong>Polling<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Interrupt<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Reaction time<\/td><td>Depends on how often you check<\/td><td>Very fast, hardware triggered<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>CPU use<\/td><td>Wastes time checking<\/td><td>Uses CPU only when needed<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Complexity<\/td><td>Simple<\/td><td>Needs care (shared data, timing)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Good for<\/td><td>Slow, non-urgent inputs<\/td><td>Urgent events, short pulses, communication, wake from sleep<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"15-2-what-happens-when-an-interrupt-fires\">15.2 What happens when an interrupt fires<\/h2>\n<ol>\n<li>A peripheral raises an interrupt request (button edge, timer overflow, byte received).<\/li>\n<li>If interrupts are enabled and the priority is high enough, the CPU finishes the current instruction.<\/li>\n<li>The CPU saves the context: program counter and key registers are pushed onto the stack.<\/li>\n<li>It looks up the address of the handler in the interrupt vector table and jumps to the ISR.<\/li>\n<li>The ISR runs and clears the interrupt flag if required.<\/li>\n<li>The CPU restores the saved context and continues the main program as if nothing happened.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>The delay between the event and the first ISR instruction is the interrupt latency. Keeping ISRs short keeps latency for other interrupts low.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"15-3-the-golden-rules-of-an-isr\">15.3 The golden rules of an ISR<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Rule<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Reason<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Keep it short and fast<\/td><td>While an ISR runs, other work and (often) other interrupts wait<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>No delay(), no waiting loops<\/td><td>The timer that delay depends on may be blocked; the system freezes<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>No printf or Serial.print<\/td><td>They are slow, may use interrupts themselves and are not safe here<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>No malloc / free<\/td><td>Not re-entrant and unpredictable in time<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Do not call functions that block<\/td><td>Anything that waits can hang the whole program<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Share data through volatile variables<\/td><td>Otherwise the compiler may never see the change<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Set a flag or push data to a buffer, process in main<\/td><td>Work in the ISR, think in main<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"15-4-example-button-interrupt-with-a-flag\">15.4 Example: button interrupt with a flag<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>const int BTN_PIN = 4;\nvolatile bool buttonFlag = false;          // shared with the ISR: volatile\nvolatile unsigned long lastIsrTime = 0;\n \nvoid IRAM_ATTR onButton() {                // ESP32: ISR must live in IRAM\n    unsigned long now = millis();\n    if (now - lastIsrTime &gt; 50) {          // ignore bounces within 50 ms\n        buttonFlag = true;                 // just set a flag\n        lastIsrTime = now;\n    }\n}\n \nvoid setup() {\n    Serial.begin(115200);\n    pinMode(BTN_PIN, INPUT_PULLUP);\n    attachInterrupt(digitalPinToInterrupt(BTN_PIN), onButton, FALLING);\n}\n \nvoid loop() {\n    if (buttonFlag) {                      // main code does the real work\n        buttonFlag = false;\n        Serial.println(\"Button pressed\");\n    }\n    // other tasks keep running here\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<ul>\n<li>IRAM_ATTR is for ESP32 and ESP8266 (ICACHE_RAM_ATTR on older ESP8266 cores). It keeps the ISR in fast RAM so it can run while flash is busy. On an Arduino Uno you omit it.<\/li>\n<li>FALLING, RISING, CHANGE choose which edge triggers the interrupt. LOW triggers repeatedly while the pin is low.<\/li>\n<li>On an Uno only pins 2 and 3 support external interrupts. On ESP32 almost every GPIO does.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"15-5-race-conditions-and-atomic-access\">15.5 Race conditions and atomic access<\/h2>\n<p>A race condition happens when main code and an ISR use the same data and the result depends on timing.<\/p>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>Example of the problem<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>An 8-bit AVR reads a 16-bit variable in two steps. If an interrupt changes the variable between the two reads, main gets the low byte of the old value and the high byte of the new value: a number that never existed.<\/p><\/aside>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>volatile uint16_t pulseCount = 0;      // incremented in an ISR\n \nuint16_t readCount(void) {\n    noInterrupts();                    // start critical section\n    uint16_t copy = pulseCount;        // read safely\n    interrupts();                      // end critical section\n    return copy;\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Keep critical sections as short as possible. On 32-bit chips a 32-bit read is a single instruction and is already atomic, but read-modify-write sequences like count++ still are not.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"15-6-priority-nesting-and-flags\">15.6 Priority, nesting and flags<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Each interrupt source has a priority. A higher priority interrupt can pre-empt a lower one if nesting is enabled (ARM Cortex-M NVIC supports it).<\/li>\n<li>Many peripherals have an interrupt flag that you must clear in the ISR; otherwise the ISR is called again immediately.<\/li>\n<li>Global enable: noInterrupts() or interrupts() on Arduino; __disable_irq() and __enable_irq() on ARM.<\/li>\n<li>Each source also has its own enable bit, so you can turn on only the interrupts you need.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"15-7-using-the-avr-timer-interrupt-uno\">15.7 Using the AVR timer interrupt (Uno)<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>volatile unsigned long ticks = 0;\n \nISR(TIMER2_COMPA_vect) {      // ISR macro from avr/interrupt.h\n    ticks++;\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The Timer topic (16) shows how to configure this timer so the ISR fires every millisecond.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes-15\">Common mistakes<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Mistake<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Fix<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Not declaring shared variables volatile<\/td><td>Add volatile for everything the ISR and main both use<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Calling Serial.print or delay inside an ISR<\/td><td>Set a flag and print in loop()<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Long processing inside the ISR<\/td><td>Store data and exit quickly; process in main<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Not debouncing a button interrupt<\/td><td>Ignore interrupts that come within a few tens of milliseconds<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Reading multi-byte shared data without protection<\/td><td>Disable interrupts briefly while copying<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Forgetting IRAM_ATTR on ESP32 ISRs<\/td><td>Add it; otherwise random crashes may occur<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"practice-programs-type-each-one-yourself-15\">Practice programs (type each one yourself)<\/h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Toggle an LED from a button interrupt using a flag handled in loop().<\/li>\n<li>Count pulses from a button or sensor in an ISR and print the count every second.<\/li>\n<li>Show the race condition: read a 16-bit counter with and without noInterrupts on an Uno.<\/li>\n<li>Measure the time between two button presses using micros() inside the ISR.<\/li>\n<li>Build a stopwatch with start and stop buttons, both using interrupts.<\/li>\n<li>Wake an ESP32 from deep sleep with a button on an RTC-capable pin.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"interview-questions-with-short-answers-15\">Interview questions with short answers<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Q1. What is an interrupt? A hardware signal that makes the CPU pause, run a handler and then resume.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q2. What is an ISR and what rules does it follow? The handler function; keep it short, no blocking or printing, use volatile shared data.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q3. Why not call printf in an ISR? It is slow, may block and is not re-entrant.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q4. What is interrupt latency? The time from the event to the first instruction of the ISR.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q5. What is a race condition? A bug where the result depends on the timing between an ISR and main code accessing shared data.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q6. Polling vs interrupt: when to use which? Polling for simple, slow, non-urgent inputs; interrupts for urgent or short events and to save power.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q7. What is the vector table? A table of handler addresses, one per interrupt source.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>TOPIC 16<\/strong><\/p>\n<h1 id=\"timers-pwm-and-adc\">Timers, PWM and ADC<\/h1>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>In one line<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>A timer counts clock ticks to measure and create precise time; PWM uses fast on-off switching to control power; an ADC converts a real-world voltage into a number.<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"16-1-timers\">16.1 Timers<\/h2>\n<p>A hardware timer is a counter that increases by one on every tick of a clock, completely independent of your program. It can raise an interrupt on overflow or when it reaches a chosen compare value.<\/p>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>Analogy: a kitchen timer<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>You set the number of minutes, go on cooking, and it rings at the right time. A hardware timer keeps counting while the CPU does other jobs.<\/p><\/aside>\n<p>Key terms:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Clock: the source, for example 16 MHz on the Uno or 80 MHz APB clock on an ESP32.<\/li>\n<li>Prescaler: divides the clock to make slower ticks. Timer clock = system clock / prescaler.<\/li>\n<li>Overflow: the counter reaches its maximum (255 for 8-bit, 65535 for 16-bit) and wraps to 0.<\/li>\n<li>Compare match: the counter equals a value you chose; used to create exact periods.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>How to calculate a period, step by step (Uno: 16 MHz clock, want 1 ms):<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Choose prescaler 64: timer clock = 16,000,000 / 64 = 250,000 ticks per second.<\/li>\n<li>One tick lasts 1 / 250,000 = 4 microseconds.<\/li>\n<li>1 ms = 1000 us needs 1000 / 4 = 250 ticks.<\/li>\n<li>The counter counts from 0, so set the compare value to 250 - 1 = 249.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>// Arduino Uno: Timer2 generates an interrupt every 1 ms\nvolatile unsigned long ms = 0;\n \nvoid setup() {\n    noInterrupts();\n    TCCR2A = (1 &lt;&lt; WGM21);                  // CTC mode: clear counter on match\n    TCCR2B = (1 &lt;&lt; CS22);                   // prescaler 64\n    OCR2A  = 249;                           // compare value\n    TIMSK2 = (1 &lt;&lt; OCIE2A);                 // enable compare-match interrupt\n    interrupts();\n}\n \nISR(TIMER2_COMPA_vect) {\n    ms++;                                   // runs every 1 ms\n}\n \nvoid loop() {\n    // use ms as a millisecond clock here\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This register-level code is for the Uno (AVR). Using Timer2 leaves Timer0 free for millis() and delay(), but tone() also uses Timer2. On ESP32 and ESP8266 the timer API differs between core versions, so check the documentation of the core you installed. The calculation is the same on every chip.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"16-2-non-blocking-timing-with-millis\">16.2 Non-blocking timing with millis()<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>unsigned long previous = 0;\nconst unsigned long INTERVAL = 1000;     // 1 second\nbool ledOn = false;\n \nvoid loop() {\n    unsigned long now = millis();\n    if (now - previous &gt;= INTERVAL) {    // subtraction is safe when millis() wraps\n        previous = now;\n        ledOn = !ledOn;\n        digitalWrite(2, ledOn);\n    }\n    // loop keeps running: read sensors, check buttons...\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"16-3-pwm-pulse-width-modulation\">16.3 PWM: Pulse Width Modulation<\/h2>\n<p>PWM switches a pin on and off very fast. The fraction of time it is ON is the duty cycle. A load that cannot follow such fast changes (an LED to the eye, a motor with inertia) sees the average.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Duty cycle<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Average voltage (3.3 V)<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Effect<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>0%<\/td><td>0 V<\/td><td>Always off<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>25%<\/td><td>0.83 V<\/td><td>Dim LED, slow motor<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>50%<\/td><td>1.65 V<\/td><td>Half brightness or speed<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>75%<\/td><td>2.48 V<\/td><td>Bright<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>100%<\/td><td>3.3 V<\/td><td>Always on<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<ul>\n<li>Duty cycle (%) = ON time / total period x 100.<\/li>\n<li>Frequency = 1 / period. LED dimming works well above about 500 Hz (no visible flicker). Motors often use 1 to 20 kHz.<\/li>\n<li>Resolution = number of steps. 8-bit PWM has 256 levels (0 to 255); 10-bit has 1024.<\/li>\n<li>Arduino Uno: analogWrite(pin, 0 to 255) on PWM pins 3, 5, 6, 9, 10, 11 (marked with ~).<\/li>\n<li>ESP32: the LEDC peripheral gives PWM on any output pin. The function names changed between Arduino-ESP32 core versions 2 and 3, so follow the documentation for your installed version.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>// Arduino Uno: fade an LED on pin 9\nint brightness = 0;\nint step = 5;\n \nvoid setup() { pinMode(9, OUTPUT); }\n \nvoid loop() {\n    analogWrite(9, brightness);            // 0 = off, 255 = full\n    brightness += step;\n    if (brightness &lt;= 0 || brightness &gt;= 255) step = -step;\n    delay(20);\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Servo motors use a specific PWM: 50 Hz (20 ms period) with a pulse of 1 ms (one end), 1.5 ms (centre) and 2 ms (other end). Use a Servo library so the timing is exact.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"16-4-adc-analog-to-digital-converter\">16.4 ADC: Analog to Digital Converter<\/h2>\n<p>The world is analog: temperature, light, sound and battery level are smoothly changing voltages. The CPU understands numbers. The ADC measures a voltage and returns a number.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Board<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Resolution<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Count range<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Input range<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Arduino Uno<\/td><td>10 bit<\/td><td>0 to 1023<\/td><td>0 to 5 V<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>ESP32<\/td><td>12 bit<\/td><td>0 to 4095<\/td><td>about 0 to 3.3 V<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>ESP8266 (NodeMCU A0)<\/td><td>10 bit<\/td><td>0 to 1023<\/td><td>0 to 3.3 V (board divider)<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>Conversion formula (using the full-scale count):<\/p>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>voltage = count x Vref / maxCount<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Example (Uno): count 512, Vref 5 V, max 1023: voltage = 512 x 5 / 1023 = 2.50 V.<\/p>\n<p>Resolution (step size) = Vref / 2^N. A 10-bit ADC at 5 V has steps of about 4.9 mV; a 12-bit ADC at 3.3 V has steps of about 0.8 mV.<\/p><\/aside>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>// Read an LM35 temperature sensor (10 mV per degree C) on an Uno\nconst int SENSOR = A0;\n \nvoid setup() { Serial.begin(9600); }\n \nvoid loop() {\n    int count = analogRead(SENSOR);               // 0 to 1023\n    float volts = count * 5.0f / 1023.0f;\n    float tempC = volts * 100.0f;                 // 10 mV per degree\n    Serial.println(tempC);\n    delay(1000);\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"16-5-getting-clean-adc-readings\">16.5 Getting clean ADC readings<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Noise: average several samples (for example 16) or use a moving average.<\/li>\n<li>Keep analog wires short, and add a 0.1 uF capacitor close to the sensor.<\/li>\n<li>Reference voltage: the result is only as accurate as Vref. A battery-powered board can have a drifting 5 V or 3.3 V supply.<\/li>\n<li>ESP32 notes: the ADC is not perfectly linear (use calibration functions), and ADC2 channels cannot be used while Wi-Fi is on. Prefer ADC1 pins (GPIO 32 to 39) in Wi-Fi projects.<\/li>\n<li>Never apply more than the maximum input voltage (3.3 V on ESP boards) to an ADC pin. Use a voltage divider for higher voltages.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>int readAveraged(int pin, int samples) {\n    long sum = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; samples; i++) {\n        sum += analogRead(pin);\n        delay(2);\n    }\n    return (int)(sum / samples);\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes-16\">Common mistakes<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Mistake<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Fix<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Using delay() for all timing<\/td><td>Use millis() or hardware timers<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Forgetting that counters start at 0 (compare value off by one)<\/td><td>Use ticks - 1<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Comparing millis() values as absolute times<\/td><td>Use now - previous &gt;= interval<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Expecting analogWrite to give a true analog voltage<\/td><td>It is PWM; add an RC filter if you need a smooth voltage<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Feeding more than 3.3 V into an ESP ADC pin<\/td><td>Use a voltage divider<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Using int for a time that overflows quickly<\/td><td>Use unsigned long<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"practice-programs-type-each-one-yourself-16\">Practice programs (type each one yourself)<\/h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Calculate by hand the compare value for a 10 ms timer at 16 MHz with prescaler 1024.<\/li>\n<li>Blink two LEDs at different rates using millis() with no delay().<\/li>\n<li>Fade an LED with PWM and display the duty cycle percentage on the serial monitor.<\/li>\n<li>Control the LED brightness with a potentiometer (ADC input, PWM output).<\/li>\n<li>Read a temperature or light sensor, average 16 samples, and print the voltage and value.<\/li>\n<li>Make a simple voltmeter that reads 0 to 3.3 V through a divider and prints the real input voltage.<\/li>\n<li>Drive a hobby servo to 0, 90 and 180 degrees.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"interview-questions-with-short-answers-16\">Interview questions with short answers<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Q1. What is a prescaler? A divider that slows the clock fed to a timer so it can count longer periods.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q2. How do you get a 1 ms interrupt from a 16 MHz clock? Prescaler 64 gives 250 kHz; count 250 ticks; compare value 249 in CTC mode.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q3. What is PWM and what is duty cycle? Fast on-off switching; duty cycle is the percentage of the period the signal is high.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q4. How is the average voltage of a PWM signal calculated? Duty cycle x supply voltage.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q5. What is the resolution of a 12-bit ADC with 3.3 V reference? 3.3 / 4096 which is about 0.8 mV per step.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q6. Why use millis() instead of delay()? millis() lets the program do other tasks while waiting.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q7. How can you reduce ADC noise? Average multiple samples, add a capacitor, keep wires short and use a stable reference.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>TOPIC 17<\/strong><\/p>\n<h1 id=\"communication-protocols-uart-i2c-and-spi\">Communication Protocols: UART, I2C and SPI<\/h1>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>In one line<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Protocols are agreed rules for sending bytes between chips: UART is a simple two-wire serial link, I2C connects many devices on two shared wires, and SPI is a fast four-wire link.<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"17-1-serial-vs-parallel-sync-vs-async\">17.1 Serial vs parallel, sync vs async<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Serial: bits are sent one after another on a single wire. Fewer wires, so it is used almost everywhere.<\/li>\n<li>Asynchronous (UART): no shared clock wire; both sides agree on the speed (baud rate) in advance.<\/li>\n<li>Synchronous (I2C, SPI): a clock wire tells the receiver exactly when to read each bit.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"17-2-uart\">17.2 UART<\/h2>\n<p>UART (Universal Asynchronous Receiver Transmitter) connects exactly two devices. It is what the Serial Monitor, GPS modules, Bluetooth modules (HC-05) and many sensors use.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Wire<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Connection<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>TX<\/td><td>Transmit of device A goes to RX of device B<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>RX<\/td><td>Receive of device A comes from TX of device B (so TX and RX are crossed)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>GND<\/td><td>Common ground is mandatory<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>Frame format 8N1 (the most common): when idle the line is HIGH. One byte is sent as: 1 start bit (LOW), 8 data bits (least significant bit first), no parity, 1 stop bit (HIGH). That is 10 bits for 8 bits of data.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Baud rate = bits per second. Common: 9600, 115200.<\/li>\n<li>Time for one byte at 9600 baud = 10 / 9600 = about 1.04 ms. So 9600 baud moves roughly 960 bytes per second.<\/li>\n<li>Both devices must use the same baud rate and format, otherwise you see garbage characters.<\/li>\n<li>Voltage levels must match: do not connect 5 V TX to a 3.3 V RX without a level shifter or divider.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>void setup() {\n    Serial.begin(115200);                  // must match the Serial Monitor\n}\n \nvoid loop() {\n    if (Serial.available() &gt; 0) {          // a byte has arrived\n        char c = Serial.read();\n        Serial.print(\"Got: \");\n        Serial.println(c);\n    }\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Receiving commands properly: build a line in a buffer until you see a newline, then parse it. Check buffer length so a long message cannot overflow it.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>char line[32];\nuint8_t len = 0;\n \nvoid loop() {\n    while (Serial.available() &gt; 0) {\n        char c = Serial.read();\n        if (c == '\\n') {\n            line[len] = '\\0';\n            handle_command(line);          // e.g. compare with strcmp\n            len = 0;\n        } else if (len &lt; sizeof(line) - 1) {\n            line[len++] = c;               // protect against overflow\n        }\n    }\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"17-3-i2c\">17.3 I2C<\/h2>\n<p>I2C (Inter-Integrated Circuit, \"I squared C\") uses just two shared wires for many devices: SDA (data) and SCL (clock). One master (your microcontroller) talks to several slaves (sensors, displays, EEPROM). Each slave has a 7-bit address.<\/p>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>Analogy: a classroom<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The teacher (master) calls a student by name (the address) and asks a question. Only the student whose name was called answers. Everyone shares the same room (the two wires).<\/p><\/aside>\n<ul>\n<li>Both lines are open-drain, so each needs a pull-up resistor to the supply (typically 4.7 kohm; 2.2 to 10 kohm is the usual range). Many breakout boards already have them.<\/li>\n<li>Speeds: 100 kHz standard, 400 kHz fast mode.<\/li>\n<li>A transaction: START, 7-bit address plus read/write bit, ACK from the slave, data bytes each with an ACK, STOP.<\/li>\n<li>Default pins: Uno SDA = A4, SCL = A5. ESP32 SDA = GPIO 21, SCL = GPIO 22. ESP8266 NodeMCU SDA = D2 (GPIO 4), SCL = D1 (GPIO 5).<\/li>\n<li>Two devices with the same address cannot share a bus without a multiplexer or an address pin.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>// I2C scanner: finds the addresses of all connected devices\n#include &lt;Wire.h&gt;\n \nvoid setup() {\n    Serial.begin(115200);\n    Wire.begin();\n    for (uint8_t addr = 1; addr &lt; 127; addr++) {\n        Wire.beginTransmission(addr);\n        if (Wire.endTransmission() == 0) {           // 0 = device answered (ACK)\n            Serial.print(\"Found device at 0x\");\n            Serial.println(addr, HEX);\n        }\n    }\n}\n \nvoid loop() {}<\/code><\/pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>// Read two bytes from a sensor register (generic pattern)\nWire.beginTransmission(0x48);       // device address\nWire.write(0x00);                   // register to read\nWire.endTransmission();\n \nWire.requestFrom(0x48, 2);          // ask for 2 bytes\nif (Wire.available() &gt;= 2) {\n    uint8_t hi = Wire.read();\n    uint8_t lo = Wire.read();\n    int16_t raw = (hi &lt;&lt; 8) | lo;   // combine: byte order from the datasheet\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Typical I2C devices: OLED SSD1306 (0x3C), MPU6050 (0x68), BMP280 (0x76 or 0x77), RTC DS3231 (0x68), LCD backpack (0x27 or 0x3F). A common error: SDA and SCL swapped or missing pull-ups, so the scanner finds nothing.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"17-4-spi\">17.4 SPI<\/h2>\n<p>SPI (Serial Peripheral Interface) is fast and simple: one master and one or more slaves. Data goes in both directions at the same time (full duplex).<\/p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Wire<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Meaning<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>SCK<\/td><td>Clock from the master<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>MOSI<\/td><td>Master Out, Slave In: data from master to slave<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>MISO<\/td><td>Master In, Slave Out: data from slave to master<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>CS (SS)<\/td><td>Chip Select, one per slave; pull LOW to talk to that slave<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<ul>\n<li>Speeds from 1 MHz to tens of MHz, much faster than I2C. Used for SD cards, displays (TFT), flash memory, ADCs and radio modules (NRF24, LoRa).<\/li>\n<li>Every bit sent by the master shifts one bit in from the slave at the same time. To read, the master sends dummy bytes.<\/li>\n<li>Four modes (0 to 3) set the clock idle level (CPOL) and which edge samples data (CPHA). Master and slave must use the same mode; check the datasheet.<\/li>\n<li>Each extra slave needs one more CS pin, so SPI uses more wires than I2C.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>#include &lt;SPI.h&gt;\n \nconst int CS_PIN = 5;\n \nvoid setup() {\n    pinMode(CS_PIN, OUTPUT);\n    digitalWrite(CS_PIN, HIGH);          // idle: slave not selected\n    SPI.begin();\n}\n \nuint8_t spiReadRegister(uint8_t reg) {\n    SPI.beginTransaction(SPISettings(1000000, MSBFIRST, SPI_MODE0));\n    digitalWrite(CS_PIN, LOW);           // select the slave\n    SPI.transfer(reg | 0x80);            // send register address (read flag is device specific)\n    uint8_t value = SPI.transfer(0x00);  // send dummy byte, get the answer\n    digitalWrite(CS_PIN, HIGH);          // release the slave\n    SPI.endTransaction();\n    return value;\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"17-5-comparison\">17.5 Comparison<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><\/th><th><strong>UART<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>I2C<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>SPI<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Wires<\/td><td>2 (TX, RX) + GND<\/td><td>2 shared (SDA, SCL)<\/td><td>3 + one CS per slave<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Devices<\/td><td>2<\/td><td>Many (addresses)<\/td><td>Many (CS lines)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Clock<\/td><td>None (baud rate)<\/td><td>Master clock<\/td><td>Master clock<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Speed<\/td><td>Up to about 1 Mbps<\/td><td>100 kHz to 400 kHz (up to MHz)<\/td><td>10 MHz and more<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Direction<\/td><td>Full duplex<\/td><td>Half duplex<\/td><td>Full duplex<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Best for<\/td><td>PC link, GPS, modules<\/td><td>Sensors, small displays, RTC<\/td><td>SD card, fast displays, flash<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>Quick rule: need to talk to a PC or a module with a serial port, use UART; many slow sensors on few pins, use I2C; high data rate, use SPI.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"17-6-multi-byte-values-and-byte-order\">17.6 Multi-byte values and byte order<\/h2>\n<p>A 16-bit sensor value arrives as two bytes. The datasheet says which byte comes first. Combine as (high &lt;&lt; 8) | low if the high byte arrives first. Mixing this up is a very common bug (see endianness in Topic 12).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes-17\">Common mistakes<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Mistake<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Fix<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Wrong baud rate or format<\/td><td>Match both devices (for example 115200 8N1)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>TX connected to TX instead of RX<\/td><td>Cross the lines: TX to RX, RX to TX<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>No common ground between the devices<\/td><td>Always connect GND<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>I2C without pull-up resistors<\/td><td>Add 4.7 kohm to the supply on SDA and SCL<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Two I2C devices with the same address<\/td><td>Change the address pin or use a multiplexer<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>SPI mode or bit order mismatch<\/td><td>Read the datasheet; set CPOL, CPHA and MSB or LSB first<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Mixing 5 V and 3.3 V logic<\/td><td>Use level shifters<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"practice-programs-type-each-one-yourself-17\">Practice programs (type each one yourself)<\/h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Send a message from the ESP32 to the PC and echo characters typed in the Serial Monitor.<\/li>\n<li>Write a command parser: \"LED ON\" and \"LED OFF\" received over UART control an LED.<\/li>\n<li>Connect two boards by UART and send a sensor reading from one to the other.<\/li>\n<li>Run the I2C scanner with an OLED or a sensor and note the address.<\/li>\n<li>Read a temperature/humidity or pressure sensor over I2C and print the values.<\/li>\n<li>Read a value from an SPI device (for example an SD card module or a sensor) and print it.<\/li>\n<li>Write a function that combines two received bytes into an int16_t and test with known values.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"interview-questions-with-short-answers-17\">Interview questions with short answers<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Q1. What is baud rate? The number of symbols (bits) sent per second on a serial line.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q2. What does 8N1 mean? 8 data bits, no parity, 1 stop bit.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q3. Why does I2C need pull-up resistors? The lines are open-drain, so devices can only pull them low; resistors pull them high.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q4. How many devices can an I2C bus have? Up to 112 usable 7-bit addresses in theory, fewer in practice because of capacitance and address clashes.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q5. What are MOSI, MISO, SCK and CS? Master out slave in, master in slave out, clock, and chip select.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q6. I2C vs SPI? I2C uses two wires and addresses but is slower; SPI is faster and full duplex but needs more pins.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q7. Why is the ground connection important? Signals are voltages relative to ground; without a common ground the receiver has no reference.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>TOPIC 18<\/strong><\/p>\n<h1 id=\"embedded-design-patterns-buffers-state-machines-watchdog-sle\">Embedded Design Patterns: Buffers, State Machines, Watchdog, Sleep and RTOS<\/h1>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>In one line<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>These are the reusable building blocks of real firmware: a ring buffer to pass data safely, a state machine to organise behaviour, a watchdog to recover from hangs, sleep modes to save power and an RTOS to run several tasks.<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"18-1-circular-ring-buffer\">18.1 Circular (ring) buffer<\/h2>\n<p>A fixed-size buffer that wraps around. New data is written at the head; old data is read from the tail. Both indexes wrap to 0 when they reach the end. It is used for UART receive data, sensor samples and logging.<\/p>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>Analogy: a circular conveyor belt<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>One person puts items on the belt (head), another takes them off (tail). The belt never ends, it loops. If the putter is faster than the taker for too long, the belt fills up (overflow).<\/p><\/aside>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>#include &lt;stdint.h&gt;\n#include &lt;stdbool.h&gt;\n \n#define BUF_SIZE 8                   // use a power of 2 so we can wrap with a mask\n \ntypedef struct {\n    uint8_t data[BUF_SIZE];\n    volatile uint8_t head;           // written by the producer (ISR)\n    volatile uint8_t tail;           // written by the consumer (main)\n} RingBuffer;\n \nbool rb_is_empty(const RingBuffer *rb) { return rb-&gt;head == rb-&gt;tail; }\nbool rb_is_full (const RingBuffer *rb) {\n    return ((rb-&gt;head + 1) &amp; (BUF_SIZE - 1)) == rb-&gt;tail;   // one slot stays empty\n}\n \nbool rb_put(RingBuffer *rb, uint8_t byte) {\n    if (rb_is_full(rb)) return false;                    // overflow: report it\n    rb-&gt;data[rb-&gt;head] = byte;\n    rb-&gt;head = (rb-&gt;head + 1) &amp; (BUF_SIZE - 1);          // wrap: same as % 8\n    return true;\n}\n \nbool rb_get(RingBuffer *rb, uint8_t *byte) {\n    if (rb_is_empty(rb)) return false;\n    *byte = rb-&gt;data[rb-&gt;tail];\n    rb-&gt;tail = (rb-&gt;tail + 1) &amp; (BUF_SIZE - 1);\n    return true;\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Empty: head == tail. Full: advancing head would hit tail. Keeping one slot unused avoids confusing empty with full.<\/li>\n<li>With one producer (ISR) and one consumer (main), each writes only its own index, so no lock is needed on a single-core chip as long as index updates are atomic (8-bit on AVR, 32-bit on ARM).<\/li>\n<li>Size a power of two so the wrap is a fast AND mask instead of a slow divide.<\/li>\n<li>Always decide what to do on overflow: drop new data, drop old data, or raise an error flag.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Typical use: the UART receive ISR calls rb_put for each byte; loop() calls rb_get and processes whatever has arrived.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"18-2-finite-state-machine-fsm\">18.2 Finite state machine (FSM)<\/h2>\n<p>Many devices behave differently depending on what they are doing: waiting, running, error. A state machine keeps a variable for the current state and decides what to do next from the state and the event. It replaces tangled if-else flags with clear, testable logic.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Current state<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Event<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Action<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Next state<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>RED<\/td><td>Timer (10 s)<\/td><td>Turn red off, green on<\/td><td>GREEN<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>GREEN<\/td><td>Timer (10 s)<\/td><td>Turn green off, yellow on<\/td><td>YELLOW<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>YELLOW<\/td><td>Timer (3 s)<\/td><td>Turn yellow off, red on<\/td><td>RED<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>typedef enum { STATE_RED, STATE_GREEN, STATE_YELLOW } State;\n \nState state = STATE_RED;\nunsigned long stateStart = 0;\n \nvoid setLights(bool r, bool y, bool g) {\n    digitalWrite(RED_PIN, r);  digitalWrite(YELLOW_PIN, y);  digitalWrite(GREEN_PIN, g);\n}\n \nvoid loop() {\n    unsigned long now = millis();\n \n    switch (state) {\n        case STATE_RED:\n            setLights(1, 0, 0);\n            if (now - stateStart &gt;= 10000) { state = STATE_GREEN;  stateStart = now; }\n            break;\n        case STATE_GREEN:\n            setLights(0, 0, 1);\n            if (now - stateStart &gt;= 10000) { state = STATE_YELLOW; stateStart = now; }\n            break;\n        case STATE_YELLOW:\n            setLights(0, 1, 0);\n            if (now - stateStart &gt;= 3000)  { state = STATE_RED;    stateStart = now; }\n            break;\n    }\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Draw the state diagram first (circles for states, arrows for events). Code follows the drawing.<\/li>\n<li>The program never blocks: every pass of loop() checks the event and moves on, so it can serve other tasks too.<\/li>\n<li>Add an ERROR state and make sure every state handles unexpected events.<\/li>\n<li>Bigger machines use a table of function pointers, one per state (Topic 13).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>You already built a traffic signal project; rebuilding it as an explicit state machine is a very good interview story.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"18-3-watchdog-timer\">18.3 Watchdog timer<\/h2>\n<p>A watchdog is a hardware timer that resets the chip if your software does not \"kick\" (reset) it within a set time. If the code hangs in an infinite loop or crashes, the watchdog restarts the device automatically.<\/p>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>Analogy: a dead-man's switch<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>A train driver must press a pedal every few seconds. If the driver stops pressing, the train brakes automatically. The watchdog is the pedal; the reset is the brake.<\/p><\/aside>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>// Arduino Uno (AVR)\n#include &lt;avr/wdt.h&gt;\n \nvoid setup() {\n    wdt_enable(WDTO_2S);        // reset if not kicked within 2 seconds\n}\n \nvoid loop() {\n    do_work();\n    wdt_reset();                // kick the watchdog: \"I am still alive\"\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Kick the watchdog only when the whole system is healthy, at one place in the main loop. Kicking it from a timer ISR defeats its purpose, because the ISR keeps running even if main is stuck.<\/li>\n<li>Choose a timeout longer than your slowest legitimate loop.<\/li>\n<li>After a reset, check the reset reason and log it so you know the watchdog fired.<\/li>\n<li>ESP32 has task watchdogs under FreeRTOS: a task that runs too long without yielding triggers a reset message. Add small delays or yield in long loops.<\/li>\n<li>Safety-related products require a watchdog.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"18-4-low-power-and-sleep-modes\">18.4 Low-power and sleep modes<\/h2>\n<p>Battery products spend most of their time asleep. A CPU running at full speed may draw tens of milliamps; deep sleep can be microamps.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Mode<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>What happens<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Wake-up sources<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Active<\/td><td>Everything runs<\/td><td>-<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Light / idle sleep<\/td><td>CPU stops, peripherals and RAM stay on; quick wake<\/td><td>Timer, UART, GPIO interrupts<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Deep sleep<\/td><td>Almost everything off; RAM often lost; slow wake<\/td><td>RTC timer, special pins, touch<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>// ESP32: sleep 10 seconds, wake, read the sensor, sleep again\nvoid setup() {\n    Serial.begin(115200);\n    read_and_send_sensor();                       // do the work quickly\n \n    esp_sleep_enable_timer_wakeup(10ULL * 1000000ULL);   // time in microseconds\n    esp_deep_sleep_start();                       // the chip restarts at setup() on wake\n}\n \nvoid loop() {}                                    // never reached<\/code><\/pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Waking from deep sleep runs setup() again; variables in normal RAM are lost. Keep important values in RTC memory (RTC_DATA_ATTR) if needed.<\/li>\n<li>The sleep strategy matters more than the sleep function: wake, measure, transmit, sleep. Turn off Wi-Fi and unused peripherals.<\/li>\n<li>Average current = (active current x active time + sleep current x sleep time) / total time. Short active bursts give very long battery life.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"18-5-introduction-to-an-rtos\">18.5 Introduction to an RTOS<\/h2>\n<p>A Real-Time Operating System lets you write several independent tasks (read sensor, update display, handle Wi-Fi) that appear to run at the same time. A scheduler switches between them. The ESP32 Arduino core already runs on FreeRTOS, and your loop() is one task.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Term<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Meaning<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Task<\/td><td>An independent function with its own stack and priority<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Scheduler<\/td><td>Decides which task runs, usually the highest priority one that is ready<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Delay<\/td><td>vTaskDelay lets other tasks run while this one waits (unlike delay-busy waiting)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Queue<\/td><td>Thread-safe way to pass data between tasks or from an ISR to a task<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Semaphore / Mutex<\/td><td>Signals an event or protects a shared resource from two tasks at once<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>// ESP32 (Arduino core): two tasks\nvoid blinkTask(void *param) {\n    pinMode(2, OUTPUT);\n    while (true) {\n        digitalWrite(2, !digitalRead(2));\n        vTaskDelay(500 / portTICK_PERIOD_MS);       // sleep 500 ms, others run\n    }\n}\n \nvoid sensorTask(void *param) {\n    while (true) {\n        int v = analogRead(34);\n        Serial.println(v);\n        vTaskDelay(1000 / portTICK_PERIOD_MS);\n    }\n}\n \nvoid setup() {\n    Serial.begin(115200);\n    xTaskCreate(blinkTask,  \"blink\",  2048, NULL, 1, NULL);   // name, stack bytes, param, priority, handle\n    xTaskCreate(sensorTask, \"sensor\", 4096, NULL, 1, NULL);\n}\n \nvoid loop() {}<\/code><\/pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Use an RTOS when you have several activities with different timing. Use a simple super-loop with a state machine when the program is small.<\/li>\n<li>Shared data between tasks needs a mutex or a queue, otherwise you get race conditions just like with interrupts.<\/li>\n<li>Each task needs enough stack; too little causes crashes (check with uxTaskGetStackHighWaterMark).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes-18\">Common mistakes<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Mistake<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Fix<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Ring buffer size not a power of 2 but using a mask<\/td><td>Use % or choose 8, 16, 32, 64...<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Not handling ring buffer overflow<\/td><td>Return false, count dropped bytes, or enlarge the buffer<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Kicking the watchdog inside a timer interrupt<\/td><td>Kick it from main code after checking system health<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Using delay() inside a state machine<\/td><td>Use millis comparisons so the machine never blocks<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Forgetting that deep sleep restarts the program<\/td><td>Do everything in setup() and store state in RTC memory<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Sharing variables between RTOS tasks without protection<\/td><td>Use a queue or mutex<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"practice-programs-type-each-one-yourself-18\">Practice programs (type each one yourself)<\/h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Implement the ring buffer on a PC with a test that puts 20 bytes into a size-8 buffer and checks the overflow handling.<\/li>\n<li>Use the ring buffer for UART receive: the ISR or serial event puts bytes, loop() prints them.<\/li>\n<li>Rebuild your traffic light project as an explicit state machine with a pedestrian-button event.<\/li>\n<li>Add a watchdog to a program, then deliberately hang it with while(1) and watch it reset.<\/li>\n<li>Build a battery-friendly logger: wake every 30 seconds, read a sensor, print it, sleep again.<\/li>\n<li>Create two FreeRTOS tasks on the ESP32 that blink two LEDs at different speeds.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"interview-questions-with-short-answers-18\">Interview questions with short answers<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Q1. What is a circular buffer and where is it used? A fixed-size FIFO that wraps around; used for UART data, sensor streams and logs.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q2. How do you tell a full buffer from an empty one? Empty when head equals tail; full when head + 1 equals tail (one slot kept free), or use a count variable.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q3. What is a finite state machine? A model with a finite set of states and rules that move between them when events occur.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q4. What is a watchdog timer? A hardware timer that resets the system if the software fails to reset it in time.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q5. Why should you not kick the watchdog from an ISR? The ISR can keep running while main is stuck, hiding the hang.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q6. What is the difference between light sleep and deep sleep? Light sleep stops the CPU but keeps RAM and quick wake; deep sleep powers down almost everything for the lowest current but restarts the program.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q7. Super-loop vs RTOS? A super-loop is one endless loop (simple); an RTOS schedules several tasks with priorities and timing guarantees.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q8. What is a mutex? A lock that lets only one task use a shared resource at a time.<\/strong><\/p>\n<h1 id=\"final-checklist\">Final checklist<\/h1>\n<p>Tick a box only when you have actually done it, not when you have only read it.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>No.<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Topic<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>I can explain it<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>I wrote the code<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>I can answer Qs<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>1<\/td><td>Basics and Data Types<\/td><td>☐<\/td><td>☐<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>2<\/td><td>Operators and Control Flow<\/td><td>☐<\/td><td>☐<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>3<\/td><td>Functions and Recursion<\/td><td>☐<\/td><td>☐<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>4<\/td><td>Arrays and Strings<\/td><td>☐<\/td><td>☐<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>5<\/td><td>Pointers<\/td><td>☐<\/td><td>☐<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>6<\/td><td>Structures, Unions, Enums and typedef<\/td><td>☐<\/td><td>☐<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>7<\/td><td>Dynamic Memory Allocation<\/td><td>☐<\/td><td>☐<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>8<\/td><td>Storage Classes and the Preprocessor<\/td><td>☐<\/td><td>☐<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>9<\/td><td>File Handling<\/td><td>☐<\/td><td>☐<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>10<\/td><td>Bitwise Operators and Bit Manipulation<\/td><td>☐<\/td><td>☐<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>11<\/td><td>Type Qualifiers and Fixed-Width Types<\/td><td>☐<\/td><td>☐<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>12<\/td><td>Memory Layout, Startup Code, Padding and Endianness<\/td><td>☐<\/td><td>☐<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>13<\/td><td>Registers, Memory-Mapped I/O and Function Pointers<\/td><td>☐<\/td><td>☐<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>14<\/td><td>GPIO and Debouncing<\/td><td>☐<\/td><td>☐<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>15<\/td><td>Interrupts and ISR<\/td><td>☐<\/td><td>☐<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>16<\/td><td>Timers, PWM and ADC<\/td><td>☐<\/td><td>☐<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>17<\/td><td>Communication Protocols: UART, I2C and SPI<\/td><td>☐<\/td><td>☐<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>18<\/td><td>Embedded Design Patterns<\/td><td>☐<\/td><td>☐<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"the-10-most-asked-topics-interview-priority\">The 10 most asked topics (interview priority)<\/h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Pointers (all of Topic 5), especially pointer arithmetic, dangling pointers, function pointers.<\/li>\n<li>Bit manipulation: set, clear, toggle, check, power of 2, count bits.<\/li>\n<li>volatile and where it is needed.<\/li>\n<li>const, static and their effect on memory and linkage.<\/li>\n<li>Memory layout: .text, .data, .bss, stack, heap, and what runs before main().<\/li>\n<li>Interrupts: ISR rules, latency, shared variables, race conditions.<\/li>\n<li>UART, I2C and SPI: differences, wiring, speed.<\/li>\n<li>Structure padding, unions and endianness.<\/li>\n<li>Dynamic memory problems: leaks, dangling pointers and why embedded avoids malloc.<\/li>\n<li>Your own projects: be able to explain each one in two minutes, including the bug that taught you the most.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>Final advice<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Do not try to memorise this guide. Understand each idea, then prove it by writing a short program. A candidate who can write a clean bit-manipulation function and explain volatile in plain words beats one who recites definitions.<\/p>\n<p>Link every topic to something you built: debouncing in your door lock, ADC in your soil sensor, I2C in your OLED projects, interrupts in your traffic signal. Interviewers love real examples.<\/p><\/aside>", "cheatsheet": "<header class=\"doc-head\"><p class=\"title\">C and Embedded C<\/p><p class=\"subtitle\">Interview Cheat Sheet<\/p><p class=\"meta\">Read this the night before and again one hour before the interview.<\/p><\/header><aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>How to answer any question (30 seconds)<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>1. Say the definition in one line.   2. Give a tiny example.   3. Say why it matters in embedded.<\/p>\n<p>Example: \"volatile tells the compiler a value can change outside normal code flow, so it must read it from memory every time. I use it for hardware registers and flags changed in an ISR.\"<\/p><\/aside>\n<h1 id=\"1-top-30-one-line-answers\">1. Top 30 one-line answers<\/h1>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Question<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Answer<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Pointer?<\/td><td>A variable that stores a memory address. &amp; = address of, * = value at.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Dangling pointer?<\/td><td>Points to memory that was freed or went out of scope.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Wild pointer?<\/td><td>Pointer never initialised, holds a random address.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>NULL pointer?<\/td><td>Pointer holding 0, points to nothing. Always check before use.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>void pointer?<\/td><td>Generic pointer without a type. Cast before dereferencing.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>const vs #define?<\/td><td>const is a typed variable the compiler protects; #define is typeless text replacement.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>static (local)?<\/td><td>Keeps its value between calls; lives for the whole program.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>static (global/function)?<\/td><td>Visible only inside that .c file.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>extern?<\/td><td>Declares a variable defined in another file.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>volatile?<\/td><td>Value can change outside the code (hardware, ISR). Compiler must not cache it.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>const volatile?<\/td><td>Read-only for code, changed by hardware (status register).<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>struct vs union?<\/td><td>Struct: members have separate memory. Union: all members share one memory.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Struct padding?<\/td><td>Gaps added for alignment. Order members large to small to save RAM.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Stack vs heap?<\/td><td>Stack: automatic, fast, for locals. Heap: manual (malloc/free), flexible, can leak.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Memory leak?<\/td><td>malloc without free; memory becomes unreachable.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>malloc vs calloc?<\/td><td>calloc zeroes memory and takes (count, size).<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>.text .data .bss?<\/td><td>Code | initialised globals | zero-initialised globals.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Why globals are 0, locals garbage?<\/td><td>Startup code clears .bss; locals sit on the stack with old data.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>What runs before main()?<\/td><td>Reset handler: set stack, copy .data to RAM, zero .bss, init clock, call main.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Endianness?<\/td><td>Byte order of multi-byte values. ESP32, AVR, most ARM are little-endian.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>ISR rules?<\/td><td>Short, no delay, no printf, no malloc, shared data volatile, set flag and return.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Interrupt latency?<\/td><td>Time from the event to the first instruction of the ISR.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Polling vs interrupt?<\/td><td>Polling checks repeatedly (simple, wastes CPU); interrupt reacts to events (fast, saves CPU).<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Race condition?<\/td><td>Result depends on timing between ISR and main using the same data.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Switch bounce?<\/td><td>Contacts make/break for 1 to 20 ms. Fix: RC filter or software timing.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Pull-up resistor?<\/td><td>Holds an input HIGH when nothing drives it; avoids a floating pin.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>PWM?<\/td><td>Fast on/off switching; duty cycle sets average power.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Watchdog?<\/td><td>Hardware timer that resets the chip if software does not kick it in time.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>UART / I2C / SPI?<\/td><td>UART: 2 devices, async. I2C: 2 shared wires, addresses. SPI: 4 wires, fast, chip select.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Why avoid malloc in firmware?<\/td><td>Fragmentation, unpredictable timing, tiny RAM.<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h1 id=\"2-c-language-quick-reference\">2. C language quick reference<\/h1>\n<h2 id=\"type-sizes-bytes-and-ranges\">Type sizes (bytes) and ranges<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Type<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>PC<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Uno (AVR)<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>ESP32 / STM32<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Range (signed)<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>char<\/td><td>1<\/td><td>1<\/td><td>1<\/td><td>-128 to 127<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>short<\/td><td>2<\/td><td>2<\/td><td>2<\/td><td>-32,768 to 32,767<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>int<\/td><td>4<\/td><td>2<\/td><td>4<\/td><td>16-bit: +/-32K   32-bit: +/-2.1 billion<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>long<\/td><td>4 or 8<\/td><td>4<\/td><td>4<\/td><td>+/-2.1 billion (4 bytes)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>long long<\/td><td>8<\/td><td>8<\/td><td>8<\/td><td>+/-9.2 x 10^18<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>float / double<\/td><td>4 / 8<\/td><td>4 / 4<\/td><td>4 / 8<\/td><td>6-7 / 15-16 digits<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>pointer<\/td><td>8<\/td><td>2<\/td><td>4<\/td><td>holds an address<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>Unsigned: 8 bit 0..255, 16 bit 0..65,535, 32 bit 0..4,294,967,295. Signed n-bit: -2^(n-1) to 2^(n-1) - 1.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Negative numbers: two's complement = invert bits, add 1.  -5 in 8 bit = 11111011.<\/strong><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"format-specifiers\">Format specifiers<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>%d<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>int<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>%u<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>unsigned<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>%c<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>char<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>%ld<\/code><\/td><td>long<\/td><td><code>%lld<\/code><\/td><td>long long<\/td><td><code>%s<\/code><\/td><td>string<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>%f<\/code><\/td><td>float/double<\/td><td><code>%lf<\/code><\/td><td>double (scanf)<\/td><td><code>%p<\/code><\/td><td>pointer<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>%x<\/code><\/td><td>hex<\/td><td><code>%o<\/code><\/td><td>octal<\/td><td><code>%zu<\/code><\/td><td>size_t<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"operator-precedence-high-to-low\">Operator precedence (high to low)<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Brackets  &gt;  Unary (! ~ ++ -- * &amp; sizeof)  &gt;  * / %  &gt;  + -  &gt;  &lt;&lt; &gt;&gt;  &gt;  &lt; &lt;= &gt; &gt;=  &gt;  == !=  &gt;  &amp;  &gt;  ^  &gt;  |  &gt;  &amp;&amp;  &gt;  ||  &gt;  ?:  &gt;  =<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Remember: &amp; is LOWER than ==, so write (x &amp; 4) == 4. When in doubt, use brackets.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"pointers\">Pointers<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Declaration<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Meaning (read right to left)<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>int *p<\/code><\/td><td>p is a pointer to int<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>const int *p<\/code><\/td><td>pointer to constant int: cannot change *p<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>int *const p<\/code><\/td><td>constant pointer: cannot move p<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>int **pp<\/code><\/td><td>pointer to pointer to int<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>int *arr[5]<\/code><\/td><td>array of 5 pointers to int<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>int (*fp)(int, int)<\/code><\/td><td>pointer to function(int, int) returning int<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>arr[i]  ==  *(arr + i)<\/code><\/td><td>indexing is pointer arithmetic; p + 1 moves by sizeof(type)<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>Mnemonic: &amp; = WHERE (address), * = WHAT (value there).<\/strong><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"storage-classes\">Storage classes<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Class<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Scope<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Lifetime<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Note<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>auto<\/code><\/td><td>block<\/td><td>block<\/td><td>default for locals, garbage value<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>register<\/code><\/td><td>block<\/td><td>block<\/td><td>hint only, modern compilers ignore<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>static<\/code><\/td><td>block or file<\/td><td>whole program<\/td><td>default value 0, private to file<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>extern<\/code><\/td><td>whole program<\/td><td>whole program<\/td><td>declared here, defined elsewhere<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"strings-and-memory-rules\">Strings and memory rules<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>String = char array ending with '\\0'. \"hello\" needs 6 bytes. strlen = 5, sizeof = 6.<\/li>\n<li>Compare strings with strcmp (0 means equal), never ==.<\/li>\n<li>char s[] = \"hi\" is modifiable. char *s = \"hi\" points to read-only text.<\/li>\n<li>malloc: check for NULL, use n * sizeof *p, free once, set pointer to NULL after free.<\/li>\n<li>realloc: assign to a temporary pointer first, otherwise a failure leaks the old block.<\/li>\n<li>Macros: put every parameter and the whole expression in brackets. Header files: add include guards.<\/li>\n<li>Call by value: functions get copies. Pass a pointer to change the caller's variable.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h1 id=\"3-embedded-c-quick-reference\">3. Embedded C quick reference<\/h1>\n<h2 id=\"bit-manipulation\">Bit manipulation<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Goal<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Code<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Mnemonic<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Set bit n<\/td><td><code>x |= (1U &lt;&lt; n);<\/code><\/td><td>SET = OR<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Clear bit n<\/td><td><code>x &amp;= ~(1U &lt;&lt; n);<\/code><\/td><td>CLEAR = AND-NOT<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Toggle bit n<\/td><td><code>x ^= (1U &lt;&lt; n);<\/code><\/td><td>TOGGLE = XOR<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Check bit n<\/td><td><code>(x &gt;&gt; n) &amp; 1<\/code><\/td><td>CHECK = AND<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Read field<\/td><td><code>(x &gt;&gt; pos) &amp; mask<\/code><\/td><td>shift down, then mask<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Write field<\/td><td><code>x = (x &amp; ~(mask &lt;&lt; pos)) | (val &lt;&lt; pos);<\/code><\/td><td>clear it, then set it<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>Shift left n = multiply by 2^n.   Shift right n = divide by 2^n (unsigned).   Use unsigned types and 1U for bit work.<\/p>\n<p><strong><code>12 &amp; 10 = 8     12 | 10 = 14     12 ^ 10 = 6     ~12 = -13     12 &lt;&lt; 1 = 24     12 &gt;&gt; 1 = 6<\/code><\/strong><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"volatile-needed-for-3-things\">volatile: needed for 3 things<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Mnemonic RIS:  Register (hardware)   |   Interrupt-changed flag   |   Shared between tasks.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>volatile does NOT make access atomic. For multi-byte shared data, disable interrupts briefly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"memory\">Memory<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Section<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Holds<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Where<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>.text<\/code><\/td><td>code<\/td><td>Flash<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>.rodata<\/code><\/td><td>const data, string literals<\/td><td>Flash<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>.data<\/code><\/td><td>initialised globals / statics<\/td><td>Flash copy -&gt; RAM<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>.bss<\/code><\/td><td>zero or uninitialised globals<\/td><td>RAM (zeroed at startup)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>Heap<\/code><\/td><td>malloc, grows up<\/td><td>RAM<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>Stack<\/code><\/td><td>locals, return addresses, grows down<\/td><td>RAM<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>Startup order: vector table -&gt; Reset handler -&gt; set stack -&gt; copy .data -&gt; zero .bss -&gt; init clocks -&gt; main().<\/p>\n<p>Little-endian: 0x12345678 stored as 78 56 34 12.   Padding: struct {char; int; char} = 12 bytes, reorder to {int; char; char} = 8 bytes.<\/p>\n<p>Stack overflow causes: deep recursion, large local arrays. Fix: make buffers static, avoid recursion, increase task stack.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"interrupts\">Interrupts<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Flow: event -&gt; flag -&gt; finish instruction -&gt; save context -&gt; vector table -&gt; ISR -&gt; restore -&gt; resume.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>ISR mnemonic: SHORT, SIMPLE, no SLEEP (delay), no PRINT. Set a flag, return, do the work in main.<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Shared variables: volatile. Multi-byte reads on 8-bit chips: noInterrupts() ... interrupts().<\/li>\n<li>ESP32 ISRs need IRAM_ATTR. Uno external interrupts only on pins 2 and 3.<\/li>\n<li>Clear the interrupt flag if the hardware requires it, or the ISR fires again forever.<\/li>\n<li>Debounce button interrupts (ignore triggers within about 50 ms).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"gpio\">GPIO<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Active-low button: INPUT_PULLUP, pressed = LOW. Input floating = random values.<\/li>\n<li>Push-pull drives high and low. Open-drain pulls low only, needs pull-up (I2C).<\/li>\n<li>LED resistor: R = (Vsupply - Vled) / I.  Example: (3.3 - 2.0) / 0.01 = 130 ohm, use 220 ohm.<\/li>\n<li>Motors, relays, solenoids: use a transistor or driver with flyback diode, never a bare GPIO.<\/li>\n<li>ESP32 GPIO 34 to 39: input only, no internal pull-ups. ESP32 / ESP8266 pins are 3.3 V only.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h1 id=\"4-formulas-to-remember\">4. Formulas to remember<\/h1>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>What<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Formula and example<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Timer tick rate<\/td><td>f_timer = f_clock / prescaler.   16 MHz / 64 = 250 kHz (4 us per tick)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Timer compare value<\/td><td>ticks = time x f_timer, compare = ticks - 1.   1 ms: 250 ticks, OCR = 249<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>PWM duty cycle<\/td><td>duty % = ON time / period x 100.   Average voltage = duty x Vcc<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>PWM 8-bit<\/td><td>256 levels (0 to 255).   value 128 is about 50%<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>ADC voltage<\/td><td>V = count x Vref / maxCount.   Uno: 512 x 5 / 1023 = 2.5 V<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>ADC step size<\/td><td>Vref / 2^N.   10-bit at 5 V = 4.9 mV;   12-bit at 3.3 V = 0.8 mV<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>ADC counts<\/td><td>Uno / ESP8266: 0 to 1023 (10-bit).   ESP32: 0 to 4095 (12-bit)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>UART byte time<\/td><td>10 bits / baud.   9600 baud = 1.04 ms per byte;  115200 = 87 us<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>LM35 temperature<\/td><td>degC = volts x 100  (10 mV per degree)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Average current<\/td><td>(I_active x t_active + I_sleep x t_sleep) / t_total<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>millis() safe compare<\/td><td>now - previous &gt;= interval  (works across wrap-around, unsigned)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Servo<\/td><td>50 Hz (20 ms), pulse 1 ms to 2 ms for full travel<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h1 id=\"5-protocols-side-by-side\">5. Protocols side by side<\/h1>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><\/th><th><strong>UART<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>I2C<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>SPI<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Wires<\/td><td>TX, RX, GND<\/td><td>SDA, SCL (+ pull-ups)<\/td><td>SCK, MOSI, MISO, CS<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Devices<\/td><td>2 only<\/td><td>many (7-bit address)<\/td><td>many (one CS each)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Clock<\/td><td>none, same baud<\/td><td>master clock<\/td><td>master clock<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Speed<\/td><td>9600 to 1M+ baud<\/td><td>100 / 400 kHz<\/td><td>1 to 50+ MHz<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Duplex<\/td><td>full<\/td><td>half<\/td><td>full<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Used for<\/td><td>PC, GPS, Bluetooth<\/td><td>sensors, OLED, RTC<\/td><td>SD card, TFT, flash<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Gotcha<\/td><td>cross TX/RX, same baud, common GND<\/td><td>pull-ups 4.7k, address clash<\/td><td>mode (CPOL/CPHA), CS<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>UART frame 8N1: start bit, 8 data bits (LSB first), no parity, stop bit.   I2C default pins: Uno A4/A5, ESP32 GPIO 21/22.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Choose: PC or module with serial port = UART.  Many slow sensors on few pins = I2C.  High speed data = SPI.<\/strong><\/p>\n<h1 id=\"6-design-patterns-in-one-line-each\">6. Design patterns in one line each<\/h1>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Pattern<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Remember<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Ring buffer<\/td><td>head writes, tail reads, wrap with (i + 1) &amp; (SIZE - 1); empty: head == tail; full: next(head) == tail.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>State machine<\/td><td>enum for states + switch; each state checks an event or millis() and sets the next state; never blocks.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Watchdog<\/td><td>Kick from main loop only when healthy, never from an ISR.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Deep sleep<\/td><td>Wake, measure, send, sleep. Restarts at setup(); keep data in RTC memory.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>RTOS task<\/td><td>Own stack and priority; use vTaskDelay, queues and mutexes for sharing.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Callback<\/td><td>Driver stores a function pointer; calls it when the event happens.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Super-loop<\/td><td>while(1) { read inputs; update state; write outputs; }<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h1 id=\"7-code-you-must-be-able-to-write-from-memory\">7. Code you must be able to write from memory<\/h1>\n<h2 id=\"bit-macros\">Bit macros<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>#define SET_BIT(x, n)     ((x) |=  (1U &lt;&lt; (n)))\n#define CLEAR_BIT(x, n)   ((x) &amp;= ~(1U &lt;&lt; (n)))\n#define TOGGLE_BIT(x, n)  ((x) ^=  (1U &lt;&lt; (n)))\n#define CHECK_BIT(x, n)   (((x) &gt;&gt; (n)) &amp; 1U)<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"power-of-2-count-set-bits-swap\">Power of 2, count set bits, swap<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>int is_pow2(unsigned n) { return n &amp;&amp; !(n &amp; (n - 1)); }\n \nint count_bits(unsigned n) {\n    int c = 0;\n    while (n) { n &amp;= n - 1; c++; }      // removes lowest 1 each loop\n    return c;\n}\n \nvoid swap(int *a, int *b) { int t = *a; *a = *b; *b = t; }\n// no temp: a ^= b; b ^= a; a ^= b;<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"strings\">Strings<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>int my_strlen(const char *s) {\n    int n = 0;\n    while (*s++) n++;\n    return n;\n}\n \nvoid reverse(char *s) {\n    int i = 0, j = my_strlen(s) - 1;\n    while (i &lt; j) { char t = s[i]; s[i++] = s[j]; s[j--] = t; }\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"endianness-check\">Endianness check<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>uint32_t x = 1;\nif (*(uint8_t *)&amp;x == 1) puts(\"little-endian\");\nelse                     puts(\"big-endian\");<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"register-access-and-function-pointer\">Register access and function pointer<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>#define REG  (*(volatile uint32_t *)0x40020014)   // example address\nREG |= (1U &lt;&lt; 5);\n \ntypedef void (*Callback)(void);\nCallback cb = my_handler;\ncb();<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"ring-buffer-put-and-get\">Ring buffer (put and get)<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>bool rb_put(RB *r, uint8_t b) {\n    uint8_t next = (r-&gt;head + 1) &amp; (SIZE - 1);\n    if (next == r-&gt;tail) return false;      // full\n    r-&gt;buf[r-&gt;head] = b;  r-&gt;head = next;  return true;\n}\nbool rb_get(RB *r, uint8_t *b) {\n    if (r-&gt;head == r-&gt;tail) return false;   // empty\n    *b = r-&gt;buf[r-&gt;tail];  r-&gt;tail = (r-&gt;tail + 1) &amp; (SIZE - 1);  return true;\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"non-blocking-blink-and-debounce-skeleton\">Non-blocking blink and debounce skeleton<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>if (millis() - previous &gt;= 1000) { previous = millis(); toggle(); }\n \nif (reading != lastReading) lastChange = millis();\nlastReading = reading;\nif (millis() - lastChange &gt; 30 &amp;&amp; reading != stable) {\n    stable = reading;                       // accepted: real press or release\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<h1 id=\"8-tricky-output-questions\">8. Tricky output questions<\/h1>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Code<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Result<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Why<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>7 / 2<\/td><td>3<\/td><td>integer division<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>-7 / 2   and   -7 % 2<\/td><td>-3   and   -1<\/td><td>truncates toward zero; remainder takes the sign of the left side<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>sizeof(\"hello\")   strlen(\"hello\")<\/td><td>6   5<\/td><td>sizeof counts \\0<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>sizeof('a') in C<\/td><td>4 (int size)<\/td><td>a character constant is an int in C<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>unsigned char c = 255; c++;<\/td><td>0<\/td><td>unsigned wraps around<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>if (-1 &lt; 1u)<\/td><td>false<\/td><td>-1 converts to a huge unsigned value<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3<\/td><td>false<\/td><td>binary floating point is approximate<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>int x = 012;<\/td><td>x = 10<\/td><td>leading 0 means octal<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>5 &amp; 3   5 | 3   5 ^ 3   ~5<\/td><td>1   7   6   -6<\/td><td>bit by bit<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>5 &amp;&amp; 3   !5   !0<\/td><td>1   0   1<\/td><td>logical operators give 0 or 1<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>int a = 5; b = a++;   c = ++a;<\/td><td>b = 5, c = 7<\/td><td>post uses then adds; pre adds then uses<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>printf(\"%d\", printf(\"hi\"));<\/td><td>hi2<\/td><td>printf returns the number of characters printed<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>sizeof(arr) inside a function with int arr[]<\/td><td>pointer size<\/td><td>array parameter decays to a pointer<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>char *p = \"hi\"; p[0] = 'H';<\/td><td>undefined behaviour<\/td><td>string literal is read-only<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>int arr[5]; arr[5] = 1;<\/td><td>undefined behaviour<\/td><td>out of bounds, C does not check<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>1 &lt;&lt; 31 on a 32-bit signed int<\/td><td>undefined behaviour<\/td><td>shifts into the sign bit; use 1U &lt;&lt; 31<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>i = i++ + ++i;<\/td><td>undefined behaviour<\/td><td>modified twice without a sequence point<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>#define SQ(x) x*x    SQ(2+3)<\/td><td>11<\/td><td>2+3*2+3; brackets missing<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h1 id=\"9-mnemonics\">9. Mnemonics<\/h1>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Topic<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Memory trick<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Bit operations<\/td><td>Set = OR, Clear = AND NOT, Toggle = XOR, Check = AND.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Pointers<\/td><td>&amp; = where, * = what. Read declarations right to left.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>volatile (RIS)<\/td><td>Register, Interrupt flag, Shared data.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>ISR<\/td><td>Short, Simple, no Sleep, no Print.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Memory sections<\/td><td>Text = code (flash), Data = given values, BSS = Zeros at start.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Heap vs stack<\/td><td>Stack = automatic hotel room; heap = rented hall you must return.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>SPI wires<\/td><td>SCK clock, MOSI master out, MISO master in, CS choose.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>I2C<\/td><td>Two wires, many names (addresses), pull-ups required.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Debounce<\/td><td>Wait until the signal stays steady for 20 to 50 ms.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Endianness<\/td><td>Little-endian: little end (lowest byte) first.<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h1 id=\"10-interview-day-checklist\">10. Interview day checklist<\/h1>\n<ul>\n<li>Know your projects cold: purpose, hardware, sensors, protocols, the hardest bug, what you would improve. Practise a 2-minute explanation for each.<\/li>\n<li>Say \"I don't know, but I would find out by checking the datasheet or testing it\" instead of guessing. Honesty plus method impresses.<\/li>\n<li>Think aloud when solving a coding question: state the approach, write the code, test with an edge case (0, 1, maximum, empty input).<\/li>\n<li>Draw diagrams when asked about memory, interrupts or protocols. Diagrams show understanding.<\/li>\n<li>Link answers to your own work: debouncing in your door lock, ADC in your soil sensor, I2C in your OLED projects, interrupts in your traffic signal.<\/li>\n<li>Ask one good question at the end: \"What does a typical day or first project look like for this role?\"<\/li>\n<li>Sleep well, carry a pen and paper, and be 15 minutes early.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>Last-minute rule<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>If you remember only 5 things: pointers, bit manipulation, volatile, memory sections and ISR rules. These come up in almost every embedded interview.<\/p><\/aside>", "datatypes": "<header class=\"doc-head\"><p class=\"title\">Data Types in C<\/p><p class=\"subtitle\">with a deep dive into int<\/p><p class=\"meta\">Plain explanations, analogies, step-by-step examples and practice<\/p><\/header><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Part<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>What you will learn<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>1<\/td><td>What a data type is and why C needs it<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>2<\/td><td>The families of data types and the basic ones at a glance<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>3<\/td><td>int in detail: size, range, binary, signed vs unsigned, overflow, division, literals<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>4<\/td><td>int on your boards (Arduino Uno vs ESP32) and the safe stdint.h types<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>5<\/td><td>Common mistakes, practice programs and interview questions<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>6<\/td><td>Quick look at char, float, double, void<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h1 id=\"1-what-is-a-data-type\">1. What is a data type?<\/h1>\n<p>A data type tells the computer three things about a variable:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>How much memory to reserve for it (the size).<\/li>\n<li>How to read the bits stored there (as a whole number, a letter, a decimal number...).<\/li>\n<li>Which operations are allowed on it (you can use % on whole numbers, but not on decimals).<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>Analogy: boxes and labels<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Think of memory as a warehouse full of boxes. A data type is the box size plus a label on the box.<\/p>\n<p>int is a medium box labelled \"whole numbers only\". char is a tiny box labelled \"one character\". double is a big box labelled \"very precise decimal numbers\".<\/p>\n<p>Without the label, the same contents can mean different things: the bits 01000001 can be the number 65 or the letter A. The data type decides how they are read.<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"why-does-c-need-data-types\">Why does C need data types?<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>C is a statically typed language: you must declare the type before using a variable.<\/li>\n<li>The compiler uses the type to reserve the right amount of memory. Too big wastes RAM, too small loses data. On a microcontroller with 2 KB or 520 KB of RAM, this matters a lot.<\/li>\n<li>The compiler uses the type to catch mistakes and to pick the right machine instruction (integer add vs floating-point add).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"how-you-declare-a-variable\">How you declare a variable<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>int age;            // declaration: reserves memory, value is garbage\nint marks = 85;     // declaration + initialization\nfloat price = 99.5f;\nchar grade = 'A';<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>General form:  data_type  variable_name = value;<\/strong><\/p>\n<h1 id=\"2-families-of-data-types\">2. Families of data types<\/h1>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Family<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Examples<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Meaning<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Basic (primary)<\/td><td>char, int, float, double, void<\/td><td>Built into the language. Everything else is built from these.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Derived<\/td><td>array, pointer, function<\/td><td>Made from basic types (for example, an array of int).<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>User-defined<\/td><td>struct, union, enum, typedef<\/td><td>You design them to group or name data.<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"basic-types-at-a-glance\">Basic types at a glance<\/h2>\n<p><em>Sizes below are typical for a modern PC (64-bit). The C standard only guarantees minimums, so sizes can differ on microcontrollers (see Part 4).<\/em><\/p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Type<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Size<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Typical range<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Format<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Stores<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>char<\/code><\/td><td>1 byte<\/td><td>-128 to 127 (or 0 to 255)<\/td><td><code>%c or %d<\/code><\/td><td>A single character or a small number<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>short<\/code><\/td><td>2 bytes<\/td><td>-32,768 to 32,767<\/td><td><code>%hd<\/code><\/td><td>Small whole numbers<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>int<\/code><\/td><td>4 bytes<\/td><td>-2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647<\/td><td><code>%d<\/code><\/td><td>Whole numbers (most common)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>long<\/code><\/td><td>4 or 8 bytes<\/td><td>depends on system<\/td><td><code>%ld<\/code><\/td><td>Bigger whole numbers<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>long long<\/code><\/td><td>8 bytes<\/td><td>about -9.2 x 10^18 to 9.2 x 10^18<\/td><td><code>%lld<\/code><\/td><td>Very big whole numbers<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>float<\/code><\/td><td>4 bytes<\/td><td>about 6 to 7 digits of precision<\/td><td><code>%f<\/code><\/td><td>Decimal numbers<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>double<\/code><\/td><td>8 bytes<\/td><td>about 15 to 16 digits of precision<\/td><td><code>%lf<\/code><\/td><td>Precise decimal numbers<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>void<\/code><\/td><td>-<\/td><td>no value<\/td><td><code>-<\/code><\/td><td>\"Nothing\" (functions that return nothing, generic pointers)<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>Tip: you can check the size on your own machine with sizeof:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n \nint main(void) {\n    printf(\"char      : %zu byte\\n\",  sizeof(char));\n    printf(\"short     : %zu bytes\\n\", sizeof(short));\n    printf(\"int       : %zu bytes\\n\", sizeof(int));\n    printf(\"long      : %zu bytes\\n\", sizeof(long));\n    printf(\"long long : %zu bytes\\n\", sizeof(long long));\n    printf(\"float     : %zu bytes\\n\", sizeof(float));\n    printf(\"double    : %zu bytes\\n\", sizeof(double));\n    return 0;\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong><\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>char      : 1 byte\nshort     : 2 bytes\nint       : 4 bytes\nlong      : 8 bytes     (4 on Windows)\nlong long : 8 bytes\nfloat     : 4 bytes\ndouble    : 8 bytes<\/code><\/pre>\n<h1 id=\"3-the-int-data-type-in-detail\">3. The int data type in detail<\/h1>\n<h2 id=\"3-1-what-is-int\">3.1 What is int?<\/h2>\n<p>int is short for integer. It stores whole numbers: positive, negative or zero, with no decimal point. Examples: 0, 25, -400, 100000.<\/p>\n<p>Use int for things you count: number of students, loop counters, marks, age, array index.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>int students = 60;\nint temperature = -5;\nint total = students * 2;\n \nprintf(\"Students = %d\\n\", students);   // %d prints an int\nscanf(\"%d\", &amp;students);                // %d reads an int (note the &amp;)<\/code><\/pre>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>Remember<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>%d is the format specifier for int. Using the wrong one (for example %f for an int) prints garbage.<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"3-2-how-much-memory-does-int-use\">3.2 How much memory does int use?<\/h2>\n<p>The C standard says int must be at least 16 bits (2 bytes). In practice it depends on the processor:<\/p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Platform<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>int size<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Range<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>PC / laptop (Windows, Linux, macOS)<\/td><td>4 bytes<\/td><td>-2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>ESP32, ESP8266, ARM Cortex-M (STM32)<\/td><td>4 bytes<\/td><td>-2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Arduino Uno / Nano (AVR, 8-bit)<\/td><td>2 bytes<\/td><td>-32,768 to 32,767<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<aside class=\"callout warn\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>Why this matters for you<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The same line of code, int x = 40000;, works on your ESP32 but breaks on an Arduino Uno, because 40000 does not fit in 2 bytes.<\/p>\n<p>This is why embedded engineers use fixed-width types like int32_t (see Part 4).<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"3-3-how-the-range-is-calculated\">3.3 How the range is calculated<\/h2>\n<p>A byte has 8 bits and each bit is 0 or 1. With n bits you can make 2^n different patterns.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>4 bytes = 32 bits, so 2^32 = 4,294,967,296 different values.<\/li>\n<li>Signed int must cover negative and positive numbers, so the values are split roughly in half.<\/li>\n<li>Range of signed n-bit integer: -2^(n-1) to 2^(n-1) - 1.<\/li>\n<li>For 32 bits: -2,147,483,648 to +2,147,483,647 (one more negative value than positive, because zero takes one slot on the positive side).<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Bits<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Signed range<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Unsigned range<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>8<\/td><td>-128 to 127<\/td><td>0 to 255<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>16<\/td><td>-32,768 to 32,767<\/td><td>0 to 65,535<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>32<\/td><td>-2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647<\/td><td>0 to 4,294,967,295<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>64<\/td><td>-9,223,372,036,854,775,808 to 9,223,372,036,854,775,807<\/td><td>0 to 18,446,744,073,709,551,615<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>Print the exact limits of your compiler with limits.h:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;limits.h&gt;\n \nint main(void) {\n    printf(\"INT_MIN  = %d\\n\", INT_MIN);\n    printf(\"INT_MAX  = %d\\n\", INT_MAX);\n    printf(\"UINT_MAX = %u\\n\", UINT_MAX);\n    return 0;\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong><\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>INT_MIN  = -2147483648\nINT_MAX  = 2147483647\nUINT_MAX = 4294967295<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"3-4-int-modifiers-short-long-signed-unsigned\">3.4 int modifiers: short, long, signed, unsigned<\/h2>\n<p>Modifiers change the size or the sign of int.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Declaration<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Typical size<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Format<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Notes<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>short int  (or short)<\/code><\/td><td>2 bytes<\/td><td>%hd<\/td><td>Saves memory for small values<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>int<\/code><\/td><td>4 bytes<\/td><td>%d<\/td><td>The default, most common<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>long int  (or long)<\/code><\/td><td>4 or 8 bytes<\/td><td>%ld<\/td><td>4 bytes on Windows, 8 on 64-bit Linux<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>long long int<\/code><\/td><td>8 bytes<\/td><td>%lld<\/td><td>Use for very large values<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>unsigned int<\/code><\/td><td>4 bytes<\/td><td>%u<\/td><td>Only 0 and positive values<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>unsigned long long<\/code><\/td><td>8 bytes<\/td><td>%llu<\/td><td>Huge positive values (for example, microsecond counters)<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>signed (default): can be negative, positive or zero. Writing int and signed int is the same thing.<\/strong><\/li>\n<li><strong>unsigned: never negative. The extra bit (no sign needed) doubles the positive range.<\/strong><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>unsigned int count = 4000000000U;   // fits only in unsigned 32-bit\nlong long big = 9000000000LL;       // LL suffix = long long\nunsigned int u = 10;\n \nprintf(\"%u\\n\", count);\nprintf(\"%lld\\n\", big);<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"3-5-how-int-is-stored-in-binary-two-s-complement\">3.5 How int is stored in binary (two's complement)<\/h2>\n<p>Computers only store 0s and 1s. A positive int is stored as plain binary. Example with 8 bits for simplicity (a real int uses 32):<\/p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Decimal<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Binary (8-bit)<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Note<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>5<\/td><td><code>00000101<\/code><\/td><td>Plain binary: 4 + 1<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>127<\/td><td><code>01111111<\/code><\/td><td>Largest signed 8-bit value<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>-1<\/td><td><code>11111111<\/code><\/td><td>All bits 1 means -1 in two's complement<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>-5<\/td><td><code>11111011<\/code><\/td><td>See the steps below<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>-128<\/td><td><code>10000000<\/code><\/td><td>Smallest signed 8-bit value<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>How negative numbers are stored (two's complement). To store -5:<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Write 5 in binary: 00000101<\/li>\n<li>Invert every bit (0 becomes 1, 1 becomes 0): 11111010<\/li>\n<li>Add 1: 11111011. This is -5.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sign bit: the left-most bit (MSB). 0 means positive, 1 means negative.<\/strong><\/li>\n<li><strong>Why this system? The CPU can use the same adder circuit for positive and negative numbers. 5 + (-5) gives 00000000 (the carry out is thrown away).<\/strong><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>See the bits yourself:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n \nvoid print_bits(unsigned char n) {\n    for (int i = 7; i &gt;= 0; i--)\n        printf(\"%d\", (n &gt;&gt; i) &amp; 1);\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n}\n \nint main(void) {\n    print_bits(5);\n    print_bits((unsigned char)-5);\n    return 0;\n}<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong><\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>00000101\n11111011<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"3-6-overflow-when-the-number-does-not-fit\">3.6 Overflow: when the number does not fit<\/h2>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>Analogy: the car odometer<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>A 3-digit odometer shows 999. Drive one more kilometre and it rolls over to 000. A fixed number of bits behaves the same way: after the maximum value it wraps around.<\/p><\/aside>\n<p><strong>Unsigned overflow is well defined in C: the value wraps around (modulo 2^n).<\/strong><\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>unsigned char x = 255;\nx = x + 1;\nprintf(\"%d\\n\", x);      // 0\n \nunsigned char y = 0;\ny = y - 1;\nprintf(\"%d\\n\", y);      // 255<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Signed overflow is undefined behaviour in C. It often wraps around in practice, but the compiler is allowed to assume it never happens, so your program can behave strangely. Never rely on it.<\/strong><\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>int a = INT_MAX;\na = a + 1;              // undefined behaviour: do not do this<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Real example: factorial overflows quickly with 32-bit int.<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>12! = 479,001,600 fits in a 32-bit int.<\/li>\n<li>13! = 6,227,020,800 does NOT fit. Use long long (it holds up to 20!) or check before multiplying.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>Embedded example<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>millis() on Arduino returns an unsigned 32-bit counter that wraps to 0 after about 49.7 days. Code that compares times as (now - start) &gt;= interval keeps working across the wrap, because unsigned subtraction also wraps correctly.<\/p><\/aside>\n<h2 id=\"3-7-integer-division-and-the-operator\">3.7 Integer division and the % operator<\/h2>\n<p>When both operands are int, the result is an int. The decimal part is thrown away (truncated toward zero).<\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>printf(\"%d\\n\", 7 / 2);     // 3   (not 3.5)\nprintf(\"%d\\n\", 7 % 2);     // 1   (remainder)\nprintf(\"%d\\n\", -7 / 2);    // -3  (truncates toward zero)\nprintf(\"%d\\n\", -7 % 2);    // -1  (sign follows the left number)\nprintf(\"%f\\n\", 7 / 2);     // WRONG: int passed to %f\n \nprintf(\"%f\\n\", 7 / 2.0);   // 3.500000  (one operand is double)\nprintf(\"%f\\n\", (float)7 / 2); // 3.500000  (type cast)<\/code><\/pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Dividing by zero (7 / 0 or 7 % 0) crashes the program or gives undefined behaviour. Always check the divisor.<\/li>\n<li>% works only with integers. 7.5 % 2 is a compile error.<\/li>\n<li>Handy uses of %: check even/odd (n % 2), get the last digit (n % 10), wrap an index (i % size).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"3-8-writing-int-values-literals-and-number-systems\">3.8 Writing int values (literals and number systems)<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Literal<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Value<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Meaning<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>10<\/code><\/td><td>10<\/td><td>Decimal<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>012<\/code><\/td><td>10<\/td><td>Octal (starts with 0). Easy to write by mistake!<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>0xA  or  0x0A<\/code><\/td><td>10<\/td><td>Hexadecimal (starts with 0x), very common in embedded<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>0b1010<\/code><\/td><td>10<\/td><td>Binary (GCC extension, standard in C23)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>100U, 100L, 100UL, 100LL<\/code><\/td><td>100<\/td><td>Suffix sets the type: unsigned, long, unsigned long, long long<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<aside class=\"callout warn\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>Careful<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>int x = 012; stores 10, not 12. A leading zero makes the number octal. Do not pad numbers with zeros in C source code.<\/p><\/aside>\n<p>Printing the same value in different systems:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>int n = 255;\nprintf(\"%d\\n\", n);    // 255  decimal\nprintf(\"%x\\n\", n);    // ff   hexadecimal\nprintf(\"%X\\n\", n);    // FF\nprintf(\"%o\\n\", n);    // 377  octal<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"3-9-mixing-int-with-other-types\">3.9 Mixing int with other types<\/h2>\n<p>When you mix types in one expression, C converts them automatically (implicit conversion). Two rules cause most bugs:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Smaller types (char, short) are promoted to int before arithmetic.<\/li>\n<li>If you mix signed and unsigned of the same size, the signed value is converted to unsigned.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>int a = -1;\nunsigned int b = 1;\n \nif (a &lt; b)\n    printf(\"a is smaller\\n\");\nelse\n    printf(\"b is smaller\\n\");   // this prints!<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Why? -1 is converted to unsigned, which becomes 4,294,967,295, bigger than 1. This is one of the most common hidden bugs. Avoid comparing signed and unsigned values.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Type casting changes a type on purpose:<\/strong><\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>int total = 7, count = 2;\nfloat avg = (float)total / count;   // 3.5, cast BEFORE dividing\nint x = (int)3.99;                  // 3, decimals are cut, not rounded<\/code><\/pre>\n<h1 id=\"4-int-in-embedded-systems-use-fixed-width-types\">4. int in embedded systems: use fixed-width types<\/h1>\n<p>Because int can be 2 bytes on one chip and 4 bytes on another, embedded code uses types from stdint.h whose size never changes.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Type<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Size<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Range<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Typical use<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>int8_t<\/code><\/td><td>1 byte<\/td><td>-128 to 127<\/td><td>Small signed values<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>uint8_t<\/code><\/td><td>1 byte<\/td><td>0 to 255<\/td><td>Raw bytes, 8-bit registers, buffers<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>int16_t<\/code><\/td><td>2 bytes<\/td><td>-32,768 to 32,767<\/td><td>Sensor readings with sign<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>uint16_t<\/code><\/td><td>2 bytes<\/td><td>0 to 65,535<\/td><td>ADC values, 16-bit registers<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>int32_t<\/code><\/td><td>4 bytes<\/td><td>-2.1 billion to 2.1 billion<\/td><td>General signed numbers<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>uint32_t<\/code><\/td><td>4 bytes<\/td><td>0 to 4.29 billion<\/td><td>32-bit registers, timestamps<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>int64_t / uint64_t<\/code><\/td><td>8 bytes<\/td><td>very large<\/td><td>Long counters<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>#include &lt;stdint.h&gt;\n \nuint8_t  led_state = 0;        // exactly 1 byte on every chip\nuint16_t adc_value = 3120;     // 12-bit ADC result fits\nuint32_t timestamp_ms = 0;\nint32_t  offset = -50000;<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Printing fixed-width types: the simplest portable way is to cast to a standard type.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>printf(\"%lu\\n\", (unsigned long)timestamp_ms);\nprintf(\"%d\\n\",  (int)led_state);<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"which-type-should-i-choose\">Which type should I choose?<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Situation<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Use<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Loop counter, simple count on a PC or ESP32<\/td><td>int<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Value that is never negative (length, count, register)<\/td><td>unsigned type (uint8_t, uint16_t, uint32_t)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Hardware register or byte buffer<\/td><td>uint8_t / uint16_t / uint32_t<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Value that can be negative and size matters<\/td><td>int16_t / int32_t<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Number may exceed 2 billion<\/td><td>int64_t or long long<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Code must run on both Arduino Uno and ESP32<\/td><td>Fixed-width types only<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h1 id=\"5-common-mistakes-practice-and-interview-questions\">5. Common mistakes, practice and interview questions<\/h1>\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes-with-int\">Common mistakes with int<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Mistake<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Fix<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Using an int before giving it a value (garbage inside)<\/td><td>Always initialise: int x = 0;<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>5 / 2 expecting 2.5<\/td><td>Use 5 / 2.0 or cast: (float)5 / 2<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Wrong format specifier (%f for int, %d for float)<\/td><td>Match the type: %d int, %u unsigned, %ld long, %lld long long, %f float<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Forgetting &amp; in scanf(\"%d\", &amp;x)<\/td><td>scanf needs the address of the variable<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Assuming int is always 4 bytes<\/td><td>Check with sizeof or use int32_t<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Comparing signed and unsigned values<\/td><td>Make both the same type, or cast carefully<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Overflow in factorial, sum or multiplication<\/td><td>Use a bigger type and check ranges<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Leading zero in a number (012)<\/td><td>Do not write leading zeros<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"practice-programs-write-each-one-yourself\">Practice programs (write each one yourself)<\/h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Print the size and the min and max value of every integer type on your computer.<\/li>\n<li>Read two ints and print their sum, difference, product, quotient and remainder.<\/li>\n<li>Swap two int variables using a temporary variable, then without one.<\/li>\n<li>Check whether a number is even or odd using %.<\/li>\n<li>Reverse the digits of an int (use % 10 and / 10 in a loop).<\/li>\n<li>Count the digits of an int.<\/li>\n<li>Write print_bits for an int (32 bits) and print 5, -5 and 255.<\/li>\n<li>Show unsigned overflow: increase an unsigned char past 255 and print it.<\/li>\n<li>Compute factorial for n = 1 to 15 with int. Find the first n where the answer becomes wrong. Then fix it with unsigned long long.<\/li>\n<li>Print the number 255 in decimal, hexadecimal and octal.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"interview-questions-with-short-answers\">Interview questions with short answers<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Q1. What is the size of int in C? It depends on the compiler and the processor. At least 16 bits by the standard; 32 bits on most modern systems (PC, ESP32, ARM), 16 bits on 8-bit AVR (Arduino Uno).<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q2. What is the range of a 32-bit signed int? -2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647, which is -2^31 to 2^31 - 1.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q3. How are negative numbers stored? In two's complement: invert all bits of the positive number and add 1. The left-most bit acts as the sign bit.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q4. Difference between signed and unsigned int? Signed can hold negative values; unsigned cannot but has twice the positive range.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q5. What happens on integer overflow? Unsigned wraps around (modulo 2^n). Signed overflow is undefined behaviour.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q6. What is the result of 7 / 2 in C? 3, because both operands are int and the decimal part is truncated.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q7. What is the result of -7 % 2? -1. The sign of the remainder follows the left operand (since C99).<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q8. Why use uint8_t instead of unsigned char or int in embedded code? Its size is guaranteed (exactly 8 bits), so the code behaves the same on every chip, and it clearly shows the data is a raw byte.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q9. What does this print: int a = -1; unsigned int b = 1; printf(\"%d\", a &lt; b); 0, because a is converted to unsigned (4,294,967,295) and the comparison is false.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Q10. What is the difference between int x = 012 and int x = 12? 012 is octal and equals 10; 12 is decimal and equals 12.<\/strong><\/p>\n<h1 id=\"6-quick-look-at-the-other-basic-types\">6. Quick look at the other basic types<\/h1>\n<p>Each of these deserves its own detailed chapter. Here is the short version so the overview is complete.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"char\">char<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Size: 1 byte. Stores one character such as 'A', or a small number.<\/li>\n<li>A character is stored as its ASCII number: 'A' is 65, 'a' is 97, '0' is 48.<\/li>\n<li>Use %c to print as a letter and %d to print the number.<\/li>\n<li>Whether plain char is signed or unsigned depends on the compiler, so use uint8_t for raw bytes.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>char ch = 'A';\nprintf(\"%c %d\\n\", ch, ch);    // A 65\nprintf(\"%c\\n\", ch + 1);        // B<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"float-and-double\">float and double<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Store numbers with a decimal point. They are stored in binary scientific form (IEEE 754), so many decimals are only approximate.<\/li>\n<li>float: 4 bytes, about 6 to 7 correct digits. double: 8 bytes, about 15 to 16 correct digits.<\/li>\n<li>Never compare two floats with ==. Compare the difference with a small tolerance instead.<\/li>\n<li>Many small microcontrollers have no floating-point hardware, so float maths is slow there. Prefer integers when you can (for example, store 25.5 degrees as 255 tenths).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>float a = 0.1f + 0.2f;\nprintf(\"%.10f\\n\", a);   // 0.3000000119 (not exactly 0.3)<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"void\">void<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Means \"no value\". Used for functions that return nothing (void blink(void)) and for generic pointers (void *).<\/li>\n<li>You cannot create a variable of type void.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<aside class=\"callout info\" style=\"--c:#2A7F8E\"><p><strong>Next step<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Practise all 10 programs from Part 5 before moving on. Once int feels easy, ask for the same detailed chapter on char, float/double and pointers.<\/p><\/aside>", "plan": "<header class=\"doc-head\"><p class=\"title\">C and Embedded C<\/p><p class=\"subtitle\">Complete Study Plan with Daily Calendar<\/p><p class=\"meta\">30 October 2026 to 30 November 2026<\/p><\/header><h1 id=\"1-how-this-plan-works\">1. How this plan works<\/h1>\n<p>The plan has 4 parts: the C language (2 weeks), Embedded C fundamentals (1 week), Embedded C on real hardware (1 week), and interview preparation (last days and December).<\/p>\n<p><strong>Golden rule: for every 1 hour of theory, spend 2 hours writing code. You learn programming with your hands, not by watching videos.<\/strong><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"daily-routine-college-days\">Daily routine (college days)<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Time<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>What to do<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Commute (bus)<\/td><td>Theory only: watch or read that day's topic. Note the 3 key points on your phone.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>10 am to 4 pm<\/td><td>College / course.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>6:30 to 7:30 pm<\/td><td>Reach home, freshen up, dinner, rest.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>7:30 to 9:30 pm<\/td><td>Practice: write code for that day's topic. Run it, break it, fix it.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>9:30 to 9:45 pm<\/td><td>Write 3 lines in a notebook: what I learned, what confused me, what I do tomorrow.<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"weekend-routine\">Weekend routine<\/h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Day<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>What to do<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Saturday (4 hrs)<\/td><td>Finish the weekly mini-project. Push the code to GitHub with a README.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Sunday (3 hrs)<\/td><td>Revise the week, then solve 10 interview questions out loud. Keep the evening free to rest.<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h1 id=\"2-weekly-overview\">2. Weekly overview<\/h1>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Week<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Dates<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Focus and weekend project<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Start<\/td><td>30 Oct to 1 Nov<\/td><td>C basics: data types, operators, control flow, loops<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Week 1<\/td><td>2 to 8 Nov<\/td><td>Functions, recursion, arrays, strings. Project 1: Contact book<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Week 2<\/td><td>9 to 15 Nov<\/td><td>Pointers, structs, dynamic memory, preprocessor, files. Project 2: Student record manager<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Week 3<\/td><td>16 to 22 Nov<\/td><td>Embedded C fundamentals: bits, volatile, memory, registers. Project 3: Bit manipulation library<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Week 4<\/td><td>23 to 29 Nov<\/td><td>GPIO, interrupts, timers, ADC, UART, I2C, SPI. Project 4: Ring buffer and state machine<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Final<\/td><td>30 Nov<\/td><td>Mock interview, GitHub cleanup, resume update<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h1 id=\"3-part-a-c-language-topics-in-detail\">3. Part A: C language topics in detail<\/h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-basics-and-data-types\">1. Basics and data types<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Compile and run cycle (source, compiler, executable)<\/li>\n<li>int, char, float, double, sizeof<\/li>\n<li>Variables, constants, type casting<\/li>\n<li>printf / scanf and format specifiers<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Practice: <\/strong>Print the size of every data type and add two numbers typed by the user.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"2-operators-and-control-flow\">2. Operators and control flow<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Arithmetic, relational, logical, ternary operators; precedence<\/li>\n<li>if / else if / else, switch<\/li>\n<li>for, while, do-while, break, continue<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Practice: <\/strong>Calculator using switch; leap year; prime check; pyramid patterns.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"3-functions-and-recursion\">3. Functions and recursion<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Declaration, definition, call<\/li>\n<li>Call by value and return values<\/li>\n<li>Recursion and how the stack grows<\/li>\n<li>Header files (split code into .c and .h)<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Practice: <\/strong>Factorial, Fibonacci, GCD, power, sum of digits using recursion.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"4-arrays-and-strings\">4. Arrays and strings<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>1D and 2D arrays<\/li>\n<li>Strings as char arrays ending in '\\0'<\/li>\n<li>string.h: strlen, strcpy, strcmp, strcat<\/li>\n<li>Searching and sorting basics<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Practice: <\/strong>Write your own strlen, strcpy, strcmp; reverse a string; palindrome; matrix addition.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"5-pointers-most-important\">5. Pointers (most important)<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Address-of (&amp;) and dereference (*)<\/li>\n<li>Pointer arithmetic; arrays and pointers are closely related<\/li>\n<li>Pointer to pointer, void pointer, NULL pointer<\/li>\n<li>Dangling and wild pointers<\/li>\n<li>Function pointers and callbacks<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Practice: <\/strong>Swap two numbers using pointers; traverse an array using only pointers; call a function through a function pointer.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"6-structures-unions-enums-typedef\">6. Structures, unions, enums, typedef<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>struct definition and arrays of structs<\/li>\n<li>union: members share the same memory<\/li>\n<li>enum for named constants<\/li>\n<li>typedef to shorten type names<\/li>\n<li>Bit-fields<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Practice: <\/strong>Student record array; print sizeof a struct and a union and explain the difference.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"7-dynamic-memory\">7. Dynamic memory<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>malloc, calloc, realloc, free<\/li>\n<li>Memory leaks and double free<\/li>\n<li>Stack vs heap<\/li>\n<li>Linked list basics<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Practice: <\/strong>Create an array with malloc, grow it with realloc, free it; build a singly linked list (insert, delete, reverse).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"8-storage-classes-and-preprocessor\">8. Storage classes and preprocessor<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>auto, register, static, extern<\/li>\n<li>static variable inside a function keeps its value<\/li>\n<li>#define, macros with arguments, #ifdef, #ifndef<\/li>\n<li>Include guards<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Practice: <\/strong>Static counter function; MAX/MIN macros; a project split into 3 files with a header and include guard.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"9-file-handling\">9. File handling<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>fopen, fclose, modes (r, w, a)<\/li>\n<li>fprintf, fscanf, fgets, fputs<\/li>\n<li>fread and fwrite for binary data<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Practice: <\/strong>Save and load student records to a file.<\/p>\n<h1 id=\"4-part-b-embedded-c-topics-in-detail\">4. Part B: Embedded C topics in detail<\/h1>\n<h2 id=\"10-bitwise-operators-and-bit-manipulation\">10. Bitwise operators and bit manipulation<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>&amp;, |, ^, ~, &lt;&lt;, &gt;&gt;<\/li>\n<li>Set, clear, toggle and check a bit using masks<\/li>\n<li>Count set bits, check power of 2, swap nibbles, reverse bits<\/li>\n<li>Why embedded code uses bit operations on registers<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Practice: <\/strong>Write macros SET_BIT, CLEAR_BIT, TOGGLE_BIT, CHECK_BIT and test them.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"11-qualifiers-and-fixed-width-types\">11. Qualifiers and fixed-width types<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>volatile: tells the compiler a value can change outside the code (hardware, ISR)<\/li>\n<li>const and const volatile (read-only status register)<\/li>\n<li>static for file-private variables<\/li>\n<li>uint8_t, uint16_t, uint32_t from stdint.h<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Practice: <\/strong>Explain in your own words why a flag changed in an ISR must be volatile.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"12-memory-layout-startup-and-padding\">12. Memory layout, startup and padding<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Flash vs RAM<\/li>\n<li>Sections: .text, .data, .bss, stack, heap<\/li>\n<li>What startup code does before main()<\/li>\n<li>Linker script idea<\/li>\n<li>Struct padding and alignment<\/li>\n<li>Endianness (little vs big)<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Practice: <\/strong>Write a program that detects endianness; print struct sizes with and without padding.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"13-registers-and-hardware-access\">13. Registers and hardware access<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Memory-mapped I/O<\/li>\n<li>Pointer to a fixed address: *(volatile uint32_t*)0x40021000<\/li>\n<li>Register structs and macros<\/li>\n<li>Function pointers for callbacks and drivers<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Practice: <\/strong>Simulate a GPIO register in a plain C program and control its bits.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"14-gpio-and-debouncing\">14. GPIO and debouncing<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Input and output pins, pull-up and pull-down<\/li>\n<li>Push-pull vs open-drain<\/li>\n<li>Polling<\/li>\n<li>Button bounce and software debouncing<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Practice: <\/strong>Button toggles an LED with proper debounce (ESP32 or Arduino).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"15-interrupts-and-isr\">15. Interrupts and ISR<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Vector table and interrupt priority<\/li>\n<li>ISR rules: keep short, no printf, no blocking, use volatile flags<\/li>\n<li>Race conditions and critical sections<\/li>\n<li>Polling vs interrupt<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Practice: <\/strong>Button interrupt that sets a flag; main loop handles the work.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"16-timers-pwm-and-adc\">16. Timers, PWM and ADC<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Prescaler, overflow, compare match<\/li>\n<li>Timer interrupt for a periodic task<\/li>\n<li>PWM duty cycle and frequency (LED dimming, servo)<\/li>\n<li>ADC resolution and sampling<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Practice: <\/strong>Blink an LED with a timer (no delay); read a sensor with ADC and dim an LED with PWM.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"17-communication-protocols\">17. Communication protocols<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>UART: baud rate, start/stop bits, parity<\/li>\n<li>I2C: SDA/SCL, address, ACK, pull-up resistors<\/li>\n<li>SPI: MOSI, MISO, SCK, CS and modes<\/li>\n<li>When to choose which<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Practice: <\/strong>Print sensor data over UART; scan I2C addresses; read one SPI device.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"18-embedded-design-patterns\">18. Embedded design patterns<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Circular (ring) buffer<\/li>\n<li>Finite state machine<\/li>\n<li>Watchdog timer<\/li>\n<li>Low-power sleep modes<\/li>\n<li>Intro to RTOS: tasks, semaphore, queue<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Practice: <\/strong>Ring buffer for UART receive; traffic light as a state machine.<\/p>\n<h1 id=\"5-daily-calendar-30-oct-to-30-nov\">5. Daily calendar (30 Oct to 30 Nov)<\/h1>\n<p>Tick the box when the day is done. Weekends are shaded. If you miss a day, do not panic: move the topic to the next weekend slot instead of skipping it.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th><strong>Date<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Day<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Topic<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Practice (evening)<\/strong><\/th><th><strong>Done<\/strong><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>30 Oct<\/td><td>Fri<\/td><td>Setup + data types, variables, I/O<\/td><td>Install GCC or use OnlineGDB. Print sizeof of every type. Add two typed numbers.<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>31 Oct<\/td><td>Sat<\/td><td>Operators, if/else, switch<\/td><td>Calculator with switch, even/odd, leap year. Solve 10 small programs.<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>1 Nov<\/td><td>Sun<\/td><td>Loops + revision<\/td><td>Patterns, prime numbers, reverse a number, sum of digits. 10 questions.<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>2 Nov<\/td><td>Mon<\/td><td>Functions<\/td><td>Write max, power, GCD, isPrime as functions. Split into .c and .h.<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>3 Nov<\/td><td>Tue<\/td><td>Recursion<\/td><td>Factorial, Fibonacci, sum of digits, decimal to binary.<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>4 Nov<\/td><td>Wed<\/td><td>Arrays<\/td><td>Min/max, reverse, sort, search, 2D matrix addition.<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>5 Nov<\/td><td>Thu<\/td><td>Strings<\/td><td>Write your own strlen, strcpy, strcmp. Reverse, palindrome.<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>6 Nov<\/td><td>Fri<\/td><td>Matrix + string problems<\/td><td>Transpose, multiply, vowel count, anagram check.<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>7 Nov<\/td><td>Sat<\/td><td>PROJECT 1: Contact book<\/td><td>Arrays + strings + functions. Push to GitHub.<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>8 Nov<\/td><td>Sun<\/td><td>Revise week 1 + interview Qs<\/td><td>Solve 10 interview questions on arrays, strings, loops.<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>9 Nov<\/td><td>Mon<\/td><td>Pointers: basics<\/td><td>&amp;, *, NULL, pointer to pointer. Draw memory diagrams on paper.<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>10 Nov<\/td><td>Tue<\/td><td>Pointer arithmetic + arrays<\/td><td>Swap with pointers; traverse arrays and strings with pointers.<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>11 Nov<\/td><td>Wed<\/td><td>Structs, unions, enums, typedef<\/td><td>Student struct array; compare sizeof struct vs union.<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>12 Nov<\/td><td>Thu<\/td><td>Dynamic memory<\/td><td>malloc, realloc, free. Deliberately create a leak and fix it.<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>13 Nov<\/td><td>Fri<\/td><td>Storage classes + preprocessor<\/td><td>Static counter, macros, include guards, 3-file project.<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>14 Nov<\/td><td>Sat<\/td><td>File handling + PROJECT 2<\/td><td>Student record manager that saves and loads from a file.<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>15 Nov<\/td><td>Sun<\/td><td>Revise + linked list<\/td><td>Singly linked list: insert, delete, reverse.<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>16 Nov<\/td><td>Mon<\/td><td>Embedded: bitwise operators<\/td><td>&amp;, |, ^, ~, &lt;&lt;, &gt;&gt; with small examples. Binary and hex practice.<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>17 Nov<\/td><td>Tue<\/td><td>Bit manipulation<\/td><td>SET_BIT, CLEAR_BIT, TOGGLE_BIT, CHECK_BIT macros. Count set bits.<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>18 Nov<\/td><td>Wed<\/td><td>volatile, const, static, fixed-width types<\/td><td>Rewrite earlier code with uint8_t/uint32_t. Explain volatile aloud.<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>19 Nov<\/td><td>Thu<\/td><td>Memory layout, padding, endianness<\/td><td>Endianness check program; struct padding experiments.<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>20 Nov<\/td><td>Fri<\/td><td>Registers + function pointers<\/td><td>Simulate a GPIO register in C; driver using function pointers.<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>21 Nov<\/td><td>Sat<\/td><td>PROJECT 3: Bit manipulation library<\/td><td>Header + source + test file. Push to GitHub.<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>22 Nov<\/td><td>Sun<\/td><td>Revise + 15 Embedded C questions<\/td><td>Answer each question out loud in 1 minute.<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>23 Nov<\/td><td>Mon<\/td><td>GPIO + debouncing<\/td><td>Button toggles LED with software debounce on your board.<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>24 Nov<\/td><td>Tue<\/td><td>Interrupts + ISR<\/td><td>Button interrupt sets a volatile flag; main loop handles it.<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>25 Nov<\/td><td>Wed<\/td><td>Timers + PWM<\/td><td>Blink with a timer (no delay). LED dimming with PWM.<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>26 Nov<\/td><td>Thu<\/td><td>ADC + UART<\/td><td>Read a sensor, print values over serial.<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>27 Nov<\/td><td>Fri<\/td><td>I2C + SPI<\/td><td>I2C scanner; read one I2C or SPI sensor.<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>28 Nov<\/td><td>Sat<\/td><td>PROJECT 4: Ring buffer + state machine<\/td><td>UART ring buffer and traffic light state machine. Push to GitHub.<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>29 Nov<\/td><td>Sun<\/td><td>Watchdog, sleep modes, revision<\/td><td>Read about watchdog and low power. Revise weak topics.<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>30 Nov<\/td><td>Mon<\/td><td>FINAL: mock interview + GitHub cleanup<\/td><td>Explain each project in 2 minutes. Update README files and resume.<\/td><td>☐<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h1 id=\"6-interview-questions-to-master\">6. Interview questions to master<\/h1>\n<p>Answer each one out loud in under 1 minute. If you cannot explain it simply, you do not know it yet.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>What is the difference between a pointer and an array?<\/li>\n<li>What is a dangling pointer? How do you avoid it?<\/li>\n<li>Difference between malloc and calloc?<\/li>\n<li>What is a memory leak?<\/li>\n<li>What does static do (inside a function, and at file level)?<\/li>\n<li>Difference between struct and union?<\/li>\n<li>What is struct padding? Why does it happen?<\/li>\n<li>What does volatile mean? Give an example where it is needed.<\/li>\n<li>Can a variable be both const and volatile? Example?<\/li>\n<li>How do you set, clear, toggle and check a bit?<\/li>\n<li>How do you check if a number is a power of 2?<\/li>\n<li>How do you detect endianness in code?<\/li>\n<li>What are the .text, .data, .bss sections?<\/li>\n<li>What happens before main() runs on a microcontroller?<\/li>\n<li>What rules should an ISR follow?<\/li>\n<li>Why should you not call printf inside an ISR?<\/li>\n<li>Polling vs interrupt: when to use which?<\/li>\n<li>What is debouncing and how do you do it in software?<\/li>\n<li>Difference between UART, I2C and SPI?<\/li>\n<li>Why do I2C lines need pull-up resistors?<\/li>\n<li>What is PWM and how is duty cycle calculated?<\/li>\n<li>What is a watchdog timer?<\/li>\n<li>What is a circular buffer and where is it used?<\/li>\n<li>What is a function pointer? Where is it used in embedded code?<\/li>\n<li>Swap two numbers without a temporary variable.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h1 id=\"7-tools-and-resources\">7. Tools and resources<\/h1>\n<ul>\n<li>Compiler: GCC on your laptop, or OnlineGDB in the browser<\/li>\n<li>Practice problems: HackerRank (C domain), GeeksforGeeks, Exercism (C track)<\/li>\n<li>Hardware: your ESP32, ESP8266 and Arduino boards for Part B<\/li>\n<li>Notes: one notebook with a page for each topic (3 key points + 1 example)<\/li>\n<li>Code storage: GitHub, one repository per project with a clear README<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h1 id=\"8-after-30-november-december-plan\">8. After 30 November (December plan)<\/h1>\n<ul>\n<li>Week 1: Revise pointers, bit manipulation and ISR. Redo weak interview questions.<\/li>\n<li>Week 2: Rewrite 2 of your older Arduino projects in clean embedded C with proper structure.<\/li>\n<li>Week 3: Mock interviews with a friend or mentor. Explain every project on your resume.<\/li>\n<li>Week 4: Apply daily to embedded and IoT roles; keep solving 2 problems a day.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h1 id=\"9-tips-to-stay-on-track\">9. Tips to stay on track<\/h1>\n<ul>\n<li>Study at the same time every day. Habit beats motivation.<\/li>\n<li>Phone on silent during the 7:30 to 9:30 slot.<\/li>\n<li>Do not skip pointers or bit manipulation. They are the most asked topics.<\/li>\n<li>Type every program yourself. Never copy and paste.<\/li>\n<li>Sleep at least 7 hours. A tired brain learns nothing.<\/li>\n<\/ul>"}};